1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._R._R._Tolkien '''John Ronald Reuel Tolkien''', CBE FRSL] was an English writer, poet, philologist, and university professor, best known as the author of the classic high fantasy wo
2 CONC rks ''The Hobbit'', ''The Lord of the Rings'', and ''The Silmarillion''.  He died 2 September 1973 (aged 81) Bournemouth, Hampshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tolkien was born on 3 January 1892 in Bloemfontein in the Orange Free State (now Free State Province in South Africa) to [http://www.geni.com/people/Mabel-Suffield/6000000009688582123 Mabel (née Suffield)] and [http://www.geni.com
2 CONC /people/Arthur-Tolkien/6000000009688428722 Arthur Reuel Tolkien], an English bank manager. His parents left England when his father was promoted to head the Bloemfontein office of the British bank for which he worked. Tolkien had 
2 CONC one sibling, his younger brother, [http://www.geni.com/people/Hilary-Tolkien/6000000009690132896 Hilary Arthur Reuel], who was born on 17 February 1894.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of three, Tolkien's father died of rheumatic fever, and his mother took he and his brother to live with her parents, [http://www.geni.com/people/John-Suffield/6000000009690008861 John] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Em
2 CONC ily-Jane-Suffield/6000000009690152634 Edith Jane Suffield], in Kings Heath, Birmingham. His maternal grandparents were Baptists and owned a shop in the city center.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Suffield family had run various businesses out of the same building, called Lamb House, since the early 19th century. From 1812 Tolkien's great-great-grandfather [http://www.geni.com/people/William-Suffield/6000000010014496778
2 CONC  William Suffield] had a book and stationery shop there; from 1826 Tolkien's great-grandfather, also named [http://www.geni.com/people/John-Suffield/6000000009690732907 John Suffield], had a drapery and hosiery business there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1904, when Tolkien was 12, his mother, Mabel Tolkien, died of acute diabetes at Fern Cottage in Rednal, which she was then renting. Prior to her death, she had assigned the guardianship of her sons to Fr. Francis Xavier Morgan 
2 CONC of the Birmingham Oratory, who was assigned to bring them up as good Catholics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tolkien married [http://www.geni.com/people/Edith-Mary-Bratt/6000000009691322694 Edith Mary Bratt], whom he met at the age of 16, at St. Mary Immaculate Roman Catholic Church, Warwick, on 22 March 1916. The Tolkiens had four child
2 CONC ren: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.geni.com/people/John-Tolkien/6000000009690976800 John Francis Reuel]
2 CONT * [http://www.geni.com/people/Michael-Tolkien/6000000009691674270 Michael Hilary Reuel]
2 CONT * [http://www.geni.com/people/Christopher-Tolkien/6000000009691630239 Christopher John Reuel]
2 CONT *[http://www.geni.com/people/Priscilla-Tolkien/6000000009691769286 Priscilla Mary Anne Reuel]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lifelong member of their small group of close friends called themselves the T.C.B.S. (Tea Club and Barrovian Society, named after the Barrow Stores at King Edward’s, where the group often met)
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Vincent/6000000101027520846
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Lieutenant-Robert/6000000100997377425
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Lieutenant-Geoffrey/6000000101013490896
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Christopher/6000000101013722844
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/details/L7N7-YGR
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/1456/jrr-tolkien
2 CONT
2 CONT Author. He is famed for his Middle Earth series of books, including the bestselling The Lord of the Rings trilogy. Born to a bank clerk and his wife in Bloemfontein, South Africa, he was the eldest son of two boys. When his father
2 CONC  died in 1896, his mother, Mabel Suffield Tolkien, took the two boys and returned to the West Midlands of England, where she had grown up. When he was twelve, his mother died suddenly of diabetes, and a Catholic priest, Father Fra
2 CONC ncis Morgan, eventually took in the two orphans. Showing a clear gift for languages, Ronald (as he was known to his friends) went to Exeter College in Oxford, England, where he obtained a degree in June 1915. World War I had broke
2 CONC n out, so Ronald enlisted, and, due to his mastery of several ancient and modern European languages, was quickly promoted to 2nd Lieutenant in the Lancashire Fusiliers. After participating in the Battle of the Somme in 1916, he ca
2 CONC me down with trench fever and, in November 1916, was evacuated back to England to convalesce. Most of his school friends had been killed in the war, and they served as the inspiration for many of the stories in his first book, The
2 CONC  Book of Lost Tales, published after his death. With the ending of the war in 1918, he demobilized and became an Assistant Lexicographer on the New Oxford English Dictionary. In 1920, he was hired as Associate Professor in English
2 CONC  Language at the University of Leeds. In 1925, he was appointed to the Rawlinson and Bosworth Professorship of Anglo-Saxon at Oxford University. In 1945, he became the Merton Professor of English Language and Literature at Oxford,
2 CONC  a position he retained until his retirement in 1959. In 1937, his first major book, The Hobbit, was published. When The Hobbit was immediately successful, his publisher asked for additional writings, which became his 16-year effo
2 CONC rt to write The Lord of the Rings trilogy, first published in 1954. Receiving mixed literary reviews, the trilogy was made by BBC Radio into a 12-episode radio program in 1956, which made the book a best seller. In 1965, a pirated
2 CONC  paperback version of The Lord of the Rings was printed and rapidly achieved cult status by 1968 for its imagination and poeticness. Suddenly rich, he moved to an unlisted address in Bournemouth, to escape his numerous fans. After
2 CONC  his wife passed away, he returned to Oxford, where he died on September 2, 1973. Buried in Wolvercote Cemetery, Oxfordshire, the names Luthien and Beren on their grave marker refer to their names for each other, which also served
2 CONC  as the inspiration for two characters in his books about Middle-Earth. Since his death, many of his manuscripts have been published under his son Christopher Tolkien's editorship, and nearly all have been best-sellers.
