1 NAME Alan Arnold /Klass/
2 GIVN Alan Arnold
2 _MARNM Klass
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 AUG 1907
2 PLAC Ekaterinoslav
2 ADDR
3 CITY Dnepropetrovsk
3 STAE Dnipropetrovs'ka
3 CTRY Ukraine
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 JAN 2000
1 OCCU
1 TITL Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Alan Klass MD, FRCS (Edin and Can), LLD (Hon) (U of M). 1907-2000 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan Arnold Klass was born in Ekaterinoslav, now Dnepropetrovsk, Ukraine, in 1907, the sixth child of what was to be an octet. Shortly before the onset of the Great War, in 1914, his father, William Klass fled Imperial Russia, bri
2 CONC nging his family directly to Winnipeg, where he established a dry cleaning and tailoring business on Osborne Street between River and Stradbrook Avenues. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan attended Gladstone, Earl Grey and Kelvin High Schools. He was a gifted student who moved far beyond his age cohort in school. He chose to do a full Arts degree and then entered medical school, assisted by loans from his famil
2 CONC y and from the Winnipeg Jewish Community. He graduated in 1932, then completed a surgical residency at the Winnipeg General Hospital, and undertook additional anatomical training as a student of the great anatomist J.C.B.Grant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was determined to acquire a surgical fellowship from the then renowned University of Edinburgh, but having little money, took a series of peculiar jobs for a surgical trainee, including working on a farm despite his fear of hor
2 CONC ses, and painting electricity transmission pylons despite a severe case of fear of heights from which he suffered all his life. But these jobs, in the great depression, were needed to pay his way to Halifax where he would board a 
2 CONC ship for the United Kingdom. He gave most of his earnings to his mother for the only holiday of her life, then made it to a freighter and worked his passage across the Atlantic. His only sustenance on the train ride east from Winn
2 CONC ipeg consisted of a tin of sardines, opened with great difficulty because the key was missing. Forever after, a tin of sardines, especially with a slice of raw onion was considered, by Alan Klass, to be a king’s feast. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the United Kingdom he learned the art and science of surgery, obtaining the Fellowship of the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh by examination. He supported himself with locums in the south of England, and as ship's surgeo
2 CONC n on the British India Lines SS Mombasa and SS Madura, sailing through the Suez Canal from London to Calcutta and Bombay with stops in Kenya and Ethiopia. It was on those trips that he began his, eventually, extensive collection o
2 CONC f African carvings, and also where he acquired his great love of Indian food. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return to Winnipeg in 1937, he embarked on a private practice in Surgery, locating his office in the Boyd Building in downtown Winnipeg. His assistant was his baby sister Beatrice, who served as receptionist and billing cle
2 CONC rk. She later reported that her brother actually billed for less than half of his cases. In those days before Medicare, he felt that since most of his patients simply couldn’t afford to pay, he wouldn’t bother to send them bills. 
2 CONC This financially unrewarding practice was interrupted by the onset of World War II. Despite having just married Helen Jacob, he enlisted in the Canadian Army Medical Corps on the day war was declared against Germany. He served as 
2 CONC medical officer at Camp Shiloh, Manitoba and was then stationed at an internment camp for German nationals at Red Rock, Ontario. Letters and mementoes he received from that secondment reveal that he was revered by staff and intern
2 CONC ees in equal measure. He eventually served more than 2 years overseas, in France and in England. He disembarked at Juno Beach on D-Day plus one, where having established a surgical triage station, he operated for 36 consecutive ho
2 CONC urs on badly injured Canadian and German soldiers trucked back to the beach from the battles of the hedgerows. He then served as field surgeon in St. Omer, France as the Normandy campaign continued. He was one of the last Canadian
2 CONC  Army Surgeons to be demobilized after VE-Day. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned to Winnipeg, and after studying the chaotic health systems of that era, he, along with a small group of local physicians and surgeons, many from similar military backgrounds, established the Mall Medical Group, one of 
2 CONC the first models of a health maintenance organization in North America. This clinic depended upon a unique contract forged among three partner groups; the organization of local garment manufacturers (which, ironically, was led by 
2 CONC his father-in-law, Mr. Ben Jacob of Jacob and Crowley Manufacturing Ltd), the local chapter of the International Ladies Garment Workers Union, and the doctors of the Mall Medical Group. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During his long surgical career, Dr. Klass established a reputation as a careful craftsman and innovative surgical pioneer. He was the first surgeon to record the direct removal of an embolic blood clot from the superior mesenteri
2 CONC c artery, an early milestone in the field of vascular surgery. He published regularly in the scientific literature, especially on the topics of vascular surgery, arterial embolectomy and developmental aspects of pilonidal sinuses.
2 CONC  He was a popular professor of surgery and clinical anatomy, teaching several generations of University of Manitoba medical students in memorable lectures about the developmental rotation of the gut and the life of a doctor. He wa
2 CONC s deeply concerned with professional ethics and standards, writing notable papers on the subject. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an active supporter of the University of Manitoba, and was appointed to the faculty in the Departments of Surgery and Anatomy, elected to the Presidency of the Alumni Association and to the University Board of Governors. He
2 CONC  was awarded the honorary degree, LLD, for his many services to his alma mater. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His community service was extensive as well. He was President of the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Manitoba, President of the Canadian Institute of International Affairs and a member of the Rotary Club, for which organizat
2 CONC ion he was particularly involved for many years as President of the Model United Nations Association. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a man of many hobbies, including collecting old cars, African sculpture and English portrait miniatures. He invested in high fidelity sound equipment before the concept of stereo was known. As part of an ill fated venture i
2 CONC nto the kosher meat packing business, he became the only practising surgeon known to be a card carrying member of the Union of Amalgamated Meat Cutters of America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest recognized contribution to the Province of Manitoba resulted from his Chairmanship of a Provincial Commission established in the early 1970’s to investigate the cost of pharmaceuticals in Manitoba. Because of this com
2 CONC mission’s recommendations, the first Pharmacare program in Canada was established, the precursor of the drug insurance plan now in operation. His studies for this Commission led to his writing a book oddly entitled “There’s Gold i
2 CONC n Them Thar Pills; An Inquiry into the Medical-industrial Complex", published in 1975 by Penguin. The book was topical when published and has proved to be prophetic to this day, as was a playscript he began but never finished - be
2 CONC fore the thalidomide scandal - on the subject of the systematic concealment by the pharmaceutical industry of dangerous side effects of new drugs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During all of this activity, Alan Klass practised medicine and surgery. He earned the respect of his colleagues and was loved by his patients. Beyond the fact of his skills, there resided a rare human being, a man of good judgemen
2 CONC t and humanity, with a twinkle in his eye, and a listening heart. He gathered whole families under his spell, and it was a rare Sunday that a surprise package of fresh clotted cream, or Kulbasa or Panini didn’t arrive at his door 
2 CONC as testament to his patients’ appreciation. Long after his retirement from practice, in 1985, cards and calls from his beloved patients continued to arrive. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1990 he had a cerebral stroke that began a process of permanent loss of recent memory, and in 1991 the death of his wife of 52 years, Helen, brought an end to his autonomy. He spent the rest of his life with his son, Dr. Daniel
2 CONC  Klass, and his family in Berwyn PA, with annual visits to his daughter, Baillie Tolkien, and her family in France. In the deepening fog of memory loss, he magically maintained his courtesy and kindness, gentle charm and humour. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His story was in many ways a typical one for an immigrant to Canada of that epoch. His days nearly spanned the 20th century, and included two great wars and a debilitating depression. But throughout those hard times he embraced hi
2 CONC s work, his profession, his colleagues, his patients, his friends and family, with a rare combination of optimism, critical detachment, curiosity and love. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A biographical note written by Baillie Tolkien and Daniel Klass, children of Alan and Helen Klass for the inaugural event of the Alan Klass Program for Health Equity at the University of Manitoba Faculty of Medicine, April 2008. 
