1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Christian Edmonstone''' was born in 1473 in Strathblane, Stirlingshire, Scotland. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-20727424 Sir Archibald Edmonstone, 3rd Laird of Duntreath] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-20727
2 CONC 423 Janet Schaw]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian married [https://www.geni.com/profile-38484576 John Ross, 2nd Lord of Halkhead] on September 27, 1490 in Hawkhead, Renfrewshire, Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-54663629 Ninian Ross, 3rd Lord of Halkhead]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34721004845 Thomas Ross]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34721004832 Andrew Ross]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-64238935 Helen Ross].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in May 1551 in Scotland. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ====BOOK
2 CONT * '''McGregor, G. (2022)''' ''The Red Books of Scotland''. 4th ed., pp. 173-75. Glasgow, Scotland: University of & Glasgow Press. Available at: [https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/62525/2016 Ancestry.com]:—
2 CONT ** Sir Archibald Edmonstone, 3rd of Duntreath, succeeded his father and was served heir to him on 20 April 1487, and infeft in the lands of Duntreath and others, as his heir on 1 June following [4GD97/1/22]. He m. firstly, to Jane
2 CONC t, daughter of Sir James Schaw of Sauchie, with whom, on his own resignation, he had a charter under the Great Seal for the lands of Cambuswallace on 14 February 1491[C2/12/261. Printed Vol. 1424-1513. No. 2008], and secondly, bef
2 CONC ore 27 April 1498, to Janet Melville[Acta Dom Conc. Vol. ii pp. 176-77. Her mother is then referred to as Christian Balfour, wife of Alexander Inglis of Tarvit], and d. before 31 May 1503. He was father of:
2 CONT *** Sir William Edmonstone, 4th of Duntreath, (see below).
2 CONT ***  James Edmonstone, was infeft in the lands of Ardbechlorn and Derry, in the Earldom of Strathearn, on 26 June 1506, and is then styled brother to William Edmonstone of Duntreath.7 He disponed those lands to James Reidheugh of 
2 CONC Tulliechettill on 25 February 1511,1 and was father of…etc.
2 CONT ***  Janet Edmonstone, m. William Graham, 1st Earl of Montrose, (he was widow of Annabella, daughter of John, 1st Lord Drummond, and after Janet’s death, he m. thirdly, to Christina, daughter of Thomas Wavan of Stevenson, he was k
2 CONC . at the battle of Flodden in September of 1513).
2 CONT ***  Catherine Edmonstone, m. John Montgomery, Master of Eglinton, eldest son and heir of Hugh, Lord Montgomery, (c/m 1 June 1498 [GD3/2/1/16]).
2 CONT ***  Christian Edmonstone, m. firstly, to John, 2nd Lord Ross of Halkheid, and had issue; and secondly, to Mr. George Knolls from whom she was divorced in 1514 on the grounds that she and Grisel Rattray, his first wife, were withi
2 CONC n the forbidden degrees of consanguinity [Liber Officialias Sancti Andreas. No. 3].
2 CONT ***  Margaret Edmonstone, m. by 2 January 1509, to George Buchannan of that Ilk, when they had a joint charter for the lands of Strathyre, (he survived her and m. secondly, by April of 1530, Janet Cunningham. He d. in 1561,
2 CONT ***  Beatrix Edmonstone, m. James Muschet of Burnbank, (he d. on 13 September 1546 [Dunblane Tests], and had issue.
2 CONT ***  He also had a natural son: James Edmonstone in Balewin, had letters of legitimation under the Great Seal on 2 September 1533, in which he is styled natural son to the late Archibald Edmonstone of Duntreath [RMS 1546-1580. No.
2 CONC  838]. He had issue, (see Edmonstone of Broich & Spittal).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===='''''The Complete Peerage''''', Vol. 11, pp. 156-57
2 CONT * SIR JOHN ROSS of Halkhead was a witness on 6 August 1447, as ‘Jon Rosse de Haukede’.[Reg. Mag. Sig., fol. Vol. 243, 1] He was appointed heritable constable of the castle of Renfrew, with a grant of the custom at the principal fa
2 CONC irs of tat borough.[Fraser’s Maxwells of Pollok, 147]  He was succeeded by another Sir John Ross of Halkhead, probably his son,
2 CONT ** '''I. 1500 or 1501''' — 2. JOHN (ROSS), LORD Ross of Halkhead [S.], grandson and h., being s. and h. of Robert Ross and Agnes and his wife, both abovenamed, was returned heir of his mother in the barony of Melville, 16 May 1496
2 CONC ; knighted before 3I May 1499, when his grandfather granted to his h. ap. "''Johanni Ross de Malevyn militi''," the lands of Walterstoun, co. Linlithgow.[''Reg. Mag. Sig''. [S.], vol. ii, no. 2499.] James IV, 11 Mar. 1501/2, confi
2 CONC rmed the grants made to his late grandfather by the King's father and grandfather of the island. The King's Inch in the water of Clyde near Kenfrew.[''Idem'', no. 2629. See too ''Reg. Sec. Sig''. [S.], vol. i, no. 784.] He was gra
2 CONC nted the wardship of the lands of the late Sir Stephen Lockhart, of Cleghorn, 8 Feb. 1505/6. James IV Visited him at Halkhead, 25 Apr. 1506.[The King gave drink-money to the masons there (Accounts of the L. High Treasurer [S.], vo
2 CONC l. iii, pp. xxv, 192).]
2 CONT ** He '''m.''', before 27 Sep. 1490, '''Christian''', 2nd da. of Sir Archibald EDMONSTONE, of Duntreath. He d. 9 Sep. 1513, being slain at the battle of Flodden. [See vol. v, Appendix D, for a list of the Scottish nobility slain a
2 CONC t Flodden.] His widow[see Fn. below.] '''m.''' George KNOLLIS, but the marriage was annulled about 1515, on account of her relationship to his 1st wife, Grizel Rattray. She d. May 1551.
2 CONT *** Fn. Her procurator protested, 18 Aug. 1517; "that sen sche is reddy to pay hir part for the furnesing of the speris to the bordouris and to lay the samyn down before the lordis, that sche suld nocht be pundit" (''Acts of the L
2 CONC ords of the Council [S.] in Public Affairs'', 1501-54, P. 100).]
2 CONT
2 CONT
