1 NOTE Ist Earl of Queensbury
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=f1ef3ea1-c060-4af0-bcc8-10865e46a1c4&tid=2456826&pid=71178267
1 NOTE Drumlanrig in the Queensberry Estate is situated astride the valley of the River Nith, its focal point being Drumlanrig Castle. Sir James Douglas of Drumlanrig, King Robert Bruce's right hand man, was the founder of what later gre
2 CONC w into the
2 CONT Queensberry Estate as it is today.
1 NOTE The present Castle was built of local sandstone from 1679 - 1691 on the site of an old Douglas stronghold. It is built round an open courtyard with a circular staircase tower in each corner. Drumlanrig is one of the first and most
2 CONC  important
2 CONT Renaissance buildings in the grand manner in Scottish domestic architecture.
1 NOTE 120 000 acres of the Queensberry Estate surround Drumlanrig Castle - "the Douglas properties". Most of the Estate is tenanted farms. Many of these are hill farms producing lambs and beef cattle. The 3000 acres of in hand farms con
2 CONC centrate
2 CONT mainly on dairy farming althought there are some sheep, beef cattle and arable crops, including potatoes. The high rainfall in this area favours livestock and milk rather than cereals.
1 NOTE 120 000 acres of the Queensberry Estate surround Drumlanrig Castle - "the Douglas properties". Most of the Estate is tenanted farms. Many of these are hill farms producing lambs and beef cattle. The 3000 acres of in hand farms con
2 CONC centrate
