1 NOTE {geni:about_me} >'''Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colin_Campbell,_1st_Earl_of_Argyll Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll]'''
2 CONT >There is good reason not to  and copy and paste entire Wikipedia write-ups in Geni profile overviews. Though Wikipedia's bio for Colin is rather replete, it's subject to future updates, source additions, and corrections, If it we
2 CONC re ever to become apparent that Wikipedia would become extinct, that'd be the only reason to splash it's content into overview or posting PDF's of it (if it's scholarly and replete) in sources. I removed the copy-and-paste jobs fr
2 CONC om Wikipedia for this reason.—Ken Shelley, 27 Feb 2026.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Archibald Campbell, Master of Campbell and Elizabeth Somerville. Married Isabel, daughter of John Stewart of Lorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/356/mode/2up page 356] of ''The Scots Peerage'', Vol. 1
2 CONT
2 CONT CAMPBELL, DUKE OF ARGYLL 335
2 CONT
2 CONT By Isabel Stewart, his wife, already described, who died at Dumbarton 26 October 1510, and was buried at Kilmun, he had issue :
2 CONT # ARCHIBALD, second Earl of Argyll.
2 CONT # Thomas, ancestor of the first family of the Campbells of Lundie, in Forfarshire.
2 CONT # Margaret, married after 1469 9 to George, Lord Seton, and had issue.
2 CONT # Isabel, married to William, Master of Drummond, eldest son of John, first Lord Drummond, generally said to have been executed in his father's lifetime, 1490, 1 and had issue.
2 CONT # Helen, married, 21 April 1478, to Hugh, first Earl of Eglintoun ; he died June 1545, aged eighty-four. 2
2 CONT # Elizabeth, married to John, second Lord Oliphant ; he died 1516.
2 CONT # Mary, married to Angus Macdonald, natural son and heir of tailzie of John, Earl of Ross. He was treacherously slain by MacOairbre, an Irish harper, at Inverness, 1490 ; they had no issue. 3
2 CONT # Agnes, said to have been married to Alexander Mackenzie of Kintail, ancestor of the Earls of Seaforth, but this can be proved to be quite erroneous. 4
2 CONT # Catherine, married, 1498, to Torquil Macleod of Lewis. Catherine is said by Macfarlane to have married Lachlan ' Oig ' Maclean. 5
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT "He married Isabelle Stewart, daughter of John Stewart, 2nd Lord Lorne in 1465. His oldest son was Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll, his middle son was Thomas Campbell, and his youngest son was Sir Colin Campbell of Glen Orc
2 CONC hy . Sir Colin Campbell of Glen Orchy was the ancestor of the Earls of Breadalbane). The poet Iseabail Ní Mheic Cailéin was Sir Colin Campbell of Glen Orchy's daughter."
2 CONT
2 CONT Not in the ''Scots Peerage.''
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ====FindAGrave®: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/157581530/coile%C3%A1in_'colin'-mclphryar-campbell Coileáin 'Colin' Mclphryar Campbell]
2 CONT BIRTH: 10 Sep 1433 • Lochawe, Argyll and Bute, Scotland
2 CONT DEATH: 10 May 1493 (aged 59) ª Tulliemet, Perth and Kinross, Scotland
2 CONT BURIAL: Kilmun Parish Church and Argyll Mausoleum, Kilmun, Argyll and Bute, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT COLIN CAMPBELL, FIRST EARL OF ARGYLL,  was granted that title by King James II in 1457. Under King James III he held the offices of lord privy seal, master of the household, and lord high chancellor. His wife was Isabel, daughter 
2 CONC of coheir of John Stewart lord Lorn. Their children were Archibald, Thomas, Margaret, Isabel, Helen, Mary, Catherine, and two other daughters whose names are not known. Colin died in 1493.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan "Na-Adh" had a charter to Menstrie 6 Feb. 1392/3. He succeeded his father about 1414 when with the consent of his son Celestin he confirmed a grant which had been made by his father to his kinsman Ronald Malcolmson of Craig
2 CONC nish. He is called "Cambel de Ergyle" in the list of hostages for the release of James I in 1423 and his annual income was given as 1500 marks. He was a member of the privy council and justiciar and lord lieutenant of Argyllshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story goes that while Sir Colin was off fighting in the Holy Land his wife built for him the castle of Kilchurn on its peninsula at the end of Loch Awe. He was so long absent that it was said he was dead, and the lady, like Pe
2 CONC nelope in the classic tale, was besieged by suitors. After long delays a neighbouring baron, MacCorquodale, it is said, forced her to a marriage. While the marriage feast was going on, a beggar came to the door. He refused to drin
2 CONC k the health of the bride unless she herself handed him the cup. This she did, and as the beggar drank and returned it she gave a cry, for in the bottom lay Sir Colin’s signet ring. The beggar was Sir Colin himself, returned just 
2 CONC in time to rescue his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilchurn was occupied by the Campbells until the 1740's and was greatly enlarged in 1693. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: ''The Scots Peerage'', Vol. 1, [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft#page/356/mode/2up pp. 332-335] .
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
