2 CONT Notas (1) [Isiaha Lee.ged]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st Earl Of Argyll, so created 1457; Master King's Household 1464; Lord High Chancellor of Scotland 1483. [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT 17 Apr 1470-Lord Of Lorne
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin Campbell, 2nd Lord Campbell, d. 19 May 1493, Justiciar of Scotland, cr 1457 [?]; m. bef 9 Apr 1465 Isobel (or Elizabeth), eldest daughter of John Stewart, 2nd Lord Lorn. [Magna Charta Sureties]
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT BARONY OF CAMPBELL (II) 1453
2 CONT
2 CONT EARLDOM OF ARGYLL [SCT] (I) 1457
2 CONT
2 CONT COLIN (CAMPBELL), LORD CAMPBELL [SCT], son and heir of Archibald CAMPBELL, of Lochow, Argyll, styled MASTER OF CAMPBELL, by, as is said (aa), Elizabeth, daughter of John (SOMERVILE), 3rd LORD SOMERVILE, which Archibald was son and
2 CONC  heir apparent of Duncan, 1st LORD CAMPBELL [SCT], but died v.p., between April 1431 and March 1440, probably before 1439.  He succeeded to the dignity of a Lord of Parliament [SCT] by the death of his said grandfather, in 1453, b
2 CONC eing then a minor, and was created by James II, in 1457 Earl Of Argyll [SCT].  He was employed in various embassies to England and France, was Master of the Household 1464, and Chancellor [SCT] 1483 to February 1488, and again Jun
2 CONC e 1488 till his death.  In 1460 he had a commission as Bailie of Cowal.  In 1471 he had a charter of the heritable offices of Justiciary and Sheriff within the lordship of Lorne.  In 1479 he had a charter confirming to him the off
2 CONC ices of Lieutenant and Commissary of Argyll, as held by his ancestors Gillespic and Colin Cambel under a charter of 1382.  In 1487 he joined the conspiracy of the nobles against James III.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, before 9 April 1465, Elizabeth or Isabel, daughter and senior coheir of John (STEWART), 2nd LORD LORNE [SCT].  On the resignation of her uncle, Walter STEWART, the heir male of the family, till then usually designated 
2 CONC LORD LORNE, but afterwards LORD INNERMEATH [SCT], he obtained a crown charter of the Lordship of Lorne (dominium De Lorne), 17 April 1470, to him and the heirs male of his body, with remainder to Sir Colin Campbell of Glenorchy, t
2 CONC o Colin Campbell Nelesoun, to Duncan Campbell (brother of Sir Colin, of Glenorchy), &C., each in like manner respectively.  It was made a condition of this resignation that the resigner should continue a Lord of Parliament by the 
2 CONC style of Lord of Innermeath "or any other honourable place that pleases him."  From that time the Earl is usually designated (as are his successors) "Earl Of Argyll, Lord Campbell and Lorne."  After the rebellion in 1487, which en
2 CONC ded in the death of James III at Sauchieburn, he was deprived of the office of Chancellor, but was reinstated by James IV in 1488, as above.  He died in May 1493.  His widow died 26 October 1510, at Dunbarton, and was buried at Ki
2 CONC lmun. [CP I:198, XIV:33, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin, second Lord Campbell, already referred to as Colin McGillespic in a writ of 1450 noted above.  He succeeded his grandfather in 1453, and is said, but not on good authority, to have been a minor under the guardianship of his
2 CONC  uncle, Sir Colin Campbell of Glenurcy.  It is probable his uncle had only a gift of his marriage, and he arranged a union between his nephew and Isabel or Elizabeth Stewart, one of the three daughters and co-heirs of John, Lord O
2 CONC f Lorne, and sister of his own wife, a very advantageous match for the house.  He was created Earl Of Argyll 1457.  In 1462, he and Lord Boyd were acting as justiciars of Scotland south of the Forth, an office which, after the fal
2 CONC l of his colleague, he continued to exercise by himself for many years.  He was one of the commissioners for negotiating a truce with King Edward IV of England 1463, and had the appointment of Master of the King's Household 1464. 
2 CONC  He obtained a confirmation of a charter of Walter, Lord Lorne, to him of the lands of Kippane Le Muretoun et Le Myretoun in Perthshire, 7 May 1466; and three days later he had a grant to himself and his wife, daughter of the late
2 CONC  John, Lord Of Lorne, of a third part of the lands of Kuldrane (Coldraine) and other lands in Fife and Perth, on the resignation of Mariota, his wife's sister, and on 8 February 1466-67 a grant of the lands of Pannel, co Perth.  O
2 CONC n 30 November 1469 he entered into a transaction with Walter, Lord Lorne, uncle of his wife, by which Walter resigned the lordship of Lorne in favour of the Earl, in exchange for the barony of Innermeath, and the Earl Of Argyll ob
2 CONC tained a charter of the whole lordship of Lorne, 17 April 1470, to himself and the heirs-male of his body, whom failing to a series of other subsitutes with a similar remainder in each case.  The Earl thereupon added the designati
2 CONC on of Lord Lorne to his other titles, and took the galley of Lorne into his own achievement, and from this time the Earls of Argyll are usually designated in addition Lords Campbell and Lorne.  On 18 January 1472-73 he had a gift 
2 CONC of the keepership of the Castle of Dunoon.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a charter of the office of Justiciar, Chamberlain, Sheriff, and Bailie, within the limits of the King's lordship of King's Cowal, 26 February 1472-73, and another, erecting his town of Innowreyra (Inveraray) into a burgh of
2 CONC  barony, 8 May 1474.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl was one of the commissioners who settled the treaty of alliance with King Edward IV, 1474, by which James, Prince of Scotland was affianced to Cecilia, Edward's youngest daughter.  He had a joint commission of the lieuten
2 CONC ancy of the districts of Argyll, Lorne, Menteith, and others, along with Laurence, Lord Oliphant, John Drummond of Stobhall, and William Stirling of Keir, 4 December 1475. On 26 February 1480-81 he had a charter of certain lands i
2 CONC n Knapdale, and the keepership of the royal castle of Castelsone (Castle Sweyn).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointe Lord High Chancellor of Scotland early in 1483, as on 29 April that year he obtained a charter under that designation, erecting the lands of Pinkerton in the constabulary of Haddington into a free barony.  In 1485 
2 CONC he had a charter of the lands of Craigdrum, co Argyll.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl was one of the commissioners sent to France in 1484, to renew the ancient league with that crown, which was confirmed at Paris, 9 July of that year.  He was also one of the commissioners who concluded a pacification with 
2 CONC Richard III at Nottingham, 21 September 1484, where it was agreed that Prince James should marry Anne De La Pole, only daughter of the Duke of Suffolk, and niece of the English monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1488 the Earl Of Argyll and other noblemen and prelates obtained a safe-conduct from King Henry IV to come into England, where he was at the period of the murder of James III.  He was continued in the Chancellor's office by
2 CONC  King James IV, and had a charter from that King, expressed in very flattering terms, of the lands of Roseneath, 9 Januray 1489-90.  He also obtained an Act of Parliament on the third of the following month, changing the name of h
2 CONC is stonghold on the south slope of the Ochils from Castle Gloom to Castle Campbell, under which designation its picturesque ruins are still known.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the conservators of a truce with England for five years, 21 December 1491, and died 10 May 1493.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Isabel Stewart, his wife, already described, who died at Dumbarton 26 October 1510, and was buried at Kilmun, he had issue. [The Scots Peerage I:332-334]
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>——————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin Campbell, second Lord Campbell, and first Earl of Atholl (d 1493), was the son of Archibald, second, but eldest, surviving son of Dir Duncan Campbell of Lochow, created Lord Campbell in 1445.  He succeeded his grandfather in
2 CONC  1453.  On the death of his father he was placed under the care of his uncle, Sir Colin Campbell of Glenorchy, who concluded a match between him and Isabel Stewart, the eldest of three daughters, and coheiresses of John, third Lor
2 CONC d Of Lorne.  Having acquired the principal part of the landed property of the two sisters of his wife, he exchanged certain lands in Perthshire for the lordship of Lorne with Walter, their uncle, on whom the lordship of Lorne, whi
2 CONC ch stood limited to heirs male had devolved.  In 1457 he was created, by James II, Earl Of Argyll.  He was one of the commissioners for negotiating a truce with Edward IV of England, in 1463. In 1465 he was appointed, along with L
2 CONC ord Boyd, lord justiciary of Scotland on the south of the Forth, and after the flight of Lord Boyd to England he acted as sole justiciary.  In 1474 he was appointed one of the commissioners to settle the treaty of alliance with Ed
2 CONC ward IV, by which James, prince of Scotland, was affianced to Cecilia, youngest daughter of Edward.  Early in 1483 he received the office of lord high chancellor of Scotland.  He was one of the commissioners sent to France in 1484
2 CONC  to renew the ancient league with the crown, which was confirmed at Paris 9 July, and also one of the commissioners who concluded the pecification at Nottingham with Richard III, 21 Sept of the same year.  In 1487 he joined the co
2 CONC nspiracy of the nobles against James III, and at the time of the murder of the king, after the battle of Sauchisburn, he was in England on an embassy to Henry VII.  After the accession of James IV he was restored to the office of 
2 CONC high lord chancellor.  He died 10 May 1492.  He had two sons and seven daughters.  It is from him that the greatness of the house of Argyll properly dates.  Besides the lordship of Lorne he also acquired that of Campbell and Cable
2 CONC  Campbell in the parish of Dollar, and 1481 he received a grant of many land in Knapdale, along with the keeping of Castle Sweyn, which had formerly been held by the lords of the Isles. In the general political transactions of Sco
2 CONC tland he acted a leading part, and as regards the south-western highland he laid the foundation of that unrivalled influence which the house of Argyll has enjoyed for many centuries. [Dictionary of National Biography III:797-798]
2 CONT
2 CONT ...x
2 CONT
2 CONT Fuentes (11) 1. Brent W. Ruesch  2. The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, 2000 G.E. Cokayne, with Vicary Gibbs  3. Calvin W. Sweat  4. Isiaha Lee  5. Thomas Charles Renehan  6. 
2 CONC Burke's Peerage & Baronetage, 106th Edition, 1999 Charles Mosely, Editor-in-Chief  7. A History of the Clan Maclean J.P. Maclean  8. Macdonald of the Isles A M W Stirling  9. The Scots Peerage Sir James Balfour Paul, Lord Lyon Kin
2 CONC g of Arms, editor  10. The Magna Charta Sureties, 1215, 5th Edition, 1999 Frederick Lewis Weis with William R. Beall, additions by Walter Lee Sheppard Jr.  11. Linda Lollis 
2 CONT
