2 PLAC Scotland
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Robert Montgomerie of Skelmorlie</strong>, in 1572, entered into a bond of mutual support with Lord Sempill (whose mother was a daughter of the first Earl of Eglinton). Robert was deeply engaged in the feud between the fa
2 CONC milies of Eglinton and Glencairn. He slew the Commendator of Kilwinning, second son of Lord Glencairn sometime in March, 1582-3. The Maxwells of Newark, whose mother was a Cunninghame of Craigens, were also concerned in the quarre
2 CONC l. In a conflict in January, 1583-4, Patrick Maxwell of Stainlie, was slain by the Montgomeries of Skelmorlie, and many others were killed and wounded on both sides. In April of the same year, another encounter took place with the
2 CONC  Maxwells, in which both the elder and younger lairds of Skelmorlie were slain. These slaughters occasioned much trouble to the criminal courts, but apparently without any decisive result. The Laird of Skelmorlie married Mary, dau
2 CONC ghter of Robert, Lord Sempill, who was his kinswoman, and had issue."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhist00mont#page/136/mode/2up A genealogical history of the family of Montgomery]; by Thomas Harrison Montgomery; 1863; page 137
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
