2 CONT *  [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Murray,_John_(d.1510)_(DNB00)_ “ Murray, John (d.1510)“]  Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 39. by Thomas Finlayson Henderson. “ By his wife Janet Forrester (Exchequer Rolls, x.
2 CONC 732, 757), widow of '''Schaw of Knockhill''' (ib. p. 727), he [John Murray] had, besides other children, four sons; ....
2 CONT *” Logie; A Parish History.”  by Fergusson, Robert Menzies.  Publication date 1905.  Page 89.  [https://archive.org/details/logieparishhisto00ferg/page/n112/mode/1up Archive.Org] chart Page 180.  [https://archive.org/details/logie
2 CONC parishhisto00ferg/page/n112/mode/1up Archive.Org] In 1492, Fossoquhy is let to Janet Forrester and John Murray, her spouse, for j?10, and as much of grassum with the usual services.1 In 1494, Fossoquhy (iTO) is let to '''John Murr
2 CONC ay of Hangandschaw, and Janet Forester''', his spouse.2 The above John Murray of Falahill and Philiphaugh, is reputed to have been the original of the "Outlaw Murray" of the ballad. His wife, Janet Forrester, was the widow of John
2 CONC  Schaw of Knockhill, son of Sir James Schaw of Sauchie.3 In 1499, Fossoquhy is let to John Murray of Fallowhill, and Janet, his spouse.4 In 1510, Fossoquhy is now set in feuferme to George Schaw for i?18, and 18s. for entry.5 Page
2 CONC  n112
