1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''John Ross, 2nd Lord of Halkhead''' was born in 1455 in Hawkhead, Renfrewshire, Scotland. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-38485077 Robert Ross of Halkhead] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-38485076 Agnes Melville
2 CONC  of that Ilk]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married [https://www.geni.com/profile-64182936 Christian Edmonstone] on September 27, 1490 in Hawkhead, Renfrewshire, Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-54663629 Ninian Ross, 3rd Lord of Halkhead]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34721004845 Thomas Ross]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34721004832 Andrew Ross]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-64238935 Helen Ross].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on September 9, 1513 in Flodden Field, Northumberland, England (United Kingdom) from Killed in action, Battle of Flodden. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandson of the 1st Lord and Marjory Mure and son of Robert Ross and Agnes Melville, daughter of Thomas Melville of that Ilk. He built up the family estates and was in favour with James IV, whom he hosted at Halkhead in 1506. He d
2 CONC ied at Battle of Flodden Field. He was one of the ambassadors extraordinary at the court of England, when a peace was concluded, anno 1484. Sat as a lord of parliament in the years 1489, 1491, and 1494. During the Anglo-Scottish W
2 CONC ars, John Ross, Lord of Halkhead, died when leading his forces against the English at the Battle of Flodden Field on September the 9th, 1513. Fought in the county of Northumberland, in northern England, between an invading Scots a
2 CONC rmy under King James IV and an English army commanded by Thomas Howard, Earl of Surrey. It ended in a bloody defeat for the Scots. It is sometimes referred to as the Charge of the Earls. BARONY OF ROSS OF HALKHEAD (II) 1500 or 150
2 CONC 1
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN (ROSS), LORD ROSS of Halkhead [SCT], grandson and heir, being son and heir of Robert Ross and Agnes, his wife, was returned heir of his mother in the barony of Melville, 16 May 1496; knighted before 31 May 1499, when his gran
2 CONC dfather granted to his heir apparent "Johanni Ross de Malevyn militi," the lands of Walterstoun, co. Linlithgow. James IV, 11 March 1501/,2, confirmed the grants made to his late grandfather by the King's father and grandfather of
2 CONC  the island The King's Inch in the water of Clyde near Renfrew. He was granted the wardship of the lands of the late Sir Stephen Lockhart, of Cleghorn, 8 February 1505/6. James IV visited him at Halkhead, 25 April 1506.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, before 27 September 1490, Christian, 2nd daughter of Sir Archibald EDMONSTONE, of Duntreath. He died 9 September 1513, being slain at the battle of Flodden. His widow married George KNOLLIS, but the marriage was annull
2 CONC ed about 1515, on account of her relationship to his 1st wife, Grizel Rattray. She died May 1551. [Complete Peerage XI:156-7, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===='''''The Complete Peerage''''', Vol. 11, pp. 156-57
2 CONT * SIR JOHN ROSS of Halkhead was a witness on 6 August 1447, as ‘Jon Rosse de Haukede’.[Reg. Mag. Sig., fol. Vol. 243, 1] He was appointed heritable constable of the castle of Renfrew, with a grant of the custom at the principal fa
2 CONC irs of tat borough.[Fraser’s Maxwells of Pollok, 147]  He was succeeded by another Sir John Ross of Halkhead, probably his son,
2 CONT ** '''I. 1500 or 1501''' — 2. JOHN (ROSS), LORD Ross of Halkhead [S.], grandson and h., being s. and h. of Robert Ross and Agnes and his wife, both abovenamed, was returned heir of his mother in the barony of Melville, 16 May 1496
2 CONC ; knighted before 3I May 1499, when his grandfather granted to his h. ap. "''Johanni Ross de Malevyn militi''," the lands of Walterstoun, co. Linlithgow.[''Reg. Mag. Sig''. [S.], vol. ii, no. 2499.] James IV, 11 Mar. 1501/2, confi
2 CONC rmed the grants made to his late grandfather by the King's father and grandfather of the island. The King's Inch in the water of Clyde near Kenfrew.[''Idem'', no. 2629. See too ''Reg. Sec. Sig''. [S.], vol. i, no. 784.] He was gra
2 CONC nted the wardship of the lands of the late Sir Stephen Lockhart, of Cleghorn, 8 Feb. 1505/6. James IV Visited him at Halkhead, 25 Apr. 1506.[The King gave drink-money to the masons there (Accounts of the L. High Treasurer [S.], vo
2 CONC l. iii, pp. xxv, 192).]
2 CONT ** He '''m.''', before 27 Sep. 1490, '''Christian''', 2nd da. of Sir Archibald EDMONSTONE, of Duntreath. He d. 9 Sep. 1513, being slain at the battle of Flodden. [See vol. v, Appendix D, for a list of the Scottish nobility slain a
2 CONC t Flodden.] His widow[see Fn. below.] '''m.''' George KNOLLIS, but the marriage was annulled about 1515, on account of her relationship to his 1st wife, Grizel Rattray. She d. May 1551.
2 CONT *** Fn. Her procurator protested, 18 Aug. 1517; "that sen sche is reddy to pay hir part for the furnesing of the speris to the bordouris and to lay the samyn down before the lordis, that sche suld nocht be pundit" (''Acts of the L
2 CONC ords of the Council [S.] in Public Affairs'', 1501-54, P. 100).]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balnagown_Castle Balnagown Castle] (also Balnagowan)(Ross Castle) is beside the village of Kildary in Easter Ross, part of the Highland area of Scotland
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kildary Kildary ]  is a small village in Easter Ross, Ross and Cromarty, Highland, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Easter Ross   is a loosely defined area in the east of Ross, Highland, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cromartyshire Cromartyshire]  was a county in the Highlands of Scotland, comprising the medieval "old shire" around the county town of Cromarty and 22 enclaves and exclaves transferred from Ross-shir
2 CONC e in the late 17th century. The largest part, six times the size of the old shire, was Coigach, containing Ullapool and the area north-west of it. In 1889, Cromartyshire was merged with Ross-shire to become a new county called Ros
2 CONC s and Cromarty, which in 1975 was merged into the new council area of Highland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Settlements listed for Cromartyshire  Achiltibuie, Altandhu, Badenscallie, Cromarty, Inver, Jemimaville, Kildary, Milton, Polbain Polglass, Portmahomack, Strathpeffer, Ullapool,
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 14th century, a castle was begun at Balnagown by Hugh, Mormaer (Earl) of Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT
