2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton (d 1549) 4th [or 6th] Lord Seton.  He was the 4th George Seton, and the 6th lord.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''George Seton IV, 6th Lord Seton (died 1549)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton IV, 6th Lord Seton (died 1549) was a Lord of the Parliament of Scotland. He was the son of Jean Hepburn, daughter of Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell. His father, the 5th Lord Seton was killed at the battle of Fl
2 CONC odden and George's mother survived her husband by 45 years till 1558, managing the family's interests. When George came of age she joined the Convent of St Catherine at Sciennes in Edinburgh. After her son's death in 1549, she arr
2 CONC anged the marriage of two of her granddaughters.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Seton,_6th_Lord_Seton
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton was appointed an Extraordinary Lord of Session on 5 March 1542. Like many Scottish nobles he gave his assent to the marriage proposed between Mary, Queen of Scots and Prince Edward of England in 1543. In that year, Se
2 CONC ton was given custody of Cardinal Beaton who opposed the English marriage, but Seton allowed the Cardinal to escape. He died in 1549, and was first buried at Culross Abbey as Seton Collegiate Church was in the occupied zone during
2 CONC  the war of the Rough Wooing. The English had burnt [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seton_Palace Seton Palace] and the church in May 1548.[2] Sir Richard Maitland, who had worked for the family in their legal affairs, began his His
2 CONC tory of the House of Seytoun at the request of the 6th Lord Seton, the fourth George of that name, but finished the work in the lifetime of his son George Seton, 7th Lord Seton.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Hay
2 CONT
2 CONT George first married Elizabeth Hay, daughter of John Hay, 3rd Lord Hay of Yester. They had seven children;[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT # George Seton, 7th Lord Seton, married Isobel Hamilton, daughter of William Hamilton of Sanquhar.
2 CONT # John Seton, who married Isobel Balfour, heiress of Carrauldstoun or Carriston in Fife.[5]
2 CONT # Janet, died aged 2.
2 CONT # Marion Seton, lady in waiting to Mary of Guise, married firstly John Graham, Earl of Menteith in 1548, with a dowry from Mary of Guise,[6] by whom she had five children, and secondly John Gordon, Earl of Sutherland, the couple w
2 CONC ere poisoned at Helmsdale Castle by Isobel Sinclair, and died at Dunrobin Castle on 23 June 1567.[7]
2 CONT # Margaret Seton, married; firstly, Sir Robert Logan of Restalrig, without issue; secondly David Hamilton of Ladyflat.
2 CONT # Beatrix Seton, married George Ogilvy of Banff, heir of Sir Walter Ogilvy of Dunlugus. Ancestors of Theodore Roosevelt.[8]
2 CONT # Heleanor Seton, married Hugh, Master of Somerville
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Pieris
2 CONT
2 CONT George married secondly Marie Pieris, a French lady-in-waiting of Mary of Guise after handfasting at Falkland Palace in February 1539.[6] A grant from Mary, Queen of Scots to Marie in 1565 was addressed to "Dame Marie Pier, Lady S
2 CONC eytoun and Brieane."[7] The couple were given a charter of the lands of Winchburgh and East Niddry on 27 June 1548. Marie's father wrote to Mary of Guise from Le Plessis Badouin in France. An undated letter survives from Marie to 
2 CONC Mary of Guise, written from Niddry Castle, wherein she warns her that Lord Seton had heard of a plot by Regent Arran to seize the infant Queen.[8] 
2 CONT
2 CONT George and Marie had children:
2 CONT #Robert
2 CONT #James and 
2 CONT #Mary Seton.[9] 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 August 1570, Marie and her son Robert were arrested for giving letters for Mary, Queen of Scots, to a messenger, John Moon. They were put on trial in Tolbooth at Edinburgh, on the charge that their letters denied the authori
2 CONC ty of James VI of Scotland and his representatives. They were released on their promise they would not write to Queen Mary again.[10] 
2 CONT
2 CONT
