1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == ARCHIBALD DOUGLAS Earl of Angus ==
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Bell the Cat===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/192/mode/1up The Scots Peerage I: pp. 178-86]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # ''']https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/192/mode/1up The Scots Peerage I: 178-86]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/dd/douglas02.php Stirnet: Douglas 92]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus, was a Scottish nobleman, peer, politician, and magnate. He became known as "Bell the Cat". He became the most powerful nobleman in the realm through a successful rebellion and established his 
2 CONC family as the most important in the kingdom. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The fable concerns a group of mice who debate plans to nullify the threat of a marauding cat. One of them proposes placing a bell around its neck, so that they are warned of its approach. The plan is applauded by the others, until
2 CONC  one mouse asks who will volunteer to place the bell on the cat. All of them make excuses. The story is used to teach the wisdom of evaluating a plan not only on how desirable the outcome would be, but also on how it can be execut
2 CONC ed. It provides a moral lesson about the fundamental difference between ideas and their feasibility, and how this affects the value of a given plan.  read more at https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belling_the_cat 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Archibald Douglas''', 5th Earl of Angus (1449 – 19 November 1513), became known as the "Great" Earl of Angus and, perhaps more famously, as "Bell the Cat".
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus, born about 1449 at Tantallon Castle in East Lothian, succeeded his father, George the 4th earl, in 1462 or 1463.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1481, Angus became Warden of the east marches, but the next year he joined the league against James III and his favourite Robert Cochrane at Lauder. Here he earned his nickname by offering to "bell the cat" – specifically, to d
2 CONC eal with Cochrane – beginning the attack upon him by pulling his gold chain off his neck, and then ordering the hanging of Cochrane and others of the king's favourites. (The phrase "to bell the cat" comes from one of Aesop's fable
2 CONC s, "The Mice in Council", and refers to a dangerous task undertaken for the benefit of all.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Subsequently he joined Alexander Stewart, 3rd Duke of Albany, in league with Edward IV of England on 11 February 1483, signing the convention at Westminster which acknowledged the overlordship of the English king. However, in Marc
2 CONC h Albany and Angus returned, outwardly at least, to their allegiance, and received pardons for their treason.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, Angus became one of the leaders in the rebellion against James III in 1487 and 1488 which ended in the latter's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus became one of the guardians of the young king James IV. but soon lost influence, to the Homes and Hepburns, and the wardenship of the marches went to Alexander Home. Though outwardly on good terms with James, Angus treachero
2 CONC usly made a treaty with Henry VII around 1489 or 1491, by which he undertook to govern his relations with James according to instructions from England. He also agreed to hand over Hermitage Castle, commanding the pass through Lidd
2 CONC esdale into Scotland, on the condition of receiving English estates in compensation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1491 he fortified his castle of Tantallon against James, but had to submit and to exchange his Liddesdale estate and Hermitage Castle for the lordship of Bothwell.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1493 Angus again returned to favour, receiving various grants of lands. He became Chancellor, which office he retained till 1498. In 1501, in disgrace once more, he was confined to Dumbarton Castle. At the disaster at Flodden F
2 CONC ield in 1513, though absent himself, Angus lost his two eldest sons. He won appointment as one of the councilors of Margaret Tudor the queen regent. He died at the close of this year, or in 1514.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT * On 4 March 1468: Elizabeth (d. 1498), daughter of Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd[4]
2 CONT * In the summer of 1500: Katherine Stirling, daughter of Sir William Stirling of Keir[4]
2 CONT * Janet, daughter of John Kennedy, 2nd Lord Kennedy, was one of his mistresses.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT * 4.  Norman Macdougall, ‘Douglas, Archibald , fifth earl of Angus (c.1449–1513)’, Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, Oxford University Press, 2004
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children by first marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Name 	Birth 	Death 	Notes'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * George Douglas, Master of Angus 	1469 	9 September 1513 	married in March 1488, Lady Elizabeth Drummond; had issue, killed at the Battle of Flodden 
2 CONT * Lady Mariot Douglas 	1470 		married Cuthbert Cunningham, 2nd Earl of Glencairn; had issue 
2 CONT * Sir William Douglas 	1471 	9 September 1513 	married Lady Elizabeth Auchinleck; had issue, killed at the Battle of Flodden 
2 CONT * Gavin Douglas, Bishop of Dunkeld 	1472 		  
2 CONT * Lady Elizabeth Douglas 	1474 		  
2 CONT * Sir Archibald Douglas of Kilspindie 	1475 		Given the nickname Greysteil by James V 	  
2 CONT * Lady Janet Douglas 	1476 		 
2 CONT
