1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Annabel Drummond Baroness Graham
2 CONT
2 CONT Conflicting information about date of death. Did she die in childbrirh (1494) or later or earlier (1492)?
2 CONT
2 CONT _______
2 CONT '''Annabel Drummond'''1
2 CONT Last Edited	4 Apr 2020
2 CONT F, #37567, '''d. after 1492'''
2 CONT '''Father'''	John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond b. c 1438, d. 1519
2 CONT '''Mother'''	Elizabeth Lindsay d. a 22 Sep 1509
2 CONT Charts	Pedigree of Rayburn Lamson Lewis
2 CONT '''     Annabel Drummond married William Graham, 1st Earl Montrose, 3rd Lord Graham''', son of William Graham, 2nd Lord Graham and Eleanor (Elene) Douglas, '''on 25 November 1479 at Muthyll, Perthshire, Scotland. Annabel Drummond 
2 CONC died after 1492.'''
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT '''William Graham, 1st Earl Montrose, 3rd Lord Graham''' b. bt 1463 - 1464, d. 9 Sep 1513
2 CONT '''Children	'''
2 CONT *William Graham, 2nd Earl Montrose, 4th Lord Graham+ d. 24 May 1571
2 CONT *Elizabeth Graham+2
2 CONT *Catherine Graham+3 b. c 1489
2 CONT *Jean Graham+4 b. c 1491
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S11415] Unknown author, The Complete Peerage, by Cokayne, Vol. IV, p. 469, Vol. IX, p. 146.
2 CONT 2.[S11568] The Complete Peerage, by Cokayne, Vol. IV, p. 470.
2 CONT 3.[S11597] Ancestry.com, Information submitted by brentwr.
2 CONT 4.[S11597] Ancestry.com, Information submitted by FrSmith550.
2 CONT https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1251.htm#i37567
2 CONT ____
2 CONT '''Annabella Drummond'''1 
2 CONT F, #30443
2 CONT Last Edited=8 Dec 2018
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.04%
2 CONT '''     Annabella Drummond was the daughter of''' John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond and Lady Elizabeth Lindsay.1 '''She married William Graham, 1st Earl of Montrose''', son of William Graham, 2nd Lord Graham and Elene Douglas, '''o
2 CONC n 25 November 1479.'''1
2 CONT '''     Her married name became Graham. After her marriage, Annabella Drummond was styled as Countess of Montrose in 1503.'''
2 CONT '''Children of Annabella Drummond and William Graham, 1st Earl of Montrose'''
2 CONT 1. William Graham, 2nd Earl of Montrose+1 d. 24 May 1571
2 CONT 2. Walter Graham1
2 CONT 3. Janet Graham2
2 CONT 4. Lady Jean Graham3
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 2752. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT 2.[S1916] Tim Boyle, "re: Boyle Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 16 September 2006. Hereinafter cited as "re: Boyle Family."
2 CONT 3.[S6286] Clan MacFarlane and associated clans genealogy, online http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info. Hereinafter cited as Clan MacFarlane.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p3045.htm#i30443
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''William Graham, 1st Earl of Montrose''' (1464 – 9 September 1513) was a Scottish Lord of Parliament, who was raised to an earldom by James IV of Scotland and who died with his monarch at the Battle of Flodden.
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT '''Montrose''' was the eldest son and heir of William Graham, 2nd Lord Graham by Eleanor, or Elene, the daughter of William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus. The Grahams were a long-established family of Norman origin, who first rose to
2 CONC  prominence in the reign of David I.[1]
2 CONT Career
2 CONT '''He succeeded to the peerage as the 3rd Lord Graham''', as a minor, on the death of his father in about 1471 and sat in the Parliaments of James III in 1479, 1481, 1482 and 1487. He supported James III in his struggle with his s
2 CONC on and was present at the Battle of Sauchieburn on 11 June 1488. He was then received into the favour of James IV, as was the case for many of James III's supporters, and sat in his first two Parliaments of 6 October 1488 and 6 Fe
2 CONC bruary 1492.
2 CONT In 1503, '''William Graham was created 1st Earl of Montrose''' (from his ancestral estate at Old Montrose) and he sat, in that capacity, in the Parliament of 3 February 1506.[2]
2 CONT '''Montrose''' accompanied James IV on his invasion of England in 1513 and was killed at the Battle of Flodden on 9 September 1513, together with his brother, George Graham of Callendar, (Calendar) and his brother-in-law, Sir Will
2 CONC iam Edmonstone of Duntreath.[2]
2 CONT Estates
2 CONT Not long before his elevation to an earldom, Montrose acquired the estates of Aberuthven and Inchbrakie in Perthshire. Shortly after the creation of the earldom, on 3 March 1505, his ancestral lands of Old Montrose were erected in
2 CONC to the free barony and earldom of Montrose and were re-granted to him on his surrender of them to the King. On the same day, he had three other charters to three other new baronies: Kincardine, Aberuthven and Kynnaber, in Forfarsh
2 CONC ire.[2]
2 CONT Family
2 CONT '''Montrose married first (on 25 November 1479) Annabel Drummond, one of the five daughters''' of John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond, secondly Janet Edmonstone, daughter of Sir Archibald Edmonstone of Duntreath and thirdly Christian
2 CONC  Wawane of Seggie, in Fife, the widow of Patrick Haliburton, 5th Lord Haliburton.
2 CONT '''By his first wife, Montrose had:'''
2 CONT *William Graham, 2nd Earl of Montrose
2 CONT *Walter Graham, of Little Cairnie.
2 CONT By his third wife, Montrose had a further son, Patrick Graham of Inchbraikie (grandfather of Bishop George Graham), to which lands Patrick received a charter from his father on 20 June 1513.
2 CONT Montrose also had three daughters:
2 CONT *Helen Graham, who married (dispensation 13 July 1509) Humphrey Colquhoun, Younger of Luss
2 CONT *Nicolas Graham (a daughter of Graham's second marriage), who married (11 February 1504) John Moray, 6th of Abercairney
2 CONT *Elizabeth Graham (a daughter of Graham's second marriage), who married (February 1514) Walter Drummond, Master of Drummond, the grandson of John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond
2 CONT *Margaret Graham (a daughter of Graham's second marriage), who married (contract 10 July 1510) Sir John Somerville of Cambusnethan
2 CONT *Jean Graham, who married David Graham, 3rd of Fintry.
2 CONT Other reputed (but doubtful) progeny include Andrew Graham, who was consecrated Bishop of Dunblane in 1575, and Jean Graham, who was said to have had a daughter by William Chisholm (I), who was consecrated Bishop of Dunblane in 15
2 CONC 26.[2][3]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Graham,_1st_Earl_of_Montrose
2 CONT _____
