2 CONT
2 CONT ====Evidence from the National Records of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''8 November 1555:''' Dame Mariota Montgomery, Lady Symple, [Note] Came in the Queen's Will for Ratihabition of the Slaughter of Gilbert Rankyne in Lecheland, committed by the fervants of the faid Lady, Mar. 17, 1553, under filen
2 CONC ce of night: And for Ratihabition of the cruel Hurting and Wounding  of John  Fynn, and Mutilation of his arm; and for Hurting  and Wounding of John Roger in fundry parts of his body, to the effufion of his blood, committed at the
2 CONC  fame time — by Refetting of her fervants, who had committed the faid crimes, red-hand, that fame night, within THE Castle  of Laven, immediately after the perpetration thereof: And alfo for Ratihabition of the taking and apprehen
2 CONC ding of Humphry  Malcolmefoun  and  Archibald  Scherare, they being conducted by her fervants on the fame night to the Caftle of Laven, feeing she received  them into her faid Caftle; Item, for Incarceration and fubjection of the 
2 CONC faid perfons in the forefaid Caftle by the fpace of twenty-four hours, without food or drink; thereby Ufurping the Queen's  authority. . . . . . John Campbell of Skijipynche became furety for fatisfying the parties - and alfo for 
2 CONC the entry of Ewfame  Stewarte, at the next Aire of Renfrew, to underly the law for art and part of the Slaughter of the faid Gilbert  Rankyne.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note by Robert Pitcairn:''' She was eldest daughter of Hugh first Earl of Eglintoun; and was, at the time of this Trial, relict of William second Lord Sempill, who died anno 1548. There seems no doubt that her name was Mariota,
2 CONC  she having been named after her mother, Mariota Seytoun, Mistress of Borthwick, and sister of Gerge  Iord Seytoun. [https://archive.org/details/criminaltrialsin01pitc/page/n428/mode/1up Ancient Criminal Trials I - II: 381*]
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''8 January 1559:''' Charter by which Donald, Abbot of Cupar, confirms that he has given a feu charter for the lands of Cowpargrange, in the lordship of Cupar and sheriffdom of Perth, to John Campbell of Skippinche, to be held by
2 CONC  him and his lawful male heirs, whom failing by his nearest lawful male heir bearing the name and arms of Campbell. The charter was confirmed under the Great Seal of Scotland on 4 June 1566. [https://archive.org/details/registrumm
2 CONC agnisi04scot/page/427/mode/1up RMS 1546-80: charter number 1735]
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''22 June 1559:''' Reversion by John Campbell of Skippininche and Marian Montgomery, his spouse, to Hugh Montgomery of Hasilhead, and Margaret Fressell, his mother, of the 10 mark lands of Lyoncros, containing the reversion price
2 CONC  of 250 marks. Dated Glasgow, 22 Jun 1559. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD8%2f164&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=marian+montgomery&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1550&dt=1600&di=n National Records of
2 CONC  Scotland, Boyd Papers, Burgh of Kilmarnock, reference GD8/164]
2 CONT
2 CONT                     4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''22 February 1562-63:''' Copy precept of sasine by Archibald, earl of Argyll, in favour of Colin Campbell of Glenurquhay and Catherine Ruthven, his spouse, of £20 land of old extent in Balquhiddir called Glenampill Ednambill wit
2 CONC h mill, Achyveve, Balleveir, Ballivoyllin, Cascheblair, Catrone, Letter and Lie Port with island called Ellen Vragran and fishing of Locherne, 22 February 1562/3. Witnesses: Colin Campbell of Bowquhane, kt., John Campbell of Skipi
2 CONC nche, Mr John Karswell, rector of Kilmertyne, Donald Campbell, 'his' brother, and Andrew Quhyt, notary. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD112%2f2%2f65%2f1&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=s
2 CONC kipinche&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Campbell Family, Earls of Breadalbane (Breadalbane Muniments), reference GD112/2/65/1]
2 CONT
2 CONT                     5
2 CONT
2 CONT '''7 August 1569:''' Procuratory by Marion Montgomery, widow of late William Lord Semple, with consent of John Campbell, provost of Kilmun, now her husband, for renouncing all claims to lands of Spango Campbell. [http://catalogue.
2 CONC nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f2%2f7%2f15&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=William+lord+Semple&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=y National Records of Scotland, Montgomerie Family, Earls of Eglinton: Titles, ref
2 CONC erence GD3/2/7/15]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Evidence from the Protocol Books of the Town Clerks of Glasgow
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''8 June 1559:''' Charter by which Hugh Mungumry of Hasilhead confirms that he has sold the ten merk lands of old extent of Liandcross in the barony of Renfrew to John Campbell of Skippinche and his wife Lady Marion Mungumry. [ht
2 CONC tps://archive.org/stream/abstractsofproto05glas#page/12/mode/1up Glasgow Protocols V: 1349]
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2 January 1559-60:''' Acknowledgement by John Campbell of Skippinche and his wife Marion Mungumry, that they had received from Hugh Mungumry of Hasilheid, 250 merks in a reversion for the redemption of the ten merk lands of old
2 CONC  extent of Lyencors. Witnessed by George Mungumry, brother of Hugh Mungumry of Hasilheid. [https://archive.org/stream/abstractsofproto05glas#page/14/mode/1up Glasgow Protocols V: 1352]
2 CONT
