1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "was taken prisoner at Pinkie ... he fought under the banners of his chief, against Queen Mary at the battle of Langside. He died in 1574 in his bed, and is reputed to have been the first Laird of Wedderburn who ha
2 CONC d that privilege.". [The Scottish Nation, by William Anderson on page 484.]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT * parents:  Sir David Home, David 4th Baron of Wedderburn (1489-1524) & Alison Douglas (1491-)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT #  in 1543 to Mariota Johnstone.  She was the daughter of Andrew Johnstone of Elphinstone
2 CONT # to Margaret Ker, widow of _____ Pringle of Whitebank. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by Mariota Johnstone
2 CONT # George 7th Baron of Wedderburn (Sir )+
2 CONT # David of Godscroft+
2 CONT # James of Hilton
2 CONT # John
2 CONT # Isobel
2 CONT # Margaret
2 CONT # Julia
2 CONT # Janet
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by Margaret Ker
2 CONT # Patrick (disputed) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~hume/tree/1363.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Family Links Spouses/Children: 1. Johnstone, Mariota Home, George 7th Baron of Wedderburn, Sir + Hume, David of Godscroft+ Home, James of Hilton Home, John Home, Isobel Home, Margaret Home, Julia Home, Janet 2. Ker, Margaret Home,
2 CONC  Patrick Home, David 6th Baron of Wedderburn, Sir 12
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1520, Wedderburn Castle, Berwickshire, Scotland Marriage (1): Johnstone, Mariota in 1543 12 Marriage (2): Ker, Margaret Died: 10 Sep 1574 at age 54 General Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT 6th Baron of Wedderburn
2 CONT
2 CONT "was taken prisoner at Pinkie ... he fought under the banners of his chief, against Queen Mary at the battle of Langside. He died in 1574 in his bed, and is reputed to have been the first Laird of Wedderburn who had that privilege
2 CONC ." [The Scottish Nation, by William Anderson on page 484.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Present at Battles of Ancrum Moor and Pinkie, M.P.
2 CONT
2 CONT Infeft as brother-german and heir of George Home 21st Nov. 1550 in the lands of Kimmyrghame. Served heir to his grandfather 31st July, 1551. Died about August 1574.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Case on the Part of Sir Hugh Hume Campbell of Marchmont, Baronet, in relation to the Claim of Francis Douglas Home, Esquire, to the Titles, Honours and Dignities of Earl of Marchmont, Viscount of Blazonberry, Lord Polwart of Polw
2 CONC art, Reidbraes and Greenlaw. Presented to the Lords in 1843. Printed by Spottiswoode and Robertson, Westminster; pp. 76, with chart.]
2 CONT
2 CONT David was prevailed on by George to keep out of the battle, that both might not fall. After the defeat, he fled to the castle of Dalkeith, which was surrendered to the English next day by James Douglas Earl of Morton, and afterwar
2 CONC ds Regent of Scotland, and he was carried captive to England, where he remained two years till his ransom was paid. He was a good Latin scholar, and had studied the laws; he also sang, and played on the harp. He was a very upright
2 CONC  man, and though he might have enriched himself with forfeited estates as others did, he steadfastly refused to receive what he believed to be the property of others. When it was represented to him that if he did not get a share o
2 CONC f his enemies' estates, his own party would not trust him, he still peremptorily refused. He remained steadfast to the Queen until she abdicated. After that, he sided with her at Carberry, though his chief, Lord Home, and his cous
2 CONC in, Earl Morton, were on the other side. Bothwell prevailed on the Queen to ask him and his uncle Blackadder whether she could depend upon them, to which he replied, that he had joined her standard for that end, and he hoped she w
2 CONC ould not disgrace him by suspicions of his fidelity. His uncle Blackadder said the same, and enraged at Bothwell, added to him, "We will stay as long, and perhaps longer with our Royal Mistress than you will," and they did remain 
2 CONC with her after Bothwell had fled. Wedderburn supported his vassals entirely at his own expense. When a Warden was to be chosen for the Eastern Marches, he was named as the fittest person, and the Regents, Marr and Morton, offered 
2 CONC it to him. He desired one day to consider, as he did not anything rashly, and this delay was represented to the Regent as caused by his desire to consult his chief, Lord Home, which so enraged the Regent, that he appointed Home of
2 CONC  Cowdenknows. At this, Wedderburn was angry, and never would pay any deference to Cowdenknows as Warden. The Regent attempted to draw Wedderburn into a snare, by ordering all the gentlemen of the Merse and Teviotdale to sign an as
2 CONC sociation on behalf of the King, but David, suspecting some fraud, refused to do so. Morton, fearing the influence of his example upon others, set him in an inner room by himself, until the others had signed it. When Morton went t
2 CONC o dinner, he remembered David, and sent for him, who jocosely said, "As I was lawfully committed, I stayed till I was lawfully released."
2 CONT
2 CONT His uncle, Sir G. Douglas, the Regent's father, often resided at Wedderburn; during one of these visits, Lord Home was at Manderston, which is within a mile of Wedderburn, and they went out to hunt together with a large party, and
2 CONC  rode up to the very doors of Wedderburn, intending it as an insult to Sir G. Douglas, with whom Lord Home was angry on account of a dispute respecting Colburnspeth. David mounted his horse, and ordered all his servants to attend 
2 CONC him, and hunted in his turn up to the walls of Manderston, even into the garden. Next a confederacy called the Black band was formed against him, headed by Hume of Manderston, which he overcame. Manderston next attempted to carry 
2 CONC off the teinds of Kello, with the assistance of the Abbot of Coldinghame, which had always belonged to the Lairds of Wedderburn, but Wedderburn with five hundred horse dispersed the faction, and put them all to flight. A French ge
2 CONC ntleman, D'Oysel, in the neighbourhood, made an irruption into England without giving him notice, and on returning, was hard pressed, and would have been destroyed, had not Wedderburn, who saw his distress from a neighbouring hill
2 CONC , gone to his assistance, and saved him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wedderburn was a very swift runner, fond of racing, hunting, fencing, throwing the javelin. His chief, Lord Hume, was persuaded by Manderston that Wedderburn wanted his ruin, but on the contrary, he refused to take any of his esta
2 CONC tes when the were confiscated, comforted him when in prison, and became his surety, and Lord Hume then became sensible of his true merit. Died 1574, and is said to be the first of his family who died a natural death, all having be
2 CONC en killed in the defence of their country.
2 CONT
2 CONT [From History of Dunbar Hume and Dundas from Drummond's Noble British Families, William Pickering, London 1846, pages 21-22]
2 CONT
2 CONT David married Mariota Johnstone, daughter of Andrew Johnstone and Unknown, in 1543.12 (Mariota Johnstone died in 1564.)
2 CONT
2 CONT David next married Margaret Ker. (Margaret Ker died on 24 Feb 1589 88 and was buried in 1588-1589 in Melrose, Roxburghshire, Scotland.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source [familysearch.org]
2 CONT
