1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Lindsay,_1st_Duke_of_Montrose
2 CONT
2 CONT David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose (1440-December 25, 1495) was a Scottish nobleman.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford, and inherited the Earldom of Crawford on his father's death in 1453. During his political career he held the posts of Lord High Admiral of Scotland, Master of the Royal Ho
2 CONC usehold, Great Chamberlain and Justiciar.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1488 he was created Duke of Montrose, the first Scotsman not of royal blood to be granted a Dukedom. Lindsay had won the favour of James III, by remaining loyal to the king during the rebellion of his son Prince James. Lindsay 
2 CONC was deprived of his dukedom by James IV when he acceded to the throne later that year, but it was restored in 1489 for life only. On his death in 1495 the title therefore became extinct, although the Earldom continues to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Elizabeth Hamilton, daughter of James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton in 1459. They had three children before divorcing in the 1480s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, Master of Crawford (1485-September 16 1489)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Lindsay (b. 1495)
2 CONT
2 CONT John Lindsay, 6th Earl of Crawford (c. 1495-1513)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p11034.htm#i110335
2 CONT
2 CONT David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose was born in 1440.3 He was the son of Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford and Margaret Dunbar.2 He married, firstly, Elizabeth Hamilton, daughter of James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton and Lady
2 CONC  Eupheme Graham, in 1459.1 He and Elizabeth Hamilton were divorced before 1484.1 He married, secondly, Margaret Carmichael before 27 September 1484.4 He died on 25 December 1495 at Finhaven Castle, Angus, Scotland.4 He was buried 
2 CONC at Dundee, Angus, Scotland.4
2 CONT
2 CONT David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose succeeded to the title of 5th Earl of Crawford [S., 1398] in September 1453.2 He held the office of Sheriff of Forfarshire in October 1466.2 He held the office of Keeper of Berwick between 1473 
2 CONC and 1476.2 On 9 March 1472/73 he was granted the Lordships of Brechin and Navar for life.2 He held the office of High Admiral of Scotland in May 1476.2 He held the office of Master of the Household in 1480.5 He held the office of 
2 CONC Great Lord Chamberlain [Scotland] before 1483.5 He held the office of Joint High Justiciary of the North in 1487.5 He was created 1st Duke of Montrose [Scotland] on 18 May 1488.5 He fought in the Battle of Sauchieburn on 9 June 14
2 CONC 88, where he distinguished himself on the side of King James III.6 On 17 October 1488 his Dukedom was forfeited by the Rescissory Act.6 On 19 September 1489 he was granted a new patent for the Dukedom of Montrose. However, the Res
2 CONC cissory Act itself was rescinded in March 1503/4, so it has been argued that the first creation is in effect the correct one.6 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland] in February 1489/90.5
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose and Elizabeth Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Lady Elizabeth Lindsay+7
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Alexander Lindsay, Master of Crawford4 d. 16 Sep 1489
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.John Lindsay, 6th Earl of Crawford4 d. 9 Sep 1513
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Lady Margaret Lindsay7 b. b 1494
2 CONT
