4 CONT Father of Elizabeth Isabel (Gordon) Glendonwyn
4 CONT Died [date unknown] [location unknown]
4 CONT Profile manager: Robert Harter private message [send private message]
4 CONT Last modified 1 September 2015.
4 CONT This page has been accessed 53 times.
4 CONT
4 CONT Biography
4 CONT
4 CONT XIII. Sir ROBERT GORDON, formerly designed of Accarsan of Glen, now of Loch∣invar, who got a charter from king James V. Roberto de Gordon, militi, of the lands of Ken∣mure, Lagan, &c. dated in 1517.
4 CONT
4 CONT He obtained from queen Mary a grant of the clerkships of the sheriffdom of Wigton, and stewarty of Kirkcudbright during life, with power to officiate by deputes.
4 CONT He married Marian, daughter and sole heiress of John Accarsan of Glenshyreburn, now called Rusco, by whom he got all these lands, and built the house of Rusco, &c.
4 CONT By her he had issue six sons, and three daughters.
4 CONT 1. Sir James.
4 CONT 2. John, who, in 1518, got a charter of the lands of Barnbarrock, and Barnhausie, and was ancestor of the Gordons of Haslefield.
4 CONT 3. Alexander, who acquired the lands of Gaitgill, anno 1517; also the lands of Darmo∣lane, and Markbane, in vicecom. de Wigton.
4 CONT 4. George.
4 CONT 5. Roger.
4 CONT 6. David, who married Isabel, daughter of John Muirhead of Culroch, by whom he got the lands of Carstramine, &c.
4 CONT 1st daughter, Catharine, married to Patrick Agnew of Salquhary, ancestor of sir Andrew Agnew of Lochnaw.
4 CONT 2. Elizabeth, married, 1st, to Uthred MacDowal of Machermore; 2dly, to Alex∣ander Livingston of Little-Airds. (NOTE: Sir Robert Douglas does not include marriage to John Glendonwyn, but he does include this marriage in his 1796 bo
4 CONC ok)
4 CONT 3. Janet, married to James, son and heir of Alexander Ogilvie of that ilk, ancestor of the earl of Finlater.
4 CONT And dying about 1520, was succeeded by his eldest son.[1]
4 CONT
4 CONT Robert must have been born before 1490, or absolutely no later than about 1495, for his daughter to have been married to John Glendonwyn prior to his being named Earl of Glendonwyn in 1531. [2]
4 CONT
4 CONT In 1527 he was outlawed for the slaughter of Sir Thomas Maclellan of Bomby, in Edinburgh. [3]
4 CONT
4 CONT There is a possibility that Sir Robert married a Mariota ACARSANE, but this information comes from an inadequately referenced family tree.
4 CONT
4 CONT Sources
4 CONT
4 CONT ↑ 1764. Sir Robert Douglas. The peerage of Scotland: containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom, ... collected from the public records, and ancient chartularies of this nation.
4 CONT ↑ 1798, Sir Robert Douglas, "Baronage of Scotland"
4 CONT ↑ Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003),, volume 1, page 45.
