2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From The peerage of Scotland: containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom, ... collected from the public records, and ancient chartularies of this nation, ... Illustrated with copper-plates. B
2 CONC y Robert Douglas, Esq;. Douglas, Robert, Sir, 1694-1770.  Page 367-368
2 CONT
2 CONT https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/ecco/004896980.0001.000/1:141?rgn=div1;view=fulltext
2 CONT
2 CONT X. ROGER de GORDON, who by a char∣ter, as proprietor of the lands and barony of Stitchel, confirms to St. Mary, and the monks of Jedburgh, a grant formerly made to them,* of two ox-gangs of land by William de Gor∣don, sometime lor
2 CONC d of Stitchel, dated 1st June 1431. In 1439, he made a resignation of his lands of Stitchel in favours of his son William,* re∣serving to himself the liferent. And dying about the year 1442, was suc∣ceeded by his son the said,
2 CONT
2 CONT XI. '''WILLIAM de GORDON''', promiscu∣ously designed of Stitchel and Lochinvar; which last was part of the lands in Galloway, acquired by sir Adam Gordon from John de Maxwell in 1297, before noticed, and which continued to be the 
2 CONC chief title of the family till they were nobilitate, and this William was the first of them that settled and resided in Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT He got from king James II. a charter of the lands and barony of Stitchel, Willielmo de Gordon, filio et haeredi apparenti Rogeri de Gordon, domini de Stitchel,*&c. dated 7th February 1440.
2 CONT
2 CONT He '''married—''', by whom he had four sons and one daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. John, his heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Alexander, who,* in 1490, purchased the lands of Auchinreoch, &c. and was ance∣stor of the Gordons of Aird, now Earlstoun, and others.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. George, who acquired the lands of Tro∣quhain in 1494, and of him the present George Gordon of Troquhain is lineally de∣scended.*
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Roger, who married—MacNaught, heiress of Crago,* and was ancestor of the pre∣sent Alexander Gordon of Crago.
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter, Margaret, married to sir Thomas MacLellan of Bombie,* ancestor of lord Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT
2 CONT He survived the year 1450, and was suc∣ceeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT XII. Sir JOHN GORDON of Lochinvar, ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~alanmilliken/rega...
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1487, King James III granted to James Barbour and his son John Barbour, chaplain, ‘ward of all and singular the lands of the late Andrew McNaucht wherever they are within the Kingdom of Scotland until the heirs of the same will
2 CONC  have recovered hereditary sasines’[1]. Andrew was probably killed at the battle of Kirkconnel in 1484 repelling the Duke of Albany, King James III’s eldest son. It would take nearly fourteen years before Andrew’s daughters succes
2 CONC sfully recovered their hereditary rights to the lands of Crogo and Dalquhairn. In 1498, King James IV granted Egidia and Marion, the co-heiresses of Andrew McNaucht of Crogo, each a precept of sasine as heir-portioners[2]. The Lai
2 CONC rd of Crogo had three daughters in all: Egidia married Roger Gordon fourth son of William Gordon of Lochinver, Marion married Cuthbert Amuligane and Janet married John Gordon son of Alexander Gordon of Auchenreoch.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==============
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-Gordon-of-Auchenreoch/60000000... married ....
2 CONT
2 CONT His son John Gordon married Janet, daughter of the Laird of Crogo
2 CONT
2 CONT Her sister Egidia "married Roger Gordon fourth son of William Gordon of Lochinver"
2 CONT
2 CONT Who would be http://www.geni.com/people/Roger-Gordon/6000000023598069994 I believe
2 CONT
