1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://era.ed.ac.uk/handle/1842/6875 ''Lairds and gentlemen: A study of the landed families of the Eastern Anglo-Scottish Borders c.1540-1603''] Vol 2 pp 55-8 & 118-9
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT
2 CONT TITLE: Laird of FERNIHIRST
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Legend says they are of Norman descent from Ralph, one of two brothers who lived near Jedburgh in the 14th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Carr, Ker, Kerr or Karre is as old as the Norman Conquest by William of Normandy in 1066. One of the followers of William I is recorded in the Roll Of The Gattle Abbey as the name of Karre. The early descendants of thi
2 CONC s Norman soldier and succeeding generations spread on both sides of the border of England and Scotland. Direct lines can be traced through various peerage books of England and Scotland to Andrew Kerr I, the 6th Baron of Ferniehirs
2 CONC t, Scotland. He was born in 1450, created Baron in 1480 and knighted in 1483. He and his son Andrew II, 7th Baron of Ferniehirst, were remarkable men for talent and undaunted courage, conspicious in reigns of James IV and James V.
2 CONC  Andrew Kerr II, died in 1543. His son Sir John Kerr, 8th Baron of Ferniehirst, did great service against the English and rescued Queen Mary from incursions by the English against the Scots. Sir Thomas, 9th Baron and son of Sir Jo
2 CONC hn, was also devoted to Queen Mary's interests. (Quoted from Watson's 'The House of Carr'1926). 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Andrew Kerr, 6th Baron of Ferniehirst (b. 1450, succeeded 1480, knighted 1483), father of
2 CONT *Andrew Kerr, 7th Baron of Ferniehirst (d. 1543), father of
2 CONT *John Kerr, 8th Baron of Ferniehirst
2 CONT *Thomas Kerr, 9th Baron of Ferniehirst
2 CONT *John Kerr
2 CONT
2 CONT http://myweb.cableone.net/nicks/myersgenealogy/carr.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT After the disastrous defeat of the Scottish armies at Flodden in 1513 Andrew Ker of Ferniehirst (known as Dand) secured the abbey in his bid to establish a line of defence in expectation of an English attack following the battle. 
2 CONC Dand installed his younger brother Thomas as Commendator.  Source http://clankerr.co.uk/a%20visit%20to%20jedburgh/kelso%20abbey.html
