1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://era.ed.ac.uk/handle/1842/6875 ''Lairds and gentlemen: A study of the landed families of the Eastern Anglo-Scottish Borders c.1540-1603''] Vol 2 pp 55-8 & 118-9
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===SIR JOHN EDMONSTONE OF THAT ILK AND EDNAM===
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Sir John Edmonestone of that Ilk, knight (1584) Jo. Edmestoun de eodem (1588) D. Jo. Edmestoun de eodem (1590)'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''According to Sir Archibald Edmonstone of Duntrath, Baronet, Sir John Edmonstone of that Ilk and Ednam, here treated, died in 1592 and was succeeded by his son Andrew, [https://archive.org/details/genealogicalacco00inedmo/page/9
2 CONC 4/mode/1up Duntreath: 94] although it would appear that he was still alive on 2 February 1594. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi06scot/page/17/mode/1up RMS 1593-1608: 56] Stirnet suggests that he was dead by 1594. [htt
2 CONC ps://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ee/edmonstone3.php Stirnet: Edmonstone 3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the Exchequer Rolls of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''17 March 1564-65: Roxburgh. Vicecomes respondebit pro 2d. argenti per duplicationem albefirme terrarum et baronie de Ednem, cum molendino eorundem, tenentibus, tenandriis, advocatione et donatione hospitalis de Ednem etc, jacen
2 CONC tium infra balliam suam, regine debitis per sasinam datam Johanni Edmanstoun ; exoeptis illis quadraginta marcatis earundem que prins fuerunt Edwardi Hume, burgensis de Edinburgh, hereditarie, que nunc existunt in manibus Eufamie 
2 CONC Wauchope, relicte quondam Johannis Edmanstoun de eodem, domine conjuncte infeodationis earundem, reservato tamem vitali redditu unius annui redditos quadraginta librarum monete Scotie annuatim percipiendi etc ad duos anni terminos
2 CONC  oonsuetos etc., Penthecostes etc., de terris et baronia de Ednem, molendino ejusdem, et una tenandria de Ednem etc. Agneti Ker, spouse dicti Johannis Edmanstoun, junions, pro toto tempore vite sue. lEdinburgh, 17 March.] [https:/
2 CONC /archive.org/details/exchequerrollss03exchgoog/page/n615/mode/1up Exchequer Rolls of Scotland, 1557-1567: p.536]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the Laing Charters===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''28 June 1584: Instrument of Sasine narrating that Sir John Edmonestone of that Ilk, knight, personally gave sasine to his first-born son, Andrew Edmestoune, of the lands of the Mains of Edmonstoun, which the said Andrew accepte
2 CONC d for himself and his heirs-male of his body, whom failing, the heirs of tailzie of the said Sir John, in the old infeftment to the late John Edmonstone, his father, himself, and brothers, as contained in the charter by the said S
2 CONC ir John to the said Andrew, dated 19 August 1578, and confirmed on 17 April 1582, by Robert [Pitcairn], commendator of Dunfermline. Sasine given 28 June 1584; reserving the granter's liferent. Witnesses, David Edmestone of Wolmet,
2 CONC  Robert Dicksone, John Patersone and Ninian Blakwod, domestics of the said Sir John, and John Broun, servant of Mr. John Makcalzeane, M.A., the notary public. [322, Box 10. [https://archive.org/details/calendaroflaing00edin/page/2
2 CONC 67/mode/1up Laing Charters: 1083]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4 April 1588: Charter in favour of Euphamie Makdowell, daughter of Thomas Makdowell of Stodrig and future spouse of John Dickson of Loanhead. Witnessed by Jo. Edmestoun de eodem and others. [https://archive.org/details/registru
2 CONC mmagnisi07scot/page/343/mode/1up RMS 1609-20: charter number 947]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 November 1590: Charter by which James VI, King of Scots, confirms a charter by which Alexander, Lord Home, and Cristine Douglas, his spouse, confirmed that they had sold various lands to Sir John Edmonstone of that Ilk, and h
2 CONC is son and apparent heir, Andrew Edmonstone. The lands in question were Brigham in the sheriffdom of Berwick, the Mains of Dunglas and the lands of Thornton in the Constabulary of Haddington, and sheriffdom of Edinburgh. Sir John 
2 CONC Edmonstone was to hold the lands conveyed in liferent, and his son Andrew and his lawful male heirs in heritage. Andrew's second sister, Agnes Edmonstone, and his third sister, Elizabeth Edmonstone, are named as his heirs in remai
2 CONC nder. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi05scot/page/629/mode/1up RMS 1580-93: charter number 1862]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2 February 1593-94: Charter under the Great Seal of Scotland by which James VI, King of Scots, confirms possession of of the land and barony of Ednam in the sheriffdom of Roxburgh to JOANNI EDMONDSTOUN son and heir apparent of 
2 CONC Andree Edmondstoun fiar of that Ilk (feodatarii de eodem). The charter also confirms his possession of the lands of Ryislaw and Little Blakburne in the sheriffdom of Berwick, and a one third part of the land and mill of Nudrie-Mer
2 CONC schell in the sheriffdom of Edinburgh. The office of Coroner of Lothian was also confirmed to him. Andrew's father Sir John Edmondstoun of that Ilk continued to hold a liferent interest in these lands, and after his decease this w
2 CONC as to pass to Andrew. John Edmondstoun junior, and his lawful heirs, were to hold these lands, whom failing they were to pass to Patrick Edmondstoun, brother german of Sir John, whom failing they were to pass to Andrew's nearest m
2 CONC ale heir bearing the name and arms of Edmondstoun. John Edmonston, junior, is said to be the grandson of Sir John Cockburn of Ormiston by his daughter Alison Cockburn: "D. Joannis Cokburne de Ormestoun militis justiciarie clerici,
2 CONC  avi dicti Jo. E. minoris (ex quondam Alisona C. ejus filia legitima nati)". [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi06scot/page/17/mode/1up RMS 1593-1608: 56]'''
