1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===ANDREW EDMONSTONE OF THAT ILK AND EDNAM===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Andrew Edmonstone of that Ilk and Ednam, here treated, is the eldest son and heir of Sir John Edmonstone of that Ilk and Ednam.  [https://archive.org/details/calendaroflaing00edin/page/267/mode/1up Laing Charters: 1083]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Andrew Edmonstone of that Ilk and Ednam, here treated, married Maria Gordon. They are noticed as man and wife on 9 November 1602. [https://archive.org/details/calendaroflaing00edin/page/347/mode/1up Laing Charters: 1428]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the Laing Charters===
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''28 June 1584: Instrument of Sasine narrating that Sir John Edmonestone of that Ilk, knight, personally gave sasine to his first-born son, Andrew Edmestoune, of the lands of the Mains of Edmonstoun, which the said Andrew accepte
2 CONC d for himself and his heirs-male of his body, whom failing, the heirs of tailzie of the said Sir John, in the old infeftment to the late John Edmonstone, his father, himself, and brothers, as contained in the charter by the said S
2 CONC ir John to the said Andrew, dated 19 August 1578, and confirmed on 17 April 1582, by Robert [Pitcairn], commendator of Dunfermline. Sasine given 28 June 1584; reserving the granter's liferent. Witnesses, David Edmestone of Wolmet,
2 CONC  Robert Dicksone, John Patersone and Ninian Blakwod, domestics of the said Sir John, and John Broun, servant of Mr. John Makcalzeane, M.A., the notary public. [322, Box 10. [https://archive.org/details/calendaroflaing00edin/page/2
2 CONC 67/mode/1up Laing Charters: 1083]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''9 November 1602: Instrument of Sasine following on a precept (directed to Robert Young, dweller in Edmonstone, as bailie) in a charter of alienation (dated 5 November 1602) by Andrew Edmonstone of that Ilk, and Maria Gordoun, h
2 CONC is spouse, in favour of George Preston of that Ilk and of Craigmillar, of the meadow called the South Dolf meadow of Edmonstone, lying between the Dolf burn on north, the highway running from the ' Lady brig end ' southward to Dal
2 CONC keith, by the ' Dolf brig ' on west, the arable lands of Mains of Edmonstoun, with ditch and earthen mound cast from west towards east on south, and a ditch and earthen mound and marsh called ' Loch-levin ' dividing the said meado
2 CONC w from the lands of Niddrie merschell on east; with power to make another ditch and earthen mound on the south six feet broader than the present ditch and old mound, the better to protect the grass and hay in the said meadow. Sasi
2 CONC ne given 9 November 1602, and recorded in the secretary's register at Edinburgh, 14 December, same year. The witnesses to the charter are John Haitlie of Mellerstane, and Richard Abercromby of Poltoun, Alexander Lawson in Nydrie, 
2 CONC John Diksoun, Ninian Blakwod and Robert Young, the granter's servitors. Witnesses to sasine, William Hering, gardener in Craigmillar, Alexander Horshoup, servant there, John Hunter, the 'hynd' there, Daniel Hill, his servant, and 
2 CONC Alexander Neilson, smith in Ladybrig-end. Mr. William Kellie, of St. Andrews diocese, notary public. [345, Box 10. [https://archive.org/details/calendaroflaing00edin/page/347/mode/1up Laing Charters: 1428]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 November 1590: Charter by which James VI, King of Scots, confirms a charter by which Alexander, Lord Home, and Cristine Douglas, his spouse, confirmed that they had sold various lands to Sir John Edmonstone of that Ilk, and h
2 CONC is son and apparent heir, Andrew Edmonstone. The lands in question were Brigham in the sheriffdom of Berwick, the Mains of Dunglas and the lands of Thornton in the Constabulary of Haddington, and sheriffdom of Edinburgh. Sir John 
2 CONC Edmonstone was to hold the lands conveyed in liferent, and his son Andrew and his lawful male heirs in heritage. Andrew's second sister, Agnes Edmonstone, and his third sister, Elizabeth Edmonstone, are named as his heirs in remai
2 CONC nder. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi05scot/page/629/mode/1up RMS 1580-93: charter number 1862]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2 February 1593-94: Charter under the Great Seal of Scotland by which James VI, King of Scots, confirms possession of of the land and barony of Ednam in the sheriffdom of Roxburgh to JOANNI EDMONDSTOUN son and heir apparent of 
2 CONC Andree Edmondstoun fiar of that Ilk (feodatarii de eodem). The charter also confirms his possession of the lands of Ryislaw and Little Blakburne in the sheriffdom of Berwick, and a one third part of the land and mill of Nudrie-Mer
2 CONC schell in the sheriffdom of Edinburgh. The office of Coroner of Lothian was also confirmed to him. Andrew's father Sir John Edmondstoun of that Ilk continued to hold a liferent interest in these lands, and after his decease this w
2 CONC as to pass to Andrew. John Edmondstoun junior, and his lawful heirs, were to hold these lands, whom failing they were to pass to Patrick Edmondstoun, brother german of Sir John, whom failing they were to pass to Andrew's nearest m
2 CONC ale heir bearing the name and arms of Edmondstoun. John Edmonston, junior, is said to be the grandson of Sir John Cockburn of Ormiston by his daughter Alison Cockburn: "D. Joannis Cokburne de Ormestoun militis justiciarie clerici,
2 CONC  avi dicti Jo. E. minoris (ex quondam Alisona C. ejus filia legitima nati)". [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi06scot/page/17/mode/1up RMS 1593-1608: 56]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''15 December 1614: Charter under the Great Seal of Scotland by which James VI, King of Scots, confirms that Sir Patrick Home of Ayton has given various lands in Berwickshire in liferent to his wife Lady Elizabeth Edmonstone. Sir
2 CONC  Patrick's gift followed from their marriage contact, which was dated at Ayton on 9 April 1602, and at Greenknow in the Berwickshire parish of Gordon on I June 1602. The contact was entered into with the consent of Sir Patrick's f
2 CONC ather, William Home of Ayton, and Elizabeth's brother, Andrew Edmonstone of that Ilk, both of whom died before 15 December 1614. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi07scot/page/422/mode/1up RMS, 1609-20: charter number 11
2 CONC 46]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''13 June 1615: Extract ratification by John Edmondstoun of that ilk, of contract, 15 November, 22 April 1610, whereby deceased Andrew Edmondstoun of that ilk, his father, disponed to Sir Patrick Home of Aytoun, kt., and dame Eli
2 CONC zabeth Edmondstoun, his spouse, lands of Litill Blackburne, in sheriffdom of Berwick. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=RH15%2f16%2f13&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=andrew+edmondstoun&ko=p&
2 CONC r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=y National Records of Scotland, Writs relating to lands in Coldingham, reference RH15/16/13]'''
