2 CONT * John Rutherfoord, of Hunthill, who succeeded him, and was designed "the Cock of Hunthill" he left issue three sons :
2 CONT ** I. Thomas, his heir.
2 CONT ** II. John Rutherfoord, father of John of Bankend, whose male line is said to be extinct.
2 CONT ** III. Richard Rutherfoord, of Littlehaugh, who carried on the line of the family.
2 CONT * He was succeeded by his eldest son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Extract from The Rutherfurds of that Ilk, and their cadets. by Thomas H. Cockburn-Hood (Edinburgh 1884) pages xxxviii (http://archive.org/details/rutherfurdsoftha00ruth)
2 CONT * Much importance, however, cannot be attached to the correctness of Christian names given in these old records, as, amongst numberless instances of errors, John the Cock appears called James in one public document. His son and he
2 CONC ir, Thomas Ruthirfurd, had charter under the Great Seal, 30th June 161 2, to himself, " filio et haeredi Johannis Ruthirfurde de Hunthill et Jeanae sposae ejus," of various lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Extract from The Scots Peerage (Volume VII) by Sir James Balfour Paul (Edinburgh 1910) page 376-377 (http://archive.org/details/cu31924092516248)
2 CONT * John Rutherfurd of Hunthill, called 'The Cock of Hunthill,' as son and heir, had a charter from his father to him and Agnes Kirktoun his wife, of certain lands in Seraisburgh on 18 June 1558. In 1565 he signed the bond to suppor
2 CONC t Queen Mary, on 6 April 1659 the bond to the King by the barons and others on the Borders, for the suppression of thieves, etc., and on 12 February 1571 the bond against the Laird of Ferniehurst. He was present with all his sons 
2 CONC at the battle of Redswyre in 1575, and on 6 March 1576, as younger of Hunthill, he had a charter by Archibald, Earl of Angus, confirming him in the lands of Hardaneheid in the barony of Oxnam. He married Agnes Kirktoun, and is als
2 CONC o said to have married his cousin Grizel Home of the Cowdenknowe family.
