1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===WALTER SCOTT OF BUCCLEUCH===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''19 March 1554-55: Instrument of Sasine following on a precept of clare constat by Walter Scot, grandson of deceased Walter Scot of Branxholm, knight, and son of deceased William Scot of Kirkurd, knight, with consent of Robert S
2 CONC cot of Allanhauch, his tutor, in favour of James Geddes as heir to Edward Geddes, his father, in lands in GD89/1/311.  Dated 19 March 1554[/5] and witnessed by William Gib, James Naper, John Melros and Sir John Allane, notary publ
2 CONC ic, 26 March, 1555. Witnesses: James Allane, William Thomsone, John Gib and James Murhouse. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD89%2f1%2f322&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=kirkurd&ko=p&r=&ro
2 CONC =s&df=&dt=1600&di=y National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Carmichael Family of Skirling, reference GD89/1/322]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Walter Scott, 4th Baron of Buccleuch==
2 CONT '''Walter Scott, 4th Baron of Buccleuch (1549–1574) was head of the Border family of Scott and, despite his youth, played a prominent part in the turbulent politics of 16th century Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Scott was the eldest son''' of Sir William Scott of Kirkurd, Younger of Buccleuch (died 1552), who was himself the son of Walter Scott, 3rd of Buccleuch (who was murdered on 4 October 1552, having been pre-deceased by his son).
2 CONC  '''His mother''' was Grisel, second daughter of John Betoun of Creich.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Scott succeeded his grandfather at the age of only three. His uncle''', Walter Scott of Goldielands, a natural half-brother of his father, led the Scotts during his minority.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''On 24 March 1566, Queen Mary appointed him captain of Newark Castle, Selkirkshire, whereupon he supported her with a force of over 3,000 men.[1] In January 1570 (in reliance upon the distraction likely to be caused by the assas
2 CONC sination of the Regent Moray, of which he had advance knowledge), he engaged in depredations on the English side of the Border. By way of retaliation the English, under the Earl of Sussex and Lord Scrope, destroyed his stronghold 
2 CONC at Branxholme Castle.[2] Scott rebuilt the castle in the following year.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was a principal leader of the raid to Stirling on 4 September 1571, when an attempt was made to seize the Regent Lennox, who was slain by one of the Hamiltons during the mêlée. Buccleuch, who had interposed to save the Regen
2 CONC t Morton, his kinsman, whom the Hamiltons intended also to have slain, was during the retreat taken prisoner by Morton, and was for some time confined in Doune Castle in Menteith.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Scott died at Branxholme on 17 April 1574.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Scott married Margaret Douglas''', daughter of David Douglas, 7th Earl of Angus. ('''She survived him and married''' secondly Francis Stewart, 5th Earl of Bothwell.) '''They had three children''':[1]
2 CONT *Walter Scott, later 1st Lord Scott of Buccleuch
2 CONT *Margaret (sometimes, though doubtfully, said to be married to Robert Scott of Thirlestane)
2 CONT *Mary, who married William Elliott of Lariston
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Scott,_4th_of_Buccleuch
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Walter Scott, 4th of Buccleuch1
2 CONT *'''M, #109618, b. circa 1549, d. 17 April 1574
2 CONT *Last Edited=4 Sep 2008
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.0%
2 CONT *'''     Sir Walter Scott, 4th of Buccleuch was born circa 1549.2 He was the son''' of Sir William Scott of Kirkurd and Buccleuch, younger and Grizel Bethune.3 '''A contract for the marriage of Sir Walter Scott, 4th of Buccleuch a
2 CONC nd Lady Margaret Douglas was signed on 19 June 1568.1 He died on 17 April 1574.4
2 CONT *'''     He held the office of Captain of Newark Castle between 1566 and 1579.3 He lived at Branxholme, Roxburghshire, Scotland.2 He lived at Buccleuch, Roxburghshire, Scotland.4
2 CONT *'''Child of Sir Walter Scott, 4th of Buccleuch and Lady Margaret Douglas
2 CONT **Sir Walter Scott, 1st Lord Scott of Buccleuch+2 b. 1565, d. 15 Dec 1611
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S8] BP1999 volume 1, page 1282. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S8]
2 CONT *[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct
2 CONC  or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 364. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 1, page 560. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume II, page 240.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p10962.htm#i109618 ____________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 51
2 CONT * Scott, Walter (1565-1611)      by Thomas Finlayson Henderson
2 CONT *SCOTT, WALTER, first Lord Scott of Buccleugh (1565–1611), born in 1565, was the '''only son of Sir Walter Scott of Buccleuch (d. 1574), by his wife, Lady Margaret Douglas''', eldest daughter of David, seventh earl of Angus, who a
2 CONC fterwards married Francis Stewart Hepburn, fifth earl of Bothwell. '''The father, who latterly became a devoted adherent of Mary Queen of Scots, was privy to the design for the assassination of the regent Moray, and, counting on i
2 CONC ts occurrence, set out the day before with Ker of Ferniehirst on a devastating raid into England. In revenge his lands were laid waste by the Earl of Sussex and Lord Scrope, and his castle of Branxholm blown up with gunpowder. He 
2 CONC was a principal leader of the raid to Stirling on 4 Sept. 1571, when an attempt was made to seize the regent Lennox, who was slain by one of the Hamiltons during the mêlée. Buccleuch, who had interposed to save the regent Morton, 
2 CONC his kinsman, whom the Hamiltons intended also to have slain, was during the retreat taken prisoner by Morton (Diurnal of Occurrents, p. 248), and was for some time confined in the castle of Doune in Menteith''' (Reg. P. C. Scotl. 
2 CONC ii. 156).
2 CONT *'''The son succeeded his father on 17 April 1574''', and on 21 June was infeft in the baronies of Branxholm as heir to David Scott, his grandfather's brother. Being a minor, the Earl of Morton—failing whom, the Earl of Angus—was 
2 CONC appointed his guardian. On account of a feud between Scott and Lord Hay, both were on 19 Aug. 1586 ordered to find caution of 10,000l. each for their good behaviour (ib. iv. 98). On 2 June 1587 he and other border chiefs were summ
2 CONC oned to appear before the privy council on 9 June to answer ‘touching good rule and quietness to be observed on the borders hereafter, under pain of treason’ (ib. p. 183); and on the 9th Robert Scott gave caution for him in five t
2 CONC housand merks that he would appear on the 21st (ib. p. 189). Towards the close of the year he and the laird of Cessford were, however, committed to ward for making incursions in England (Calderwood, History, iv. 641); but on 13 De
2 CONC c. he found caution in 10,000l. that on being liberated from the castle of Edinburgh he would by 10 Jan. find surety for the relief of the king and his wardens of ‘all attempts against the peace of England bygone and to come’ (Reg
2 CONC . P. C. Scotl. iv. 234).
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From:  http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Scott,_Walter_(1565-1611)_(DNB00) _______________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 51
2 CONT * Scott, Walter (1490?-1552)     by John Anderson
2 CONT *SCOTT, Sir WALTER (1490?–1552), of Buccleuch and Branxholm, Scottish chieftain, born about 1490, was eldest son of Sir Walter Scott of Buccleuch (d 1504). He was fourth in lineal descent from Sir Walter Scott (1426–1469), who fir
2 CONC st took the territorial designation of Buccleuch, and was the first to acquire the whole barony of Branxholm, with the castle, which remained the residence of the family for several generations. His mother, Elizabeth Ker of the Ce
2 CONC ssford family, was attacked in her residence of Catslack in Yarrow by an English force under Lord Grey de Wilton in 1548, and, with other inmates of the tower, was burnt to death.
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *Sir Walter Scott was thrice married: first, to Elizabeth Carmichael (of Carmichael), with issue two sons; secondly, to Janet Ker (of Fernihierst), from whom he was apparently divorced; and, thirdly, to Janet Betoun or Beaton, who
2 CONC se name is well known as the heroine of the ‘Lay of the Last Minstrel,’ and by whom he had two sons and three daughters. She was given to Sir Walter ‘in mariag by the Cardinall [Beaton], his other wif being yet on lif’ (Hamilton P
2 CONC apers, i. 740). Sir Walter Scott's eldest son died unmarried, while his second son, Sir William Scott, predeceased him, '''leaving a son Walter, afterwards Sir Walter (d 1574), who was father''' of Walter Scott, first Lord Scott o
2 CONC f Buccleuch [q. v.]
2 CONT * [William Fraser's The Scotts of Buccleuch, 2 vols. 1878; Captain Walter Scott's A True History of several Honourable Families of the Right Honourable Name of Scott, &c. ed. 1786; Letters and Papers Henry VIII, Foreign and Dom., 
2 CONC vols. i. ii.]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Scott,_Walter_(1490%3F-1552)_(DNB00) ____________
