1 OCCU Provost of Kirkcudbright; gentleman in the bedchamber of James VI
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir Thomas Maclellan''' (died 1597) was Provost of Kirkcudbright and father of Robert Maclellan, 1st Lord Kirkcudbright. He was responsible for the construction of MacLellan's Castle in the town.  He was appoint
2 CONC ed as a gentleman in the bedchamber of James VI in October 1580.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Maclellan_of_Bombie
2 CONT
2 CONT During his illness in June 1597, Maclellan travelled to Bath, Somerset in England to seek a cure. He wrote to William Cecil, 1st Baron Burghley asking permission to send things he had bought for his house in Kirkcubright by sea fr
2 CONC om London. Physicians in Bath told him there was no cure for his "tympanicall hydropsie".[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The peerage of Scotland: containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom, ... collected from the public records, and ancient chartularies of this nation, ... Illustrated with copper-plates. By Rob
2 CONC ert Douglas, Esq;.  Douglas, Robert, Sir, 1694-1770. < [https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/ecco/004896980.0001.000/1:149?rgn=div1;view=fulltext link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Ancestry 
2 CONT
2 CONT V. Sir WILLIAM, who got charters un∣der the great seal,*Willielmo MacLellan de Bombie, of several lands and baronies, inter 1507 et 1512.mmHe was in great favour with king James IV.* whom he accompanied to the fatal field of Flowd
2 CONC on, where he lost his life, anno 1513, leaving issue, by ''Elizabeth Mure'' his wife, a son and successor,
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT VI. Sir THOMAS, who got charters un∣der the great seal,*Thomae MacLellan de Bom∣bie, of the lands of Plintoun, Blackmark, and several others, inter 1516 et 1520.  This sir Thomas had the misfortune to be killed in the street of Ed
2 CONC inburgh,* in a fami∣ly feud between him and his neighbours the barons of Drumlanrig and Lochinvar, in Ju∣ly 1526, leaving issue a son,
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. Sir THOMAS who succeeded him. He got a charter, under the great seal,* of all the lands that his father sir Thomas, and his grandfather sir William died possest of, do∣mino Thomae MacLellan de Bombie, &c. anno 1542. He marrie
2 CONC d ''Helen'',* daughter of sir James Gordon of Lochinvar, by whom he had a son,
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT VIII, Sir THOMAS who succeeded him, and got charters under the great seal,*domino '''Thomae MacLellan de Bombie''', of several lands and baronies, inter 1590, et 1595. He married ''Grizel Maxwell'', daughter of John lord Herries, 
2 CONC by whom he had three sons.
2 CONT *1. Sir Robert, afterwards lord Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT *2. William MacLellan of Glenshannoch, whose son Thomas succeeded to the honours of Kirkcudbright, of whom afterwards.
2 CONT *3. John MacLellan of Bourg, whose son John succeeded also to the lordship of Kirkcud∣bright, as will be shown hereafter..
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Thomas''' died anno 1607, and was suc∣ceeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Descent 
2 CONT
2 CONT IX. Sir Robert, who, in his father's life∣time, got a charter under the great seal,*Ro∣berto filio et haeredi apparenti domini Thomae MacLellan de Bombie, terrarum baroniae de Bombiae, &c. anno 1595.  He was served and retoured he
2 CONC ir to his fa∣ther sir Thomas,*anno 1608.  He got a charter under the great seal,*do∣mino Roberto MacLellan de Bombie, militi, of several lands, anno 1610.  He afterwards got charters under the great seal,* of many other lands and 
2 CONC baronies, inter 1612, et 1616.  He was a man of great worth and merit, and highly esteemed by king James VI. who conferred the honour of knighthood upon him, and made him one of the gentlemen of his bed-chamber; and being in no le
2 CONC ss favour with king Charles I. was by that prince first created a baronet, then raised to the dignity of the peerage, by the title of lord baron of Kirkcudbright,* 25th May 1633, by patent to his heirs male general, they carrying 
2 CONC the name and arms of MacLellan, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 1st, ''Margaret'', daughter of sir Matthew Campbell of Loudoun, by whom he had a daughter.
2 CONT # Marian, married to sir Robert Maxwell of Orchardtown.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 2dly, ''Mary Montgomery'', daughter of Hugh viscount Airds, of the king∣dom of Ireland, by whom he had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT And dying without issue-male anno 1641, his estate and honours devolved upon his ne∣phew and heir-male,
2 CONT
2 CONT X. THOMAS MACLELLAN, son of his brother William of Glenskennoch before mentioned, who was next brother to the pa∣tentee.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * http://www.kirkcudbright.co/historyarticle.asp?ID=116&p=19&g=4
2 CONT * https://www.clanmaclellan.net/History.htm
2 CONT *http://www.heritagearchaeology.com.au/history-kirkcudbright.html
2 CONT *https://www.clanmaclellanancestry.com/MacLellan%20Castle%20Site.htm Built by Sir Thomas MacLellan of Bombie, Castle MacLellan is not a castle in the traditional sense but stands in the centre of the town of Kircudbright (pronounc
2 CONC ed Kirkoobray) in what is now known as the county of Dumfries and Galloway.  It was built on the site of the convent of Greyfriars which had been established by King James II in 1449.  Within nine years of the Reformation Act of 1
2 CONC 560,  Sir Thomas, the local Provost, acquired the site and the convent and used the buildings as a quarry for his new house. It was not built as a fortified castle (the gun loops are more a defense against potential robbers and ri
2 CONC ots rather than an invading army). But it was a house on a grand scale to impress the townspeople of its owners importance and prestige. In 1587 Sir Thomas entertained King James VI in Kircudbright and the king gave him a silver g
2 CONC un which is still in the local museum.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Brief Life History of Thomas
2 CONT
2 CONT When Sir Thomas MacClellan III, of Bombie,  Gentleman of the King's Bedchamber was born about 1526, in Kirkcudbright, Dumfriesshire, Scotland, his father, Thomas Mcclelland of Bombie, was 18 and his mother, Lady Marion Kennedy, wa
2 CONC s 18. He married Lady Helen Gordon in January 1556, in Kirkcudbrightshire, Scotland. They were the parents of at least 2 daughters. He died on 5 July 1598, in Kirkcudbright, Dumfriesshire, Scotland, United Kingdom, at the age of 7
2 CONC 3, and was buried in Kirkcudbright, Dumfriesshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT Scottish nobleman, nephew of Robert Maclellan, 1st Lord Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maclellan’s support for the Covenanters led to his ruin. Thomas Maclellan was charged with the raising of a feudal army in the parishes of Dunrod, Galtway and Kirkcudbright. In 1640 he was appointed Colonel of the South Regiment, 
2 CONC and accompanied the Scottish army into England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was present at the Battle of Philiphaugh with his regiment, where, by their gallantry they greatly contributed towards the victory of the Scottish forces. From his habit of always marching at the head of his regiment with a bar
2 CONC rel of brandy, which upon long marches and other needful occasions he would freely distribute to his followers, he became very popular among the troops.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.scotclans.com/scottish-clans/clan-maclellan/maclellan-people/
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT "McLellan and Kirkudbright", The Scots Peerage. volume V. pp.256-274 < [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/256/mode/1up?view=theater Archive.Org] >
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Thomas Maclellan of Bombie, He died in July 1597. He married, first, Helen, daughter of Sir James Gordon of Lochinvar, who died 26 November 1581; and, secondly (contract January 1584), Grisel, daughter of John Maxwell, fourth Lo
2 CONC rd Herries,' by whom he had issue : —
2 CONT
2 CONT **1. William of Glenshinnoch, Provost of Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT **2.  William of Glenshinnoch, Provost of Kirkcudbright. Died before 26 November 1631,^ having married Rosina, daughter of Sir Andrew Agnew of Lochnaw. He had by her : —
2 CONT ***(1) Willifnn, mentioned 1631.* Died s.p.
2 CONT ***(2) Thomas, second Lord Kirkcudbright.
