2 CONT Margaret Fleming  Margaret Fleming was the daughter of Malcolm Fleming, 3rd Lord Fleming and Janet Stewart. She married, firstly, Robert Graham, Lord Graham, son of William Graham, 2nd Earl of Montrose and Lady Janet K
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT Margaret Fleming Graham
2 CONT
2 CONT Cumbernauld North Lanarkshire, Scotland Death: 	Mar. 15, 1586 Auchterarder Perth and Kinross, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links:  Parents: Malcolm Fleming, Lord Fleming (1494 - 1547) Janet Stewart Fleming (1502 - 1562)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Robert Graham (1521 - 1547)*
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: John Graham (1547 - 1608)*
2 CONT
2 CONT Sibling: James Fleming, Lord Fleming (1534 - 1558)* Margaret Fleming Graham (1536 - 1586)
2 CONT
2 CONT *Calculated relationship
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: jmkeifer Record added: Feb 13, 2012  Find A Grave Memorial# 84934827
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Fleming was born c. 1528, daughter to Malcolm Fleming, 3rd Lord Fleming and his wife Janet Stewart, natural daughter of King James IV. by Isabel Stewart, Countess of Bothwell, daughter of James, third Earl of Buchan.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She married firstly, Robert Graham, Master of Graham, who was killed at the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh on 10 Sep 1547[1] along with her father and a brother in law. This marriage likely occurred c. 1545.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married, secondly (contract 30 Jan 1549), Thomas Erskine, Master of Erskine, without issue.[1] She had a charter in her viduity (widowhood) in liferent from John Cunningham of Drumquhassell on 1 March 1549 in implement of her 
2 CONC marriage-contract with Thomas, Master of Erskine.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married, thirdly, (contract 1 Apr 1557), to John Stewart, 4th Lord of Atholl Lord Chancellor.[2][3] They had a charter of the lands of Inglassey. They had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine, m. James Chisholm, Lord Madderty[4][5]
2 CONT She is said to have possessed powers of incantation, and at the birth of James VI. cast the pains of labour from the Queen on the Lady Hires.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was dead before 15 Mar 1587.
