1 OCCU Master of the Household to Mary Queen of Scots (1563). waggoner; Scottish Ambassador to France (1583), 7th Lord Seton, fourth Lord
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''''George, fifth Lord Seton''''' seems to have been born in 1531, and was educated in France. He was served heir to his father at Linlithgow, in the lands of West Niddrie 19 May 1550, in the lands, lordship and b
2 CONC arony of Seytoun and Wintoun 2 May 1552, and in Wincliburgh 5 October 1557. He interdicted himself from alienating any portion of his heritage until he attained a certain age; this disability was relaxed by the Lords of Council 7 
2 CONC May 1554. He had a commission of justiciary 19 May 1554. He was Provost of Edinburgh on 20 October 1557.
2 CONT The Queen Regent endeavoured to procure his re-election when his term of office expired, but failed to do so, and he vacated 27 October 1559. On 14 December 1557 he was appointed member of a commission sent to France by Parliament
2 CONC , and he was present at the marriage of Queen Mary to the Dauphin 24 April 1558, when "he had ane noble present sent him of silver plate by Henry II., then King of France, with ane yearly pension of 1200 francks." He returned to S
2 CONC cotland in the following October. In 1560 he accompanied the Bishop of Glasgow to Paris, arriving 3 August. He seems to have gone home and again returned to France, whence he accompanied the Queen to Scotland, arriving 19 August 1
2 CONC 561. He entertained Her Majesty at his magnificent house, later known as Seton Palace (which he repaired and greatly enlarged), on 4 and 5 January 1561-62. Kingston says that the Queen offered Lord Seton an earldom. If this be the
2 CONC  fact, the tender may probably have been made during that visit.
2 CONT In the same month Lord Seton was reported by an English agent to have disclosed certain designs of the French on Berwick, etc.; this may have been done of set purpose, to deceive Queen Elizabeth's Government. He was Master of the 
2 CONC Royal Household in 1563. On 15 March 1565 report was made to Cecil that Lord Seton was obliged to fly to France, and a fortnight later application was made for permission for him to pass through England on his way, and on 24 Septe
2 CONC mber following for his return. When Bothwell first publicly announced his ambitious design of marrying the Queen, at the famous supper on 19 April 1567, a band giving a general consent to the marriage was subscribed by the assembl
2 CONC ed nobles. It is said that Lord Seton was among those who signed it; but this appears doubtful. Queen Mary passed the night of 14 June 1567 at Seton.
2 CONT The next day she surrendered to the confederate Lords at Carbery Hill. Lord Seton was deep in the plot for her escape from the island fortress of Lochleven, was in waiting ready to receive her at her landing on the shore of the la
2 CONC ke, and escorted her to his castle of Niddrie 2 May 1568. He was one of the nobles who signed a band in her support at Hamilton on 8 May, and was present with her at the battle of Langside, where he was taken prisoner by the confe
2 CONC derates on 13 May. He was confined in Edinburgh Castle, but in June 1569 Queen Elizabeth granted him leave to pass from Berwick to London, on his way to France. On 10 August 1570 he was commissioned on behalf of the captive Queen 
2 CONC Mary to apply for assistance to Ferdinand, Duke of Alva, the Spanish Viceroy in the Low Countries. While there he incurred great risk by intriguing with the Scottish troops in the pay of the States of Holland, which were then in r
2 CONC ebellion, and endeavouring to win them over to the Spanish interest. 
2 CONT On 31 May 1571 he was in Paris. Queen Mary wrote to Alva on 18 April 1571 that he was her most faithful and devoted subject and servant, and that she was convinced that next to the honour of God, his duty to his Queen was his sole
2 CONC  object. But early in 1573 he was fain to submit himself to King James VI. Soon afterwards he appears as a Privy Councillor; he first sat at the board 3 June 1573. In the following year it was reported to Pope Gregory XIII. that L
2 CONC ord Seton and his three sons had been excommunicated by the Established Church.
2 CONT In 1577 Lord Seton was again in Paris, and the next year in Flanders, on the Queen's business. In 1579 he and his three sons were ordered into confinement, apparently on suspicion of harbouring a servant of the Bishop of Glasgow, 
2 CONC Queen Mary's agent in Paris. On 26 October 1583 King James VI. wrote to King Henry III. of France that he was sending George, Lord Seton, one of his council, as Ambassador to negotiate for a renewal of the treaty of friendship bet
2 CONC ween the two countries. The envoy assured Queen Mary in a letter dated 16 September previous, which was intercepted by the English and deciphered, that his main object in accepting the mission was to advance her service. He wrote 
2 CONC to Pope Gregory XIII. from Paris on 14 March 1584, informing his Holiness of the existence in Scotland of rebellion abetted by England, and imploring assistance. Sir E. Stafford wrote to Sir F. Walsingham from Paris on 29 May foll
2 CONC owing, that Lord Seton aimed at getting liis son appointed Captain of the Scottish Guard. He returned for the last time to Scotland towards the end of 1585, died 8 January 1585-86, and was buried at Seton Church.
2 CONT George, fifth Lord Seton married (contract 2 August 1550) Isabell, daughter of Sir William Hamilton of Sanquhar, one of the Lords of Council. She survived him, died 13 November 1604 aged about seventy-five years, and was buried at
2 CONC  Seton Church.
2 CONT They had issue :
2 CONT 1. ''George'', Master of Seton, who died in boyhood. it is said in March 1562.
2 CONT 2.''' ''Robert,''''' who succeeded as sixth Lord Seton.
2 CONT 3.'' Sir John'', who had from his father a charter of the lands of East and West Barnes, dated 10 May 1583. He bore in his coat armorial the sword supporting an imperial crown said to have been granted as an augmentation to his an
2 CONC cestor Sir Alexander Seton along with those lauds. He was brought up at the Court of Spain. Lord Kingston states that King Philip II. made him a Knight of Santiago, a Gentleman of the Bedchamber and Caballero de la hoca, and grant
2 CONC ed to him and his heirs a pension of two thousand crowns yearly. In 1575 he visited the English Court, and was well received. He was appointed Master of the Horse 9 May 1581, having been so styled on the tenth of the previous mont
2 CONC h, when he received instructions for his conduct as envoy to the Court of England. The mission proved abortive; Sir John was not allowed to pass the frontier; the English warden believed him to be a servant of the King of Spain, a
2 CONC nd suspected him of a design to communicate with the French and Spanish ambassadors in London. He seems to have been in Spain in 1583 or 1584. In 1586 he was nominated Scottish Ambassador to the Court of Spain. On 3 January 1586-8
2 CONC 7 he was appointed for life First Master of the Royal Household. On 1 July 1587 he is styled Miles, sive Eqiies auratus. On 8 July 1587 he was appointed for life Comptroller of the Royal property and on 17 February 1587-88 he was 
2 CONC admitted an Extraordinary Lord of Session. He died in May 1594 Sir John married on 8 September 1588 (contract dated 10 and 12 of the previous month), Anna, youngest daughter of William, seventh Lord Forbes, and was by her ancestor
2 CONC  of the Setons of Barnes.
2 CONT 4.'' Alexander'', who was created Earl of Dunfermline. He became Chancellor of Scotland.
2 CONT 5. ''Sir William'', of Kylesmure, Knight, is said to have been born in 1562. On 3 January 1584-85 he had
2 CONT from his father a grant of the provision of the College Church of Seton, vacant through the decease of Thomas Raith, last Provost thereof. On 15 December 1602 he had a letter of gift of a reward for his trouble and charges in brin
2 CONC ging of certain craftsmen clothmakers for the great benefit of his native country. On 15 June 1611 a commission issued appointing him and three others to be justiciaries, with jurisdiction in the Border counties; and on 1 July fol
2 CONC lowing he was named member of a joint commission for the settlement and maintenance of peace on the Borders between Scotland and England. He wrote in the capacity of Sheriff of Edinburgh to the Secretary of State on 10 February 16
2 CONC 16; and on 18 June 1618 he received £1000 for services rendered as one of the Masters of the Household during the King's visit to Scotland. In 1620 he was Postmaster-General of Scotland, with patent for a monopoly of the supply of
2 CONC  post horses throughout the kingdom. He died in July 1635, and was buried in Seton Church. Sir William married Agnes Stirling (who is said to have come of the family of Stirling of Glorat), and had issue by her; but his male line 
2 CONC seems to have failed in the person of his eldest son, Sir William Seton of Grange, His Majesty's Chief Postmaster, who died unmarried in the year 1662, and was buried in Seton Church.
2 CONT 6. ''Margare''t, married at Niddry Castle, on 1 August 1574 (contract 15 and 16 June 1574"), to Lord Claud Hamilton, fourth and youngest son of James, second Earl of Arran and first Duke of Chatelherault (afterwards first Lord Pai
2 CONC sley), and had issue. She died a few days before 10 February 1616.
2 CONT '''''Source: "The Scots Peerage" Vol 8, page 585.'''''
2 CONT
