2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page for George Seton, 5th Lord Seton:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Seton,_5th_Lord_Seton
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton, 7th Lord Seton (1531-1586) was a Lord of the Parliament of Scotland, Master of the Household of Mary, Queen of Scots and Provost of Edinburgh. He was the eldest son of George Seton, 6th Lord Seton and Elizabeth Hay, 
2 CONC a daughter of John Hay, 3rd Lord Hay of Yester. His childhood and schooling were in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edinburgh and the Reformation
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton was Provost of Edinburgh in 1557, and from time to time would send his carpenter, Robert Fendour, to the Burgh Council as his representative. In February 1558, George Seton was one of eight commissioners sent to Henry
2 CONC  II of France to negotiate the marriage of Mary, Queen of Scots, to the Dauphin.[1] On 29 November 1558, the Parliament of Scotland granted that Seton and the others had fulfilled their commission. In February 1559, the town counc
2 CONC il gave him funds to prepare a banquet for Mary of Guise on their behalf.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Seton and the burgh council began to encounter difficulties, due only in part to the Scottish Reformation. Then, after rioting in Perth, Edinburgh was occupied by the Protestant Lords of the Congregation in June 1559. Set
2 CONC on tried unsuccessfully to protect the Blackfriars and Greyfriars monasteries. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Protestant Lords left Edinburgh in July, but made an agreement with Guise permitting freedom of conscience in religion. Seton, the Earl of Huntly and Châtellerault were asked to meet the people of Edinburgh to discuss the rest
2 CONC oration of Mass in St Giles. According to John Knox, they met with refusal and the people would not allow the Mass in any other church.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the time the Lords of the Congregation occupied Edinburgh for the second time in October 1559, another rival council had already formed, lead by Archibald Douglas of Kilspindie. The Protestant lords retreated and Seton's counci
2 CONC l was re-instated. When the English army mobilised by the Treaty of Berwick arrived in April 1560, Kilspindie's council was back in power.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the subsequent Siege of Leith in 1560, Seton fought for Mary of Guise against the Scottish Protestants and the English army. On 24 April he attacked the English camp at Restalrig. He was seized by an English cavalryman who 
2 CONC broke his sword and staff, but was rescued by French musketeers.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In France in October 1560, he secretly met the English ambassador, Nicolas Throckmorton, asking him for a passport to return to Scotland through England. He managed to convince Throckmorton that he regretted his service of Mary of
2 CONC  Guise and French causes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Seton had gained a French pension as a gentleman of the King's Chamber. He intended to take a portrait of Queen Mary and her letter to Elizabeth, but Mary disappointed him of this mission, saying the picture was not ready. Seton l
2 CONC eft France in November 1560, accompanied by an archer of the Scottish Guard called Alexander Clark, whose loyalty Throckmorton thought he had bought.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary in Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT During the personal reign of Mary in Scotland George had a loyal inscription set in large carved letters and gilded above the entrance to Seton Palace;
2 CONT *"UN DIEU, UN FOY, UN ROY, UN LOY"
2 CONT *One God for all time: One loyalty to the monarch.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was unsuccessful in proposing Seton as Provost of Edinburgh again in October 1561. Next year she made a better choice by backing Kilspindie, now associated with the success of the Reformation.[8] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Randolph, the English diplomat, reported that he heard that Mary and Lord Darnley went to Seton Palace and were 'bedded' after their marriage at Holyroodhouse.[9] Days after the death of Lord Darnley, on 17 February 1567 Ma
2 CONC ry had a blue costume for her fool called George Steven delivered to her at Seton Palace, in April she was there with her council.[10] With other supporters of Mary's marriage to James Hepburn, 4th Earl of Bothwell Seton signed th
2 CONC e Ainslie Tavern Bond on 19 April 1567.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary's marriage and continued rule in Scotland was opposed by the Confederate Lords. Mary stayed at Seton Palace before her capture nearby at the battle of Carberry Hill. She was then imprisoned at Lochleven Castle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She escaped to Seton's castle at Niddry but her supporters were defeated again at the battle of Langside. Seton was taken prisoner and early reports thought him killed. The son of Lord Ochiltree, John Knox's brother-in-law, would 
2 CONC have killed him in revenge for his father's injury, but he yielded and was saved by James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray.[11] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Mary went into England, where her companion, Seton's half-sister Mary Seton quickly joined her. With the other Marian lords Seton was imprisoned in Edinburgh Castle where Mary was worried that he was at risk from plague.[12]
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Seton was finally allowed to go into exile in France. Elizabeth I of England sent him a passport on 1 June 1569.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Supporter of the exiled queen
2 CONT
2 CONT Three Scottish supporters of Mary, the Duke of Châtellerault, the Earl of Huntly and Earl of Argyll gave Seton a commission to treat with the Duke of Alba, Viceroy of Lower Germany, as Mary's ambassador in August 1570. Seton was t
2 CONC o ask for Spanish help to re-instate Mary in Scotland and expel her son's supporters who depended on English power.[14] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 1570, Lord Morley met him in the household of Katherine Neville, the Countess of the exiled Earl of Northumberland at Bruges. Seton told Morley that he had come to escort the Countess, who had previously sought refuge
2 CONC  in Scotland, into France.[15] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The author of the Historie and Life of James the Sext, recorded what must have been a popular account of the mission to the Duke of Alba. Seton tried to convince Alba to provide an army of 10,000 men by persuading the Scottish sol
2 CONC diers fighting against Spain to change sides. The soldiers were unresponsive until Seton himself was captured and tortured on the rack. The soldiers then mutinied only till he was released. There was an offer of money, but Alba co
2 CONC uld not spare the men, and Seton's mission was unsuccessful.[16] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile in Scotland, the goods of his French stepmother, Marie Pieris, and half-brother Robert Seton were seized by Regent Moray's officers.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1572, Seton was in the Spanish Netherlands with the Duke of Alba and still in contact with the Countess of Northumberland. Seton's return to Scotland through England prompted thorough searches of Scottish shipping in En
2 CONC glish waters.[18] 
2 CONT
2 CONT An Italian called Battista di Trento wrote a long letter to Elizabeth I of England in 1577, which alleged to reveal a plot some years earlier involving Seton and his sons, including Alexander, then a student in Rome. Mary would ha
2 CONC ve married the Duke of Norfolk and be restored to the Scottish throne. Seton would pay to secure Edinburgh Castle on her behalf. Elizabeth would marry the Earl of Leicester. Battista laid out five schemes for the plot and the 19th
2 CONC  century editor of William Cecil's papers believed his circumstancial details to show some "sub-stratum of truth" in his statements.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1579, during the suppression of the Hamilton family, Lord Seton and three of his sons were ordered to enter ward at Brechin Castle. Seton pleaded with the King's keepers at Stirling Castle to mitigate their charge of treaso
2 CONC n. His son John Seton of Barnes, known as the 'Cavalier de Bucca' from his post at the Spanish court, had returned to Scotland and was suspected to have brought messages from the exiled Queen Mary.[20] On 2 June 1581, Seton and tw
2 CONC o of his sons watched the execution of Regent Morton from a forestair on the Royal Mile of Edinburgh.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ambassador in France, 1583
2 CONT
2 CONT After the collapse of the Gowrie Regime, Seton was sent as ambassador to France in December 1583. He was accompanied by William Schaw, Master of Works to James VI of Scotland. An English observer heard that the young Laird of Fint
2 CONC ry, a Catholic, would accompany them to escape his excommunication from the Church of Scotland, and the Master of Livingston would go to bring Catherine, Duchess of Lennox, widow of Esmé Stewart, and her son Ludovic back to Scotla
2 CONC nd. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A French diplomat, François de Rocherolles, Seigneur de Maineville, according to Sir Robert Bowes, was behind the mission and the choice of Lord Seton.[22] When he was selected for the embassy in September 1583, Seton wrote to Que
2 CONC en Mary from Seton Palace to explain his mission. He said he was to continue the Auld Alliance with France, follow the advice of the Duke of Guise, and complete the treaty with her and her son. He explained that the poverty of Kin
2 CONC g James frustrated his plans, which lead to Seton undertaking the diplomatic mission at his own expense, and he hoped she could help. Her service was his principal motive. He mentioned that the English envoy Francis Walsingham had
2 CONC  left Scotland on 15 (or 25) September 1583, and had a very poor reception and entertainment in Scotland.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scottish embassy was keenly observed by an English diplomat, Sir Edward Stafford. Stafford noted his audience with the French king in February 1584, supported by the Dukes of Guise and Joyeuse. He said that Seton was lavish in
2 CONC  his entertainment and display of silver plate, which resulted in a suspicion that he was funded by Spain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stafford thought that Seton's mission concerned a marriage for James VI to the Princess of Lorraine. Seton had a commercial mandate from the Burgh of Edinburgh which had also contributed 2000 marks to his hire of Andrew Lamb's shi
2 CONC p.[24] By May 1584, Seton had run out of money and pawned his silver plate and the guns of his ship at Dieppe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Seton asked Stafford about the rebel leaders of the Raid of Ruthven who had fled into England, and Stafford wrote to Francis Walsingham that Seton was foolish in this conversation.[25] On 21 June 1584, Stafford remarked in another
2 CONC  letter that Seton's phrases echoed those of Mary, Queen of Scots, and clearly the two maintained frequent communication.[26]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Seton's return to Scotland, de Maineville wrote to James VI in November 1584 that Seton had been earnest in this embassy, but the time was not right. Primarily, France was anxious to maintain good relations with England.[27]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and epitaph
2 CONT
2 CONT Seton remained in France till July 1585 or later. The Jesuit Robert Parsons wrote that he was uncertain whether to return or just send his son Alexander back to Scotland. (Although the ultra-Protestant Gowrie Regime was defeated, 
2 CONC the political situation in Scotland was not as Seton had hoped) Back in Scotland, in January Sir John Colville twice noted him as ill in his letters, and he died in February 1586.[28] On 22 June 1586, his son Alexander, Prior of P
2 CONC luscarden, returned to Edinburgh council copies of their papers regarding French import duties sent with George to Henry II.[29] George was buried at Seton Collegiate Church and his memorial has a lengthy Latin epitaph which also 
2 CONC describes his children's careers. The Latin text is signed 'A.S.F.C.F.F.,' presumbly referring to Alexander Seton as its author.[30]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Isobel Hamilton, daughter of Sir William Hamilton of Sanquhar. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children included;
2 CONT *1. George, Master of Seton, (died 1562)
2 CONT *2. Robert Seton, 1st Earl of Winton
2 CONT *3. Sir John Seton of Barnes, Master Carver to Philip II of Spain and Master of Horse to James VI.
2 CONT *4. Alexander Seton, 1st Earl of Dunfermline, Lord Urquhart, Lord Fyvie, and Prior of Pluscarden
2 CONT *5. Sir William Seton of Kylesmuir
2 CONT *6. Margaret Seton, married Claud Hamilton, 1st Lord Paisley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Seton's painter
2 CONT
2 CONT An anonymous portrait of Lord Seton was kept by the family of Hugh Somerville, 7th Lord Somerville, and is now part of the collection of the Scottish National Portrait Gallery. It shows him with his baton of office as Master of Qu
2 CONC een Mary's household, and may have been a commemorative piece made in later decades. The picture is reduced in size and the last figure of a date "157-" is missing. A motto on this painting, "in adversity, unyielding; in prosperit
2 CONC y generous," matches an inscription recorded under a version of the painting at Seton Palace by Alexander Seton, 1st Viscount of Kingston; "In adversity patiens; in prosperitie, benevolus; Hazard yet forward."[31] Seton's portrait
2 CONC  is now attributed to Adrian Vanson. In January 1582, a painter in his employ, "Lord Seton's painter," was paid for drawing the King's portrait for coinage. The painter may perhaps have been Adrian Vanson or Arnold Bronckorst, who
2 CONC  were both portrait painters to James VI.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT Seton was also painted in a family group by Frans Pourbus the Elder, now in the National Gallery of Scotland. The 18th century heraldic writer Alexander Nisbet described some of his additions to the interior of Seton Palace. Above
2 CONC  the fireplace in the Great Hall were carved his coat of arms quartered with the Earl of Buchan encircled with a collar which Nisbet claimed to repesent the Order of the Thistle. The ceiling of another room, called Samson's Hall, 
2 CONC encorporated 28 armorial achievements of families of France, Scotland and Lorraine, "curiously embossed and illuminated."[33] Viscount Kingston mentions seeing a mural painting on the end wall of the Long Gallery at Seton Palace, 
2 CONC which he believed showed the exiled Lord Seton driving a wagon during his years of exile in France following the abdication of Mary, Queen of Scots.[34]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Painting 
2 CONT
2 CONT George V, 7th Lord Seton (about 1531 - 1585) and his Family by Frans Pourbus the Elder ('''without wife''') 
2 CONT
2 CONT >George Seton is shown here surrounded by his four sons and his daughter. Seton acted as her ambassador to the Duke of Alva in the Spanish Netherlands, and it was during this posting in 1572 that this portrait was painted. The fig
2 CONC ures each have their initials and age inscribed beside them, although it is not certain if these were added by Pourbus or by another hand at a later date.
2 CONT
