1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===SIR DUNCAN CAMPBELL OF GLENORCHY===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''8 June 1615: Precept of clare constat by Sir Duncan Campbell of Glenurquhay, kt., in favour of Colin Campbell of Ardbeith, as heir of deceased Colin Campbell of Ardbeith, granter's brother, his father, in 4 merkland of Dowinche
2 CONC , Stronnuo and Corrieantran in lordship of Glendochart, sheriffdom of Perth. With sasine, procuratory of resignation in favour of said Sir Duncan and Colin Campbell, his eldest son, fiar of Glenurchy, and instrument following, 8-1
2 CONC 0 June 1615. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD112%2f2%2f34%2f24&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=campbell+of+ardbeith&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the C
2 CONC ampbell Family, Earls of Breadalbane (Breadalbane Muniments), reference GD112/2/34/24]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00046221&tree=LEO Biographical Summary by Genealogics] ==
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LBQS-X77
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Duncan Campbell, 7th Laid of Glenorchy, born 7 Aug 1550, Glenorchy, Argyllshire, Scotland; died Jun. 23, 1631, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also known as:<br>
2 CONT Black Duncan<br>
2 CONT Black Duncan of the Cowl<br>
2 CONT Black Duncan of the Castles
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Sir Colin Campbell and Katherine Ruthven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married first: 1573 (age of 23) Janet Stewart (died 1593) in Perth, Perthshire, Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married second around 1610 to Elizabeth Sinclair.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Janet Stewart and Duncan Campbell:<br>
2 CONT 1. Archibald<br>
2 CONT 2. Duncan<br>
2 CONT 3. Jean<br>
2 CONT 4. Margaret<br>
2 CONT 5. Colin<br>
2 CONT 6. Robert<br>
2 CONT 7. Agnes<br>
2 CONT 8. John<br>
2 CONT 9. Alexander
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Elizabeth Sinclair and Duncan Campbell:<br>
2 CONT 10. Catherine<br>
2 CONT 11. Elizabeth<br>
2 CONT 12. Juliana<br>
2 CONT 13. Patrick<br>
2 CONT 14. William
2 CONT
2 CONT Weblinks:
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=40326939 Find A Grave Memorial]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I3524&tree=CC Clan MacFarlane]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://TinyURL.com/25c7amr A Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerage etc.]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Notes: ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Duncan was the eldest son of "Grey Colin" Campbell, whom he succeeded in 1583. He was knighted c. 1590 on the occasion of the King's marriage. He had a long and successful career as the 7th Laird of Glenorchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT "But he was chiefly known for his building of castles.... These were: Finlarig, at the west end of Loch Tay; Achalader, on the north end of his lands, guarding the entrance to Rannoch; Lochdochart, on an island in the loch of that
2 CONC  name...; and Barcaldine, in Benderloch. In addition, he repaired and added to Kilchurn Castle. Because of this, he went by the name of 'Duncan of the Castles.'" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT from Burke's Peerage via Google Books: http://preview.tinyurl.com/25c7amr
2 CONT
2 CONT I. Sir DUNCAN CAMPBELL(knighted 1590),7th of Glenurchy or Glenorchy, called "Black Duncan," and "Donacha dhu na Curich," t. e. "Duncan with the Coul," who was in high favour with James VI., and was made by that monarch, in 1617, h
2 CONC eritable keeper of the forests of Mamlorn, Bendaskerlie, Ac., with many valuable privileges, and created, 30 May, 1625, a Baronet of Nova Scotia, and high-sheriff of tho co. Perth, for life. He w. 1st, 1574, Jean, 2nd dau. of John
2 CONC , 4th Earl of Athole, lord chancellor of Scotland, and by her had, Colin Robert, five other sons, and four daus. He m. 2ndly, Elizabeth, only dau. of Patrick, 5th Lord Sinclair, and by her had two sons and four daus. Sir Duncan wa
2 CONC s «. at his decease, 1631, by the eldest son of his 1st wife,
2 CONT
2 CONT for another viewpoint on Duncan Campbell:
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://www.spaceless.com/fletcher/flet2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT It was only through the trickery of the infamous Sir Duncan Campbell, the seventh Laird of Glenorchy, in 1587, that the Fletchers were ousted from their ancient stronghold. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The late Angus Fletcher of Tyndrum used to tell the story, which he said had been passed down to him by natives of Glenorchy - and briefly it happened like this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Duncan Campbell, the Black Laird of Glenorchy, had been appointed by the King to keep the peace between the feuding clans in Argyll and Perthshire. He was accordingly authorised to maintain a large force of armed retainers, in
2 CONC  order to give strength to his purpose. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had the reputation of preferring to stir up trouble rather than quell it, and he was traditionally more interested in the plundering and acquisition of lands than in endeavoring to calm the turbulence which existed between his 
2 CONC contemporaries, inevitably he had among his armies a number of lawless men, not even of Highland blood, who would pick a fight with anyone at a word from their master. This was how so much territory came into the hands of Sir Dunc
2 CONC an, and he had his eyes set on Achallader for some long time. He made a pretense at friendship with MacInleister, although he was secretly determined to have his lands, in spite of Fletcher's consistent refusal to sell to the Lair
2 CONC d.
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell made his plans. He left Finlarig Castle one evening with a number of his men, and by dawn he was in the vicinity of Achallader. He deployed his band of followers behind a small hill, and ordered three of his hired 'strang
2 CONC ers' to tether their horses in a cornfield which belonged to Fletcher. He then withdrew to await developments. Fletcher soon discovered the trespassers, and ordered them off his land. The 'strangers' laughed derisively and stayed 
2 CONC where they were, not attempting to move their animals. The Fletcher chieftain tried once more, but when the men again ignored his remonstrations, he lost his temper, pulled one of the iron stakes from the ground, to which the hors
2 CONC es were tethered, and threw it with all his might at one of the intruders. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The man fell dead, and at this point Sir Duncan 'happened to come along' and professed great shock and surprise at the incident, He showed great concern at the predicament of his dear friend, Fletcher, but in spite of his friendsh
2 CONC ip he felt obliged to report the fact that Fletcher had murdered an officer in the discharge of his duty. He was very much afraid, he told Fletcher, that when the King learned of the matter he would surely order Fletcher to be han
2 CONC ged and his lands forfeited. He suggested that Fletcher should take refuge with some friends in Rannoch until the danger of discovery had passed, and said that he (Campbell) would turn a blind eye to his escape. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To avoid forfeiture of Fletcher's lands, Campbell suggested that he should help matters by agreeing to their conveyance to himself, and said they could be re-conveyed to Fletcher when the whole thing had blown over. Fletcher, havi
2 CONC ng regard to the dilemma in which he found himself, was therefore prevailed upon to convey his lands to Campbell, who forthwith lodged the document in the Registry Office in Edinburgh; but of course, when Fletcher later wanted his
2 CONC  property back Campbell would have none of the idea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works Cited:
2 CONT
2 CONT Burke, Bernard, and Ashworth P. Burke. A Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerage and Baronetage, the Privy Council, Knightage and Companionage. London: Harrison and Sons, 1902. Print. Volume 42, Part 1
2 CONT
2 CONT read online: http://preview.tinyurl.com/25c7amr
2 CONT
2 CONT find in a library: http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/10387923
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell, Alastair. A History of Clan Campbell: Vol. 2. Edinburgh: Edinburgh University Press, 2002. Print. 
2 CONT
2 CONT find in a library: http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/186134386
2 CONT
2 CONT Innes, Cosmo, and William Bowie. The Black Book of Taymouth: With Other Papers from the Breadalbane Charter Room. Edinburgh: T. Constable, 1855. Print.
2 CONT
2 CONT read online: http://www.archive.org/details/blackbookoftaymo00inneuoft
2 CONT
2 CONT find in a library: http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/4174780
2 CONT
2 CONT General resources:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.darkisle.com/scotland.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.rcahms.gov.uk/<br>
2 CONT --------------------<br>
2 CONT Also known as: Black Duncan, Black Duncan of the Cowl, and Black Duncan of the Castles. His parents were: Sir Colin Campbell and Katherine Ruthven.<br>
2 CONT He married first on November 18, 1573 at the age of 23 to Jean Stewart (she died 1593 in Glenorchy, Scotland). He then married for the second time to Elizabeth Sinclair in 1610.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children with Jean Stewart were :<br>
2 CONT 1. Archibald<br>
2 CONT 2. Duncan<br>
2 CONT 3. Jean<br>
2 CONT 4. Margaret (Our connection)<br>
2 CONT 5. Colin<br>
2 CONT 6. Robert<br>
2 CONT 7. Agnes<br>
2 CONT 8. John<br>
2 CONT 9. Alexander
2 CONT
