2 CONT
2 CONT ACHALLADER CASTLE<br>
2 CONT This Campbell of Glenorchy stronghold was built in 1600 by the 7th laird, Black Duncan, known as Duncan of the Seven Castles, after he had taken possession of the lands from the Fletcher's around 1590.<br>
2 CONT Duncan Campbell of Glenorchy<br>
2 CONT son and heir of Grey Colin and Katherine Ruthven
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 	
2 CONT
2 CONT  1 - Sir Duncan Campbell, "Black Duncan", born 1550, Glenorchy, Argyllshire. Died 23/6/1631, Balloch. 1st Baronet Glenorchy, and Glenfalloch. Built Edinample Castle on the western side of Loch Earn in the late 1500's, possibly on 
2 CONC the site of an earlier MacGregor building. Another of his castles was Finlarig Castle, Killin built in 1629 (at the western end of Loch Tay, just to the north of Edinample). Legend has it that a pit there was where nobles were exe
2 CONC cuted; commoners were hanged on a nearby oak tree. In 1646 another of Duncan's castles, Loch Dochart Castle (in the same area) was destroyed by the McNab's. In total Duncan had 6 castles built. Was known as 'Sir Duncan of the seve
2 CONC n castles'. On 4/4/1600 Edward Toschoch gave a guarantee of 300 marks to Donald Monteth (or Menteith), Baron of Carwhin, not to harm Sir Duncan Campbell of Glenorchy (ie: Edward paid off Donald Monteith so that he would not attack
2 CONC  Sir Duncan, his uncle-in-law). Sir Duncan was accused by Monteith of burning Carwhin, subsequently arrested and made to pay a 4000 mark fine. Prior to Edward Toschoch's guarantee, Sir Duncan had paid (25/7/1599) a 3000 mark guara
2 CONC ntee to Donald Montieth. Given the three payments, one would be inclined to think that the accusations against Sir Duncan may well have been valid, despite his claim they were "false lies and inventions"! Married Jean Stewart of A
2 CONC tholl, 18/11/1573, daughter of John Stewart, 4th Earl of Atholl, and Margaret Flemming. Jean died 9/1593.<br>
2 CONT  [ http://roots-boots.net/ft/l-campbl.txt ]
2 CONT
2 CONT  2 - Edinample Castle Situated on the south side of Loch Earn. Built by 'Black' Duncan Campbell of Glenorchy in the 16thC possibly on the site of an earlier building. Originally Macgregor lands but with the well known demise of th
2 CONC at clan, the area came under Campbell ownership. A 6thC monk, St Blane, is said to have cursed the lands saying that the owners would never prosper. The Macgregors certainly didn't do well there. Another legend says that the castl
2 CONC e was built from stolen gravestones and that a local witch further cursed the laird.<br>
2 CONT  'Black' Duncan had wanted a parapet to top his castle so he could walk round it. The unfortunate builder forgot to provide this feature and is said to have tried to show that it was perfectly possible to walk on the roof. 'Black'
2 CONC  Duncan pushed him off and therefore avoided paying the poor man! His ghost is said to have been seen walking on the roof.<br>
2 CONT  Extended in both the 18thC & early 20thC, it lay unoccupied until restoration in the late 1970's.
2 CONT
2 CONT  3 - Barcaldine, the "Black Castle" of Benderloch is one of the few mediaeval castles in Scotland which is still habitable and which appears practically unchanged. Throughout its 400 year existence, Barcaldine has been a military 
2 CONC outpost, a dwelling house, a ruin. Barcaldine owes its position to a design for defence. The builder was Sir Duncan Campbell, seventh Knight of Glenorchy, known to this day as Black Duncan of the Cowl. In Argyll, where turbulence 
2 CONC and lawlessness had followed the break-up of the Lordship of the Isles, the hierarchy abolished by James IV in 1493. It was during this period of lawlessness that Sir Duncan Campbell came to power. He was a very vigorous and force
2 CONC ful character with ambition.<br>
2 CONT  Sir Duncan's lands stretched from Barcaldine in the west to Taymouth Castle on Loch Tay in the east. Not surprisingly, such a wide distribution of property was extremely difficult to control and protect. To this end, Sir Duncan b
2 CONC uilt a string of seven castles. Kilchurn Castle on Loch Awe was repaired, as was Taymouth Castle, formally known as Balloch at the east end of Loch Tay. Edinample Castle at the west end of Loch Earn, Achallader at the head of Glen
2 CONC orchy, Finlarig at the west end of Loch Tay and the island Castle on Loch Dochart made up the rest. The building of Barcaldine, derived from the Gaelic "Ban a challtuin" meaning hazel knoll, was begun between 1591 and 1601. Its co
2 CONC nstruction was recorded in the "Black Book of Taymouth", one of the most informative books of the period Black Duncan installed his third son, John of Auchinryre, to look after the newly erected castle.<br>
2 CONT  Barcaldine Castle is built on the "L" plan with the smaller wing on the south side projecting to the west beyond the end part of the main wall of the castle. This projection enabled the main wall to be protected and covered with 
2 CONC small arms. The walls are approximately 9 feet thick, thus allowing the stair passage inside them, and rise to approximately 32 feet.
2 CONT
2 CONT  4 - ACHALLADER CASTLE - This Campbell of Glenorchy stronghold was built in 1600 by the 7th laird, Black Duncan, known as Duncan of the Seven Castles, after he had taken possession of the lands from the Fletcher's around 1590.<br>
2 CONT  It had 3 storeys and a garret and was well supplied with shot -holes. It was burned during the jacobite rebellion of 1689 and never restored, but in 1691 John Campbell, Earl of Breadalbane, summoned jacobite leaders here to sign 
2 CONC the Treaty of Achallader, an armistice in their fight to restore James VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT  5 - Sir Duncan Campbell, Laird of Glenorchy for forty-eight years prior to his death in 1631, was a very advanced landlord and estate improver.<br>
2 CONT  He bred excellent horses; established fallow deer and a rabbit warren on the Isle of Innischoiian on Loch Awe; planted numerous trees, including oak, fir, and birch; and maintained a small flock of sheep. His household books show
2 CONC  that the family enjoyed food such as chickens, brawn, venison, mutton, smoked ham, wildfowl, rabbits, salmon, herring, and butter, with spices such as saffron, mace, ginger, sugar, and pepper. They drank claret, white wine, ale, 
2 CONC and aqua l'itae. They slept on feather beds, with bolsters, blankets, linen, and bed hangings. All of this was luxurious living by the standards of the day.<br>
2 CONT  Among the regulations laid down in the Court Book of GIenorchy at this time were some points of good estate management. These included:<br>
2 CONT  1 Moors only to be burnt in March.<br>
2 CONT  2 No man to shoot at roe deer, or blackgame, etc., or to kill salmon kelts without
2 CONT  permission.<br>
2 CONT  3 All head and fold dykes to be repaired each year with turf and stones.<br>
2 CONT  5 Peats to be cut with proper peat spades.<br>
2 CONT  6 All dwellings to be in as good repair when tenants and cottars leave as when they took possession.<br>
2 CONT  7 All tenants to plant six young trees yearly in their kail yard for each merkland held and every cottar three trees to be oak, ash or plane.<br>
2 CONT  10 60 loads of earth to be applied yearly to each merkland as manure.<br>
2 CONT  11No swine to be kept.<br>
2 CONT  15 Every tenant must make yearly four iron traps for killing wolves.<br>
2 CONT  16 No tenant to allow rooks, hooded crows, or magpies to nest on their holding.<br>
2 CONT  17 No quarrelling.<br>
2 CONT  27 No young trees to be destroyed.<br>
2 CONT  35 Fine for cutting wood without licence.<br>
2 CONT  36 No one to drink at a brewhouse unless they have travelled eight miles thereto.<br>
2 CONT  39 Tenants obliged to cut and lead fern and thatch for the laird's houses<br>
2 CONT  Although their aims were decidedly enlightened there is a feudal note about these regulations which was alien to the old Highland way of life, foreshadowing the changes in the relationship between chieftain and clansmen which wer
2 CONC e to take place in the eighteenth century. The court cases at Glenorchy recorded about the same time included murder, cattle and sheep stealing, shooting at deer and grouse, selling ale to drunkards, and cutting trees. A pointer t
2 CONC o the still unsettled nature of the countryside was the number of weapons listed in the household books
2 CONT
2 CONT  6 - The great Highland family had in 1590 for its head Sir Duncan Campbell of Glenurchy, ordinarily called Donacha Dhu nan Curich (Black Duncan of the Cowl), a man of considerable force of character, and, for his time, large mean
2 CONC s, who died at an advanced age in 1631. He was distinguished for building, planting, and improving; had the taste to hire artists to decorate his house; and, some years after this time, was one of the most prominent patrons of the
2 CONC  Scottish Vandyke, George Jameson.<br>
2 CONT  The household books of this great Celtic chief exhibit his style of life about the time here noted. His rents were principally paid in kind, and the corn, cattle, and poultry thus supplied by the tenantry went directly to the sup
2 CONC port of the laird and his household. 'In 1590, the family spent their time between Balloch and Finlarig. The oatmeal consumed was 364 bolls; the malt, 207 bolls (deducting a small quantity of struck barley used in the kitchen). Th
2 CONC ey used 90 beeves ("neats," "stirks," or "fed oxen"), more than two-thirds consumed fresh; 20 swine; 200 sheep; 424 salmon, far the greater portion being from the western rivers; 15,000 herrings; 30 dozen of hard fish; 1805 "heads
2 CONC " of cheese, new and old, weighing 325 stone; 49 stones of butter; 26 dozen loaves of wheaten bread; of wheat flour, 3Ùb bolls. The wine brought from Dundee was claret and white wine, old and new, in no very large quantities. [The
2 CONC  malt furnished abundance of-ale of three kinds— ostler ale, household ale, and best ale, serving, doubtless, for the different grades of persons in the family] Of spices and sweet-meats, we find only notice on one occasion of sma
2 CONC ll quantities of saffron, mace, ginger, pepper, "raises of cure," plumdamas, and one sugar-loaf.'<br>
2 CONT  The rude abundance shewn in this establishment, taken in connection with the account given of the outward state of the Marquis of Huntly, the reports afforded by the Water Poet of the hospitalities he experienced in the braes of 
2 CONC Aberdeenshire and Morayshire, ought somewhat to modify the prevalent notions as to the poverty of the Celtic part of Scotland in this age. There was, indeed, no manufacturing industry worth speaking of; but the natural wealth of t
2 CONC he country, the cattle, the wild animals, and the rain, seem to have furnished the people with no inconsiderable share of the comforts of life. It will be found, too, that the mansions of Glenurchy and Huntly, a few years after th
2 CONC is date, exhibited elegant architecture and decoration. [8]
2 CONT
