1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.archive.org/stream/bookwallace00clubgoog#page/n179/mode/2up/
2 CONT
2 CONT From ODNB:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kennedys were a Gaelic kindred who from the thirteenth century onwards extended their activities well beyond, but without losing sight of, the Gaelic milieu whence they came. On the evidence of William Dunbar's poem of c.1500,
2 CONC  ‘The Flyting of Dunbar and Kennedy’ (Walter, the Kennedy in question, was a younger son of Gilbert, first Lord Kennedy), the family still spoke Gaelic as well as Scots, and the Gaelic form of lordship exercised by them, the kenky
2 CONC nnol, was clearly still worth contending over in the fifteenth century ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
