1 OCCU 1st Laird of Hessilheid
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===HUGH MONTGOMERY OF HESSILHEID (aka HUGH MONTGOMERY OF BALGRAY)===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''According to the Reverend John Anderson, Assistant Curator Histotical Department, H.M. General Register House (1906), Hugh Montgomery of Hessilheaid was the son of Alexander Montgomery, Master of Montgomery. [https://archive.or
2 CONC g/stream/scotspeeragefou02paulgoog#page/n448/mode/1up The Scots Peerage III, pp. 432-33]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Writing earlier (1847), James Paterson says this of him: "Hugh Montgomerie of Hessilhead, - or, as he was rather designed, of Bawgraw (Balgray) - was third son of Alexander, Master of Montgomerie, and grandson of Alexander, fir
2 CONC st of the name, Lord Montgomerie. He had a charter, under the great seal, of the lands of Freeland, in Lanarkshire, in the fifteenth century. balgray is part of the Hessilhead estate, in the barony of Giffen. He married Janet, dau
2 CONC ghter of Maxwell of Pollock, by whom her had issue."[https://archive.org/stream/historyofcountyo01pateuoft#page/290/mode/1up History of the County of Ayr, etc. Volume I, p. 290]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1859 William Fraser observed: "Hugh of Hesilheid. His descendanr Robert, had an only daughter, who was married to Macaulay of Ardincaple.[https://archive.org/stream/memorialsofmonv100fras#page/25/mode/1up Memorials of the Mo
2 CONC ntgomeries, etc., Volume I, p. 25]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hessilhead Hessilhead]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The first recorded holder of the lands of Hessilhead was Hugh de Eglintoun, who obtained the lands following forfeiture. Eglin, Lord of Eglintoun is the first of the family recorded, living during the reign of King Malcolm Canm
2 CONC ore; he may have been one of the Saxon barons who accompanied King Malcolm (who died in 1093) on his successful return to Scotland. The family continued until Elizabeth de Eglintoun, the sole heir, married Sir John de Montgomerie 
2 CONC of Eaglesham. Elizabeth's mother was Giles, daughter of Walter, lord high steward of Scotland, and sister of King Robert II.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''When Hugh Eglintoun of that Ilk, her father, died soon after 1378 the Montgomerie family inherited the lands and hereafter Hessilhead's history is bound up with that family.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir John Montgomerie of Hessilhead and Corsecraigs inherited the estate from his father, Hugh Montgomerie of Bawgraw (Balgray). John was slain at the battle of Flodden in 1513 and the estate passed to his son Hugh, who died on 
2 CONC 23 January 1556. Hugh's heir was his son John who was appointed one of the tutors to Hugh, third Earl of Eglintoun. John married Margaret Fraser of Knock and was succeed in 1558 by his son Hugh. This young Hugh was a member of the
2 CONC  Convention Parliament of 1560, at which the Protestant Confession of Faith was established.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In around 1576, Gabriel Montgomerie of nearby Scotston was slain by adherents of the Montgomeries of Hessilhead. Hugh had a son Robert who inherited in 1602, passing the estate on to his son Robert circa 1623. This Robert was a
2 CONC  Commissioner of Supply for Ayrshire and his son, also Robert, succeeded in 1648 and was one of the representatives for Ayrshire in the first parliament of King Charles II. He had a daughter, Mary, who married MacAulay of Ardincap
2 CONC le.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert Montgomerie sold Hessilhead to the seventh Earl of Eglinton's (1613–1669) second son, The Right Hon. Francis Montgomerie inherited the nearby Giffen Castle and lived an eventful life. He was one of the Lords of the Privy
2 CONC  Council, and a Commissioner of the Treasury, in the reign of William III and Queen Anne. He was appointed in 1706 as one of the Commissioners for Scotland for the Treaty of Union.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
