1 NAME Marion /Montgomery/ Lady Sempill
2 GIVN Marion
2 SURN Montgomery
2 NSFX Lady Sempill
2 _MARNM Montgomery
1 SEX F
1 TITL Dame
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == DAME MARION MONTGOMERY Lady Sempill ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Dame Marioun Montgomery, Lady Semple (1547) Dame Mariota Montgomery, Lady Symple (1555) Lady Marion Mungumry (1559) Marian Montgomery (1559) Marion Mungumry (1560) Marion Montgomery (1560) Dame Marioun Montgumrie la
2 CONC dy Sempill (1581) Dame Marioun Montgomery lady Sympill (1581)'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Disputed Ancestry'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Documentary evidence for the ancestry of Dame Marion Montgomery, Lady Sempill, here treated, has not been found, and verifiable evidence of relationship is not cited in any of the earliest published genealogies, 183
2 CONC 3-1911.'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Robert Pitcairn, WS., writing in 1833, in Ancient Criminal Trials in Scotland, claimed that Marion Montgomery, here treated, was the same person as ‘Dame Mariota Montgomery, Lady Sempill'. Without producing evidence
2 CONC  of relationship, she is said by him to have been the daughter of Hugh, Earl of Eglinton, and his wife Mariota Seytoun, the Mistress of Borthwick. [https://archive.org/details/criminaltrialsin01pitc/page/n428/mode/1up Ancient Crim
2 CONC inal Trials I - II: 381*]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Sir William Fraser had a different opinion. Writing in 1859, in Memorials of the Montgomeries, he identified Dame Marion Montgomery, Lady Sempill, here treated, as the daughter of Hugh, Earl of Eglinton, and his wif
2 CONC e Helen Campbell. Again, evidence which proves the proposed relationship is not offered. [https://archive.org/details/memorialsofmonv100fras/page/34/mode/1up Memorials of The Montgomeries I: 35]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''John Burke disagreed with Pitcairn and Fraser. Writing in 1835, in his History of the Commoners of Great Britain and Ireland, he claimed that Marion Montgomery, here treated, was the daughter of Montgomery of Hazleh
2 CONC ead. Unfortunately, he did not say which of the Montgomery’s of Hazlehead he had in mind, and he did not offer evidence which proved the proposed relationship. [https://archive.org/details/genealogicalheral01burk/page/553/mode/1up
2 CONC  Commoners: 553]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''James Paterson went further than Burke. In 1847, writing in his History of the County of Ayr, he identified Marion Montgomery as the daughter of Sir John Montgomery of Hazlehead. According to him her father was kill
2 CONC ed in action at Flodden Field on 9 September 1513, fighting under the Standard of James IV, King of Scots. [https://archive.org/details/historyofcountyo01pateuoft/page/290/mode/1up Ayr: 290] In 1863 Thomas Harrison Montgomery foll
2 CONC owed Paterson, although, he cited John Burke’s Commoners in error, rather than Paterson’s History of the County of Ayr. [https://archive.org/details/genealogicalhist00mont/page/134/mode/1up Montgomery History: 134]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Ignoring the claims made by Pitcairn and Fraser, the Reverend John Anderson’s account of the family of Montgomerie, Earl of Eglinton, written in 1906 for the Scots Peerage, makes absolutely no mention of Marion Mont
2 CONC gomery, here treated. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/page/423/mode/1up The Scots Peerage III: 423-65]'''</blockquote> 
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''John MacGregor, WS., writing in his 1911 account of the family of the Lords Sempill for the Scots Peerage, identified Marion Montgomery, here treated, as the daughter of Hugh Montgomery of Hazlehead, although he did
2 CONC  not say which Hugh Montgomery of Hazlehead he had in mind, and he did not offer evidence of relationship. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft/page/536/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VII:
2 CONC  536]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == The Question of Identity ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''It does not seem possible to identify the paternity of Dame Marion Montgomery, Lady Sempill, here treated, from the genealogies that were published during the period 1822-1911, or from the primary evidence reviewed here.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A date and place of death has not been identified for Dame Marion Montgomery, Lady Sempill, here treated. At least one pedigree published by MyHeritage claims that she died on 24 June 1580, [https://www.geni.com/people/Marion-M
2 CONC ontgomery-Lady-Sempill/6000000011056651203#/tab/source MyHeritage] This is wrong. She was still alive on 1 December 1581, the date upon which her daughter, Margaret Crawford, gave up the testament dative and inventory of her decea
2 CONC sed husband, Robert Hunter of Hunterston. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecordso45scotuoft/page/32/mode/1up Hunter Papers: 32]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == First Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marian Montgomery, here treated, is reported to have married Thomas Crawford of Auchinames, who is said to have died in 1541. [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun07paul#page/536/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VII: pp. 536]'
2 CONC ''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Children of First Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/John-Crawford-8th-Laird-of-Auchinames/6000000017145257364?through=6000000015866607645
