1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===JOHN SEMPILL, LORD SEMPILL===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir John Sempill  was created a Peer by James IV, King of Scots, under the designation of Lord Sempill, prior to 10 November 1488. The patent of this dignity is not preserved, but at the decreet of ranking in 1606 the barony of
2 CONC  Sempill was placed next above that of Sinclair, the date of which was probably considered as 26 January 1488-89. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft/page/530/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VII: pp. 531-4]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft/page/530/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VII: pp. 531-4 for the family of John, Lord Sempill]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I7050&tree=CC Clan MacFarlane and associated clans genealogy]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[http://thepeerage.com/p14749.htm#i147485 Biographical Summary by Darryl Lundy's Peerage]===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Sempill, 1st Lord Sempill married Margaret Colville, daughter of Robert Colville of Hiltoun and Elizabeth Arnot, before 9 September 1501. He died on 9 September 1513 at Flodden Field, Northumberland, England, killed in act
2 CONC ion. He was the son of Sir Thomas Sempill and Elizabeth Ross. He gained the title of 1st Lord Sempill. He fought in the Battle of Flodden on 9 September 1513. Child of John Sempill, 1st Lord Sempill and Margaret Colville: (1) Will
2 CONC iam Sempill, 2nd Lord Sempill.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Sempill,_1st_Lord_Sempill Biographical Summary by Wikipedia]===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Sempill, 1st Lord Sempill of Eliotstoun (died 9 September 1513) was a Scottish peer. Sempill was the founder of the collegiate Church of Lochwinnoch and was created Lord Sempill in the Peerage of Scotland around 1489. He w
2 CONC as killed at the Battle of Flodden in September 1513, along with many other Scottish peers. Lord Sempill was succeeded in the lordship by his son William.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://www.red1st.com/axholme/getperson.php?personID=I1750061124&tree=Axholme Biographical Summary by Red 1st]===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Sempill  1st Lord Sempill Born 1465 Renfrew, Renfrewshire, Scotland  Died  9 Sep 1513  Flodden Field, Branxton, Northumberland, England Memorial Inscription, Collegiate Church, Sempill, Lanarkshire, Scotland. Father: Sir T
2 CONC homas Sempill,   b. 1440, Eliotstoun, Renfrewshire, Scotland ,  d. 11 Jun 1488, Sauchieburn, Marykirk, Kincardineshire, Scotland   (Age 48 years)  Mother: Elizabeth Ross,  b. 1447, Renfrewshire, Scotland ,  d. 13 Nov 1495, Renfrew
2 CONC shire, Scotland  (Age 48 years)  Married 1463  Scotland Family Margaret Colville,   b. 1468, Stirlingshire, Scotland ,   d. Aft 21 May 1504, Renfrewshire, Scotland  (Age > 37 years) Married 9 Sep 1501. Children: (1) William Sempil
2 CONC l, 2nd Lord Sempill,   b. 1485, Renfrew, Renfrewshire, Scotland ,   d. 3 Jun 1552, Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland   (Age 67 years) (2) Margaret Isobel Semphill,   b. 1480  [Natural] (3) Jannet Semple,   b. 1481, Ranfurlie, Renfre
2 CONC wshire, Scotland  [Natural]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Flodden Battle of Flodden]===
2 CONT
2 CONT Invasion
2 CONT
2 CONT Using the pretext of revenge for the murder of Robert Kerr, a warden of the Scottish East March who had been killed by John "The Bastard" Heron in 1508, James invaded England with an army of about 30,000 men in 1513.[4] In keeping
2 CONC  with his understanding of the medieval code of chivalry, King James sent notice to the English, one month in advance, of his intent to invade. This gave the English time to gather an army and, as importantly, to retrieve the bann
2 CONC er of Saint Cuthbert from the Cathedral of Durham, a banner which had been carried by the English in victories against the Scots in 1138 and 1346.[5][6] 
2 CONT
2 CONT After a muster on the Burgh Muir of Edinburgh, the Scottish host moved to Ellemford, to the north of Duns, and camped to wait for Angus and Home, and then crossed the River Tweed near Coldstream. By the 29 August, Norham Castle wa
2 CONC s taken and partly demolished. The Scots moved south capturing the castles of Etal and Ford.[7] A later chronicler, Robert Lindsay of Pitscottie, tells the story that James wasted valuable time at Ford enjoying the company of Lady
2 CONC  Heron and her daughter.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle actually took place near the village of Branxton, in the county of Northumberland, rather than at Flodden — hence the alternative name is Battle of Branxton. The Scots had previously been stationed at Flodden Edge, to t
2 CONC he south of Branxton, which the Earl of Surrey compared to a fortress. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Surrey moved to block off the Scots' route north and so James was forced to move his army and artillery 2 miles to Branxton Hill.[9] The Scottish artillery included; 5 great curtals; 2 great culverins; 4 sakers; and 6 great serpen
2 CONC tines.[10] When the armies were within 3 miles of each other Surrey sent Rouge Croix pursuivant to James who answered that he would wait till noon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At 11 o'clock Lord Howard's vanguard and artillery crossed the Twissell Bridge. (Pitscottie says the king would not allow the Scots artillery to fire on the vulnerable English during this manouevre.)[11] The Scots army was in good
2 CONC  order in 5 formations, after the Almain (German) manner. On Friday afternoon the Scots host descended without speaking any word to meet the English.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to English report, first the groups commanded by the Earls of Huntly and Crawford totalling 6000 men engaged Lord Howard and were repulsed and mostly slain. Baron Dacre's company fought Huntly and the Chamberlain Lord Ho
2 CONC me's men.[13] Then James IV himself leading a great force came on to Surrey and Lord Darcy's son who bore the brunt of the battle. Lennox and Argyll's commands were met by Sir Edward Stanley.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT James was killed within a spear length from Surrey and his body taken to Berwick.[15] The 'rent surcoat of the King of Scots stained with blood' was sent to Henry VIII at Tournai.[16] The biggest error the Scots made was placing t
2 CONC heir officers in the front line, medieval style. A Scottish letter of January 1514 contrasts this loss of the nobility with the English great men who took their stand with the reserves and at the rear.[17] The English generals sta
2 CONC yed behind the lines in the Renaissance style. The loss of so many Scottish officers meant there was no one to coordinate a retreat.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Tactics and aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT Flodden was essentially a victory of bill used by the English over the pike used by the Scots. As a weapon, the pike was effective only in a battle of movement, especially to withstand a cavalry charge. The pike had become a Swiss
2 CONC  weapon of choice and represented modern warfare. The hilly terrain of Northumberland, the nature of the combat, and the slippery footing did not allow it to be employed to best effect. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Ruthall reported to Wolsey, 'the bills disappointed the Scots of their long spears, on which they relied.'[19] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The infantrymen at Flodden, both Scots and English, had fought in a fashion that in essence would have been familiar to their ancestors, and it has rightly been described as the last great medieval battle in the British Isles. Thi
2 CONC s was the last time that bill and pike would come together as equals in battle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later Francis I defeated the Swiss pikemen at the Battle of Marignano, using a combination of heavy cavalry and artillery, ushering in a new era in the history of war. An official English diplomatic report issued by Bria
2 CONC n Tuke noted the Scots' iron spears but concluded: 'the English halberdiers decided the whole affair, so that in the battle the bows and ordnance were of little use.'[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Tuke's comment (he was not present), tactically, this battle was one of the first major engagements on the British Isles where artillery was significantly deployed. John Lesley, writing sixty years later, noted the Scottis
2 CONC h bullets flew over the English heads while the English cannon was effective, the one army placed so high and the other so low.[21] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle is considered the last decisive use of the longbow, yet through the 16th century the English longbowmen continued to have success, as in the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh. Many of these archers were recruited from Lancashire 
2 CONC and Cheshire. Sir Richard Assheton raised one such company from Middleton, near Manchester. In gratitude for his safe return, he rebuilt St. Leonard's, the local parish church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after the battle there were legends that James IV had survived; a Scottish merchant at Tournai in October claimed to have spoken with him,[23] Lindsay of Pitscottie records two myths; "thair cam four great men upon hors, and 
2 CONC every ane of thame had ane wisp upoun thair spear headis, quhairby they might know one another and brought the king furth of the feild, upoun ane dun hackney," and also that the king escaped from the field but was killed between D
2 CONC uns and Kelso.[24] Similarly, John Lesley adds that the body taken to England was "my lord Bonhard" and James was seen in Kelso after the battle and then went secretly on pilgrimage in far nations.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT Every noble family in Scotland was supposed to have lost a member at Flodden. The dead are remembered by the song (and pipe tune) "The Flowers of the Forest";
2 CONT *We'll hae nae mair lilting, at the yowe-milking,
2 CONT *Women and bairns are dowie and wae.
2 CONT *Sighing and moaning, on ilka green loaning,
2 CONT *The flowers of the forest are all wede away.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casualties
2 CONT
2 CONT Surrey's army lost 1,500 men killed.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT There were various conflicting accounts of the Scottish loss. A contemporary French source, the Gazette of the Battle of Flodden, said that about 10,000 Scots were killed,[3] a claim made by Henry VIII on 16 September while he was
2 CONC  still uncertain of the death of James IV. Italian newsletters put the Scottish losses at 18 or 20 thousand and the English at 5000. Brian Tuke, the English Clerk of the Signet, sent a newsletter stating 10,000 Scots killed and 10
2 CONC ,000 escaped the field. Tuke reckoned the total Scottish invasion force to have been 60,000 and the English army at 40,000.[26] George Buchanan wrote in his History of Scotland (published in 1582) that, according to the lists that
2 CONC  were compiled throughout the counties of Scotland, there were about 5,000 killed.[2] A plaque on the monument to the 2nd Duke of Norfolk (as the Earl of Surrey became in 1514) at Thetford put the figure at 17,000.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notable men who died included:
2 CONT *James IV , King of Scots (1488–1513); died in battle
2 CONT *Alexander Stewart, Archbishop of St. Andrews and Lord Chancellor of Scotland; died in battle
2 CONT *Lieutenant General Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll; died in battle
2 CONT *John Sempill, 1st Lord Sempill of Eliotstoun; died in battle
