1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == GABRIEL SEMPILL OF CATHCART ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''16 March 1531-32: Charter by John, Lord Lyill, selling to Gabriel Sympill and Janet Sprewill his wife the £5 lands of Craigbait, Tor and Threple in the lordship of Ranfurlye Conygham. Dated at Glasgow. [http://catalogue.nrscotl
2 CONC and.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD148%2f119&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=Gabriel+Sympill&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=y National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Cuninghame Family of Craigends, Renfrewshire, refere
2 CONC nce GD148/119]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015080378733&seq=46 The Barony of Cathcart] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Barony of Cathcart being sold by Allan Lord Cathcart to Gabriel Semple of Ladymure, who was one of the younger sons of John Lord Semple, by Janet Colvil, his lady, took thence the designation of Cathcart. I have found him s
2 CONC o designed, an. 1547. He married Janet Spreul, daughter of John Spreul of Coudon, and relict of John Pollock of that ilk. William Semple of Cathcart was his son; he had likewise a daughter, Margaret, married John Pollock of that i
2 CONC lk, and had issue. To William succeeded another Gabriel, who was father of Bryce Semple of Cathcart; whose son and heir is Sir William Semple, now of Cathcart, whose Armorial bearing is, Argent, a Chevron Checquie, Argent and Gule
2 CONC s betwixt two Bugles, Sable, in chief, stringed Gules; and a Flower-de-luce in base, of the third; and for Crest, a Stag's Head; with this Motto, "Keep Tryst." [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015080378733&seq=46 A Ge
2 CONC neral Description of the Shire of Renfrew by George Crawfurd]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ss4as/sempill01.php Stirnet: Sempill 01]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Gabriel_Semple_%281%29 WikiTree]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  History of the Counties of Ayr & Wigton Scotland: Cunninghame By James Paterson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     20 May 1530 - Beginning of a feud that involves Gabriel Semple - the cautioners of John Cunninghame, David Boswell and 17 others did not appear for the murder of John Tod. 
2 CONT     23 May 1530 - Ormyscheuches, Scotland - William and David Cunninghame Lying in Wait upon the Highway - 'In feir of weir', David and William await the arrival of Gabriel Semple for his slaughter. A forethought felony and old fe
2 CONC ud. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    Ladymure 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The lands of Ladymure, are in the lordship of Duchal and the parish of Kilmacolm. 
2 CONT     See The Marty Acks Wiki - John Porterfield, Laird of Duchal (1544-1575) 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] ▼ Montgomery & Cunningham Feud S5
2 CONT
2 CONT     Montgomery became involved in the feud dating back to the 15th century between Clan Montgomery and Clan Cunningham. Montgomery was named Bailie of Cunninghame by James II, of Scotland (reigned 1437–1460), a post the Cunningham
2 CONC s claimed was rightfully theirs.
2 CONT     History of the Counties of Ayr & Wigton Scotland: Cunninghame By James Paterson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     20 May 1530 - Beginning of a feud that involves Gabriel Semple - the cautioners of John Cunninghame, David Boswell and 17 others did not appear for the murder of John Tod. 
2 CONT     23 May 1530 - Ormyscheuches, Scotland - William and David Cunninghame Lying in Wait upon the Highway - 'In feir of weir', David and William await the arrival of Gabriel Semple for his slaughter. A forethought felony and old fe
2 CONC ud. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Sctos Peerage by Paul, James 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gabriel Semple, 1st Baron Sempill of Cathcart and Ladymure, who, on 20 Aug 1533, found surety to underlie the law for the slaughter of William Cuningham, 2nd of Craigends and his servant on 11 Aug 1533, and failing to appear, 
2 CONC his brother, William Semple, 2nd Lord Sempill, who was his cautioner, was fined and Gabriel denounced rebel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Hugh Montgomerie, 4th Earl of Eglinton was assassinated by the Cunninghams at about the time of Montgomery's return to Scotland and died between 18 Apr 1586 and 20 Apr 1586, "shot dead by a party of Cunninghams, as part of
2 CONC  the feud between the two families."
2 CONT     Hugh Montgomery, 1st Viscount Montgomery's involvement in the feud began when he took offence at something that a Cunningham said. Cunningham fled to London, then to Holland, where Montgomery caught up with him and confronted 
2 CONC him in the Inner Court of the Palace at The Hague. Combating Cunningham with a sword, Montgomery delivered what he believing to be a killing thrust, but Cunningham's belt buckle deflected the blow. Montgomery believed he had kille
2 CONC d Cunningham and made to leave the palace. He was arrested and imprisoned at the Gevangenpoort in the Buitenhof. Montgomery broke out of jail with the help of a Scottish soldier. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hugh Montgomery, 1st Viscount Montgomery received a reprimand from King James VI and I (reigned 1567–1625), but was soon back in favour. In the spring of 1603 he accompanied King James VI and I to his coronation as King of Eng
2 CONC land, after the death of Queen Elizabeth I, of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ▼References
2 CONT
2 CONT       Walker, Rosa Kershaw; Pittman, Hannah Daviess. Americans of Gentle Birth and Their Ancestors: A Genealogical Encyclopedia ... Embracing Many Authenticated Lineages and Biographical Sketches of the Founders of the Colonies an
2 CONC d Their Descendants found in all parts of the United States . (Saint Louis, Missouri: Buxton & Skinner, 1903), Vol. 1, Pages 242, 243.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gabriel Semple of Cathcart, second son, married Janet Spreul; issue, William and Margaret.
2 CONT       Crawfurd, George. The peerage of Scotland: containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom. (Edinburgh: G. Crawfurd, 1716), Page 439 thru 443.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Page 440 - As this noble Lord was always very faithful to his Prince in his Life, for it is certain he went with him to the Battle of Flowdoun, where he was slain, fighting gallantly in the Service of his Country, on the memor
2 CONC able 9th September 1513. After the Battle his Body was brought off the Field, and buried in the Collegiate Church of Semple, where a Monument is erected over him, embellished with his Arms, without any other Inscription, than sign
2 CONC ifying him to be the Person there interred. His first Wife was Margaret, Daughter of Sir Robert Colvil, Lord of Ochiltree, by whom he had Two Sons, William his Successor, and Gabriel Semple of Ladymure, the Ancestor of the Semples
2 CONC  of Cathcart.
2 CONT     ↑ Paul, James Balfour. The Scots peerage: founded on Wood's edition of Sir Robert Douglas's peerage of Scotland, containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom, with armorial illustrations. (
2 CONC Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904-1914), Vol. 7.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Page 533 and 535 - Children of John Sempill, 1st Lord, by his first wife:
2 CONT     1. William, second Lord Sempill
2 CONT     2. Francis, who was, along with his father, sued by John Montgomery of Corsecraig regarding the warrandice of the lands of Soutarflat, 4 March 1501-2.
2 CONT     3. Gabriel of Cathcart and Ladymure…
2 CONT     4. Marian, contracted to be married to George, son of Patrick Maxwell of Newark.
2 CONT     5. Isabel, contracted to be married to Robert Crawford, son and heir of Malcolm Crawford of Greenock, prior to 11 March 1502-3. A dispensation for her marriage to George Chalmers was procured before 20 July 1518.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Page 533 – Gabriel of Cathcart and Ladymure, who, on 20 August 1533, found surety to underlie the law for the slaughter of William Cunynghame of Craigends and his servant, and failing to appear, his brother, Lord Sempill, who 
2 CONC was his cautioner, was fined, and Gabriel denounced rebel. He had from Allan Stewart of Cardonald a charter of the lands of Cathcart and others, to himself and Jonet Spreule his wife, 25 November 1543. On 16 July 1546 Queen Mary g
2 CONC ranted him, for his good service, a charter of the lands of Carswells. He was killed at Pinkie, 10 September 1547. He married Jonet Spreule, who was possibly a daughter of John Spreule of Coldoun, prior to 18 January 1531. She die
2 CONC d on 22 October 1550. They had issue:-
2 CONT       Patrick Hogue (Samples). The Samples / Semples Family.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hereditary Sempill Lords of Cathcart & Clan Sempill
2 CONT       Masson, David. The register of the Privy Council of Scotland : vol. VIII, A.D. 1607-1610. (Edinburgh: H. M. General Register House, 1887).
2 CONT
2 CONT     page 252. February 28, 1609.--The feud between James, Earl of Glencairne, and his friends under written, on the one part--and--Hew, Earl of Eglintoun, and Robert, Lord Semple, and their friends, on the other part, having been 
2 CONC amicably submitted, in presence of the Council, in January 1607 to certain neutral friends, the said friends had at last disagreed anent the nomination of an oversman, and the submission had been given up by them and allowed to fa
2 CONC ll in his Majesty's hands, so that now his Majesty is the only judge and oversman in that matter.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Accordingly, his Majesty having given direction for reconciling the said parties, according to a decree to be pronounced in his name hereanent, there is order to charge the following persons,--viz.
2 CONT
2 CONT     ON THE ONE PART:
2 CONT     Andro Arnot of Lochrig, younger
2 CONT     Robert Boyd in Clerkland
2 CONT     Abrahame Cunynghame, servitor to late Alex'r. Cunynghame of Aikit
2 CONT     Alexander Cunynghame of Corshill
2 CONT     Alexander Cunynghame of Craigens
2 CONT     Alexander Cunynghame of Tourlandis
2 CONT     Daniel Cunynghame of Dalkeith
2 CONT     David Cunynghame of Robertland
2 CONT     Gabriell Cunynghame, brother of the Laird of Craigens
2 CONT     Hew, son of late Hew Cunynghame of Saltcoitis
2 CONT     Mr. James Cunynghame of Montgrenane and his brothers
2 CONT     Johnne Cunynghame of Cunynghameheid
2 CONT     (2nd named) Johnne Cunynghame of Ros, brother of Earl of Glencairne
2 CONT     (3rd named) Patrick Cunynghame, goodsire-brother of James Cunynghame of Aiket,
2 CONT     Robert Cunynghame of Waterstoun and his brother Joseph
2 CONT     (4th named) William Cunynghame, uncle of Patrick Cunynghame
2 CONT     Williame Cunynghame of Brounhill
2 CONT     William Cunynghame of Caprintoun
2 CONT     William Cunynghame of Clonbaith
2 CONT     (1st named) Earl of Glencairne
2 CONT     Bartilmo Maxwell
2 CONT     Patrik Maxwell of Newark, and his brothers Johnne and David
2 CONT
2 CONT     ON THE SECOND PART:
2 CONT     Johnne (?Robert) Birsbane, elder of Bishoptoun
2 CONT     Johnne (?Robert) Birsbane, younger of Bischoptoun
2 CONT     Matthew Birsbane of Roisland
2 CONT     Symone Birsbane of Nether Walkinschaw
2 CONT     The late William Birsbane of Barnhill: His sons John, William, James
2 CONT     James Dunlop of that Ilk
2 CONT     (1st named) The Earl of Eglintoun
2 CONT     Archibald Lindsay of Creifoche
2 CONT     Hew Montgomerie of Achinheid
2 CONT     Hew (?Robert) Montgomerie of Hissilheid
2 CONT     Sir Hew Montgomerie of Braidstane
2 CONT     Johnne Montgomerie of Scotistoun
2 CONT     (3rd named) Sir Neill Montgomerie of Langschaw
2 CONT     Robert Montgomerie of Skelmourlie
2 CONT     James Mowat of Busbie
2 CONT     Robert Mure of Cauldwele
2 CONT     Hew Ralstoun of that Ilk
2 CONT     (2nd named) Lord Semple
2 CONT     William Semple of Foulwood and his brothers
2 CONT
2 CONT     to appear before the Council on 16th March next, under pain of rebellion, to hear his Majesty's decreet pronounced; with certification that, if they do not compear, they will be denounced rebels, decree will be given in the ma
2 CONC tter, and they shall be compelled to obey the same.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Earl of Eglintoun, Lord Semple and their said friends are reuqired to come Edinburg on 14th March next, and the Earl of Glencairn and his friends on Wednesday 15th March; and, at their coming to the burgh, they are to repa
2 CONC ir to their lodgings, and remain there till sent for by the Council.
2 CONT       Gabriel Sempill of Cathcart and Ladymure, Sir John’s third (A) or second (B) son, died at Pinkie 10.09.1547 (A) and who married Jonet (or Janet) Spreule, daughter of John Spreule of Coldoun (A, C) and Elizabeth Semple (A). J
2 CONC anet had previously married John Pollok, who died before 1508. He was denounced a rebel for failing to appear for the murder of William Cunynghame of Craigends after finding surety in a document dated 20 August 1533 (A). Both he a
2 CONC nd his wife received lands from Allan Stewart in a charter dated 25 November 1543 and on 16 July 1546 Queen Mary granted him the lands of Carswells (A)
2 CONT     Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT     A. The Scot Peerage, by Sir James Balfour Paul, published by David Douglas, 1904
2 CONT     B. Burke’s Peerage, entry for SEMPILL LORDS SEMPILL
2 CONT     C. The Shire of Renfrew by Crawfurd (1710) and Robertson (1818)
