1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Janet_Semple_(10)
2 CONT
2 CONT Name 	Janet Semple Gender 	Female Death? 	Aft 5 Mar 1570/71 	 [edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents and Siblings (edit)
2 CONT
2 CONT     F.  Robert Semple, 3rd Lord Sempillabt 1505 - Bet 1573
2 CONT     M.  Isabel Hamilton1518 - 1604
2 CONT
2 CONT m. 07 Apr 1538
2 CONT
2 CONT     Robert Semple, Master of Sempillabt 1536 - 1569
2 CONT     Grizzel Semple - 1575
2 CONT     Andrew Semple
2 CONT     Margaret Semple
2 CONT     James Semple
2 CONT     John Semple, of Bruntschellisabt 1537 - Bef 1587
2 CONT     Barbara SempleAbt 1538 -
2 CONT     Janet Semple - Aft 1570/71
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse and Children (edit)
2 CONT
2 CONT     H.  Alexander Fleming, of Barochan
2 CONT     W.  Janet Semple - Aft 1570/71
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse and Children (edit)
2 CONT
2 CONT     H.  William Fleming, of Barochan
2 CONT     W.  Janet Semple - Aft 1570/71
2 CONT
2 CONT     Patrick Fleming, Master of Barochanabt 1528 - 1570
2 CONT
2 CONT ▼ Barochan House
2 CONT
2 CONT     Barochan House. Located north-east of Houston, Renfrewshire, Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The main feature is a tall square tower, standing about the centre of the building, and about 20ft higher than the rest. The Ordnance Survey Name Book (ONB) states that its date of erection was unknown, though it was believed 
2 CONC to be over 500 years old. (This would imply that it was built as a successor to NS46NW 3). However, Millar states that the principal portion of the mansion was built towards the end of the 16th century by Janet Sempill, widow of A
2 CONC lexander Fleming, to replace the original structure which had been accidently destroyed by fire. It was considerably extended and improved during the 18th and 19th centuries. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ▼References
2 CONT
2 CONT       Archaeological and historical collections relating to the county of Renfrew, parish of Lochwinnoch. (Paisley: A. Gardner, 1885-1890), Vol. 1, Page 17.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''    6. Janet, married Alexander Fleming of Barrochan.
2 CONT       Douglas, Robert. The Peerage of Scotland, Containing An Historical and Genealogical Account of the NOBILITY of that Kingdom, from their Origin to the present Generation: Collected from the Public Records, and Ancient Chartul
2 CONC aries of this Nation, the Charters, and other Writings of the NOBILITY, and the Works of our best Historians. (Edinburgh: Printed by R. Fleming, 1764), Page 619.
2 CONT
2 CONT     4. _____, married to Alexander Fleming of Barrochan.
2 CONT       Paul, James Balfour. The Scots peerage: founded on Wood's edition of Sir Robert Douglas's peerage of Scotland, containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom, with armorial illustrations. (
2 CONC Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904-1914), Vol. 7, Page 550.
2 CONT
2 CONT     12. _____, married to William Fleming of Barochan, and had issue.
2 CONT       RootsWeb, 1 Jun 2005[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT   ''''''  As regards Janet Sempill was evidently the 2nd (or later) wife of William Fleming of Barrochan, as given in Scots Peerage [3]. Andrew B. W. MacEwen, looking at the varying statements and claims, noted that the evidence f
2 CONC or the marriage of William Fleming to Janet Sempill (a dau. of Robert Semple, 3rd Lord Sempill, fl. ca. 1505-1574) is reliable.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT     Further, evidence has been found for an additional generation of the Fleming family which had been undetected. Patrick Fleming was known to have been his son and heir in 1549 [4], but the career of Alexander Fleming as previou
2 CONC sly understood (and the confusion as to who was in fact the spouse of Janet Sempill) indicated that Patrick had presumably died young (probably d.v.p.), and that Alexander Fleming was the son and successor of William. Patrick did 
2 CONC in fact die during his father’s lifetime, but a document found in the Register of the Privy Seal proves that Alexander was in fact Patrick’s son, not brother.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT     1134. 5 March 1570/71. Glasgw. Precept for a charter to Jonet Houstoun and Alexander
2 CONT     Flemyng, grandson and heir apparent of William Flemyng of Barrochane:
2 CONT     to the said Jonet in sua virginitate, in liferent, in respect of the marriage to be solemnized between her and the said Alexander, of the £4 lands of old extent of Barlogane and Nethir Lostfeild, part of the £20 lands of Barro
2 CONC chane; and to the said Alexander and the heirs male of his body, whom failing his heirs male whomsoever, of the said £20 lands of Barrochan of old extent, with mill, manor, gardens and woods, in the barony and sheriffdom of Renfre
2 CONC w; which lands pertained heritably to the said William and were resigned by him, reserving life-rent and terce; to be held of the King as Prince and Steward; rendering rights and services used and wont. xxxix, 117 v.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT     As a result of the foregoing, it is evident that Margaret Fleming (married say 1567-1572 to James Cunyngham of Ashinyards) was actually the sister, not daughter, of Alexander Fleming of
2 CONT     Barrochan. These generations of the Flemings of Barrochan are now corrected, as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT     I. 1st marriage) NN = William Fleming of Barrochan = 2nd marriage) Janet Semple d. aft 5 March 1570/71 and had issue by 2nd:
2 CONT
2 CONT     II. Patrick Fleming, Master of Barrochan = Margaret Mure dvp before March 1570/71 and had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     III. Alexander Fleming, of Barrochan = Jonet Houstoun
2 CONT     III. William Fleming, of Cardona
2 CONT     III. Margaret Fleming = James Cunyngham
2 CONT
2 CONT     The foregoing will be of interest to those concerned with the ancestry of lairds of Ashinyards (now Ashgrove) and other Scots and English families, as well as those emigrants to the Americas
2 CONT     known to descend from the Flemings of Barrochan (e.g., Alexander Hamilton and Daniel Roberdeau).
2 CONT
2 CONT     A more detailed pedigree will follow; in addition, an AT for Margaret Mure (a daughter of Mungo Mure of Rowallan) will be provided as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT     References:
2 CONT
2 CONT     [1] James Paterson, History of the County of Ayr, with a
2 CONT     Genealogical Account of the Families of Ayrshire (Ayr:
2 CONT     J. Dick, 1852), II:248.
2 CONT
2 CONT     [2] For Janet Sempill as wife of Alexander Fleming, see
2 CONT     Archaeological and Historical Collections rel. to the
2 CONT     County of Renfrew (Paisley: Alexander Gardner, 1890)
2 CONT     I:17; also Sir Robert Douglas, The Peerage of
2 CONT     Scotland (Edinburgh: G. Ramsay and Company, 1813),
2 CONT     I:494.
2 CONT
2 CONT     [3] SP VII:550, sub Sempill, Lord Sempill.
2 CONT
2 CONT     [4] William Fleming of Barrochan was deputy Sheriff of Renfrew, enforcing an Instrument of Sasine of James Maxwell in the lands of Pottartoun, Cowglenn & c., 8 Nov 1549. This instrument was witnessed by his son and heir Patric
2 CONC k Fleming [ 'Patricio Fleming filio et herede apparenti dicti Willielmi Fleming' ]. William Fraser, ed., Memoirs of the Maxwells of Pollok (Edinburgh, 1863) I:291-3.
2 CONT
2 CONT     [5] Gordon Donaldson, D.Litt., ed. Registrum Secreti Sigilli Regum Scotorum: The Register of the Privy Seal of Scotland (Edinburgh: H. M. General Register House, 1963) Vol. VI, A.D. 1567-1574, pp. 215-6.
2 CONT
