1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Archibald Campbell, 2nd of Lochnell''' was born circa 1530 at Lochnell, Ardchattan, Argyllshire, Scotland.2 He died on 4 October 1594 at Glenlivet, Banffshire, Scotland 2, at the  Battle of Glenlivet.  Buried in the Tomb Of Far
2 CONC quharson, Uschriachan, Aberdeenshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of John Campbell, 1st of Lochnell and Mary Campbell.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT # He married, firstly, about 1560, Janet Macdougall, (daughter of Duncan 'na mein' Macdougall of Dunollie), circa 1560 at Dunollie, Argyllshire, Scotland.3,2 
2 CONT # He married, secondly, Janet MacLean.2  (dau. of Maclean of Dowart)
2 CONT # He married, thirdly, Jessie Keir Chain O'Donnell.2 (dau. of Cheyne)
2 CONT #  He married, fourthly, about 1574, Sibylla Drummond.3  (dau. of John Drummond, of Colquhalzie) Also known as  Isabella.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Archibald Campbell, 2nd of Lochnell, uncertain mother 
2 CONT # “eldest daughter” Margaret Campbell+3 b. c 1552 (daughter of Janet Macadougall?) [Lawler source has her as dau. of John Gorm 1st Lochnell and Mary Campbell of Ardkinglass.]. Married Hector (MacLean) MacLean of Lochbuie.
2 CONT #unknown daughter Campbell+2.  Married Duncan Stewart, 5th of Appin.
2 CONT # Ann Campbell+2 b. c 1564 (daughter of Janet Macadougall?). Married Alexander Campbell, 7th Captain of Dunstaffnage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Archibald Campbell, 2nd of Lochnell and Janet Macdougall:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Alexander Campbell, 3rd of Lochnell+2 b. c 1566, d. 1638.  Married Isabel MacDougall.
2 CONT # Colin Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Archibald Campbell, 2nd of Lochnell and Janet MacLean:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander is know to have had issue by this marriage. As Burkes Landed Gentry 1952 (Campbell of Jura) reports that Duncan was his 3rd son, and he is not mentioned under any of the other marriages, we presume that he was by this ma
2 CONC rriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT LDS Medieval Families Unit shows Duncan as son of Alexander 3rd of Lochnell (as does Stirnet Campbell09 -a double entry) and shows this marriage as having been sp. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Archibald Campbell, 2nd of Lochnell and Jessie Keir Chain O'Donnell: 
2 CONT
2 CONT #John Campbell2
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Archibald Campbell, 2nd of Lochnell and Sibylla Drummond
2 CONT
2 CONT # James Campbell of Frackersaig+2 b. c 1575, d. a 5 Dec 1628.  Had issue.
2 CONT # Janet Campbell2 b. c 1577.  Married Sir Archibald Campbell, of Barbreck.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From Burke’s (1879), “CAMPBELL OF LOCHNELL.” Page 255.  < [https://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhera01byuburk#page/254/mode/2up Archive.Org] >
2 CONT
2 CONT >The eldest son, Archibald Campbell, 2nd of Lochnell, m. 1st, Janet, dau. of Duncan Macdougall, of Dunolly, and had, Alexander, his heir; and Colin, whose issue is extinct. He m. 2ndly, a dau. of Maclean of Dowart, and had issue ;
2 CONC  3rdly, a dau. of Cheyne, and had a son, John, ancestor of Coreleigh ; and 4tlhly,Sibella, dau. of John Drummond, of Colquhalzie, and had a son, James, of Cruachan, ancestor of Stonefield and Balemo.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I119451&tree=CC clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 - 1593 - Amongst the complications of the affair between Huntly and Moray in February 1592, there were mingled the details of a plot in which Huntly and the Chancellor Maitland were connected with three chieftains of the clan Ca
2 CONC mpbell, Ardkinlas, Lochnell, and Glenurchy against the life of John Campbell of Calder, who was obnoxious to the latter persons on account of his supreme influence in the affairs of the minor Earl of Argyle. By the exertions of Ar
2 CONC dkinlas, a man called MacEllar was procured to undertake the assassination of Calder: and in the same month which saw the tragedy at Dunnibrissle, this wretched man shot Calder with three bullets, through a window, as the victim s
2 CONC at unsuspecting of danger in the house of Knepoch in Lorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT The youthful earl having threatened vengeance against Ardkinlas, the latter seems to have lost heart; and being extremely desirous of recovering his young chief's regard, he subsequently tried to accomplish his purpose by revealin
2 CONC g what he knew of another plot in which the same parties were concerned against the earl's life.  It may be sufficient to remark that MacEllar and a higher agent in the a person of John Oig Campbell of Cabrachan, a brother of Loch
2 CONC nell, were taken and executed for Calder's death; but owing to various causes, among which the complicity and friendship of Maitland was probably the chief, Ardkinlas continued for a considerable time to keep out of the grasp of t
2 CONC he law.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not till September 1596 that Ardkinlas underwent a trial for the slaughter of the Laird of Calder. The matter having doubtless been arranged beforehand, no pursuers appeared, and he was set at liberty.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT From “The house of Argyll and the collateral branches of the clan Campbell, from the year 420 to the present time” (Publication date 1871). CAMPBELLS OF LOCIINELL.  Page 166-167.  < [https://archive.org/details/houseofargyllcol00i
2 CONC nglas/page/n347/mode/2up?q=Lochnell Archive.Org] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - II. Archibald is said to have had four wives, at or about the same time. 1st. Janet, daughter to Duncan na-mein Macdougall of Dunollie, chief of the Macdougalls; by her he had
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st. Alexander, his heir, and
2 CONT 2nd. Cailen-na-kille of Kilekolmkill, in Benderloch, who was married to a daughter of Stirling of Keir, relict of Buchanan of Leni, extinct.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second was a daughter of MacLean of Duart.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jessie, the third, was daughter to Chuin O'Donell, Ireland. She was mother to Ian Connelach, married to Loup's daughter; of them are the Campbells of Corrieleigh.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth was Isabella, daughter to Drummond of Cochyle, and relict of William Redoch of Aberledmont; by her he had James of Croguan, married to a daughter of the Bishop of Ardchattan, second son to Campbell the first Laird of Ca
2 CONC lder; of him are the families of Stonefield and Balerno.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was the only one of Archibald's wives who survived him. She afterwards married the chief of the Macdougalls, Laird of Dunollie.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his different wives he had several daughters. Margaret, married to M'Lean of Lochbuy; Ann to Campbell of Dunstaffnage; Janet to Campbell of Barbrec, and the fourth to Stewart of Appin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald, with his two brothers, Donald and Colin, were killed at Glenlivet, and interred in the tomb of Farquharson, Uschriachan, Aberdeenshire, 1594.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Campbell-The House of Argyll and the collateral branches of the clan.pdf) [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Comments
2 CONT
2 CONT *There is some disagreement among sources on which wife was the parent of which children.
2 CONT *Previous curator note: He married 1) Janet MacDougall ch.: Alexander, Margaret and Ann. 2) Sybilla Drummond  ch.: James and Janet, 3) Janet MacLean,  4)  Jessie O'Donnell  Ch.: John. Son of John Campbell and Mary Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
