1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.electricscotland.com/webclans/m/genealogicalmontgomery.pdf (pg 67)
2 CONT
2 CONT XXIV. '''Sir Neil Montgomerie of Lainshaw''', who, 
2 CONT * as heir-male to the title and honors of the fifth Earl of Eglinton, 
2 CONT * should have succeeded to them on that nobleman's death in 1613; 
2 CONT * but they went with his estates, by the new grant of November, 1611, to that earl's cousin. 
2 CONT ** The '''causes of this diversion''' of the title and honors from the heir-male have been elsewhere recited, (pp. 61, 62,) and need not here be recapitulated. 
2 CONT * On that earl's death, however, Sir Neil succeeded to the lineal male representation of the family, and is therefore placed in this account numerically next to him as bearing that honor; an honor which is in the line of Sir Neil'
2 CONC s descendants to this day.
2 CONT * Sir Neil had a precept of dare constat, dated March 18, 1616, of the lands of Lainshaw, with the patronage of the Chapel of Lainshaw, Peacockbank, &c., from Alexander, Earl of Eglinton this was within a year after the latter had
2 CONC  been officially recognized in that dignity. 
2 CONT * Alexander's feudal superiority was acknowledged in this act by Sir Neil, who thus ratified that which was by this time established beyond all contest; and 
2 CONT * he could not do less, seeing that Alexander had the royal assent to his pretensions, only, 
2 CONT * however, after the king had withheld it from him for the space of two years.	
2 CONT * It was not until the year 1630, that Hugh, Viscount Montgomerie, the chief of the Irish house, by an indenture acknowledged Alexander as his chief, and became bound, when Eglinton came to Ireland, to present him with a white hor
2 CONC se, as a token of holding his estates in Scotland and Ireland from him as his feudal superior. 
2 CONT ** Thus different branches of the family tardily acknowledged Alexander Seton as Earl of Eglinton.^
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Hist. Co. of Ayr, vol., ii. 453, see p. 153.
2 CONT * 2 From a MS. Pedigree by Mrs. Macknight, wife of the Commentator, whose father Samuel McCormick, General Examiner of Excise, Scotland, was great-grandson of Ann (Montgomerie) Gordon; from him she had these facts.
2 CONT * 3 Hist. Co. of Ayr, vol., i. 282.t)» 	
2 CONT '''GENEALOGICAL HISTORY OF'''   p 68
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sir Neil Montgomerie was served heir to his mother Lady Jean Lyle, December 20, 1575, as Neil Montgomery, of Gallowberry.	
2 CONT * He never assumed the title of Lyle; but while in the year 1559, having sold his claim to the estate of Duchal, or of Lyle, and others, he still assumed the arms of Lyle, as heir of line of that noble family.
2 CONT * He '''married Elizabeth''', 
2 CONT ** '''daughter of''' John Cuninghame of Aiket, whose great-grandfather, Alexander Cunynghame, was of the Glencairn family.	
2 CONT ** This Alexander's wife, Jean Kennedie, 
2 CONT *** was sister of the first Earl of Cassilis, and 
2 CONT *** was at one time, as mistress to James IV., 
2 CONT *** '''known as Lady Bothwell.'''	
2 CONT *** This '''branch of the Cuninghames''' were active participants in all the feuds of their family, the most bitter of which was that against the Montgomeries. 
2 CONT *** It was to this feud that Hugh, the fourth Earl of Eglinton, owed his death in 1586, when on a visit to Sir Neil, at the hands of Alexander and William Cuninghame, Lady Lainshaw's brothers, and some of their cousins with their 
2 CONC adherents.	
2 CONT *** Rumor imputed to this lady a helping hand in the matter, a rumor which seemed to be credited by the earl's successor, for he cut her children off from inheriting any of the Eglinton estates.
2 CONT * Sir Neil '''died before''' the year 1613, 
2 CONT * '''leaving several children'''. 
2 CONT ** I. '''Neil, his successor'''. 
2 CONT ** II. '''William Montgomerie of Brigend''', whose descendants carried on the representation of the family. Forth is line see page71. 
2 CONT ** III. '''James, Minister of Dunlop'''. 
2 CONT *** The patronage and tithes of Diinlop Church had been granted to the Earl of Eglinton, in 1603. 
2 CONT *** James Montgomerie's successor in this charge was Hugh Eglintoun, son of Archibald Eglintoun. 
2 CONT *** His predecessor had been Hans Hamilton, father of Viscount Clandeboyes, who had died in 1008.^ ***James had '''married Elizabeth, the daughter of Robert Montgomerie''', Bishop of Argyle, the sixth son of the first Earl of Egli
2 CONC nton, p. 58.	
2 CONT *** He had a sasine in his favor, dated February 9, 1605, of the five merk land of Gallowberry, in which he is described as, " Jaoobo, alias Magro. Jaeobo Montgomerie, filio I'timo hon'lis virj d'ni Nagellj Montgomerie de Langscha
2 CONC w, militis, teriio gcnito."^	
2 CONT *** He '''died''' in 1613, and left two sons,
2 CONT **** 1. Robert.
2 CONT **** 2. James, both of whom died without issue. 
2 CONT ** IV. '''John Montgomerie of Cockilbie''', who 
2 CONT *** '''married''' Jean, daughter of Captain Daniel Forrester of Cardcn, envoy of James VI. to Spain.	
2 CONT *** His will is dated in1683.* 
2 CONT *** He was the ancestor of the second Lainshaw line, (for which see page113.) 
2 CONT *** Sir Thomas Montgomerie Cuninghame is descended from this line.
2 CONT **   V. '''A daughter''', who married Graham of Grugar. 
2 CONT **   VI. '''Mariot''', married Robert Johnston, son of Gilbert Johnston of Wamphray.
2 CONT *** Their contract of marriage is dated February 20, 1606.^
2 CONT
2 CONT *  1 Hist. Co. of Ayr, toI. ii. 49, 281.	' ibid. ii. 4.'i.
2 CONT ** His will is dated at the " town of Langshaw, the xi of May, 1613 zeiris." Paterson had not known of this sasine when he made James the second son, and William of Brigend the third son of Sir Neil. Sec. Reg. Ayrs., vol. iii. f. 
2 CONC 72. See Anderson's Geneal. Account. In the same sasine, his wife Elizabeth is "filia Magri Robortj Montgomerie, archideeani Ergadiaj, rectoris et ministri apud templum de Stewartoun." See Keith's Scottish Bishops, p. 289, where it
2 CONC  is seen that Ergadia is used for Argyle.
2 CONT * Hist. Co. of Ayr, i. 443.	^ Douglas' Baronage, vol. i. 233.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''THE FAMILY OF MONTGOMERY'''.	p 69
2 CONT
2 CONT XXV. '''Neil Montgomerie of Lainshaw''', had succeeded his father prior to the year 1621, 
2 CONT * for in a document of that year, '''he is mentioned as "now of Langschaw;"''' and 
2 CONT * '''died before''' 1625, the date of his son and successor's precept of seisin in the lands of Nether Skeldon.	
2 CONT * He '''married, about the year 1601, Marion''', 
2 CONT ** daughter of Sir William Mure of Rowallan ; 
2 CONT ** her brother, '''the second Sir William''', '''married''' Jean, daughter of Hugh Montgomerie of Hessilhead, (p. 135).	
2 CONT * '''Neil left two sons and four daughters.'''
2 CONT **      I. '''Neil''', his successor. 
2 CONT **      II. '''John''', who is mentioned in " the seasing of Langschaw, Cockilbie, Sevinaikers, and others, in the lands of Crevoch," recorded at Irvine, April 7, 1627, as "Johne Montgomerie, brother germane to Neill Montgomerie o
2 CONC f Langschaw,"and as acting as procurator and attorney for him. He had died before the year 1632.'
2 CONT **     III.  '''A daughter''' married to Buntin of Ardich. 
2 CONT **     IV. '''A daughtier''' married to Montgomerie of Auehinhood. 
2 CONT **      V. '''A daughter''' married to Campbell of Skeldon. 
2 CONT **     VI. '''Agnes''', married to George Houston of Park.
2 CONT  **************************
2 CONT
