1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Montgomery-9164
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Montgomery is an undocumented second son[1][2] of Neil Montgomery II of Lainshaw and may be the father of James aka Jacob Montgomery.
2 CONT
2 CONT * the unnamed third son of John Lord Montgomery, Master of Eglinton (1480-1520), identified as Robert Montgomerie (1510-1600).[3][4][5]
2 CONT * James Montgomerie (b aft 1567 and d. 1613) Minister of Dunlop who married Elizabeth Montgomery, son of Neil Montgomery III 1545-1613 and brother of Neil Montgomerie II of Lainshaw. [6]
2 CONT * John Montgomerie who is called 'fatheris brothir sone of the third Earl' in a Commission by the Earl dated 25th May 1571, son of the unnamed third son of John Lord Montgomery, Master of Eglinton (1480-1520).[7][8][9]
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile recognizes the first option. This profile follows the estimated birth year of 1553.[10].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===DNA Note
2 CONT
2 CONT The only Y-DNA tested descendant of James aka Jacob Montgomery is Sven Montgomery. His haplogroup is J-Z35794.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Below are quotes from the sources that pertain to the investigation of the identity of the father of James aka Jacob Montgomery:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob's father was a descendant of "Hugo of Eglinton (died 1545)...attributed to the branch Lainshaw, within which Neill dy was found to have left a second son". Swedish genealogist David Montgomery's 1913 article written in Swedi
2 CONC sh with the line of descent going from Sir Neil Montgomery, the 1st Laird of Lainshaw to his grandson James Sr of Dunlop who married Elizabeth Montgomery to James Jr aka Jacob.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Robert is the second son of Neil II of Lainshaw and his wife Lady Jean Lyle and beyond the limited information in the Swedish "Knight's Hall Pedigrees" record, very little is known about Robert's life. There is no record of who h
2 CONC e married. There is a description of a Robert the father of Jacob Montgomery, next identified, in Bo Gabriel Montgomery's book "Origins and History of the Montgomerys". However, given the stated birth year, and the years of the li
2 CONC fe experiences of the identified Robert in Bo Gabriel's book, it cannot be Robert the son of Neil II of Lainshaw."[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Montgomerie, Earl of Eglinton, third son whose name has not been ascertained, but who left issue a son John [Montgomery Memorials p 36][13]
2 CONT
2 CONT "By his marriage with Elizabeth Edmonstoun, ... John Master of Eglintoun had three sons and one daughter --
2 CONT
2 CONT # Archibald, Master of Eglintoun.
2 CONT # Hugh, second Earl of Eglintoun.
2 CONT # . . . Montgomerie, whose Christian name has not been ascertained, but his existence is proved by the mention of 'John Montgomerie fatheris brothir sone' of the third Earl' in a commission by the Earl dated 26th May 1571 (footnot
2 CONC e: Original at Eglinton"
2 CONT # Christian...."[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Robert was the third son of John Lord Montgomery, Master of Eglinton (1480-1520) and brother of Hugh, second Earl of Eglinton. His existence and also the fact that he was married have been proved, but his Christian name was appar
2 CONC ently not known to Fraser when he wrote the Memorials of the Montgomeries (vide II, 36). However, an eighteenth-century pedigree of the elder branch in Sweden states quite definitely that Robert, grandfather of Major-General David
2 CONC  Montgomery was 'brother of Eglinton.' His name is also mentioned in several Scottish documents. He must have been born about 1510, and like so many other members of the Scottish aristocracy in those days he joined the Men-at-Arms
2 CONC  of the French King, commanded by his distant cousin Jacques, Seigneur de Lorges, and later on by James Hamilton, second Earl of Arran. He appears in the muster rolls of that famous corps in 1546, the year when Lorges returned to 
2 CONC France after his expedition to Scotland, 1550 and 1554. Robert had two sons: James, recorded in the above-mentioned pedigree, and John, who is called 'fatheris brothir sone of the third Earl' in a commission by the Earl dated 26th
2 CONC  May 1571 (original at Eglinton)....No one but the Earl himself, his brother William, or his uncle Robert could have been thus described, and it seems unlikely that any other Montgomery would have been allowed to raise such a big 
2 CONC force in Scotland."[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Here its requisite to observe, that John, Son and Heir apparent to this Earl, when the Feuds run high betwixt the contending Parties of the Nobility in the Minority of King James V, he was killed in a Scuffle betwixt the Earls of 
2 CONC Aran and Angus on the Street of Edinburgh, 30 of April 1520a, leaving behind him a Son and a Daughter by Elisabeth his Wife, Daughter of Sir Archibald Edmonston of Duntreath, Hugh who succeeded his Grandfather, and Christian, Wife
2 CONC  of Sir William Douglass of Drumlanrig, Ancestor to the Duke of Queensberry."[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT "According to information from 1658 (David M 1910, p. 16, o 1913, p. 98 o 103), the ancestor of the family branch who came into Swedish service at the earliest must have been named Jacob. This has been identified (David M 1913, pp
2 CONC . 98 o 102) with a captain Jacob M[ontgomery] (d troll 1632), who like a[n] ensign Hugh M[ontgomery] (d at the earliest in 1639 as a lieutenant) was in Swedish military service in Germany from 1630. Jacob M[ontgomery] was promoted
2 CONC  to lieutenant colonel in 1632 and killed shortly afterwards in a duel (Monro). This identification has been disputed (BG de M, p. 123; cf. p. 180), but not convincingly. Later an ensign Johan M[ontgomery] appeared in 1633 and in 
2 CONC 1656 an ensign Hugh M[ontgomery] .[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT This Sir Neil Montgomery by Jean Lyle his Wife, had Sir Neil his Successor, and two Daughters . . . . married to the Laird of Ardross, and Margaret to John Pollock of that Ilk.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Montgomerie, Earl of Eglinton: third son whose name has not been ascertained, but who left issue a son John, Montgomery Memorials p 36[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
