1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnnie_Armstrong
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Johnnie Armstrong''' or '''Johnie Armstrong''' was a Scottish raider and folk-hero. Johnnie Armstrong of Gilnockie was captured and hanged by King James V in July 1530. He is related to the Baird family. Child ballad number 169
2 CONC  tells of his life.
2 CONT ----Here I wish to try to clarify a few points on the Armstrongs of Mangerton and on Johnnie in a particular way. The aim is the one  to fix a few of them  only as the entire story of our ancestors (Johnnie Is for sure my 13 g.gra
2 CONC ndfather) Is as fascinating as very mysterious. It Is probably the most interesting story I have on my background. Even if I got some hints and I spent years in trying to recall their history entirely I must admit that this goal i
2 CONC s quite impossible up to now, even if we all have the deep feeling of a great antiquity of it. Just a book would not be enough to tell the story Just on its main lines. So, here, I like to say a few things on them collected in tim
2 CONC e in order to help other people to go deeper into out lineage as well as to avoid to fall in funny stories clearly impossible and silly traps. First, It Is clearly impossible that Siward Is the First Laird as somewhere Is mentione
2 CONC d as there are three Centuries of gap between him and Alexander. This does not mean that Siward or better Siward family could not be our old ancestor, this probably is true but not in the way that this fact Is generally told. The 
2 CONC lineage should go by many inter marriages and female lines. Second, more important here, there Is no a real reason for calling Johnnie "Sir".  No way to give to Johnnie the title of Knight. It never existed a Sir Johnnie. This has
2 CONC  never been found anywhere and, logically and historically, cannot be as Sir that time was just an English title. I would like the curator points out that and cancel the knightood to Johnnie. This as far I know Is a big mistake.  
2 CONC Then, there are a lot of points even more interesting than the alleged knightood about him to say but not now and here, all Is too long and still unclear even to me. I must find out the documents of a very profound and serious  re
2 CONC searcher on the subject. Shortly I can tell that this Is a man who came from nowhere, became famous but nobody as far I do know was able to explains to xme the reasons for his fame and the those behind his power  In other words, h
2 CONC e Is reported as a great Chieftain, but what did he for having this fame? This Is not saying in a negative way as being on denial, but for saying that around him or behind him there is a mystery. This mystery Is probably the cause
2 CONC  of his fame. Going on, I like to mention two more points on the Armstrongs. The First Is the one of the name of the wife and mother. Here as elsewhere is reported as Lady Graham but I do not see the charts on which this assertion
2 CONC  Is based. Again, I am not dening, at the opposite  I am quite convinced about that marriage but It would be better to try to refers to the original documenta. It Is certainly clear that between Armstrongs and Grahams an allience 
2 CONC was on and many marriages took place between the two Scions but certainly the final proof of this specifical one misses in here. I suspect that this information comes by the american documents that a few descendents of the Mangert
2 CONC on line took to America with them. This is the second point. Specifically I might think to the Officer who married in to the old House of Mac Guires ot. Maguires, by the name of William or maybe Edward, I cannot state now on it. T
2 CONC his gentleman was pretty for sure the father of the first American General Armstrong, John, even if this point on geni Is considered just ipotetical instead the same fact is quite sure. At the present I do not remember well the so
2 CONC urce on where I found the account but this Is likely Burke ed.1848 or DNB. I Hope ti can be more detailed on the said source soon I think the two profiles should be merged. This Just and introduction as said for a better clarity a
2 CONC nd fir debunking a few mistake but the points are many and very confusing and certainly very old and, some times I suspect, of a some old historical importance . Giacomo 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT - John was called 'The Strong' had 5 sons. John was Robin (Robyn Hood) of the Border, and the stories of his exploits run through all Scottish literature and Sir Walter Scott makes frequent reference to him. John was granted lands
2 CONC  in Eskdale, & Dumfrieshire Scotland 8/4/1525 by Robert, Lord of Maxwell. (Book of Carlsvsverock vol. 2, p. 479 #102) Pamphlet on Debatable Lands by Thomas Carlyle. John had several times challenged King James V https://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/people/James-V-King-of-Scots/6000000003233882803 of England, to a duel, and John was considered an embarrassment to the Crown of England, so King James V, declared him an outlaw. King James V men, trapped John in Whithaugh, Sco
2 CONC tland with a few of John's men, and without a trial, hung them on the spot. Source-Chronicles of the Armstrongs p. 328 & 354. Source- Armstrong House of Langholm. Item 3. Source- Chart of the Ten Lords of Mangerton Castle. Source-
2 CONC  Notable Southern Families by Zella Armstrong states " All the Armstrongs of Ireland in the Seventeenth Century are decended from John, and all the American Armstrongs, who trace through the Scotch-Irish Clan." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source- The Armstrong Family History of Lancaster Co., Pa. by Mrs. Mae Armstrong Simon (Historian)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT BALLAD OF JOHNNIE OF GILNOCKIE There dwelt a man in faire Westmerland, Jonnë Armstrong men did him call, He had nither lands nor rents coming in, Yet he kept eight score men in his hall. He had horse and harness for them all, Good
2 CONC ly studs were all milke-white; O the golden bands an about their necks, And their weapons, they were all alike. Newes then was brought unto the king That there was sicke a won as hee, That livëd lyke a bold out-law, And robbëd all
2 CONC  the north country. The king he writt an a letter then, A letter which was large and long; He signëd it with his owne hand, And he promised to doe him no wrong. When this letter came Jonnë untill, His heart was as blyth as birds o
2 CONC n the tree: Never was I sent for before any king, My father, my grandfather, nor none but mee. And if wee goe the king before, I would we went most orderly; Every man of you shall have his scarlet cloak, Laced with silver laces th
2 CONC ree. Every won of you shall have his velvett coat, Laced with silver lace so white; O the golden bands an about your necks, Black hatts, white feathers, all alyke. By the morrow morning at ten of the clock, Towards Edenburough gon
2 CONC  was hee, And with him all his eight score men; Good Lord, it was a goodly sight for to see! When Jonnë came befower the king, He fell downe on his knee; O pardon, my soveraine leige, he said, O pardon, my eight score men and mee.
2 CONC  Thou shalt have no pardon, thou traytor strong For thy eight score men nor thee; For tomorrow morning by ten of the clock, Both thou and them shall hang on the gallow tree. But Jonnë looked over his left shoulder, Good Lord, what
2 CONC  a grevious look looked hee! Saying, Asking grace of a graceless face - Why there is none for you nor me? But Jonnë had a bright sword by his side, And it was made of the mettle so free, That had not the king stept his foot a side
2 CONC , He had smitten his head from his faire boddë. Saying, Fight on, my merry men all, And see that none of you be taine; For rather than men shall say we were hangd, Let them report how we were slaine! Then, God wott, faire Eddenbur
2 CONC rough rose, And so besett poore Jonnë rounde, That fower score and tenn of Jonnës best men Lay gasping all upon the ground. Then like a mad mand Jonnë laide about, And like a man then fought hee, Untill a falce Scot came Jonnë beh
2 CONC inde, And runn him through the faire boddee. Saying, Fight on, my merry men all, I am a little hurt, but I am not slain; I will lay me down for to bleed a while, Then Ile rise and fight with you again. Newes then was brought to yo
2 CONC ung Jonnë Armestrong, As he stood by his nurses knee, Who vowed if ere he lived for to be a man, O the treacherous Scots revenged heed be.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes: From: Old English Ballads Francis B. Gummere Ginn & Co., Publishers 1894 pp 127-129
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT John Armstrong, born 1480, House Gilnockle Hall, Parish, Scotland, died 8 June 1530, Caerlanrig, Whithaugh, Scotland.  He was the son of Alexander Armstrong (1445-1510).
2 CONT
2 CONT "John of Gilnockie" Armstrong  is immortalized in "The Ballad of Johnie Armstrang" attributed to Ringan's Thom.  The full account of his betrayal can be found in "The Steel Bonnets" by George MacDonald Fraser (1971).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT CAREY'S Memoirs, p. 151.
2 CONT
2 CONT >From this narrative, the power and strength of the Armstrongs, at this late period, appear to have been very considerable. Johnie Armstrong of Gilnockie, the hero of the following ballad, is a noted personage, both in history and
2 CONC  tradition. He was, it would seem from the ballad, a brother of the Laird of Mangertoun, chief of the name. His place of residence (now a roofless tower) was at the Hollows, a few miles from Langholm, where its ruins still serve t
2 CONC o adorn a scene, which in natural beauty, has few equals in Scotland. At the head of a desperate band of freebooters, this Armstrong is said to have spread the terror of his name almost as far as Newcastle, and to have levied blac
2 CONC k-mail, or protection and forbearance money, for many miles round. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT “JOHNNY ARMSTRONG AND THE MISSING HEIR” < [https://www.scotclans.com/pages/johnny-armstrong-and-the-missing-heir link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Since the death of Johnny Armstrong there have been many stories as to what happened to his vast fortune. One rarely spoken story is that it is in fact buried at Langholm Castle, under a point where there is a risen part of the g
2 CONC round where a vault could easily be hidden. What secrets lie under it may go undiscovered but the tale of Johnny Armstrong will live forever.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Johnny Armstrong painting by Henry Hetherington Emmerson at Laing Art Gallery in Newcastle today. Painted in 1886 it shows him on an ill advised journey to meet James V.
