1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Hepburn,_1st_Earl_of_Bothwell
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell (died October 18, 1508) was Lord High Admiral of Scotland. Under his territorial designation of Sir Patrick Hepburne of Dunsyre, Knt., he was Sheriff of Berwickshire, June 15, 1480. He was the
2 CONC  son of Adam Hepburn, Master of Hailes, and succeeded his grandfather Sir Patrick Hepburn as the 2nd Lord Hailes in (1482/1483).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Patrick Hepburn of Hailes was one of the Conservators of a truce with England on September 20, 1484, but was one of the leaders of the Confederate Lords who rebelled against King James III of Scotland, and he led the van again
2 CONC st the Royal array at the battle of Sauchieburn, 11 July 1488. Birrel seems to have believed that he was one of those responsible for the murder of the king after the battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the new reign of James IV he rose to great power and held many offices: included Master of the King's Household, custodian of Edinburgh Castle and Sheriff Principal of Edinburgh and Haddington, whilst his son and heir Adam was 
2 CONC made Master of the Royal Stables. Patrick Hepburn was further appointed Lord High Admiral of Scotland on September 10, 1488.
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 13, 1488, he had a Crown charter of the feudal lordships of Crichtoun (see Crichton Castle) and Bothwell, in the King's hands following the forfeiture of John Ramsay, Lord Bothwell. On the 17th of the same month the lor
2 CONC dship of Bothwell was erected into an Earldom in his favour, and he was belted Earl of Bothwell in full Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6th March 1492 he had a charter of the lands and lordship of Liddesdale, with Hermitage Castle, etc., upon the resignation of the same by Archibald Douglas, Earl of Angus, the latter getting the lordship of Bothwell [but not th
2 CONC e Earldom] which Patrick in turn resigned.
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell, was Captain of Dumbarton castle on April 1, 1495.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the plenipotentiaries sent to conclude the treaty for the marriage of King James IV with Princess Margaret Tudor of England in October 1501, and he stood proxy for the King at the ceremony of betrothal on 25 January 
2 CONC 1502.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married twice: (1) before Feb. 1481, Janet or Joanna (d. before 21 Feb 1491) daughter of James Douglas, 1st Earl of Morton by his spouse, Princess Joanna Stewart. They had one daughter, Joanna, married to George Seton, 3rd Lord
2 CONC  Seton (who fell at Flodden).(2)by contract dated February 21, 1491, Margaret, daughter of George Gordon, 2nd Earl of Huntly. They had four notable sons and a daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bothwell's eldest son by his second marriage, Adam succeeded him.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from http://uk.geocities.com/nassarawa@btinternet.com/bothwell.htm:
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandson of the 1st Lord HAILES and Ellen WALLACE and son of Adam HEPBURN, Master of HAILES born 1435 d 1479 and Helen HOME daugher of Sir Alexander HOME.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the leaders of those Lords who rebelled against the unpopular reign of James III and is believed to be one of those responsible for the king's murder after the Battle of Sauchieburn in 1488. On the accession of James
2 CONC  IV, HAILES was presented with several titles including Lord High Admiral, the Keeper of Edinburgh Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later that year he was presented with a crown charter to the lands of Crichton and Bothwell, following the forfeiture of John Ramsay, Lord BOTHWELL and was created 1st Earl of BOTHWELL.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1492 he exchanged with Archibald Douglas Earl of Angust the lordship of BOTHWELL forthose of Liddesdale, including Hermitage Castle, although keeping the titular earldom. In 1501 he was one of the Ambassadors who arranged the m
2 CONC arriage of James IV with Margaret TUDOR of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Patrick Hepburn:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10827.htm#i108262
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell [1]
2 CONT *M, #108262, 
2 CONT *d. 18 October 1508
2 CONT *Last Edited=21 Dec 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell was the son of Adam Hepburn, Master of Hailes and Helen Home.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Lady Janet Douglas, daughter of James Douglas, 1st Earl of Morton and Joan Stewart, before 1 February 1480/81.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married by contract, secondly, Lady Margaret Gordon, daughter of George Gordon, 2nd Earl of Huntly and Annabella Stewart, on 21 February 1490/91.[3] He married, secondly, Lady Margaret Gordon, daughter of George Gordon, 2nd Ear
2 CONC l of Huntly and Annabella Stewart, circa 21 February 1490/91.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 18 October 1508.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Sheriff of Berwick on 15 June 1480.5 He was invested as a Knight before February 1480/81.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded to the title of 2nd Lord Hailes [S., 1453] circa 1483.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 September 1484 he was a conservator of a truce with England.[6],[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Keeper of the Castle of Edinburgh in 1488.[2] He held the office of Master of the Household in 1488.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Sauchieburn on 11 June 1488, where he led the vanguard against King James III of Scotland.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Sheriff Principal of Edinburgh on 26 June 1488.[2] He held the office of High Admiral [Scotland] on 10 September 1488.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Earl of Bothwell [Scotland] on 17 October 1488, granted to him by the new King James IV following Hepburn's prominent part in the victory of Sauchieburn over King James III.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Steward of Kirkcudbright on 5 November 1488.[5] He held the office of Warden of the West and Middle Marches on 14 July 1489.[2] On 6 March 1491/92 he received, in exchange for the Lordship of Bothwell, the gr
2 CONC eat Lordship of Liddisdale with the Castle of Hermitage, from the family of Douglas, Earl of Angus.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 June 1493 his grants from the King were excepted from the general reversal of the King's grants made during his minority.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Captain of Dunbarton Castle on 1 April 1495.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1501 he was one of the Embassy who concluded the marriage of Kings James IV of Scotland to Margaret, daughter of King Henry VII.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell and Lady Janet Douglas
2 CONT *1. Lady Janet Hepburn+[4] b. bt 1481 - 1490, d. a 10 May 1558
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell and Lady Margaret Gordon
2 CONT *1. Lady Margaret Hepburn [7] d. 1513
2 CONT *2. William Hepburn of Rollandstoun+[8] d. b 25 Mar 1558
2 CONT *3. John Hepburn [9] d. 1558
2 CONT *4. Adam Hepburn, 2nd Earl of Bothwell+[3] b. c 1492, d. 9 Sep 1513
2 CONT *5. Patrick Hepburn, Master of Hailes+[8] b. c 1494, d. 31 Oct 1576
2 CONT
