1 OCCU Lady in Waiting to Mary of Guise - Queen of Scots
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Seton (d 1549) mar. (2) circa Feb 1538/9 '''Marie Pierres''', Lady-in-Waiting to Queen Mary of Lorraine (mar. (2) before 6 Oct 1554 Pierre de Clovis, Seigneur de Bryante; d. circa 1576), daughter of ..... Pi
2 CONC erres. of Le Plessis Baudoin.  children by second wife 
2 CONT *     1. Hon. Robert Seton of Mylis (d. after 22 Feb 1562/3) 
2 CONT *     2. Hon. James Seton of Milton (d. before 29 Jan 1562/3) 
2 CONT *     3. Hon. Marie Seton (b. c. 1541; d. after 6 Apr 1615), a Maid-of-honour to Queen Mary I and later a nun at Rheims
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Marie Pyerres:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p2185.htm#i21842
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Pyerres [1]
2 CONT *F, #21842
2 CONT *Last Edited=23 Jan 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Pyerres was born at France. (Ben M. Angel notes: her father is unknown, but was a Seigneur du Plessis-Baudouin, probably born around 1480 or so... a possible candidate would be Rene Pierres, who married on April 10, 1504, to 
2 CONC Antoinette d'Hommes, daughter of Mathurin d'Hommes and Jeanne de Montbron, but there is apparently no [http://gillesdubois.blogspot.com/2008/07/la-famille-pierres.html record of this particular parentage].
2 CONT
2 CONT She married George Seton, 4th Lord Seton, son of George Seton, 3rd Lord Seton and Lady Janet Hepburn, circa February 1538/39.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also known as Mary Pierre.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT From circa February 1538/39, her married name became Seton.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Mary Pyerres and George Seton, 4th Lord Seton
2 CONT *1. Mary Seton [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Referenced in "The Challenge to the Crown: Volume I: The Struggle for Influence in the ..." by Robert Stedall < [https://books.google.pl/books?id=d3kcAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA142&lpg=PA142&dq=Marie+Pyerres&source=bl&ots=3EN5mJABTx&sig=tVYsO1
2 CONC 3uYjgbrvyzCKFiNcPg7iE&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjt4uLij8DLAhXEE5oKHePxBrkQ6AEIQjAH#v=onepage&q=Marie%20Pyerres&f=false GoogleBooks] >
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary's household was supervised by her French chamberlain, Servais de Conde. Her nine ladies-in-waiting took precedence. These included the "four Maries", promoted from their positions in France as maids-of-honor. Two others were'
2 CONC '' Mary Seton''''s and Mary Bethune's mothers, the former Marie Pyerres and Joanne de la Reyneville, who had been ladies-in-waiting to Mary's (Queens of Scots) mother. Marie Pyerres was now Madame de Briante, having returned to Fr
2 CONC ance to remarry Pierre de Clovis, Seigneur de Briante, in 1554 following Seton's death in 1549. Briante also held a position in the household.
2 CONT
2 CONT (She returned to France widowed for a second time in 1574 to take her brother to court over payment of her jointure. Mary asked Archbishop Bethune to do all he could to help her. She died in France in 1576.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT French-born woman of Rank, and Lady in Waiting to Marie de Guise, Queen Consort of King James V of Scotland, something unusual in the Scots society at the time. The Marie, by which she is often referred, could cause some confusion
2 CONC  as it may well be from the French for a noble servant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Seton,_6th_Lord_Seton
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Pieris
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton married secondly Marie Pieris, a French lady-in-waiting of Mary of Guise after handfasting at Falkland Palace in February 1539.[9] She was the daughter of René Pierres, seigneur du Plessis Baudouin and Antoinette d'Ho
2 CONC mmes. The French Pierres family claimed to have descended from the Percy family of Northumberland.[10] A grant from Mary, Queen of Scots to Marie in 1565 was addressed to "Dame Marie Pier, Lady Seytoun and Brieane."[11] The couple
2 CONC  were given a charter of the lands of Winchburgh and East Niddry on 27 June 1548. Marie's father wrote to Mary of Guise from Le Plessis Badouin in France. An undated letter survives from Marie to Mary of Guise, written from Niddry
2 CONC  Castle, warning her that Lord Seton had heard of a plot by Regent Arran to seize the infant Mary, Queen of Scots.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Payments to "dame Marie Pierre, Lady Seyttoun" occur in the accounts of Mary, Queen of Scots.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT George and Marie had children Robert, James, and Mary Seton, a companion of Mary, Queen of Scots.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 August 1570, Marie and her son Robert were arrested for giving letters for Mary, Queen of Scots, to a messenger, John Moon. They were put on trial in Tolbooth at Edinburgh, on the charge that their letters denied the authori
2 CONC ty of James VI of Scotland and his representatives. They were released on their promise they would not write to Queen Mary again. During the Marian Civil War Marie Pieris stayed in Edinburgh Castle during the "lang siege" and help
2 CONC ed make an inventory of the jewels of Mary, Queen of Scots remaining in the castle.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT * http://thepeerage.com/p2185.htm#i21842 cites 
2 CONT *1. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 1285. Hereinafter cited as Burke's 
2 CONC Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *[9] On 29 August 1570, Marie and her son Robert were arrested for giving letters for Mary, Queen of Scots, to a messenger, John Moon. They were put on trial in Tolbooth at Edinburgh, on the charge that their letters denied the au
2 CONC thority of James VI of Scotland and his representatives. They were released on their promise they would not write to Queen Mary again.[10]
