1 OCCU Scotish nobleman., Governor of Scotland, Regent of Scotland, 2nd Earl of Arran
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===JAMES HAMILTON, DUKE OF CHATELLERAULT===
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Hamilton,_Duke_of_Ch%C3%A2tellerault Wikipedia: James Hamilton, Duke of Châtellerault]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton was the eldest legitimate son of James Hamilton, 1st Earl of Arran. Through his paternal grandmother Mary, Hamilton was the great-grandson of James II of Scotland. On the death of John Stewart, Duke of Albany, in 15
2 CONC 36, Arran became the next heir of the Kingdom of Scotland after the king's descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Regent of Scotland=
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of the immediate royal family proved to be short-lived, so on the death of James V of Scotland in 1542 the Earl of Arran stood next in line to the Scottish throne after the king's six-day-old newborn baby daughter Mar
2 CONC y, Queen of Scots, for whom he was appointed regent. In 1543, supporters of Matthew Stewart, 4th Earl of Lennox, challenged Arran's claim and legitimacy by suggesting that his father's divorce and second marriage were invalid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Initially a Protestant and a member of the pro-English party, in 1543 he was involved in negotiating the marriage of the Queen of Scots to the infant Prince of Wales (the future Edward VI of England). Cardinal Beaton, who favoured
2 CONC  the Auld Alliance, was imprisoned at Dalkeith Palace and then Blackness Castle. Henry VIII of England doubted Arran's commitment to English policy and wanted him deposed. On 18 March 1543, Sir George Douglas of Pittendreich, brot
2 CONC her of the Earl of Angus, told the English ambassador, Ralph Sadler, that;
2 CONT
2 CONT >"if there be any motion now to take the Governor from his state, and to bring the government of this realm to the king of England, I assure you it is impossible to be done at this time. For, there is not so little a boy but that 
2 CONC he will hurl stones against it, and the wives will handle their distaffs, and the commons universally will rather die in it, yea, and many noblemen and all the clergy be fully against it."
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 September 1543 there was panic in Edinburgh when it became known that Arran had quietly left town. Although he had said that he was visiting his pregnant wife at Blackness Castle, the pro-English party guessed he would try to
2 CONC  meet Cardinal Beaton. The Governor and the Cardinal were reconciled at Callendar House. Shortly after, Arran became a Catholic and joined the pro-French faction, consenting to the marriage of the Queen to the French Dauphin, late
2 CONC r Francis II of France, and earning the Duchy of Châtellerault in the process. This led to the seven-year war with England now called the Rough Wooing which was declared on 20 December 1543. The declaration of war was brought by H
2 CONC enry Ray to give to the Parliament of Scotland. Arran replied that the parliament was dissolved, and so he thought it expedient not to answer Henry VIII on the points raised at the time. In 1548 the Queen of Scots went to live in 
2 CONC the French court. For his work on negotiating her marriage, Hamilton was created Duke of Châtellerault, and made a knight of the Order of Saint Michael.
2 CONT
2 CONT =The Duke and the reformation=
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1554, Arran surrendered the regency to Mary of Guise, Queen Mary's mother. Hamilton gave up the Regency on the condition that he would be next in line after the Queen, if she died childless. But the Scottish succession had been
2 CONC  secretly promised to France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first months of the Scottish Reformation Hamilton continued to support Mary of Guise. He faced a Protestant army with the French commander at Cupar Muir in June 1559. He changed his allegiance in August 1559, joining the Pr
2 CONC otestant Lords of the Congregation to oppose the regency of Mary of Guise, and lost his French dukedom as a result. After the death of Guise, Hamilton persuaded the Parliament of Scotland to back a plan to marry his son James to E
2 CONC lizabeth I of England, and then after the death of Francis II of France in 1560 he attempted, without success, to arrange for James to marry the young widowed Queen Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Mary married Lord Darnley in 1565 he withdrew to his estates in France. In 1569, he returned to Scotland and was imprisoned until, in 1573, he agreed to recognize Mary's infant James as King of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT A building from his heyday as Regent survives at Kinneil in West Lothian, his Eastern residence, including carvings and paintings of his heraldry with the collar of Saint Michael.
2 CONT
2 CONT = Marriage and issue=
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton married in 1532, to the Lady Margaret Douglas, daughter of James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Morton, and Catherine Stewart, herself a natural daughter of James IV. His older half-brother James Hamilton of Finnart paid Morton 400
2 CONC 0 marks as part of the marriage settlement. They had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT * James Hamilton, 3rd Earl of Arran
2 CONT * Lady Anne Hamilton – married George Gordon, 5th Earl of Huntly
2 CONT * Lady Jean Hamilton – married Hugh Montgomerie, 3rd Earl of Eglinton
2 CONT * Lady Barbara Hamilton – married first Alexander Gordon, Lord Gordon, then James Fleming, 4th Lord Fleming
2 CONT * John Hamilton, 1st Marquess of Hamilton – married Margaret, daughter of John Lyon, 7th Lord Glamis as her second husband
2 CONT * Lady Margaret Hamilton – married Sir Alexander Pethein (Peden)
2 CONT * Gawain Hamilton – died in infancy
2 CONT * Lady Elizabeth – married George Hamilton
2 CONT * David Hamilton – had three children in 1575.
2 CONT * Claud Hamilton, 1st Lord Paisley – married Margaret, daughter of George Seton, 7th Lord Seton
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Hamilton-1233
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton, 2nd Earl of Arran
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia writes:
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton, Duke of Châtellerault and 2nd Earl of Arran (c. 1516 – 22 January 1575), was a Scottish nobleman and regent for Mary, Queen of Scots.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest legitimate son of James Hamilton, 1st Earl of Arran.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his paternal grandmother Mary of Scotland, Hamilton was the great grandson of James II of Scotland. On the death of John Stewart, Duke of Albany, in 1536, Arran became the next heir of the Kingdom of Scotland after the kin
2 CONC g's immediate family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of the immediate royal family were short-lived, so when James V died in 1542, the Earl of Arran was next in line for the throne ... after the king's six-day-old newborn baby daughter Mary, Queen of Scots, for whom he 
2 CONC was appointed regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1543, supporters of Matthew Stewart, 4th Earl of Lennox, challenged Arran's claim and legitimacy by suggesting that his father's divorce and second marriage were invalid
2 CONT
2 CONT Initially a Protestant and a member of the pro-English party, in 1543 he was involved in negotiating the marriage of the Queen of Scots to the infant Prince of Wales (the future Edward VI of England). Cardinal Beaton, who favoured
2 CONC  the Auld Alliance, was imprisoned at Dalkeith Palace and then Blackness Castle. Henry VIII of England doubted Arran's commitment to English policy and wanted him deposed. On 18 March 1543, Sir George Douglas of Pittendreich, brot
2 CONC her of the Earl of Angus, told the English ambassador, Ralph Sadler, that:
2 CONT
2 CONT     if there be any motion now to take the Governor from his state, and to bring the government of this realm to the king of England, I assure you it is impossible to be done at this time. For, there is not so little a boy but tha
2 CONC t he will hurl stones against it, and the wives will handle their distaffs, and the commons universally will rather die in it, yea, and many noblemen and all the clergy be fully against it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 September 1543 there was panic in Edinburgh when it became known that Arran had quietly left town. Although he said he was visiting his pregnant wife at Blackness Castle, the pro-English party guessed he would try to meet Car
2 CONC dinal Beaton. The Governor and the Cardinal were reconciled at Callendar House.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after, Arran became Catholic and joined the pro-French faction, consenting to the marriage of the Queen to the French Dauphin, later Francis, earning the Duchy of Châtellerault in the process.This led to the seven-year war
2 CONC  with England. Known as the Rough Wooing, it was declared 20 December 1543.
2 CONT
2 CONT The declaration of war was brought by Henry Ray to give to the Parliament of Scotland. Arran replied that parliament was dissolved, so he thought it expedient not to answer Henry VIII on the points raised.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1548 the Queen of Scots went to live in the French court. For his part in negotiating her marriage, Hamilton was created Duke of Châtellerault, and made a knight of the Order of Saint Michael. Titles
2 CONT
2 CONT     btw 26 Mar 1529 and 21 Jul 1529: 2nd Earl of Arran
2 CONT     btw 26 Mar 1529 - 21 Jul 1529: 3rd Lord Hamilton
2 CONT     1542 - 1554: Regent of Scotland
2 CONT     13 Mar 1542/43: Second Person of Scotland and Heir Presumptive of the Crown.
2 CONT     June 1548: Knight, Order of St. Michael of France
2 CONT     08 Feb 1548/9: Duc de Châtellérault [France] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     m. before 23 Sep 1532, Lady Margaret Douglas, daughter of James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Morton, and Catherine Stewart. They had 5 sons and 3 daughters 
2 CONT     Child: James, 3rd Earl of Arran 
2 CONT     Child: Gawain, died young or Gavin - rejected match 
2 CONT     Child: John, 1st Marquess of Hamilton 
2 CONT     Child: David, dsp 1611 
2 CONT     Child: Claud, 1st Lord Paisley 
2 CONT     Child: Lady Barbara, wife of Alexander Gordon and James Fleming 
2 CONT     Child: Lady Jean, wife of Hugh Montgomery 
2 CONT     Child: Lady Anne, wife of George Gordon of Huntly 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia names 2 more daughters, not supported by Richardson or Balfour Paul.
2 CONT
