2 CONT In the entry (in Burke's Peerage) for Lord Claud Hamilton, he is given the title of "First Baron Paisley." This is explained as being granted with the barony of Paisley on 29 July 1587. There is no rank of "Baron" in the Scottish 
2 CONC Peerage; the equivalent to a Baron of the English Peerage is, in Scotland, a Lord of Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a feudal barony of Paisley, but that was incorporated into the Lordship of Paisley which was returned to Lord Claud in 1585 (as it had been returned before in 1573 with the revocation of his previous forfeiture), and was
2 CONC  confirmed in the charter of 29 July 1587. (Prior to the turbulence of the religious wars, as the Commendator of Paisley, (lay Abbot of Paisley), he had been addressed as the Lord of Paisley, as had been his predecessor, his uncle
2 CONC , whose own predecessors in the Abbey had held Paisley as a Lordship - (that is as a unification of a barony with one or more baronies or with other substantial landholdings.)
2 CONT
2 CONT There was also the separate barony of the Burgh of Paisley that, together with the Regality of the Burgh of Paisley, was united with the Lordship of Paisley in 1587 and then confirmed by the charter of 22 March 1591/2 which grante
2 CONC d to Lord Claud Hamilton, Lord of Paisley, the title and honors of a free baron and Lord of Parliament. He then became Lord Paisley, an hereditary peerage title, in addition to being Lord of Paisley, his feudal title.[18] Links
