2 CONT
2 CONT Claud Hamilton, 1st Lord Paisley (1546–1621) was a Scottish politician. He is the ancestor of the earls, marquesses and dukes of Abercorn. (Chisholm 1911) (Henderson 1890) (Holmes 2004)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in 1546 (baptised 9 June), probably at Paisley, Scotland, as the fifth and youngest son of James Hamilton and his wife Margaret Douglas. His father was the 2nd Earl of Arran in Scotland and 1st Duke of Chatelherault in
2 CONC  France. His mother was a daughter of James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Morton.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears below at the bottom of the list of his siblings as the youngest:
2 CONT
2 CONT James (1532–1609), who succeeded him as the 3rd Earl of Arran;
2 CONT Anne (c. 1535–bef. April 1574), who married George Gordon, 5th Earl of Huntly;
2 CONT Jean, who married Hugh Montgomerie, 3rd Earl of Eglinton;
2 CONT Barbara, who married first Alexander Gordon, Lord Gordon, then James Fleming, 4th Lord Fleming;
2 CONT John (1535–1604), who became the 1st Marquess of Hamilton;
2 CONT Margaret, who married Sir Alexander Pethein (Peden);[2]
2 CONT Gawain, who died in infancy;
2 CONT Elizabeth, who married George Hamilton;
2 CONT David, who had three children in 1575.[3]
2 CONT Claud (1546–1621), the subject of this article.
2 CONT Commendator of Paisley
2 CONT His uncle John Hamilton, an illegitimate son of his grandfather, the 1st Earl of Arran, was commendatory abbot of Paisley Abbey around the time of his birth. In 1553 he succeeded David Beaton as Archbishop of St Andrews and agreed
2 CONC  to pass the position as commendator to his nephew Claud, who was then about seven years old.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Scottish politics
2 CONT In March 1560, when he was 14, he was delivered as a hostage to England by the Treaty of Berwick.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1568, he helped Mary, Queen of Scots, to escape from Loch Leven Castle,[6] afterwards fighting for her at the Battle of Langside. His estates having been forfeited because of condemnation, Hamilton was concerned in the murder o
2 CONC f the Regent James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray in 1570, and also in that of the Regent Matthew Stewart, 4th Earl of Lennox in the following year; but in 1573 he recovered his estates.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT This family tree is partly derived from the Abercorn pedigree pictured in Cokayne.[8] Also see the lists of siblings and children in the text.
2 CONT Then in 1579 the privy council decided to arrest both him and his brother, Lord John Hamilton (c. 1535–1604) (afterwards 1st Marquess of Hamilton), to punish them for their past misdeeds; but the brothers escaped to the Kingdom of
2 CONC  England, where Elizabeth I of England used them as pawns in the diplomatic game, and later Claud lived for a short time in France.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to Scotland in 1586 and mixing again in politics, he sought to reconcile James VI of Scotland with his mother; he was in communication with Philip II of Spain in the interests of Mary and the Roman Catholic religion, and
2 CONC  neither the failure of Anthony Babington's plot nor even the defeat of the Spanish Armada put an end to these intrigues.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord of Parliament
2 CONT In 1587 he was created a Scottish Lord of Parliament as Lord Paisley, when the abbey was erected as a barony.[9] With this the Hamilton family gained a second seat in Parliament, the first being held by his elder brother John for 
2 CONC his eldest brother James, during his insanity. This seat in the Scottish Parliament was occupied after his death by his grandson James, the 2nd earl of Abercorn and Lord Paysley became a subsidiary title of the earls, later marque
2 CONC sses and dukes of Abercorn, which was held by the heir apparent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1589 some of his letters were seized and he suffered a short imprisonment, after which he practically disappeared from public life.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT In 1574 he married Margaret Seton, the daughter of George Seton, 7th Lord Seton and his wife, Isabel Hamilton.[10] Among her siblings was George, Master of Seton; Robert Seton, 1st Earl of Winton; Sir John Seton of Barnes, attenda
2 CONC nt to the Earl of Leicester in 1575,[11] Master Carver to Philip II of Spain and Master of Horse to James VI; Alexander Seton, 1st Earl of Dunfermline, Lord Urquhart, Lord Fyvie, and Prior of Pluscarden; and Sir William Seton, who
2 CONC  married Janet Dunbar. Together, Lord Paisley and Margaret were the parents of many children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT James (1575–1618), who became the 1st Earl of Abercorn in 1603;[12]
2 CONT John, who married Johanna Everard, daughter of Levimus Everard;[13]
2 CONT Claud (died 1614), of Shawfield, who was appointed to the Privy Council of Ireland,[14] and whose daughter Margaret married Sir John Stewart of Methven;
2 CONT George (died before 1657) of Greenlaw and Roscrea, who went to live at Derrywoon;[15]
2 CONT Frederick (1590–1647), who built Manorhamilton and served Sweden in the Thirty Years' War;[16]
2 CONT Margaret (died 1623), who married William Douglas, 1st Marquess of Douglas.
2 CONT James, his heir-apparent was styled Master of Paisley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later years and death
2 CONT He suffered from mental illness in his later years. In November 1590 he broke down in tears after reading the Bible and it was thought he would not recover 'in regard of the infirmity haunting and falling on many descended of that
2 CONC  house'.[17] His eldest brother James Hamilton, 3rd Earl of Arran had been suffering from a mental illness since 1562. In 1598 he allowed his son, styled the Master of Paisley, to act on his behalf with regard to all the affairs c
2 CONC oncerning the town.[18] His son predeceased him in 1618. He died in 1621 and was buried in Paisley Abbey.[19][20] He was succeeded by his grandson, James Hamilton, 2nd Earl of Abercorn.
2 CONT
