1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == JEAN STEWART ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ss4tz/stewart14.php Stirnet: Stewart 14]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jean Stewart, here treated, is reported to have died on 16 August 1605, at Stilton in Cambridgeshire, England. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Stewart,_Lady_Bargany Wikipedia]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jean Stewart, here treated, married https://www.geni.com/people/Gilbert-Kennedy-of-Bargany-and-Ardstinchar/6000000219808251839?through=6000000008027616685. They were contracted on 6 January 1596. [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/
2 CONC data/british/kk/kennedy03.php Stirnet: Kennedy 03] She is noticed as Gilbert's widow on 25 March 1602. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD109%2f2939&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=gilbert+
2 CONC Kennedy+of+Bargany&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1475&dt=&di=n NRS: GD109/2939]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Children ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # ''' https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Kennedy-of-Bargany/6000000101415403838?through=6000000008027616685 '''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''26 April 1600: Charter by Gilbert Kennedy of Bargany, and Jean Stewart, his spouse, in fulfillment of contract with Quintin Crawffurd of Camlarg, 26 April, 1600, whereby said Gilbert and Jean dispone to said Quintin, £3 land of
2 CONC  their lands of Newwerk of old extent. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD109%2f656&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=gilbert+Kennedy+of+Bargany&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1550&dt=1600&di=n National Rec
2 CONC ords of Scotland, Papers of the family of Dalrymple-Hamilton of Bargany, Ayrshire, reference GD109/656]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''25 March 1602: Ratification by Jean Stewart, relict of Gilbert Kennedy of Bargany, tutor testamentar of Thomas Kennedy, son and heir, or at least apparent heir of said Gilbert, of promise of tack by him to David Kennedy, younge
2 CONC r in Maxwellistoun, and Agnes Mcalexander, his spouse, of 40s. land of Maxwellistoun, in barony of Bargany for lifetime of said David and Agnes and their heir male. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.a
2 CONC spx?reference=GD109%2f2939&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=gilbert+Kennedy+of+Bargany&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1475&dt=&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the family of Dalrymple-Hamilton of Bargany, Ayrshire, reference GD109/2939]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                      3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''29 June 1620: Signet letters at instance of Thomas Kennedy of Barganie charging Josias Stewart of Bonytoun, now called of Blarquhen, his uncle to compeir and answer for his intromissions with goods and gear of deceased Jean Ste
2 CONC wart, spouse of deceased Gilbert Kennedy of Bargany, father of said Thomas.  [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD109%2f2529&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=gilbert+kennedy+of+bargany&ko=p&r=
2 CONC &ro=s&df=&dt=&di=y National Records of Scotland, Papers of the family of Dalrymple-Hamilton of Bargany, Ayrshire, reference GD109/529]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''22 June 1622: Signet letters at instance of Sir John Hammiltoun of Lettrik, kt., charging Josias Stewart of Bonytoun, now called of Blairquhan, to answer for his intromissions as tutor of Thomas Kennedy of Bargany and as execut
2 CONC or of deceased Jean Stewart, mother of said Thomas, sister of Josias. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD109%2f60&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=jean+stewart&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1650&di=n 
2 CONC National Records of Scotland, Papers of the family of Dalrymple-Hamilton of Bargany, Ayrshire, reference GD109/60]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     5
2 CONT
2 CONT '''24 October 1625: Assignation by Gilbert Fergussoun in Mcclecheistoun, third son of deceased Gilbert Fergussoun of Dalduff, with consent of Mr John Ferguson of Kilkerane, for his interest, to John Hamiltoune, servitor of Sir Joh
2 CONC n Hamiltoune of Lettrick, kt., of contract, 20 May, 1599, between deceased Gilbert Kennedie of Barganie and deceased Jean Stewart, his spouse, on one part, and said deceased Gilbert on other part, whereby said deceased Gilbert Fer
2 CONC gussoun was infeft in annualrent of 80 merks scots from 50s. land of Barbe, parish of Gervane. [Assignation by Gilbert Fergussoun in Mcclecheistoun, third son of deceased Gilbert Fergussoun of Dalduff, with consent of Mr John Ferg
2 CONC uson of Kilkerane, for his interest, to John Hamiltoune, servitor of Sir John Hamiltoune of Lettrick, kt., of contract, 20 May, 1599, between deceased Gilbert Kennedie of Barganie and deceased Jean Stewart, his spouse, on one part
2 CONC , and said deceased Gilbert on other part, whereby said deceased Gilbert Fergussoun was infeft in annualrent of 80 merks scots from 50s. land of Barbe, parish of Gervane. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/det
2 CONC ails.aspx?reference=GD109%2f864&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=jean+stewart&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1650&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the family of Dalrymple-Hamilton of Bargany, Ayrshire, reference GD109/864]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                    6
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2 June 1636: Disposition and assignation by Margaret Blair, youngest daughter, and one of 2 heirs portioners of deceased James Blair, burgess of Air, and Thomas Neveine, fiar of Monkroddeine, her spouse, to Patrick Davidsoun of
2 CONC  Aird and Janet Blair, his spouse, sister of said Margaret, of wadset, 3 November 1601, by deceased Gilbert Kennedie of Bargany and Jean Stewart, his spouse, in favour of said James and Margaret Sinklear, his spouse, of 40s. land 
2 CONC of Nethir Newwork, in return for 2,000 merks scots. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD109%2f668&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=jean+stewart&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1650&di=n National Records 
2 CONC of Scotland, Papers of the family of Dalrymple-Hamilton of Bargany, Ayrshire, reference GD109/668]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Stewart,_Lady_Bargany Biographical Summary by Wikipedia]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jean Stewart, Lady Bargany (c.1577 – 1605) was a Scottish lady in waiting to Anne of Denmark. She was a younger daughter of Andrew, Master of Ochiltree and Margaret Stewart, Mistress of Ochiltree. Jean was a maiden in the house
2 CONC hold of Anne of Denmark and her mother was the senior lady in waiting. Her marriage is of special interest to historians...Jean Stewart fell ill from some sort of fever and went to see the queen's physician Martin Schöner at court
2 CONC  in London, but he gave her no hope and she died at Stilton in Cambridgeshire on her way back to Scotland on 16 August 1605] The chronicle said she died of "eittik" or "hectic", meaning pulmonary consumption. [https://en.wikipedia
2 CONC .org/wiki/Jean_Stewart,_Lady_Bargany Wikipedia]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[After the death of her husband] 'The Lady Bargeny rade to Edinburgh, and made her complent to the king and queen, but was little better, or least but heard; for she was compellit to buy the ward of her son, and to give thirtee
2 CONC n thousand merks for the same.' It is alleged that she afterwards used all the means she could to take the life of Lord Cassillis, in revenge for her husband's death. An ambush was laid for him at Monkton, but getting timely warni
2 CONC ng, he waited for an increase to his retinue, by which he overawed the intending assassins. Lady Bargeny died in 1605, on her way home from London, whither she had gone to consult Dr Martin for 'the eittik' [that is, hectic, meani
2 CONC ng a pulmonary consumption]. Her body was met at Sanquhar by 'the haill friends of the house,' and by them brought ceremonially to Ayr, and placed beside her deceased husband in the church. She had, however, erected a sumptuous to
2 CONC mb to her lord in the church of Ballantrae, and to this the two bodies were transferred with great state, 'the honours and all the rest being preparit very honourably.' By this is meant, a procession bearing the escutcheon, pencil
2 CONC  of honour, sword, helmet, corslet, &c., of the deceased. 'The day being come, there was of noblemen the Earls of Eglinton, Abercorn, and Winton, with the Lords Semple, Cathcart, Loudoun, and Ochiltree, the Lairds of Bombie, Blair
2 CONC whan, and Gairland [Garthland], with ane great number whilk I will not mint [attempt] to express; his honours being borne by the Guidman of Ardmillan, the Guidman of Kirkhill, with sundry mae of the friends; his sister's son, Youn
2 CONC g Auchindrain, bearing the Banner of Revenge, whereon was paintit his portraiture, with all his wounds, with his son sitting at his knees, and this ditty written betwixt his hands: "JUDGE AND REVENGE MY CAUSE, O LORD!" And sae, co
2 CONC nveyit to Ayr, bure all very honourably, to the number of ane thousand horse, of gentlemen, and laid in the foresaid tomb.'- Hist. Ken.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
