1 OCCU Scottish nobleman, 4th Earl of Angus, 4th Earl of Douglas, Ambassador to England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus 
2 CONT * Father 	William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus, b. Abt 1398, Mar, Aberdeenshire, Scotland  
2 CONT * Mother 	Margaret Hay
2 CONT * Died 	12 Mar 1463 
2 CONT * Buried 	Abernethy, Scotland   
2 CONT
2 CONT  The Earls of Angus remained loyal to James II even as their kinsmen, the Black Douglases, were making war on him. George, 4th Earl of Angus led the King's Army against his kinsman, James, 9th Earl of Douglas, at the Battle of Ark
2 CONC inholm in 1455. The result was the end of the Black Douglases. After the battle an act of parliament gave Angus the lordship of Douglas with the original possessions of his ancestors in Douglasdale. The 4th Earl died in 1463.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus married prior to his accession, Isabella Sibbald. They had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus
2 CONT # John Douglas
2 CONT # Anne Douglas, married William Graham, 3rd Lord Graham
2 CONT # Isabella Douglas, married Sir Alexander Ramsay
2 CONT # Elizabeth Douglas, married Sir Robert Graham of Fintry
2 CONT # Margaret Douglas, married sir Duncan Campbell of Glenorchy
2 CONT # Janet Douglas, married 1st Duncan Scott younger of Buccleuch, 2nd George Leslie, 1st Earl of Rothes
2 CONT # Egidia Douglas
2 CONT # Alice Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Douglas,_4th_Earl_of_Angus Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, Lord Douglas, Abernethy and Jedburgh Forest (1429-1462) was a Scottish Nobleman. He was the son of William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus and Margaret Hay of Yester. He succeeded to the Earldom at th
2 CONC e age of 16, following the death of his brother James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Angus in 1446. He was to become the first Red Chief of Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT The chief line of the family were the Earls of Douglas which represented the "Black" line, whereas Angus represented the "Red" Line. Both branches were descended through bastardy to William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas. The Earl of 
2 CONC Douglas descended through Archibald the Grim, an illegitimate son of Sir James Douglas. The 4th Earl of Angus' grandfather was an illegitimate child of William, 1st Earl of Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the representatives of both lines of the House wore the "three stars argent" and "Man's Heart gules" on their arms the 4th Earl of Angus and the 8th Earl of Douglas were third cousins. Angus had closer ties with the House
2 CONC  of Stewart than his Douglas cousins however, being the great-grandson of Robert III of Scotland through his grandmother Princess Mary of Scotland, Countess of Angus. This was to have major implications in the downfall of the Blac
2 CONC k Douglases and the rise of the Red line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglases united
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1448, Angus joined with his kinsmen the Earl of Douglas and his brother Hugh Douglas, Earl of Ormonde on a punitive raid into England and despoiled the countryside as far as Alnwick which they burnt and "come hame wele". T
2 CONC his was in response to attacks led by the Earl of Northumberland and Robert Ogle in which they had burnt Dunbar Castle in the east, and by the Earl of Salisbury who had laid waste to Dumfries in the west. The score was evened in J
2 CONC uly when the Douglases invested Warkworth Castle and "did gret scaith"[1] and the victory over Northumberland at the Battle of Sark.
2 CONT
2 CONT This action in 1448 would be the last time that the two branches of the House of Douglas would act in partnership, and ride together against their hereditary foes of Percy and Neville.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Douglas Rebellion
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1452 the 8th Earl of Douglas was murdered at Stirling Castle, by his monarch James II while under assurances of safe-conduct. Other notable magnates assisted the drunken King James in the act. The whole of Douglasdale rose in r
2 CONC ebellion under the late Earl's younger brothers, James the new 9th Earl of Douglas, his twin Archibald Douglas, Earl of Moray, and the younger Hugh Douglas, Earl of Ormonde, and John Douglas, Lord of Balvenie. During this time of 
2 CONC intermittent internecine strife in Scotland, it would have been expected that Angus would have thrown his lot in with his Douglas cousins. If this had been the case it could have spelled disaster for the House of Stewart. Contempo
2 CONC rary opinion would not have been surprised if he had, but as alluded above the ties of kinship were closer between Angus and King James were close, being first cousins within one degree. A magnate as powerful as Angus could not ab
2 CONC stain from declaring himself either way. So, when the rebellion began in earnest in early 1455, and the King took to the field against the Douglas brothers, Angus came out in full support of his Monarch, and was given high command
2 CONC  within the Royal army. It has been suggested that Angus accompanied the King at the siege of Abercorn. Following the defection of James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton, Douglas' chief lieutenant to the King's side, the Earl of Dougla
2 CONC s fled to England in an attempt to gain support from Henry VI of England. Douglas' brothers stayed in Scotland to continue the struggle. The brothers had appeared in force in Annandale and King James sent Angus to deal with them. 
2 CONC Angus mustered the Border Clans and encountered the rebels on the 18th of May near Langholm. The ensuing Battle of Arkinholm was a rout for the Douglas brothers, Moray was killed and Ormonde captured (to be executed soon after) an
2 CONC d only Balvenie escaped to England. That summer the Black Douglases were attainted and their estates declared forfeit. Angus appended his seal to the charter in token of approval.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ascendancy of the Red Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the acts of attainder Angus received the vast fiefdom and superiority of the Lordship of Douglas, obtaining a charter of confirmation in 1457
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1460, Angus accompanied King James' to the siege of English held Roxburgh Castle. The castle, as well as having been a thorn in the side of successive Scottish monarchs, also contained a garrison loyal to the House of York, Jam
2 CONC es had decided to back the House of Lancaster. On the 3rd of August James' queen, Mary of Gueldres had arrived to inspire enthusiasm amongst the besiegers. Perhaps too much, James overseeing the cannonade to greet his wife, was st
2 CONC anding too close to one of the bombards when it exploded, killing him, and wounding his constant companion Angus. Angus, though wounded, was still able to take command of much of the siege, and the castle was successfully stormed 
2 CONC and taken within the week. Furthermore, he was able to attend the coronation of James III at Kelso Abbey on the 10th of August, where he placed the Crown upon the nine year old King's head, as was his right as Lord Abernethy, appa
2 CONC rently proclaiming: "There! Now that I have set it upon your Grace's head,let me see who will be so bold as to move it."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus' had already become enormously powerful during the reign of James II and during the Regency for his son, his power continued to grow. The Scots parliament had decreed following the forfeiture of the Black Douglases, that no 
2 CONC Douglas should have the hereditary right to the wardenship of the Marches, Angus continued to exercise command over the East and Middle Marches, and was appointed lieutenant of the Realm by the Queen-regent Mary of Gueldres.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following year Angus spent a great deal of time consolidating his estates, placing trusted vassals in charge of the more far flung estates. 1462 saw Angus receive all of the goods lands and rents of the adherents of the for
2 CONC feited Douglases in Roxburghshire, apart from those already owned by his brother William Douglas of Cluny.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year Angus was involved in negotiations with the Lancastrian Henry VI of England. Henry had obtained a bond from Angus to aid him with "a stipulated force for the recovery of that realm [England] from Henry's rebels an
2 CONC d enemies". For Henry's part he promised Angus, on the recovery of his kingdom "to make the saide erle sufficiently and suerly after the lawes of England a duke withynne the said reavme of England, with stile, astate, honure and n
2 CONC ame of a duke", the dukedom was to come with a castle and land to the value of 2000 marks.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus was unable to pursue his plans for an English dukedom, when he died on the 11th of March 1463 at Abernethy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices held
2 CONT
2 CONT * Angus was a commissioner sent to treat with the English twice, in 1449 and in 1459.
2 CONT * Ambassador to England in 1451
2 CONT * Warden of the East and Middle Marches 1455-1462
2 CONT * Peerage of Scotland: Preceded by James Douglas	Earl of Angus 1446-1462	Succeeded by Archibald Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT Refs
2 CONT # Maxwell, Sir Herbert. A History of the House of Douglas. Freemantle, London 1902
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The chief line of the family were the Earls of Douglas which represented the "Black" line, whereas Angus represented the "Red" Line. Both branches were descended through bastardy to William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas. The Earl of 
2 CONC Douglas descended through Archibald the Grim, an illegitimate son of Sir James Douglas. The 4th Earl of Angus' grandfather was an illegitimate child of William, 1st Earl of Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the representatives of both lines of the House wore the "three stars argent" and "Man's Heart gules" on their arms the 4th Earl of Angus and the 8th Earl of Douglas were third cousins. Angus had closer ties with the House
2 CONC  of Stewart than his Douglas cousins however, being the great-grandson of Robert III of Scotland through his grandmother Princess Mary of Scotland, Countess of Angus. This was to have major implications in the downfall of the Blac
2 CONC k Douglases and the rise of the Red line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources 
2 CONT # [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft#page/279/mode/1up Scots Peerage.] James Balfour Paul.  Publication: 1904 - 1914 Vol. 7, page 279
