1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====From Darryl Lundy's [http://thepeerage.com/p10956.htm#i109555 Peerage page on Isabel Sibbald]
2 CONT * '''Last Edited=2 Jan 2020'''
2 CONT * '''Isabel Sibbald''' was the daughter of Sir John Sibbald.1 She married, firstly, George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, son of William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus and Margaret Hay, before 1446.1 
2 CONT * She married, ''secondly'', John Carmichael, 1st of Balmedie, son of John Carmichael of Meadowflat, before 1477.1 
2 CONT * She married, ''thirdly'', Sir Robert Douglas of Lochleven, son of Sir Henry Douglas of Lochleven and Elizabeth Erskine, after 1479.2 She died before 21 February 1497.3,4
2 CONT * From before 1446, her married name became Douglas. After her marriage, Isabel Sibbald was styled as Countess of Angus before 9 September 1446. From before 1477, her married name became Carmichael.1
2 CONT * '''Children of Isabel Sibbald and George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus—'''
2 CONT ** Lady Anne Douglas5
2 CONT ** Lady Janet Douglas+6 d. 1494
2 CONT ** Margaret Douglas+7
2 CONT ** Lady Elizabeth Douglas+
2 CONT ** John Douglas7 d. a May 1471
2 CONT ** Isabella Douglas+7
2 CONT ** Egidia Douglas7
2 CONT ** Alison Douglas7
2 CONT ** Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus+2 b. c 1453, d. bt 29 Nov 1513 - 31 Jan 1514
2 CONT * '''Children of Isabel Sibbald and John Carmichael 1st of Balmedie:—'''
2 CONT ** James Carmichael, 2nd of Balmedie+8
2 CONT ** Peter Carmichael of Dron 8 d. b 30 May 1545
2 CONT ** Elizabeth Carmichael+8
2 CONT * '''Footnotes'''
2 CONT ** [1] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, etc., editors. (2000) ''The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct or Dormant'', new ed.  (1910-1959; reprint. Gloucester, U.K.
2 CONC : Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), Vol 1, page 155.
2 CONT ** [2] Cokayne, and others, ''The Complete Peerage'', volume I, page 156.
2 CONT ** [3] Cokayne, and others, ''The Complete Peerage'', volume I, page 155, says 1500-1503.
2 CONT ** [4] BP2003 volume 1, page 695. 
2 CONT ** [5] Clan MacFarlane and associated clans genealogy, online http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info
2 CONT ** [6] BP1999 volume 1, page 1281. 
2 CONT ** [7] Paul, Sir James Balfour. ''The Scots Peerage: founded on Wood's edition of Sir Robert Douglas's The Peerage of Scotland'' (Edinburgh, Scotland: David Douglas, 1904), Vol. 1, p. 178.
2 CONT ** [8] BP2003
2 CONT '''———————————————————————————————————————'''
2 CONT ====BOOKS
2 CONT * '''Anderson, Rev John. 1904.''' “Douglas, Earl of Angus.” In The Scots Peerage; Founded on Wood’s Edition of Sir Robert Douglas’s Peerage of Scotland; Containing an Historical and Genealogical Account of the Nobility of That Kin
2 CONC gdom, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, 174–213. Edinburgh, Scotland: David Douglas. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/190/mode/1up?view=theater Archive.org], pp. 176-178:— 
2 CONT
2 CONT ** '''IV. GEORGE, fourth Earl of Angus,''' who succeeded, was the brother of the third Earl, and not his uncle, as some writers, including Wood, have asserted, Godscroft's testimony on the point, though supported by evidence, havi
2 CONC ng been ignored in the printed editions of his works (Ms. History, ii. 39, and see ’'Douglas Book’', ii. 46, for other proofs)…..[&c.]
2 CONT ** '''George, fourth Earl of Angus, married Isabella, only daughter of Sir John Sibbald of Balgony''', in Fife, Master of the Household to King James II. The date of the marriage has not been ascertained, but Godscroft asserts it 
2 CONC took place in the lifetime of James, third Earl, or before 1445. She survived the Earl for many years, but was dead before February 1503. She seems to have married twice after the Earl's death — her second husband being a person n
2 CONC amed Carmichael, whom Godscroft says was a younger brother of Carmichael of that Ilk, though he discredits the story for want of evidence, but a James Carmichael and Peter Carmichael of Dron are named as brothers of the fifth Earl
2 CONC  of Angus (The Douglas Book, iii. 146, 183), and Peter Carmichael appears as executor or intromitter with the estate of Elizabeth or Isabella, Countess of Angus, in February 1503, after her death (’'Acta Dominorum Concilii’', MS.,
2 CONC  xii. 184). The Countess's third husband was Sir Robert Douglas of Lochleven, and after 1489 he is frequently named along with her as a litigant before the Lords of Council. 
2 CONT ** '''By his wife, George, fourth Earl of Angus, had issue:—'''
2 CONT *** 1. ARCHIBALD, who succeeded as fifth Earl of Angus.
2 CONT *** 2. John, who is named in May 1471 in an action by his mother against her brother-in-law, William Douglas of Cluny, for withholding her terce (''Acta Auditorum'', 13, 14), but no other notice of him has been found.
2 CONT *** 3. Anne, who is said to have married William, second Lord Graham, ancestor of the Marquesses and Dukes of Montrose, and had issue.
2 CONT *** 4. Isabella, who, about 1464, was married to Sir Alexander Ramsay of Dalhousie, and had issue.
2 CONT *** 5. Elizabeth, who, about 1476, was married to Robert Graham of Fintry (''The Douglas Book'', iii. 107), and had issue. From her the present family of Fintry descend.
2 CONT *** 6. Margaret, who, about 1479, was married to Sir Duncan Campbell of Glenorchy, ancestor of the Earls and Marquesses of Breadalbane, and had issue (''Ibid''., 110).
2 CONT *** 7. Jane or Janet, who, in 1472, was married to David Scott, younger of Buccleuch, who seems to have died about 1484, and had issue. In or about October 1492 she became the wife of George, second Earl of Rothes, without issue. 
2 CONC She died in 1494 (''Acta Dominorum Concilii'', 293, 370. The son referred to on p. 293 was apparently her son by David Scott).
2 CONT *** 8. Egidia or Giles, and
2 CONT *** 9. Alison. These two daughters are named in 1471 and 1476 (''Acta Auditorum'', 13, 14; ''The Douglas Book'' iii. 107), but no other notice of them has been found.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Cokayne, George Edward. 1910.''' '’The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct, or Dormant'’. Edited by Vicary Gibbs. 2nd ed. Vol. 1 AB-Adam to Basing. London: The
2 CONC  St. Catharine Press. [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015066332571&seq=197 Bable.HathiTrust], p. 155-6:—
2 CONT ** “GEORGE (DOUGLAS), EARL OF ANGUS [S], br., though often supposed to be uncle,[fn] and h. He was one of the Commissioners to conclude a treaty with England, 1449 and 1459, and was AMBASSADOR there, 1451. He adhered to James II d
2 CONC uring the Douglas rebellion in 1454, and was rewarded with large grants of lands of that family. He was accounted the head of the party known as the "Old Lords" during the minority (1460) of James III. When Henry VI was a fugitive
2 CONC  in Scotland in 1461, he engaged to give the Earl lands of the yearly value of 2000 marks, and to erect them into a Dukedom. Early in 1462 he obtained a victory over the English at Alnwick. He d., probably before 1446, Isabel, onl
2 CONC y da. of SIR JOHN SIBBALD, of Balgony, co. Fife. He d. 14 Nov. 1462, or, according to his son's retour, 12 Mar. 1462/3, and was bur. at Abernethy. His widow m., 2ndly, in or before 1477, JOHN CARMICHAEL, of Balmedie. She m., 3rdly
2 CONC , after 1479, as his and wife, SIR ROBERT DOUGLAS, of Lochleven, who was slain at Flodden, 9 Sep. 1513. She d. between 1500 and Feb. 1502/3.”
2 CONT ***[fn] “That statement is refuted by Godscroft's MS., and by a document, dated 26 June 1450, at Durham, in which appears the ‘''Relaxatio Willielmi Douglas, comitis de Angus, patris comitis moderni''’” – tr., easing, relaxation, 
2 CONC or mitigation) of William Douglas, Earl of Angus, father of the modern Earl – “and by other proofs in Fraser's ''Book of Douglas'', vol. ii. pp. 45-6.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Scotland, Red Books of Scotland'', 3rd ed. “SIBBALD OF BALGONY”. [https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/62525/images/redbookofscotlandvolume8osw-smapdf2-01025?pId=56357 Ancestry.com], pp. 1010-11:—
2 CONT * SIBBALD, the presumed ancestor and eponymous of this family, appears as a witness to several late 12th century charters concerning St. Andrews Abbey. "Walter filius Sybbald" and "Walter filius Walter Sybald" are both a witness t
2 CONC o a charter by Thomas de Lundin to the Monks of Arbroath for wood from his lands of Crostach, confirmed by King William I. (d. 1214). Thomas Sibalde, no doubt their descendant, was, with other several notable other Fife landholder
2 CONC s, a member of an assise held at Berwick on 25 August 1296 which determined that Elena la Suchis died seised in the third part of the lands of Dyasrt and Strathon. It is probable that he was ancestor of:
2 CONT * SIR THOMAS SIBBALD, LORD OF BALGONIE, was a member of an inquest at Cupar on 18 January 1385 (GD82/7) and had a charter from George, Earl of March, for the lands of Inverleven on 9 May 1396 (Wemyss Charters). He was alive in 140
2 CONC 2 and had d. by 1 June 1403 (Ex. Rolls. Vol. II. P. 580) having had issue:
2 CONT ** 1. Duncan Sibbald of Balgonie, (see below).
2 CONT ** 2. Thomas Sibbald, to whom his father granted a charter for the lands of Gagy. He is presumed to be ancestor of the families of Sibbald of Over Rankeilour and Letham (GD188/21/2).
2 CONT ** 3. Anna Sibbald, m. Peter Balfour of Dovan, and had issue.
2 CONT * DUNCAN SIBBALD, YOUNGER OF BALGONIE, who, as eldest son of Sir Thomas Sibbald, ratified his father's grant of the lands of Gagy to his brother Thomas. He m. Cristina, daughter of Angus of Gagy with whom he had a charter for the 
2 CONC lands of Mill of Gagy (GD188/21/2), and appears to have d. in the lifetime of his father. He had issue,
2 CONT ** 1. Sir John Sibbald of Balgonie, (see below).
2 CONT ** 2. William Sibbald, is styled brother to sir John Sibbald in a discharge of 1425 (GD188/21/2).
2 CONT * SIR JOHN SIBBALD OF BALGONIE, is first named in 1403/04 (Ex. Rolls Vol. IV. P. 397) and had a charter under the Great Seal for the lands of Ward, Rossyve and Crambeth on 25 January 1413. He was father of:
2 CONT * SIR JOHN SIBBALD OF BALGONIE, succeeded his father by 8 May 1440 when he was party to an agreement by Sir Robert Erskine and had a charter under the Great Seal for the lands of Balgonie on 11 February 1446 (GD26/3/10). He '''m.'
2 CONC '' ''firstly'', to Johanna, daughter of Sir Alexander Livingston of Calendar, (Dispensation dated 26 January 1430 [SHS Scottish Supplications to Rome 1428-1432. P. 74. It is included in the dispensation that they had already consu
2 CONC mmated their marriage and had children]), by whom he had issue; '''and''' ''secondly'', to Marjory, daughter of John Boswell of Balmutto, (she was widow of John de Arnot of that Ilk, who had been k. in 1440). He is styled deceased
2 CONC  in 1454 and his lands of Balgonie to be then in ward with Andrew Lundy and was father of:
2 CONT ** 1. ELIZABETH SIBBALD, m. before 10 July 1471, to Andrew Lundy (¿ https://www.geni.com/people/Andrew-Lundy-of-Balgony/6000000044847265843 ?) with whom she had a charter under the Great Seal for the lands of Balgonie on 7 March 1
2 CONC 483 (GD26/3/4). They had issue, (see Lundy of Balgony). 
2 CONT ** 2. Isobel Sibbald, m. firstly, before July of 1466, to George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, and had issue; secondly, to John Carmichael of Balmeadie, and thirdly, by 11 October 1487, to Robert Douglas of Lochleven.
2 CONT **['''Note:''' Whether Red Books edition 5 includes of more children finds answer from one having access to the much enlarged edition. Until someone peruses the volumes, proof that https://www.geni.com/people/Andrew-Sibbald-Sherif
2 CONC f-of-Fife/6000000026832783902 was a provable son of this couple is unknown.]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''———————————————————————————————————————'''
2 CONT '''Wikitree: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Sibbald-12 Isabel (Sibbald) Sibbald Countess of Angus (abt. 1418– bef. 1503)]''' — Defers to Lundy's Peerage above.
2 CONT '''———————————————————————————————————————'''
