1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Lindsay,_4th_Earl_of_Crawford
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford acceded 1446, died September 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: David Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Crawford Mother: Marjory Ogilvie
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Margaret Dunbar and had 3 children, Elizabeth, David 5th Earl born 1440 and Alexander the 7th Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as "the Tiger Earl", Alexander was one of the most powerful of the Scottish nobles. For some time he was in arms against King James II as part of the Douglas rebellion. In February of 1452, William Douglas, 8th Earl of Dougl
2 CONC as was personally killed at Stirling Castle by James II for refusing to dissolve his league with Alexander. The Tiger Earl was defeated at the Battle of Brechin on May 18, and he submitted to James II in 1452.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend has it that he is the imfamous "Earl Beardie" featured in one of the myths of Glamis Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From thePeerage.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford was the son of David Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Crawford and Marjory Ogilvy. He married Margaret Dunbar, daughter of Sir David Dunbar of Cockburn. He died in September 1453 at Finhaven Castle, Ang
2 CONC us, Scotland. He was buried at Grey Friars Church, Dundee, Angus, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford also went by the nick-name of 'Earl Beardie'. Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford also went by the nick-name of 'the Tiger'. He held the office of Hereditary Sheriff of Aberdeen between 
2 CONC 1446 and 1452. On 13 January 1445/46 at Arbroath, Angus, Scotland, he was the victor of the bloody fray in which his father was fatally wounded. He succeeded to the title of 4th Earl of Crawford [S., 1398] on 17 January 1445/46. H
2 CONC e held the office of Scottish Ambassador to England in 1451. In 1452 he rebelled against the King, after the murder of Douglas by the King. He fought in the Battle of Brechin on 18 May 1452, where he was defeated by the Earl of Hu
2 CONC ntly, and was declared forfeit. He held the office of Guardian of the Marches in 1453. In April 1453 he was restored.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford and Margaret Dunbar
2 CONT
2 CONT *     Lady Elizabeth Lindsay+ d. a 22 Nov 1509
2 CONT *     David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose+ b. 1440, d. 25 Dec 149
2 CONT *     Alexander Lindsay of Auchtermonzie, 7th Earl of Crawford+ b. c 1443, d. May 1517
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Lindsay,_2nd_Earl_of_Crawford
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 2nd Earl of Crawford
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 2nd Earl of Crawford (c. 1387 - 1438), was a Scottish magnate. He was the son of David Lindsay, 1st Earl of Crawford and Elizabeth Stewart, daughter of King Robert II and Euphemia de Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded in the Earldom of Crawford by David Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Crawford.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Information on his connection to Robert II here:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://contentdm.lib.byu.edu/u?/FH29,21169
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knighted at the coronation of King James in 1424, and was a hostage for the King, being detained at the Tower of London, York, and Pontefract 1424-27.  He was ambassador to England 1430-31. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alexander was one of the most powerful of the Scottish nobles; for some time he was in arms against King James II as part of the Douglas rebellion, but he submitted in 1452, after the Battle of Brechin.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Lindsay,_4th_Earl_of_Crawford
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford acceded 1446, died September 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: David Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Crawford Mother: Marjory Ogilvie
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Margaret Dunbar and had 3 children, Elizabeth, David 5th Earl born 1440 and Alexander the 7th Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as "the Tiger Earl", Alexander was one of the most powerful of the Scottish nobles. For some time he was in arms against King James II as part of the Douglas rebellion. In February of 1452, William Douglas, 8th Earl of Dougl
2 CONC as was personally killed at Stirling Castle by James II for refusing to dissolve his league with Alexander. The Tiger Earl was defeated at the Battle of Brechin on May 18, and he submitted to James II in 1452.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend has it that he is the imfamous "Earl Beardie" featured in one of the myths of Glamis Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From thePeerage.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford was the son of David Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Crawford and Marjory Ogilvy. He married Margaret Dunbar, daughter of Sir David Dunbar of Cockburn. He died in September 1453 at Finhaven Castle, Ang
2 CONC us, Scotland. He was buried at Grey Friars Church, Dundee, Angus, Scotland. Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford also went by the nick-name of 'Earl Beardie'. Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford also went by the nick-name o
2 CONC f 'the Tiger'. He held the office of Hereditary Sheriff of Aberdeen between 1446 and 1452. On 13 January 1445/46 at Arbroath, Angus, Scotland, he was the victor of the bloody fray in which his father was fatally wounded. He succee
2 CONC ded to the title of 4th Earl of Crawford [S., 1398] on 17 January 1445/46. He held the office of Scottish Ambassador to England in 1451. In 1452 he rebelled against the King, after the murder of Douglas by the King. He fought in t
2 CONC he Battle of Brechin on 18 May 1452, where he was defeated by the Earl of Huntly, and was declared forfeit. He held the office of Guardian of the Marches in 1453. In April 1453 he was restored. Children of Alexander Lindsay, 4th E
2 CONC arl of Crawford and Margaret Dunbar
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lady Elizabeth Lindsay+ d. a 22 Nov 1509
2 CONT * David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose+ b. 1440, d. 25 Dec 1495
2 CONT * Alexander Lindsay of Auchtermonzie, 7th Earl of Crawford+ b. c 1443, d. May 1517
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Information on his connection to Robert II here:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://contentdm.lib.byu.edu/u?/FH29,21169
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knighted at the coronation of King James in 1424, and was a hostage for the King, being detained at the Tower of London, York, and Pontefract 1424-27. He was ambassador to England 1430-31.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was one of the most powerful of the Scottish nobles; for some time he was in arms against King James II as part of the Douglas rebellion, but he submitted in 1452, after the Battle of Brechin.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Not married to Margery Stewart 1345-1435. No valid source documents found for marriage to Margery Stewart. Margery Stewart daughter of John Stewart and Alicia Muir was born C 1345.
2 CONT
