2 CONT ________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tumbardine vs. Tullibardine'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Numerous websites identify the location of various Murrays as being "Tumbardine" rather than "Tullibardine."  This however seems clearly to be wrong.
2 CONT
2 CONT First, there is no place named "Tumbardine."  Rather, this misspelling seems to be the result of an incorrect  transcription of books by OCR software.  This can easily be seen by doing a search in Google books for "Tumbardine."  [
2 CONC http://www.google.com/search?tbm=bks&hl=en&q=Tumbardine+murray&btnG=#hl=en&tbm=bks&sclient=psy-ab&q=Tumbardine&oq=Tumbardine&gs_l=serp.3...55938.55938.2.56446.1.1.0.0.0.0.105.105.0j1.1.0...0.0...1c.1.11.psy-ab._EYy4-GbSSk&pbx=1&ba
2 CONC v=on.2,or.r_qf.&bvm=bv.45921128,d.eWU&fp=8d87c7cf9fd17a27&biw=1211&bih=790 That search returns a list] of some 18 books.   Most of those can be visually inspected online.  Without exception, the highlighted words of the search for
2 CONC  "Tumbardine" are actually always spelled "Tullibardine" or "Tullibardin."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tullibardine Castle no longer exists, although [http://www.stravaiging.com/history/castle/tullibardine-castle its site can be seen], but a proposed drawing (for a later reconstruction?) of it can be seen at [http://www.scotlan
2 CONC dsplaces.gov.uk/search_item/index.php?service=RCAHMS&id=26105 ScotlandsPlaces.go.uk].
