1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''James IV Stewart, King of Scots'''  was born on 17 March 1473 at Stirling Castle, Scotland and died on  9 September 1513 at The Battle of Flodden Field, Northumberland, England.  His body was lost but his head i
2 CONC s buried at St Michael Wood Street, London England. He was also known as Seumas IV Stiùbhairt, James, Duke of Rothesay,
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruled:  11 June 1488 - 9 September 1513
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by:  James III     3 August 1460 - 11 June 1488
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded by: James V    11 June 1488-14 December 1542
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation: 24 June 1488 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Basics==
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of  James III  (10 July 1451 – 11 June 1488) and Margaret of Denmark  (23 June 1456 – before 14 July 1486)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # Margaret Tudor on 8 August 1503 at Holyrood Abbey, Edinburgh (Henry VII of England's daughter)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT #James, Duke of Rothesay (21 February 1507, Holyrood Palace – 27 February 1508, Stirling Castle)
2 CONT #A stillborn daughter at Holyrood Palace on 15 July 1508.
2 CONT #Arthur, Duke of Rothesay (20 October 1509, Holyrood Palace – Edinburgh Castle, 14 July 1510).
2 CONT #James V (Linlithgow Palace, 15 April 1512 – Falkland Palace, Fife, 14 December 1542), the only one to reach adulthood, and the successor of his father.
2 CONT #A second stillborn daughter at Holyrood Palace in November 1512.
2 CONT #Alexander, Duke of Ross (Stirling Castle, 30 April 1514 – Stirling Castle, 18 December 1515), born after James's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Illegitimate  children with Marion Boyd: 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Alexander (c.1493 – Battle of Flodden Field, 9 September 1513), Archbishop of St Andrews.
2 CONT #Catherine Stewart(c. 1494 – 1554), who married James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Illegitimate children with Margaret Drummond: 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Margaret Stewart (born around 1497)
2 CONT
2 CONT Illegitimate children with Janet Kennedy:
2 CONT
2 CONT #James (before 1499 – 1544), created Earl of Moray. And two children who died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Illegitimate children with Isabel Buchan
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Lady Janet Stewart (before 1510 – 20 February 1562)
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT James IV (17 March 1473 – 9 September 1513) was King of Scots from 11 June 1488 to his death. He is generally regarded as the most successful of the Stewart monarchs of Scotland, but his reign ended with the disastrous defeat at t
2 CONC he Battle of Flodden Field, where he became the last monarch from Great Britain to be killed in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT James IV was the son of James III and Margaret of Denmark, probably born in Stirling Castle. As heir apparent to the Scottish crown, he became Duke of Rothesay. His father was not a popular king and faced two major rebellions duri
2 CONC ng his reign. During the second rebellion, the rebels set up the 15-year-old James as their nominal leader. His father was killed fighting the rebels at the Battle of Sauchieburn on 11 June 1488, and James took the throne and was 
2 CONC crowned at Scone on 24 June. When he realized the indirect role which he had played in the death of his father, he decided to do penance for his sin. From that date on, he wore a heavy iron chain cilice around his waist, next to t
2 CONC he skin, each Lent as penance, adding every year extra ounces.
2 CONT
2 CONT James IV quickly proved to be an effective ruler. He defeated another rebellion in 1489, took a direct interest in the administration of justice and finally brought the Lord of the Isles under control in 1493. For a time, he suppo
2 CONC rted Perkin Warbeck, the pretender to the English throne, and carried out a brief invasion of England on his behalf in 1496. However, he recognized that peace between Scotland and England was in the interest of both countries, and
2 CONC  established good diplomatic relations with England, at that time emerging from a period of civil war, and in 1502 signed the Treaty of Perpetual Peace with Henry VII. He also saw the importance in building a fleet that could prov
2 CONC ide Scotland with a strong maritime presence. James founded two new dockyards for the purpose and acquired a total of 38 ships for the Royal Scottish Navy, including the Margaret, and the carrack Michael or Great Michael. The latt
2 CONC er, built at great expense at Newhaven and launched in 1511, was 240 feet (73 m) in length, weighed 1,000 tons and was, at that time, the largest ship in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT James IV was a true Renaissance prince with an interest in practical and scientific matters. He granted the Edinburgh College of Surgeons a royal charter in 1506, turned Edinburgh Castle into one of Britain's foremost gun foundrie
2 CONC s, and welcomed the establishment of Scotland's first printing press in 1507. He built a part of Falkland Palace and Great Halls at Stirling and Edinburgh castles, and furnished his palaces with tapestries.[2] James was a patron o
2 CONC f the arts, including many literary figures, most notably the Scots makars whose diverse and socially observant works convey a vibrant and memorable picture of cultural life and intellectual concerns in the period. Figures associa
2 CONC ted with his court include William Dunbar, Walter Kennedy and Gavin Douglas who made the first complete translation of Virgil's Aeneid in northern Europe. His reign also saw the passing of the makar Robert Henryson.
2 CONT
2 CONT James IV was well educated and a fluent linguist. In 1499 the Spanish envoy Pedro de Ayala reported that he was able to "speak Latin, French, German, Flemish, Italian and the barbarian Gaelic, the native tongue of nearly all his s
2 CONC ubjects".  He was the last King of Scots who is known to have spoken Scottish Gaelic. James is one of the rulers reported to have conducted a language deprivation experiment, sending two children to be raised by a mute woman alone
2 CONC  on an island, to determine if language was learned or innate.  At Stirling Castle, James maintained an alchemical workshop with a furnace of the quintessence.
2 CONT
2 CONT When war broke out between England and France as a result of the Italian Wars, James found himself in a difficult position as his obligations under the Auld Alliance with France conflicted with the treaty made with England in 1502
2 CONC . The new king of England, Henry VIII, attempted to invade France in 1513, and James reacted by declaring war on England. Hoping to take advantage of Henry's absence, he led an invading army southward, only to be killed, with many
2 CONC  of his nobles and common soldiers, at the disastrous Battle of Flodden Field on 9 September, ending Scotland's involvement in the War of the League of Cambrai. A body, thought to be his, was recovered from the battlefield and tak
2 CONC en to London for burial. Because he was excommunicated, the embalmed body lay unburied for many years in the monastery of Sheen in Surrey, and was lost after the Reformation. John Stow claimed to have seen the body, and said the k
2 CONC ing's head (with red hair) was removed by a glazier and eventually buried at St Michael Wood Street.  James's bloodstained coat was sent to Henry VIII (then on campaign in France) by his queen Catherine of Aragon.  Rumors persiste
2 CONC d that James had survived and had gone into exile, but there is no evidence to support them.
2 CONT
