1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===WILLIAM RUTHVEN, EARL OF GOWRIE===
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Williame Maister of Ruthuen (1560) William Master of Ruthven (1585)'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''FAKE GENEALOGY'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''First'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''At least one pedigree published by MyHeritage falsely claims that William Ruthven, Earl of Gowrie, married Mary Gray, daughter of Patrick Gray, Lord Gray, and Marion of Ogilvy. Williiam and Mary were contacted to ma
2 CONC rry, probably when they were children, but the marriage did not actually take place. The same pedigree makes a second false claim, that Mary Gray also married George Seton, fifth Lord Seton, when she did in fact marry David Seton 
2 CONC of Parbroath. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04paul/page/262/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 262] The fake pedigree published by MyHeritage follows:'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Hon. Mary Ruthven (born Gray), 1547 - 1600. Mary Ruthven (born Gray) was born in 1547, at birth place, to Patrick (Lord Gray Airlie) Gray and Marion Gray (born Ogilvy). Patrick was born Circa March 1508, in Butterga
2 CONC sk, Perth, Perthshire, Scotland. Marion was born in 1514, in Airlie, Angus, Scotland. Mary had 16 siblings: Elizabeth Helen Bruce (born Gray), Isabel Strethan (born Gray) and 14 other siblings. Mary married George (5th Lord of Set
2 CONC on) Baron The Sir Seton, 7th Lord Seton. George was born in January 1531, in Of Seton, Tranent, East Lothian, Scotland. His occupation was Master of the Household to Queen Mary of Scots 1563, waggoner, Scottish Ambassador to Franc
2 CONC e 1583. They had 3 children: George SEATON and 2 other children. Mary married William Ruthven in 1565, at age 18 at marriage place. William was born in 1548, in Ruthven Castle, Perthshire, Scotland. His occupation was 1st Earl of 
2 CONC Gowrie. Mary passed away in 1600, at age 53 at death place. [https://www.myheritage.com/names/mary_ruthven MyHeritage]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Second'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''A second pedigree published by MyHeritage falsely claims that Mary Gray, daughter of Patrick Gray, Lord Gray, and his wife Marion Ogilvy, married William Ruthven, Lord Ruthven. William and Mary were contracted to ma
2 CONC rry, probably when they were children, but the marriage did not take place. This fake pedigree goes further, and  falsely claims that William Ruthven and Mary Gray had a son named William Ruthven. This is wrong. [https://archive.o
2 CONC rg/details/scotspeeragefoun04paul/page/262/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 262] The fake pedigree published by MyHeritage follows:'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Mary Seaton (born Gray), 1540 - 1600. Mary Seaton (born Gray) was born in 1540, at birth place, to Patrick Gray and Marion Gray (born Ogilvy). Patrick was born in February 1508, in Buttergask, Perthshire, Scotland. 
2 CONC Marion was born in 1514, in Airlie, Angus, Scotland. Mary had 9 siblings: Patrick Gray, Katherine Mortimer (born Gray) and 7 other siblings. Mary married David Seton of Parbroath. David was born circa 1535, in Of, Parbroath, Fife,
2 CONC  Scotland. His occupation was Comptroller of Scotland 1589. Mary married William Ruthven. William was born in 1528, in Ruthven, Aberdeenshire, Scotland. They had one son: Williame Ruthven. Mary passed away in 1600, at age 60 at de
2 CONC ath place. [https://www.myheritage.com/names/mary_ruthven MyHeritage]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT ===The Question of Identity===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Ruthven, Earl of Gowrie, here treated, is the second son of Patrick Ruthven, Lord Ruthven, and his wife Lady Janet Douglas. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04paul/page/262/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 262
2 CONC ]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Proposed Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William, Earl of Gowrie, here treated, was contacted to marry Mary Gray, daughter of Patrick, Lord Gray, but the marriage did not take place. [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun04paul#page/263/mode/1up The Scots Peerag
2 CONC e IV: 263]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William, Earl of Gowrie, was tried and found guilty of treason, and beheaded at Stirling on 4 May 1584. He had been the principal actor in the kidnap of the young King James VI, afterwards James I King of Great Britain, in what
2 CONC  became known as the "Raid of Ruthven".Thereafter he took part in a plot to seize Stirling Castle. [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun04paul#page/263/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 263]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William, Earl of Gowrie, here treated, married Dorothea Stewart, daughter of Henry, Lord Methven.  [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun04paul#page/263/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 263]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
