1 NAME Robert /Bruce/ Minister of the Gospel at St. Giles
2 GIVN Robert
2 SURN Bruce
2 NSFX Minister of the Gospel at St. Giles
2 _MARNM Bruce
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1554
1 DEAT
2 DATE 27 JUL 1631
1 TITL Reverend Mr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == REVEREND MR. ROBERT BRUCE ==
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Minister of the Gospel at St. Giles in Edinburgh===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 March 1581-82: Instrument of Redemption by Robert Bruce of Baldrig, brother to Sir Alexander Bruce of Airth, kt., to the said Sir Alexander, of an annualrent of 300 merks furth of the lands of Stanehous, Crawnestis and Inches
2 CONC , in the baronie of Harbertachyre on payment of 3000 merks by Mr. Robert Bruce, son of the said Sir Alexander, acting as his procurator. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD37%2f71&st=1
2 CONC &tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=baldrig&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1550&dt=1600&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Bruce family of Airth, reference GD37/71]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT <strong>Robert Bruce</strong>, born 1554, second son of Sir Alexander B. of Airth and Janet, daugh. of Lord Livingston and great-granddaughter of James I. ; educated at St Leonard's College, St Andrews ; M.A: (1572) ; studied law 
2 CONC in Paris and practised his profession in Edinburgh. He was on his way to becoming a judge, but a remarkable spiritual experience "on the last night of Aug. 1581 " sent him to study for the Church. He was licensed by the Presb. of 
2 CONC St Andrews in 1587, and almost immediately called to this charge. He was Moderator of the Assembly summoned to meet on 6th Feb. 1588 to consider means of defence against the threatened invasion of the Spanish Armada. In Oct. 1589,
2 CONC  when James XI. (who both respected and feared him) sailed to Norway to fetch his bride, and parties in Edinburgh were somewhat excited, the King appointed Bruce an extraordinary Privy Councillor, and such was his influence that h
2 CONC e kept all quiet, and on His Majesty's return received a cordial letter of thanks, 19th Feb. 1589-90. He crowned the Queen 17th March 1590, and was again Moderator May 1592. His power and success as a preacher were very remarkable
2 CONC , and he continued to enjoy the King's favour till 1596, when, giving offence by his opposition to James's prelatic tendencies, he, with others, was banished from Edinburgh. He was allowed to return after a time, and in May 1598 w
2 CONC as admitted to the Little Kirk. At first he refused the imposition of hands, not judging it an essential part of the ordination ceremony, but ultimately he consented to accept it "as a ceremony of entry only." In August 1600 the G
2 CONC owrie Conspiracy took place, and Bruce being one of those who entertained doubts as to the treason of the Ruthvens, refused to offer up thanks in the manner prescribed for the King's deliverance. As a result (and spite of the effo
2 CONC rts of his friends to get the matter settled), Bruce was ordered to quit Edinburgh, and prohibited from preaching anywhere in the kingdom upon pain of death. The last thirty years of his life were spent in various places. From 160
2 CONC 5 to 1609 he was confined to Inverness, where he met with much hard treatment from Lord Enzie and others, but where his preaching was much appreciated by his friends. On a vacancy he supplied the charge of Forres for a time, after
2 CONC  which, on the solicitation of his son, he received permission to return to his patrimonial estate of Kinnaird, near Stirling, where he repaired at his own expense the church of Larbert, and discharged all the duties of the minist
2 CONC ry, officiating sometimes also at Stirling. Occasionally he resided on his other property at Monkland. "Wherever he had an opportunity of preaching, great crowds attended ; he preached with remarkable power, and his own life being
2 CONC  in full accord with his preaching, the influence he attained was almost without parallel in the history of the Scottish Church." In 1620 he was again banished to Inverness, where, broken in health and in increasing weakness, he r
2 CONC emained till 1624. On King James's death in 1625 the severity against him was much mitigated, and by King Charles's order he was allowed to return to Kinnaird, where he died  27th July 1631. Tn person he was tall and dignified, wi
2 CONC th a majestic countenance and venerable appearance in the pulpit. He had a knowledge of the Scriptures beyond most of his time. Andrew Melville described him as a "hero adorned with every virtue, a constant confessor and almost ma
2 CONC rtyr to the Lord Jesus," whilst Livingstone of Ancrum said : " Mr Robert Bruce I several times heard, and in my opinion never man spoke with greater power since the Apostles' days. ;: He marr. 22nd Aug. 1590, Martha (died Nov. 162
2 CONC 0), second daugh. of Sir George Douglas of Pittendreich, and had issue — 
2 CONT * Robert, ancestor of James Bruce, African explorer ; 
2 CONT * Anna, bapt. 10th March 1595 ; 
2 CONT * Rebecca (marr. Andrew Young, minister : Stirling Sas., iv., 89) ; 
2 CONT * Margaret, bapt. 9th July 1598 ; 
2 CONT * Maria, bapt. 1st Sept. 1599 ; 
2 CONT * James ; 
2 CONT * Jean, bapt. 21st Jan. 1601 ; 
2 CONT * Mary (marr. 15th Feb. 1618, Michael Elphinstone of Quarrel : Reg. of Deeds, eclxxviii., 295) ; 
2 CONT * John, a clergyman of the Church of England ; 
2 CONT * Elizabeth (marr. 26th April 1624, James Campbell, younger, of Moy) ; 
2 CONT * Martha (marr. in 1648, James Ramsay, son to Barnbougie).
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/fastiecclesiaesc01scot#page/54/mode/2up Fasti ecclesiae scoticanae: the succession of ministers in the Church of Scotland from the reformation], Vol. I, page 54
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://archive.org/details/brucesofairththe00arms/page/n52/mode/1up?view=theater The Bruces of Airth and their cadets by Armstrong, William Bruce p 16-17etc]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander succeeded his father ante 8 March 1552, as at that date he was enfefted ^ as " son of the deceased Robert Brus of Erth," in the lands of Erth held of Holyrood.  By Janet Livingston, Sir Alexander Bruce had issue five son
2 CONC s and two daughters, viz. : 
2 CONT
2 CONT >1. William, died during his father's lifetime, of whom presently.
2 CONT >2. '''Robert of Kinnaird, of whom presently.''' 
2 CONT >3. Sir John of Kincavel was captain^ of Stirling Castle 1585, had a charter from his father of West Bordland in Denny, and is mentioned in several transactions f her died lSQ7i.having married Jean," eldest daughter of Sir Robert 
2 CONC Drummond, third of Medhope ; she is mentioned '"as " Dame Jeane Drummond, lady Kincavel " in her daughter's marriage settlement, 1638 ; by her Sir John had issue five sons and two daughters, viz. : —
2 CONT
2 CONT >>(1) Sir Alexander of Kincavel
2 CONT >>(2) William," succeeded to Kincavel and had a daughter Grizzel,'^ who married Gavin, second son of Gavin Hamilton of Raploch, 1666.
2 CONT >>(3) Robert, Minister of Aberdour, he was a great traveller in Palestine, etc., he is mentioned in many transactions and had succeeded to Kincavel in 1652,' for he is then styled " of Kincavel." He is guardian to his niece Grizze
2 CONC l in IGGG, and is again styled^ " of Kincavel " on that occasion. He is also mentioned in his sister Margaret's marriage contract. Robert married Janet Watson,^ relict of Mr. Andrew Aytoun ; they liad children,^ but there is no fu
2 CONC rther record of them.
2 CONT >>(4) Walter, minister of Inverkeithing,^ was guardian to his niece Grizzel, 1G52, is mentioned in his sister's mariage contract and in many other '^ transactions ; he married Jean,' daughter of Alexander Menzies of Rotenell, and 
2 CONC was dead* in 1674, when Jean is called his relict.
2 CONT >>(5) John of Wrae," procurator to his sister Anna, 1643, he had two daughters,^" heir-portioners of their father, Jean and Marion.
2 CONT >>(6) Margaret married/^ October 1638, Sir John Stirling of Garden, Knight.
2 CONT >>(7) Anna married James Bruce of Powfoullis.
2 CONT >>[Note. — Nothing more is known of tlie Braces of Kincavel.]
2 CONT
2 CONT >4.Sir Alexander of Bangour,- mentioned as " fourth son of Sir Alexander Bruce of Airth," 1588. He is mentioned," 5 September 1597, as having been " son lawful of Sir Alexander Bruce of Airth," and being then deceased. He married 
2 CONC Helen Hamilton,^* [who re-married William Congleton of that Ilk as his second wife], and had issue one son and oin daughter, viz. ; —
2 CONT
2 CONT >>(1) Alexander of Bangour, is enfefted* as heir of Alexander Bruce of Baugour in 6 oxgates of land of Hill of Airth, 10 July 1609. He resigns^ his land of Hill of Airth in favour of Sir John Bruce of Airth, 21 June 1617.
2 CONT ander Bruce of Baugour in 6 oxgates of land of Hill of Airth, 10 July 1609. He resigns his land of Hill of Airth in favour of Sir John Bruce of Airth, 21 June 1617. (2) Margaret/ married William Congleton, son of William Congleton
2 CONC  above mentioned, by his first wife, Mary, daughter of John, lord Yester.
2 CONT >>[Note. — Mrs. Gumming Bruce states that the first Alexander of Bangour was succeeded by his son Patrick, whose grandson, Robert Bruce, married Janet, daughter of Sir Jolin Lauder of Fountainhall, Bart., but from reference to App
2 CONC endix, p. xlvii., it is clear that the the first Alexander was succeeded by his son Alexander. No more is known of this branch.]
2 CONT
2 CONT >5. Robert had from his father " the eliaplanrie of our Ladie aisle founded and situated on the south side of the Kirk of Airth," 30 October 1572 ;he is executor to both his father and mother's wills and mentioned therein as their
2 CONC  youngest son, he is also stated to have had from his father " the lands of Garwell ; this ia au instance, not uncommon in those days, of two sons of the same parents bearing the same christian name. In 1638 au Alexander Bruce of 
2 CONC Garwald is retoured heir to Robert Bruce of Garwald, his fatherj
2 CONT >6.Marion, married William Menteith of Kerse. Marriage contract dated 13th May 1557.
2 CONT >7.The other  daughter died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/brucesofairththe00arms/page/n51/mode/1up?view=theater
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert , second son of Sir Alexander Bruce of Airth, by Janet, daughter of Alexander, lord Livingston, was born 1554, and was sent as a youth to France to study the civil law. His father had, it is said, procured him a patent as a
2 CONC  Lord of Session when he was qualified. His father, in a charter' dated 28 November 1582, settled upon him the lands of Kinnaird in Stirlingshire, and Garthenery and Garthluscane, lying in the district of Monklands in Lanarkshire 
2 CONC [all of which Sir Alexander had bought from Edward Bruce of Kinnaird in 1581. He also settled on him the lands of Halls of Airth. About 1583 Robert seems to have become strongly imbued with the doctrine of the Reformed Church of S
2 CONC cotland, and to have declared to his parents his intention of giving himself up to the ministry of the Kirk. To this his father does not appear to have offered much opposition, but his mother, a daughter of the great Catholic hous
2 CONC e of Livingston, violently objected, and at last, on finding that he was determined, only gave an unwilling consent, on the condition that he should divest himself of the patrimony which had been settled upon him; but he said that
2 CONC  he would give up all rather than resist the " calling of God to the studie of theologie and the ministrie ;" 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/brucesofairththe00arms/page/n46/mode/1up The Bruces of Airth and their cadets by Armstrong, William Bruce p p177-8]
2 CONT
2 CONT
