1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Hume_of_Godscroft
2 CONT
2 CONT '''David Hume''' or '''Home of Godscroft''' (1558–1629) was a Scottish historian and political theorist, poet and controversialist, a major intellectual figure in Jacobean Scotland. It has been said that "Hume marks the culminatio
2 CONC n of the Scottish humanist tradition."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the second son of Sir David Hume or Home, 7th Lord of Wedderburn, a Roman Catholic traditionalist of the Merse (now Berwickshire), who had married an active Calvinist wife in Mary Johnston of Elphinstone.[3][4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hume married in 1594 Barbara Johnston, widow of his friend James Haldane. They had five children.[4] His daughter Anna Hume was known as an editor, and his son James Hume of Godscroft [fr] (fl. 1630) as a mathematician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Five children of Barbara Johnstone and David Hume (or Home) of Godscroft include: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # Aselcane Hume (1602-1619)
2 CONT #Anna Hume (d. Aft 1644), editor 
2 CONT # James Home (b. aft 1610), mathematician 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT intro - The Philological Museum
2 CONT
2 CONT https://philological.cal.bham.ac.uk/hume2/intro.html#asel
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. One of more prominent writers and livelier intellects of early seventeenth century Scotland was David Hume of Godscroft [1558 - ca. 1630], second son of David Hume, seventh Baron of Wedderburn in Berwickshire. Having studied at
2 CONC  Dunbar grammar school under Andrew Simson, he attended the University of St. Andrews, and then pursued further studies in France and Geneva. A determined Calvinist, he and his brother George, the future eighth Baron, participated
2 CONC  in the Protestant coup of 1582 known as the Ruthven Raid, and he was one of many Protestants (including Andrew Melville, who became his friend then or later) who were obliged to take refuge in England. Subsequently he purchased a
2 CONC  farm in Berwickshire, which he named Godscroft, and there he remained for the rest of his life, indulging in his literary pursuits. NOTE 1
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://clanhomefamilytree.com/getperson.php?personID=I2080&tree=2
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES: Historian of Hume of Wedderburn and Houses of Douglas and Angus.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT David, of Godscroft, who wrote a history of the Douglas family, which is in the Advocate's library at Edinburgh, and of the Homes of Wedderburn, in Latin, which is in the possession of the family. He was also a poet, and a poem of
2 CONC  his, entitled Daphnis and Amaryllia, was written when he was only fourteen years of age. He set out for Italy, but was recalled from Geneva in 1581. He resided with the Earl of Angus as his secretary, and accompanied him when dri
2 CONC ven to England, when he left him at Newcastle, to prosecute his studies in London. In the history of his family he designates himself as Theagrius. (Godscroft, MSS. Hist.)
2 CONT
2 CONT [From History of Dunbar Hume and Dundas from Drummond's Noble British Families, William Pickering, London 1846]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.patrickspeople.scot/ourfamily/30959.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT "Godscroft's own personal history, so far as it is known, may be briefly stated. He was born in or about the year 1560, and appears to have been the third son of David Hume of Wedderburn, and his wife, Mariota Johnstone, daughter 
2 CONC of Andrew Johnstone of Elphinstone. Godscroft inherited the blood of Douglas from his grandmother, Alison Douglas, the wife of David Hume of Wedderburn, his grandfather. She was a daughter of George, Master of Angus, eldest son of
2 CONC  'Bell the Cat.' "
2 CONT
2 CONT from The Douglas Book
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT David married Barbara JOHNSTONE, daughter of James JOHNSTONE 5th of Elphinstone and Margaret RUTHVEN, by 28 September 1595(charter).1 (Barbara JOHNSTONE was born about 1562.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The lands of Godscroft were held in feu from the collegiate church of Dunglas, and the provost of that church, with consent of the patron, Alexander, Lord Home, granted, on 2d April 1589, a charter of these and other lands, to Mr
2 CONC . John Home, brother to George Home of Wedderburn. Five years afterwards, in August 1594, Mr. John Home disponed to his brother David the lands of Godscroft and Luckiesmill, granting on the 28th of the following September a formal
2 CONC  feudal charter of these lands to David Home and his spouse, Barbara Johnstone, daughter of James Johnstone of Elphinstone. Thenceforward David Hume was designated of Godscroft. The lands continued to be possessed by him till his 
2 CONC death, in or about the year 1632."
2 CONT
2 CONT from The Douglas Book 1
2 CONT
2 CONT
