1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Juliana Home'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Ker of the Hirel married Julia Home, daughter of Sir David Home 6th Baron of Wedderburn and Mariota Johnstone.
2 CONT
2 CONT They divorced, and she married James Hoppringle, 2nd of Whytbank as his 2nd wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Drummond's Histories of Noble British Families, William Pickering, London, 1844, Part VI., Dunbar, Hume and Dundas Families
2 CONT
2 CONT >The marriage of Julia Home and John Ker of Littlejohn] was greatly desired by the Kerrs, in order to secure the power of Wedderburn against the Humes of Manderston, Hutton, and Cowdenknows, with whom John had many disputes respec
2 CONC ting the alnds and teinds of Coldstream, to which he said he had beat right; but the Humes were too powerful for him, and he knew that Wedderburn's love to all his sisters was so great , that he would for their sakes espouse their
2 CONC  husbands' sides. The other Humes, however, declared that they acknowledged Wedderburn as their chief, and he used his authority and influence to make a compromise between the parties, and live in peace together. He deserted his w
2 CONC ife and children, and carried off and married the wife of Hamilton of Innerwich from her husband's house. His wife's brother, not content with having the law against him, assembled with twenty-four chosen followers, and made an in
2 CONC cursion on the Hirsel where Kerr lived, and carried off all his flocks and herds, and brought them to Wedderburn, hoping that Kerr would follow them and come to terms. Kerr's uncle Edmonston overtook them, and became surety for hi
2 CONC s nephew to give them full satisfaction, and they therefore gave back the cattle. No satisfaction, however, being given, the cattle were again seized, and twenty-four men set to guard them. On this, Kerr collects a force at Teviot
2 CONC dale; eighty horsemen were then sent from Wedderburn, and all the corn on the lands of Spylaw was carried into the castle. George hearing of this set off with seventy men, and Kerr appeared at the head of one hundred and twenty ho
2 CONC rse and three hundred foot. The Hume's band, though fewer, were better soldiers, and advanced on the others, who took to their heels, and never stopped till they arrived in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
