2 CONT When Robert the Bruce was waging battle to maintain his rights to the Scottish throne, he appeared in Dumfries and met Red Comyn. An agrument ensued and Robert stabbed him. His comrade, Kirkpatrick, is said to have also stabbed th
2 CONC is enemy. A Papal bull exonorates the Kirkpatricks of this deed and attributes the killing to Robert.  Near the end of the 18th Century, William, son of William Kirkpatrick of Conheath, became a wine merchant and married Donna Fra
2 CONC ncesca, daughter of Baron de Girvegnee and one of their daughters married the Emperor Napoleon III.  The marriage with a Kirkpatrick was being considered in aristocratic circles a mesalliance. Charles Kirkpatrick Sharpe of Hoddam 
2 CONC was consulted and produced a family tree showing a root in kings. This was shown to King Ferdinand VII who then approved the marriage.  Source: article entitled "What's in Your Name?" from unknown publication, by Charles Guarino a
2 CONC nd Albert Seddon..
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT WILLIAM KIRKPATRICK, of Kirkmichael, eldest son, who inherited his father1s estate
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Some records have the father of George ,Robert and James listed as "Thomas Kirkpatrick of Knock",including those produced to prove the ancient lineages of the Spanish branch , but I will follow the record of Mr James Campbell Grac
2 CONC ie Jnr,,of Dumfries, who, in 1860 ,cleared the overgrown family grave in Garrel Kirkyard and made notes regarding the inscription.(Garrel or Garvald is an ancient parish in Dumfriesshire.The parish was annexed c1674,partly to John
2 CONC stone ,but mainly to Kirkmichael)
2 CONT
2 CONT (Mr Gracie's wife is sometimes recorded as a niece of William Kirkpatrick of Conheath,and Mary Wilson ,of Kelton, at other times he himself is described as a grandson of a Miss Kirkpatrick,of Kirkmichael , or Kelton.He was a well 
2 CONC known and much respected antiquarian and genealogist of his time.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The grave was in good preservation and the inscription read as follows "Here lies the corps(sic) of William Kirkpatrick who departed this life 9th June 1686.His eldest son George of Knock,who departed this life 1738 aged 67 years"
2 CONC . Also engraved was the family Coat of Arms carved in high relief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Glenkiln was also buried in Garrel Churchyard,close by his father and brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT A third son ,James, is said to have married in England(there are scant records for James , but Maj.Gen Charles Kirkpatrick includes records in his family history that list him as a son of "Thomas of Knock"(pg 185) and brother of "
2 CONC Robert of Glenkiln". Alexander deLaPere Kirkpatrick recorded that Robert and George were sons of William Kirkpatrick of Conheath and Kirkmichael, but does not mention a James at all. I include him here also, after receiving record
2 CONC s from Don Enrique Kirkpatrick-Mendaro of Spain, stating James was a son of "Thomas of Knock" also.(Which begs the question -"Was William of Conheath and Thomas of Knock one and the same person or were they perhaps brothers, or fa
2 CONC ther and son"?)
2 CONT
2 CONT When William,the last Lord of Kirkmichael, , died in 1686,the remainder of estate was sold to the Charteris family of Amisfield, (who had previously purchased a large portion of the Barony in 1622 and also received the Kirkmichael
2 CONC  title at that time), the proceeds divided amongst his sons,including George Kirkpatrick of Knock and Robert Kirkpatrick of Glenkiln. These sons in turn began their own family branches, both fairing differently.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's sons lands at Knock, and Glenkiln, were a portion of the larger Kirkmichael and Pleulands estate,and were retained by them. (Research):Gerald Talbot Clindening ,in "The House of Glendonwyn" wrote that a sister of Robert 
2 CONC Kirkpatrick of Glenkiln, (Anne or Roseanne Kirkpatrick) married a William Glendinning,and that William and his family were forced to flee to Ireland in 1746(the very year Robert Kirkpatrick of Glenkiln was beheaded) ,and several o
2 CONC f their sons soon left for America.
2 CONT
2 CONT I can find no records to validate this claim, and this marriage is not mentioned in "Closeburn Kirkpatricks", by Maj.Gen Charles Kirkpatrick, but there is mention of a family relationship in Alexander de LaPere Kirkpatricks "Chron
2 CONC icles of the Kirkpatrick Family" ,which details the will of Alexander Kirkpatrick (1714-1791) in which he bequeaths funds to the children of Alex.Clendinin,Elizabeth and William.(Alexander Kirkpatrick's sister Anne (Mrs Jordan)was
2 CONC  a Miss Clendinin's grandmother).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT WILLIAM KIRKPATRICK, of Kirkmichael.	Obtained, in 1565, from the vicar of the parish of Garrel, the church-lands and glebe of the parish, though, just previous to this, he was under scandal with the Reformers for allowing mass to 
2 CONC be celebrated within his bounds. He was summoned to Parliament in 1548. 
2 CONT --------------------
