2 CONT James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton was born circa 1416.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Lady Eupheme Graham, daughter of Patrick Graham, Earl of Strathearn and Eupheme Stewart, Countess of Caithness, after 25 February 1440/41.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Mary Stewart, Princess of Scotland, daughter of James II Stewart, King of Scotland and Marie von Geldern, between February 1474 and April 1474. Dispensation from the Pope was granted 25 April 1476, thereby le
2 CONC gitimising the two children already born.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 6 November 1479.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Sir James Hamilton, 5th of Cadzow and Janet Livingston.[2],[3] 
2 CONT *He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland] in 1440.[2] 
2 CONT *He was created 1st Lord Hamilton [Scotland] on 28 June 1445, or possibly on 3 July 1445.[1],[2] 
2 CONT *In 1455 he joined the Douglas revolt against King James II of Scotland, then he went over to the King's side and helped to suppress it.[3] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Sheriff of Lanarkshire on 1 July 1455.[3] 
2 CONT *He was on various embassies to England between 1461 and 1472.[2]
2 CONT
