2 CONT James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton, 6th Lord of Cadzow (c. 1416- November 6, 1479) was a Scottish nobleman, scholar and politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton was the son of James Hamilton of Cadzow, 5th Laird of Cadzow. He was born at Cadzow Castle, South Lanarkshire. He first appears on record on a charter of 1426, granting him the rights to the lands of Dalserf, which 
2 CONC had been alienated by his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton was intimately connected with the powerful House of Douglas: his mother was a daughter of the Douglas Lord of Dalkeith, and also through his marriage in 1439/1440 with Lady Euphemia Graham, the youthful widow of Archibald
2 CONC  Douglas, 5th Earl of Douglas and daughter of Euphemia Stewart, Countess of Strathearn. Hamilton became stepfather to the young 6th Earl of Douglas, his brother David, both who would be murdered in November 1440 at the 'Black Dinn
2 CONC er' at Edinburgh Castle in the presence of James II. Furthermore he was the stepfather of Margaret Douglas, known as the "Fair Maid of Galloway", who was to marry her cousins William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas, and James Douglas
2 CONC , 9th Earl of Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to 1440 he achieved the status of Knighthood, and circa. 1440/1441 he inherited his father's estates. In 1445, Hamilton received a charter creating him a Lord of Parliament. This charter raised all his estates into the entai
2 CONC l of that rank, with its Capital Messuage at the Orchard, (later Hamilton Palace), at Cadzow. Furthermore the charter stated that henceforth the lands would be known as Hamilton as they are today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton accompanied his stepson in law, the 8th Earl of Douglas, to Rome in 1450, and there obtained permission from the Holy See to convert the Parish Church at the new burgh of Hamilton into a Collegiate Establishment, with end
2 CONC owments for a Provost and six Canons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton is thought to have accompanied the Earl of Douglas to his fatal meeting with James II at Stirling Castle in 1452. He was certainly with the 9th Earl of Douglas, a month after the murder and following the King's ravaging o
2 CONC f Douglasdale and Hamilton's lands in Clydesdale. A concord was reached between the King and the Douglas faction at Douglas Castle, in August 1452 that was to last until 1455. In 1453, Hamilton was in England, again with the 9th E
2 CONC arl of Douglas, arranging the release of his Brother in law,Malise Graham, Earl of Strathearn. For this action Strathearn granted Hamilton the lands of Elliestoun in Linlithgowshire. Hamilton was again in London the following year
2 CONC , but was back in Scotland by February 1455.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March of 1455, King James took to the field against Douglas, sacking his properties and burning his crops. Hamilton's lands, he being a partisan of the Douglases, were also particularly devastated. James turned his attention to
2 CONC  the mighty Douglas stronghold of Abercorn Castle, and set about besieging it. Douglas mustered his men from Douglasdale, Galloway and the Forest; Hamilton, his levies from Clydesdale. The troops marched to raise the siege, but th
2 CONC e Earl of Douglas' indecision on a plan of attack is said to have perplexed Hamilton, and cause him to withdraw his support for the Douglas cause. Hamilton changed sides and became a partisan of the Royal party. There is evidence 
2 CONC to suggest that Hamilton's uncle, James Livingstone, 1st Lord Livingston had a part in this change of heart. Douglas fled to England, his Castle of Abercorn was slighted, two of his brothers died at and following the Battle of Ark
2 CONC inholm; finally Douglas' great fortalice of Threave Castle fell and Douglas was attainted, all his enormous patrimony forfeit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the collapse of the Douglas rebellion, Hamilton was warded at Roslin Castle, in Midlothian for a short while. As recompense for his Volte-Face, Hamilton was created Sheriff of Lanark, in July of 1455, and certain of the 
2 CONC Earl of Douglas' forfeited lands were made over to him. These, and his existing lands, being confirmed in Royal charters of October that year. The Barony of Hamilton was increased to include the lands of Drumsergard, Cessford, Kin
2 CONC neil etc., and carefully entailed to whosoever might bear the name and Chief arms of Hamilton. Hamilton was made Bailie of the Priory of Lesmahagow, a Douglas foundation, and was granted the privilege of the lands of Finnart. Hami
2 CONC lton's new patron was the new Lord of Douglas, George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, the head of the "Red line" of the House of Douglas, and a supporter of the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton married first, Lady Euphemia Graham, by whom he had a daughter:
2 CONT *1. Elizabeth Hamilton (c.1442-c.1517)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married secondly, Princess Mary Stewart of Scotland, daughter of James II of Scotland, and widow of Thomas Boyd, Earl of Arran. By Princess Mary, Hamilton had three children:
2 CONT *1. Elizabeth Stewart - married Matthew Stewart, 2nd Earl of Lennox
2 CONT *2. James Hamilton, 1st Earl of Arran
2 CONT *3. Robert Hamilton, Seigneur d'Aubigny
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton was born circa 1416. He was the son of Sir James Hamilton, 5th of Cadzow and Janet Livingston.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Lady Eupheme Graham, daughter of Sir Patrick Graham, Earl of Strathearn and Eupheme Stewart, Countess of Caithness, after 25 February 1440/41.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Mary Stewart, Princess of Scotland, daughter of James II Stewart, King of Scotland and Marie von Geldern, between February 1474 and April 1474. Dispensation from the Pope was granted 25 April 1476, thereby le
2 CONC gitimising the two children already born. He died on 6 November 1479.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton gained the title of 6th of Cadzow. He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland] in 1440. He gained the title of 1st Lord Hamilton [Scotland] on 28 June 1445, or possibly on 3 July 1445.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1455 he joined the Douglas revolt against King James II of Scotland, then he went over to the King's side and helped to suppress it. He held the office of Sheriff of Lanarkshire on 1 July 1455. He was on various embassies to En
2 CONC gland between 1461 and 1472.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10212.htm#i102116
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page for James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Hamilton,_1st_Lord_Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton, 6th Lord of Cadzow (c. 1416- November 6, 1479) was a Scottish nobleman, scholar and politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton was the son of James Hamilton of Cadzow, 5th Laird of Cadzow. He was born at Cadzow Castle, South Lanarkshire. He first appears on record on a charter of 1426, granting him the rights to the lands of Dalserf, which 
2 CONC had been alienated by his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglas connection
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton was intimately connected with the powerful House of Douglas: his mother was a daughter of the Douglas Lord of Dalkeith, and also through his marriage in 1439/1440 with Lady Euphemia Graham, the youthful widow of Archibald
2 CONC  Douglas, 5th Earl of Douglas and daughter of Euphemia Stewart, Countess of Strathearn. Hamilton became stepfather to the young 6th Earl of Douglas, his brother David, both who would be murdered in November 1440 at the 'Black Dinn
2 CONC er' at Edinburgh Castle in the presence of James II. Furthermore he was the stepfather of Margaret Douglas, known as the "Fair Maid of Galloway", who was to marry her cousins William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas, and James Douglas
2 CONC , 9th Earl of Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laird o' Cadzow to Lord Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to 1440 he achieved the status of Knighthood, and circa. 1440/1441 he inherited his father's estates. In 1445, Hamilton received a charter creating him a Lord of Parliament. This charter raised all his estates into the entai
2 CONC l of that rank, with its Capital Messuage at the Orchard, (later Hamilton Palace), at Cadzow. Furthermore the charter stated that henceforth the lands would be known as Hamilton as they are today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton accompanied his stepson in law, the 8th Earl of Douglas, to Rome in 1450, and there obtained permission from the Holy See to convert the Parish Church at the new burgh of Hamilton into a Collegiate Establishment, with end
2 CONC owments for a Provost and six Canons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal displeasure
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton is thought to have accompanied the Earl of Douglas to his fatal meeting with James II at Stirling Castle in 1452. He was certainly with the 9th Earl of Douglas, a month after the murder and following the King's ravaging o
2 CONC f Douglasdale and Hamilton's lands in Clydesdale. A concord was reached between the King and the Douglas faction at Douglas Castle, in August 1452 that was to last until 1455. In 1453, Hamilton was in England, again with the 9th E
2 CONC arl of Douglas, arranging the release of his Brother in law,Malise Graham, Earl of Strathearn. For this action Strathearn granted Hamilton the lands of Elliestoun in Linlithgowshire. Hamilton was again in London the following year
2 CONC , but was back in Scotland by February 1455.
2 CONT
2 CONT Siege of Abercorn and fall of the Black Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT In March of 1455, King James took to the field against Douglas, sacking his propeties and burning his crops. Hamilton's lands, he being a partisan of the Douglases, were also particularly devastated. James turned his attention to 
2 CONC the mighty Douglas stronghold of Abercorn Castle, and set about besieging it. Douglas mustered his men from Douglasdale, Galloway and the Forest; Hamilton, his levies from Clydesdale. The troops marched to raise the siege, but the
2 CONC  Earl of Douglas' indecision on a plan of attack is said to have perplexed Hamilton, and cause him to withdraw his support for the Douglas cause. Hamilton changed sides and became a partisan of the Royal party. There is evidence t
2 CONC o suggest that Hamilton's uncle, James Livingstone, 1st Lord Livingston had a part in this change of heart. Douglas fled to England, his Castle of Abercorn was slighted, two of his brothers died at and following the Battle of Arki
2 CONC nholm; finally Douglas' great fortalice of Threave Castle fell and Douglas was attainted, all his enormous patrimony forfeit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Return to Favour
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the collapse of the Douglas rebellion, Hamilton was warded at Roslin Castle, in Midlothian for a short while. As recompense for his Volte-Face, Hamilton was created Sheriff of Lanark, in July of 1455, and certain of the 
2 CONC Earl of Douglas' forfeited lands were made over to him. These, and his existing lands, being confirmed in Royal charters of October that year. The Barony of Hamilton was increased to include the lands of Drumsergard, Cessford, Kin
2 CONC neil etc., and carefully entailed to whosoever might bear the name and Chief arms of Hamilton. Hamilton was made Bailie of the Priory of Lesmahagow, a Douglas foundation, and was granted the privilege of the lands of Finnart. Hami
2 CONC lton's new patron was the new Lord of Douglas, George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, the head of the "Red line" of the House of Douglas, and a supporter of the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton married first, Lady Euphemia Graham, by whom he had a daughter:
2 CONT *1. Elizabeth Hamilton (c.1442-c.1517)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married secondly, Princess Mary Stewart of Scotland, daughter of James II of Scotland, and widow of Thomas Boyd, Earl of Arran. By Princess Mary, Hamilton had three children:
2 CONT *1. Elizabeth Stewart - married Matthew Stewart, 2nd Earl of Lennox
2 CONT *2. James Hamilton, 1st Earl of Arran
2 CONT *3. Robert Hamilton, Seigneur d'Aubigny
2 CONT
2 CONT Peerage of Scotland 
2 CONT *Lord Hamilton (1445 – 1479) 
2 CONT *New title 
2 CONT *Succeeded by James Hamilton 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT *Balfour Paul, Sir James, The Scots Peerage Vol IV. Edinburgh 1907 [1] 
2 CONT *The Peerage.com 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Enobled 1445
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Scholar, Politician, Sheriff of Lanark, Bailie of the Priory of Lesmahagow
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
