1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gilbert Maclellan . In a tack in the public register, this Gilbert is designed second son of Sir Thomas of Bombie, anno 1503; and in a charter of John Dunbar of Mochrum, he is designed brother-german to Sir William
2 CONC  Maclellan of Bombie, &c. anno 1606. He married a daughter of Lord Herries.
2 CONT
2 CONT The McCoughans of Scotland and Ireland by John Alexander McCoughan "The barony of Barmagachan (McCoughan Mountain), Parish of Borgue, Kirkcudbrigjhtshire (part of the ancient kingdom of Galloway), was conveyed by deed dated 15 Aug
2 CONC ust 1511, to one Peter Muir, father-in-law to Gilbert  Maclellan, forebear to the Maclellans of Ballymaclellan in Kirkcudbrightshire. Sir Andrew Agnew in the Hereditary Sheriffs of Galloway refers to this deed and describes the pr
2 CONC operty as being the largest part of the new Maclellan barony."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1513, September 9 - Gilbert MacLellan of Barmaghan fell in battle at Flodden Field. On September 8, 1513 Patrick MacLellan of Gelstoun, Sir William MacLellan of Bombie, a personal friend of King James IV, Sir Thomas MacLellan of B
2 CONC ombie and kith and kin died in battle at Flodden Field.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT MACLELLAN Lord KIRKCUDBRIGHT.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE sirname of MacLellan is of very great antiquity in the south of Scotland. They had vast possessions in lands, and were very numerous and powerful.
2 CONT
2 CONT David MacLellan is mentioned in a char∣ter of king Alexander II.* the third year of his reign, which was anno dom. 1217.
2 CONT
2 CONT All our historians agree, that the Mac∣Lellans of Bombie were heretable sheriffs of Galloway,* flourished with great lustre for ma∣ny ages; and there were twelve knights of that sirname in the south of Scotland at one time, of whi
2 CONC ch Bombie was the chief.
2 CONT
2 CONT But as there are few of them mention∣ed in our publick records, before the reign of king James I. we shall from that aera be∣gin to deduce their descent.
2 CONT
2 CONT I. Sir PATRICK MACLELLAN of Bom∣bie, lived about 350 Years ago,* and married a daughter of sir Andrew Gray of Broxmouth, ancestor of lord Gray, by whom he had a son and heir,
2 CONT
2 CONT II. Sir PATRICK MACLELLAN of Bom∣bie, who flourished in the reigns of king James I.* and II. and having been prevailed upon to take part with lord Herries his rela∣tion against the earl of Douglas, was besieged in his own castle o
2 CONC f Raeberry, and being at last forced to surrender, was put to death an∣no 1452, leaving issue a son,
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William, of whom afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT The clan MacLellan were so incensed at their chief's being slain, that they all joined, took up arms,* and committed vast depreda∣tions upon the Douglases territories, in de∣fiance of all law and justice, for which king James outl
2 CONC awed and forseited them, and the estate of Bombie was thereupon annexed to the crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT About this time the county of Galloway was greatly infested with a company of rob∣bers or gypsies from Ireland, whereupon the king issued a proclamation, promising a con∣siderable reward to any who would disperse them, and bring i
2 CONC n their captain dead or alive; which was at last performed by the said sir William,* son of the last sir Patrick of Bombie, who, with the assistance of his friends, dispers∣ed that lawless crew, killed their chiestain, and brought
2 CONC  his head upon the point of his sword to his majesty; for which gallant behaviour the king put him immediately in possession of his barony of Bombie: and to perpetuate the memory of that brave action, he took for his crest, a Moor
2 CONC 's head on the point of a sword, with Think on for his motto.
