1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==JOHN, LORD MAXWELL==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NOT 3RD LORD MAXWELL.  JOHN WAS MASTER OF MAXWELL'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * birth: unknown
2 CONT * died:  Killed at the battle of Kirtle, July 22, 1484 (before his father)
2 CONT * Parents:  Robert, 2nd Lord Maxwell, & Janet Forester
2 CONT * Married: Janet, daughter of George, Earl of Caithness, in 1454
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://archive.org/stream/maxwellhistoryge00hous#page/10/mode/1up Maxwell History]
2 CONT
2 CONT John, third Lord Maxwell, Steward of Annandale.  Had charter of the baronies of Caeverlock, Maxwell, and Mearns, Feb 14, 1477-8.
2 CONT
2 CONT children
2 CONT * Robert (ancestor of the Maxwells of Cowhill)
2 CONT * James (ancestor of the Maxwells of Cayens)
2 CONT * Homer (ancestor of the Maxwells of Portrack)
2 CONT * John, abbot of Holywood
2 CONT * Thomas (ancestor of the Maxwells of Gleneslan)
2 CONT * Herbert (ancestor of the Maxwells of Hills and Drumcotran)
2 CONT * Janet, married William, son of John, 1st Lord Carlyle
2 CONT * John, 4th Lord Maxwell, married Agnes, daughter of Sir Alexander Stewart of Garlies.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT Died before his father so he didn't inherit a sequential title.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.electricscotland.com/webclans/families/maxwells.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ==============
2 CONT
2 CONT That is an example of how a celebrated song sung down the years with such affection can become better known in these Maxwell clan lands than the [http://www.centurylink.net/search/index.php?context=homepage&tab=Web&q=John+Maxwell%
2 CONC 2C+Master+of+Maxwell Maxwells] themselves and here we suggest a redress of the balance by telling the story of Sir John, the Master of Maxwell. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in the early years of the 16th century and educated at Sweetheart Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT ============================
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://archive.org/stream/maxwellhistoryge00hous#page/10/mode/2up Maxwell History]  has no birth dated given for  him or his father.  thus I have changed  the  birth date to reflect this.
2 CONT
2 CONT ============================
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Book of Carlavrock (by way of Laura Maxwell Nov 27, 2018): 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Master of Maxwell had by his wife, Janet Crichton, eight sons and two daughters. 
2 CONT The sons were 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. John, who succeeded him as fourth Lord Maxwell.  2. Robert, ancestor of the Maxwells of Cowhill, Killylung, Dinwiddie, and Broomholm, all in the county of Dumfries ; and of Drum park and Glenarm in the stewartry of Kirkcudbrigh
2 CONC t. Robert was witness to the infeftment of his brother John as heir to his grandfather, Robert, second Lord Maxwell, in the lands of Carnsalloch, in the county of Dumfries, 8th May 1485.  3. James, ancestor of the Maxwells of Cave
2 CONC ns, in the stewartry of Kirkcudbright, believed to be now extinct in the male fine.  4. Homer, ancestor of the Maxwells of Portrack, in the county of Dumfries.  5. John, Abbot of Holywood, in the county of Dumfries.  6. Thomas. A 
2 CONC precept was given by Nicholas Makgee of Balmagee, dated 11th December 1488, for infefting Thomas Maxwell, brother german of John Lord Maxwell, in liferent in the granter's ten pound lands of Nether Camdudzale and others, of old ex
2 CONC tent, in the lord ship of Balmagee and stewartry of Kirkcudbright.1 Thomas was ancestor of the Maxwells of Glenesland, now Gleneslin, in the county of Dumfries.  7. William. In a charter by John, fourth Lord Maxwell, to his sister
2 CONC  Christian, dated 14th October 1497, one of the witnesses was Wil liam Maxwell, the granter's brother. 1 Original Precept of Sasine at Terregles.  8. George, who was probably named after bis maternal grandfather, the Earl of Caith
2 CONC ness, was provided by his mother to the lands of Barnton, in the county of Edinburgh. The lands were resigned by her into the hands of King James the Second, who, by a charter under the Great Seal, dated 25th March 1460, confirmed
2 CONC  these  lands to George Maxwell.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughters were 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Janet, who married William 'Garble, son and heir of John Lord Carlile of Torthorwald, in the county of Dumfries.  2. Christian. Christian Maxwell, sister-german to John, fourth Lord Maxwell, obtained a charter, dated at Keltoun
2 CONC , 14th October 1497, from Herbert Maxwell of Knkconnel, who designates her his cousin, of his 1 shilling lands of Keltoun and Greynmarsh, in his lordship of Kirkconnel and shire of Dumfries, in liferent all the days of her  life, 
2 CONC for rendering thence annually a red rose at the feast of the nativity of John the Baptist upon the ground of the said lands, in the name of blench farm, if asked only. In the charter the granter states that, led not by force or fe
2 CONC ar, but by ardent affection, he granted it to her in her pure and honest vnginity, on account of the true love which he had towards her, and for her gratuitous and meritorious counsel, assistance, and service hitherto rendered and
2 CONC  to be rendered to him. Among the witnesses were John Lord Maxwell, Master Herbert Maxwell, vicar of Carlaverock, Thomas Maxwell, the granter's paternal uncle, William Maxwell, the granter's brother, Bobert Neilson, his maternal u
2 CONC ncle, and Bobert Maxwell, his cousin. 2 John, third Lord Maxwell, had also a son named Herbert, said to have been illegitimate, who was the ancestor of the Maxwells of Hibs and Drum coltran, in the stewartry of Kirkcudbright. 1 Ee
2 CONC g. Mag. Sig., Lib. v. No. 96. 2 Original Charter at Terregles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT John (Maxwell) Maxwell Lord Maxwell is a member of Clan Maxwell.
2 CONT Roll of Honor
2 CONT John (Maxwell) Maxwell Lord Maxwell was killed at the Battle of Lochmaben Fair during the Anglo-Scottish Wars.
2 CONT John Maxwell, was designated as Lord Maxwell, and is sometimes referred to as 3rd Lord Maxwell, but he died before his father and in other sources is given the title, Master of Maxwell.[1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT John was killed at the Battle of Kirtle (a.k.a. Battle of Lochmaben Fair) in Annandale on 22 Jul 1484.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT John married Janet Crichton around 1454. She was the daughter of George Crichton, Earl of Caithness. [4] They had eight sons and two daughters:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT John, who succeeded his father.[1]
2 CONT Robert, ancestor of Maxwells of Cowhill, Killylung, Dinwiddie, and Broomholm in Dumfries and of Drumpark, Glenarm in Kirkcudbright. [1]
2 CONT James, ancestor of Maxwells of Cavens in Kirkcudbright [1]
2 CONT Homer, ancestor of Maxwells of Portrack[1]
2 CONT John, Abbot of Holywood[1]
2 CONT Thomas, ancestor of the Maxwells of Glenesland, now Gleneslin in the county of Dumfries[1]
2 CONT WIlliam[1]
2 CONT George, probably named after maternal grandfather, Earl of Caithness [1]
2 CONT Janet, married William Carlile[5][1]
2 CONT Christian[1]
2 CONT John also had one illegitimate son, Herbert. He was ancestor of the Maxwells of Hills and Drumcoltran in Kirkcudright.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Sources
2 CONT
