1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Robert, who, in his father's lifetime, got a charter under the great seal, Roberto fillio et haeredi apparenti domini Thomae Maclellan de Bombie, terrarum baroniae de Bombiae, &c. anno 1595.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was served and retoured heir to his father Sir Thomas, anno 1608.
2 CONT
2 CONT He got a charter under the great seal, domino Robero Maclellan de Bombie, milini, of several lands, anno 1610.
2 CONT
2 CONT He afterwards got charters under the great seal, of many other lands and baronies, inter 1612, et 1616.
2 CONT
2 CONT he was a man of great worth and merit, and highly esteemed by King James VI. who conferred the honour of knighthood upon him, and made him one of the gentlemen of his bed-chamber, and being in no less favour with King Charles I wa
2 CONC s by that prince first created a baronet, then raised to the dignity of the peerage, by the title of lord boron of Kirkcudbright, 25 May 1633, by patent to his heirs male general, they carrying the name and arms of MacLellan, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dying without issue-male anno 1641, his estate and honours devolved upon his nephew and heir-male.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir Robert Maclellan, of Bombie</strong> in Galloway, son and heir of Sir Thomas Maclellan of the same, and Grizell, daughter of John (Maxwell), Lord Herries [S], succeeded his father July 1597; was Gentleman of the Bedch
2 CONC amber to James VI [S], before and after his accession to the English throne, as also to Charles I; was M.P. [S] for Wigtonshire, 1621, and was created a Baronet [S] about 1631, being subsequently created, 25 June 1633, <strong>Lor
2 CONC d Kirkcudbright</strong> [S], in which Peerage this Baronetcy then merged, and so continued till, on the death of the 10th holder, 19th April 1832, i became dormant."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524382#page/n411/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1900; Vol. II; page 392
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun05pauluoft#page/264/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. V, page 264-267
2 CONT
2 CONT *NOTE* In addition to his legitamate child Margaret McClellen, Sir Robert had 4 illegitimate children!   1.James McClellan, 2. William McClellan, 3. Isobel McClellan, 4. Robert McClellan****
2 CONT *Source http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~mymcclellandfamily/descendants_of_sir_william_m.htm
2 CONT *The peerage of Scotland: containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom, ... collected from the public records, and ancient chartularies of this nation, ... Illustrated with copper-plates. By Ro
2 CONC bert Douglas, Esq;.  Douglas, Robert, Sir, 1694-1770. < [https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/ecco/004896980.0001.000/1:149?rgn=div1;view=fulltext link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert Maclellan, 1st Lord Kirkcudbright''' (d. 1641)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Maclellan,_1st_Lord_Kirkcudbright
2 CONT
2 CONT Provost of Kirkcudbright in 1607, and was best known for his riotous (and violent) behavior. Robert was a direct descendant of the Laird of Bombie, Patrick Maclellan whom the 8th Earl of Douglas had murdered.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young Robert was imprisoned in Blackness Castle, after an affray in Kirkcudbright High Street. He was also imprisoned in Edinburgh Castle for shooting a relative of the Minister of the Church of Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless Robert was appointed as a gentleman of the bedchamber to James VI of Scotland and Charles I of England, who raised him to the rank of baronet, and subsequently, in 1633, elevated him to the peerage with the title Lord
2 CONC  Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT
2 CONT A zealous Presbyterian, he took a prominent part in the affairs of the Covenanters. Lord Kirkcudbright died in 1641, leaving no male heir, the title therefore was devolved upon his nephew, also named Thomas.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of '''Thomas Maclellan of Bombie''' (died 1597) was Provost of Kirkcudbright and father of Robert Maclellan, 1st Lord Kirkcudbright. He was responsible for the construction of MacLellan's Castle in the town.   He was appoi
2 CONC nted as a gentleman in the bedchamber of James VI in October 1580.[1]. [< https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Maclellan_of_Bombie Wikipedia] >
2 CONT
2 CONT With his first wife, Margaret, sixth daughter of Sir Matthew Campbell of Loudoun, he had a daughter, Anne, who married Sir Robert Maxwell of Orchardtoun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With his second wife, Mary Montgomery, daughter of Hugh Montgomery, Viscount of the Great Ards, he left no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded in the baronage by his nephew, Thomas, son of his younger brother, William. On the death of the ninth Lord Kirkcudbright, on 19 April 1832, the title became extinct.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.scotclans.com/scottish-clans/clan-maclellan/maclellan-people/
