1 NAME James Johnstone of Johnstone
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 4 JUN 1567
2 PLAC Johnstone
2 ADDR
3 CITY Dumfries
3 STAE Scotland
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 APR 1608
2 PLAC Dunskellie, Annan, Dumfries, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Laird of Johnstone
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "The great border battle of Dryfe Sands between Lord Maxwell and Sir James Johnstone of Johnstone and their respective clan followers, in the year 1592, resulted in the death of Lord Maxwell in the prime of life.  
2 CONC The subsequent assassination of Sir James Johnstone in 1608 by the next and ninth Lord Maxwell, resulted in the execution of that Lord Maxwell."  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1580 he received from King James VI a charter conferring upon him for life the abbacy of Holywood.  In 1588 he was retoured heir of his father in the lands and barony of Johnstone.  He was made a knight at the coronation of Que
2 CONC en Anne, 1590.  He and Lord Maxwell endeavoured to enter into friendly relations, but their feud burnt out more fiercely than before, and culminated in the battle of Dryfesands on 7 December 1593, when John, 8th Lord Maxwell, was 
2 CONC slain.  In 1596 he was warden of the West Marches, but fell for a time under government displeasure.  In 1602, the feuds with the Maxwells were renewed and some years later, at a meeting held to reconcile their differences, Johnst
2 CONC one was treacherously killed by John, 9th Lord Maxwell on 6 April 1608.  He married, in 1588, Sara Maxwell, daughter of John, Lord Herries, who survived and married, secondly in 1611, John, 1st Earl of Wigton and thirdly, in 1625,
2 CONC  Hew, Viscount Montgomery of Airds, dying in March 1633. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir James was killed by Lord Maxwell. Sir James was knighted at the coronation of Queen Anne of Denmark and appointed Warden of West Marches 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I29823&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT Name 	James Johnstone  [1]  Prefix 	Sir  Suffix 	of Johnstone  Born 	Abt 1567  Gender 	Male  Died 	6 Apr 1608  [1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Sir John Johnstone, of Johnstone and Dunskellie,   b. Abt 1550,   d. 5 Jun 1587  (Age ~ 37 years)  Mother 	Margaret Scott,   bur. 1 Feb 1618  Married 	1568  [1]  Marriage Contract 	7 Aug 1568  [1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 	Sara Maxwell,   d. Yes, date unknown  Married 	Y  [1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children  	
2 CONT
2 CONT +	1. James Johnstone, 1st Earl of Hartfell,   d. Apr 1653
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes  	
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 - July 22 1593
2 CONT     The feud between the Lord Maxwell and the Laird of Johnston, which had been stayed by a reconciliation, broke out again afresh in consequence of a foray by William Johnston of Wamphray, usually called, from his reckless, dissi
2 CONC pated character, the Galliard, in the lands of the Crichtons of Sanquhar and Douglases of Drumlanrig. The Galliard being taken in the fray and hanged, his friends, on being pursued for the recovery of the stolen cattle, stood at b
2 CONC ay and fought so desperately that many of their enemies bit the dust. A remarkable scene was consequently presented in Edinburgh. ÙtThere came certain poor women out of the south country, with fifteen bloody shirts, to compleen to
2 CONC  the king that their husbands, sons, and servants, were cruelly murdered by the Laird of Johnston, themselves spoiled, and nothing left them. The poor women, seeing they could get no satisfaction, caused the bloody shirts to be ca
2 CONC rried by pioneers through Ùtthe town of Edinburgh, upon Monday, the 23d of July. The people were much moved, and cried out for a vengeance upon the king and council. The king was nothing moved, but against the town of Edinburgh an
2 CONC d the ministry. The court alleged they had procured that spectacle in contempt of the king.Ùu---Cal.
2 CONT
2 CONT     1593
2 CONT     The Crichtons and Douglases, whom the Johnstons had plundered in the summer of this year, having induced Lord Maxwell to take up their cause, and enter with them into bonds of manrent for mutual support, it behoved the Laird o
2 CONC f Johnston to be stirring. To his aid came the reiving clans of Scott and Graham, and with them he fell upon and cut off a party of the Maxwells. This led to a decisive attempt of Lord Maxwell to bring the Johnstons to subjection;
2 CONC  but, though undertaken under sanction of his office as warden of the west marches, it ended in a way very unfortunate for himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dec 6 1593
2 CONT     ÙtThe Lord Maxwell, being on foot 1500 and horse together, coming to the Lochwood, having special commission of the king to have destroyed the said Lochwood, and banished and destroyed the hail Johnston; because he [the laird]
2 CONC  was ane favourer of the Earl Bothwell in some of his turns—being come over the Water of Annan—the Laird of Johnston, with the Scotts, to the number of 800 or thereby, ombeset the said lord in his way; where, without few or na str
2 CONC akes, the said lord was slain with the Laird JohnstonÙus awn hand [or, as is alleged, by Mr Gideon Murray, being servitor till Scott of Buccleuch]; never ane of his awn folks remained with him (only twenty of his awn household), b
2 CONC ut all fled through the water; five of the said lordÙus company slain; and his head and right arm were taÙuen with them to the Lochwood, and affixed on the wall thereof. The bruit ran that the said Lord Maxwell was treacherously d
2 CONC eserted by his awn company.Ùu—Jo. Hist.
2 CONT     Such was the famous clan-battle of DryfeÙus Sands, the last of any note fought in the southern part of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT     2 - May 1598
2 CONT     It does not appear that any effectual order was taken with the Laird of Johnston for his resistance to the royal authority at DryfeÙus Sands and the slaughter of Lord Maxwell (December 6, 1593). His turbulent proceedings at le
2 CONC ngth caused him to be denounced as a rebel. A few days before this event, his portrait was hung, head downwards, on the gibbet at the Cross of Edinburgh, and he declared Ùta mansworn man.Ùu—Bir. He was restored to his honours in 1
2 CONC 600.
2 CONT
2 CONT     3 - The death of the late Lord Maxwell in the battle of DryfeÙus Sands left a feeling of deadly bitterness in his sonÙus mind against the name of Johnston.
2 CONT
2 CONT     1608
2 CONT     The Lord Maxwell, being proclaimed traitor after the breaking out of ward in the Castle of Edinburgh, and thereupon driven to great straits, sent to the Laird of Johnston, craved a meeting, pretending he would now be heartily 
2 CONC reconciled with him, and not for the fashion, as he was before at the kingÙus pleasure, because he perceived he did not trouble him now, being an outlaw, as he looked for. They meet at the place appointed, Maxwell and one with him
2 CONC , Johnston and another with him; and Sir Robert Maxwell of Spotts (near cousin to the Lord Maxwell, and brother-in-law to the Laird of Johnston), who was employed by Maxwell to draw on the tryst. They meet on horseback, and salute
2 CONC  each other heartily in outward show, and went apart to confer together. While Johnston and Maxwell are conferring apart, MaxwellÙus second began to quarrel JohnstonÙus second, [and] shot a pistolet at him, whereupon he fell. John
2 CONC ston, hearing the shot, cried "Treason!" and, riding from Maxwell to the two gentlemen, to understand what the matter meant, Maxwell shooteth him behind the back. So Johnston fell, and died of the shot. Soon after, proclamation wa
2 CONC s made at the Cross of Edinburgh, that none, under pain of death, transport or carry away the Lord Maxwell out of the country, in ship or craer, seeing the king and Council was to take order with him, for the traitorous murdering 
2 CONC of the Laird of Johnston and his other offences.Ùu—Cal.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The fact was detested by all honest men, and the gentlemanÙus misfortune sore lamented; for he was a man full of wisdom and courage, and every way well inclined, and to have been by his too much confidence in this sort treache
2 CONC rously cut off, was a thing most pitiful. Maxwell, ashamed of that he had done, forsook the country, and had his estate forfeited [2, 3, 4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources  	
2 CONT
