0 @I6000000220369342825@ INDI
1 NAME Thomas /Turner/ Jr.
2 GIVN Thomas
2 SURN Turner
2 NSFX Jr.
2 _MARNM Turner
1 NAME Thomas Turner, linen draper of Lurgan
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 JUL 1662
2 PLAC Seagoe Parish
2 ADDR
3 CITY Drumonically
3 STAE Northern Ireland
1 OCCU itinerant hemp and flax man
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Turner-of-Frederick-County/6000000000652531866?through=6000000000652563463
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Turner-54664
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was a Friend (Quaker)
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Turner was born in the parish of Seagoe, county Armagh, Ireland to Quaker parents Thomas Turner and Ann Greer. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1695, the Lurgan Quakers launched an appeal for funds to build a meeting house. Thomas Turner junior appeared in the list of subscribers in a joint entry with his brother John, making a joint contribution of £7 10s, a very subs
2 CONC tantial sum of money at the time. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Then in 1697/8, the meeting recorded a long testimony against Thomas Turner junior who had married a widow called Mary Courtney. The couple had presented their intentions of marriage to the Lurgan and provincial meetings. Thomas h
2 CONC ad signed a loose paper promising to provide for the existing child but refused to sign the same when it had been written in the Quakers' record book, tore up the earlier paper and was later married by a priest. The couple were bo
2 CONC th disowned. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT A Thomas Turner, very probably the subject of this profile, is described in the '''Biographical Dictionary of Irish Quakers''' [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT >He spent most of his life around Lurgan and for much of that time was employed as an 'itinerant hemp and flax man' by the 'Board of Trustees of the Linen and Hempen Manufacturers of Ireland' set up in 1711. He was a constant inno
2 CONC vator in the improvement of flax production and 'devised a scrutch mill, reproduced foreign methods of weaving and contrived improved bleaching techniques.' In 1715 he published the pamphlet New Methods of Improving Flax and Flax 
2 CONC Seed and bleaching cloth. The Linen Board paid him to visit and instruct bleachers from Antrim to Cootehill and Monaghan. In 1721 and again in 1729 he was sent to study Dutch, Flemish, and French practice in linen production. In 1
2 CONC 728 he had received an award for devising a new bleaching method."
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Turner, linen draper of Lurgan appeared in various deed memorials. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Given his history in Ireland, this Thomas Turner was plainly not the person who emigrated to Virginia shown in Thomas Turner (1662-1744).
2 CONT
2 CONT No records of his death or of any children have been located.
