1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ='''About the Keeling-Gouldman-Golden family relationship'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT +++++++++++++++++++
2 CONT +++++++++++++++++++
2 CONT
2 CONT IMPORTANT NOTE: Thomas Gouldman is NOT the son of Leonard Keeling. This was an entry made by me some years ago based info then available. WHY CONNECTION NOT CHANGED? Have tried to correct this error in GENI but the 'Remove Relatio
2 CONC nship' button seems to be dysfunctional.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gouldmans and Keelings (via Edward Keeling, not related to Prince Anne County Keelings) did have some sort of familial relationship --- but it was not as parent and child. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As of 2022, there is significant new information pointing to the Gouldmans coming from the Gouldman family of Kent, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT +++++++++++++++++++
2 CONT +++++++++++++++++++
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no easy explanation for how Keelings became Gouldmans and then very quickly became Goulden (only one known line, my own) and then were Goldens by the late 1700s.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is certainly not a shred of known documentation to show that Gouldmans were ever Keelings, or that Thomas Keeling, Virginia Burgess, chose to become known as Thomas Gouldman in the very early 1660s.
2 CONT
2 CONT --- On the other hand,a very prominent Gouldman family historian from the Winston family notes there is no evidence that Gouldmans ever came to Virginia ... they just appeared.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidence does exist of an extremely close familial and professional relationship interwoven between the two families. See http://wdgolden.com/genes/all-goldens-your-goldens/gouldmans-and-keelings/
2 CONT
2 CONT What caused the change in name? We probably will never know. My belief is that Welsh patronymics played a role -- especially considering Thomas Gouldman was prominent in Virginia politics, and there were no shortage of Thomas Keel
2 CONC ings.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Leonard Keeling'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Keeling, who orginally arrived in Virginia 1628, would later return to the UK before arriving back in Virginia in 1635 or 1636.  Along with bringing a new bride, it appears that he brought a brother or first cousin with him
2 CONC : George Keeling, sometimes referred to as Leonard Keeling. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Both a George and a Leonard are listed as arriving in Virginia or Maryland in 1636 per Peter Coldham -- but only one passenger is referenced. There was no clear delineation between the two (MD and VA) at the time. It would appear 
2 CONC that George/Leonard Keeling continued on to what was then Rappahanock County (now reterred to as Old Rappahanock County). He would become known just as Leonard Keeling. Probable son Captain/Sheriff George Keeling would name a son 
2 CONC 'Leonard'.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''DNA'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT yDNA (male-to-male inherited DNA) also tells a story. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Our Golden line, based upon nine (9) YDNA tests, are an exact match for the Keeling male line. 
2 CONT
2 CONT No known Gouldman male has taken a yDNA test --- but I would be willing to provide one free of charge. The only criteria for the free yDNA test is that the male Gouldman must be the son of a male Gouldman with indications that the
2 CONC ir line formed in Virginia before 1800. Would be glad to help research that!
2 CONT
2 CONT atDNA tells its own story, pointing very strongly to our Goldens and the line of Captain George Keeling, probable brother to Thomas Gouldman, being very closely related. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --- Four of our Golden lines that split before 1800 and the Captain George Keeling line have so many DNA matches that it would easy to suspect that he was actually our grandfather of the era. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --- atDNA provides plenty of connection back to a specific Gouldman line, Edward Gouldman aft.1660-abt 10 Jan 1710. Edward was son of Thomas Gouldman. Playing an important role in using atDNA to connect with the Gouldmans are the 
2 CONC many DNA matches with the Awbrey, Hoskins, Meador and Tomlin families --- all which were either wife, mother or grandmother to Thomas and son Edward Gouldman.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Got info or questions?''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bill Golden
2 CONT Norfolk1956@gmail.com
