2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT The Keeling line is closely woven with Hendersons by marriages betweenthe two families.<ref name=JTP>Research of[[Taylor-17245|Jouett (Taylor) Prisley]], Sterling, Virginia.  (Need to get actual record sources, rather than just a 
2 CONC researcher's name)</ref> George and Ursula's children are generally reported as: 
2 CONT # George
2 CONT # Richard
2 CONT # Mary, b. 26 Sep 1684, m. John Williams, d. 06 Sep 1775
2 CONT # Ursula, m. Thomas Henderson It seems certain that Captain Keeling was a vestryman of old BlisslandParish and in 1704 owned 1,500 acres of land in New Kent County Virginia.  He was High Sheriff there from 1706-1709, thus a man of
2 CONC  some eminence, though not at all the "Lord" Keeling some eager descendants have chosen to dub him.  It is "proven" by several researchers that he came to New Kent County sometime after 1695 from York County.<ref name=JTP/> One so
2 CONC urce reportedly shows that George was elected Captain of the Militia on 04 Jul 1702; was elected Sheriff of New Kent County on 28 Apr 1708; served as a Justice of the Peace in New Kent County; and served as a member of the vestry 
2 CONC of Blissland Parish in New Kent County about1725. <ref>Blissland Parish Vestry Book at the Episcopal Theological Seminary, near Alexandria (Too vague, unable to reproduce/locate as I found several seminaries..need location address
2 CONC  and book/page number)</ref> It is reported that his will was executed in Vance County (now Granville County, North Carolina, formerly Edgecombe County), recorded at Pemfield (then the county seat) and later transferred to the Hal
2 CONC ifax, North Carolina. <ref name=JTP/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage information from imported profile: : Wife:  [[Fleming-1199|Ursula Browne]] : Child:  [[Keeling-80|Mary Keeling]] : Child:  [[Keeling-78|Ursula Keeling]]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT <references />* Worth Stickley Ray, [https://www.worldcat.org/title/colonial-granville-county-and-its-people-loose-leaves-from-the-lost-tribes-of-north-carolina/oclc/1432176&referer=brief_results <i>Colonial Granville and Its Peop
2 CONC le,</i> Loose Leaves from "The Lost Tribes of North Carolina"]. (Austin, TX:  Self-published, 1945] Repository: New England Historic Genealogical Society Library. (page numbers?)* Ancestor Lineages of Members Texas Society/Nationa
2 CONC l Society ColonialDames Seventeenth Century Record for Ancestor Lineages of Members Texas Society/National Society Colonial Dames Seventeenth Century. (whichmembers, member numbers etc?, too vague)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT George Keeling died after 27 Oct 1729 when he was excused from vestry New Kent Co., Virginia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt George Keeling desiring to be Excused from being a vestry man is dischard [sic] from his Office and William Mackain Gen, is Chosen in his Steade and Sworn accordingly by M' John Sclater a Majestrate for this County.
2 CONT
2 CONT His will of record in both Halifax and Granville Cos.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT I don't have a copy of his will. I have misplaced my Granville Co. NC book, but will keep looking for Keeling info.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some notes, but you may want to verify.
2 CONT 1731 & 1746- Capt. Keeling; Bk14 p197/8 of Land patents and Grants of Hanover Co 1721-1800 by Charles Blount. /
2 CONT - - -
