3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 DEC 1637
2 PLAC Dumfries Scotland, of Kilquhonnedie, Glencairn, Dumfries, Scotland (United Kingdom)
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Stephen Laurie=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Johne Lawrie and Marion Bickerston 
2 CONT * STEPHEN LAURIE (1st Laird of Maxwelton), who carried on the family name, & who in 1614 purchased from the Earl of Glencairn the estate of Maxwelton ; he married MARION CORSONE, daughter of JOHN CORSONE (Corsane) of Mickleknox.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married:  '''Marian Corsane,''' on 10th Nov 1604, Stephen Lawrie espoused Marior, daughter of the Provost Corsane, proprietor of Meikleknox, getting with her a handsome marriage portion. About the same time he obtained a charter
2 CONC  from John, Lord Herries, of the ten-merk land and barony of Redcastle, parish of Urr. His wealth enabled him afterwards to purchase, from Sir Robert Gordon of Lochinvar, Bithbought, Shancastle, and Maxwelton, for which estates he
2 CONC  received a royal charter, dated 3rd November, 1611. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Jonet Lowrie''' -  Maxwelton, was christened 27 Sep 1606, and was buried 24 Jan 1685.
2 CONT * '''Sir John Laurie of Maxwelton,''' Christening:  21 Sep 1609 in Dumfries, Dumfriesshire, Scotland. lands and title went to his son, John, who married in 1630 to Agnes, daughter of Sir Robert Grierson of lag.
2 CONT * '''Thomas Laurie,''' born around July 1611 in Dumfries, Dumfriesshire, Scotland. Married: Jonet Peeres on 18 Nov 1621 in Dumfries, Dumfriesshire, Scotland.
2 CONT * '''Adam Laurie,''' Christening:  20 Aug 1612 In Dumfries, Dumfriesshire, Scotland, United Kingdom 
2 CONT * '''Agnes Laurie,''' born  in 1615 in Maxwelton, Dumfries-shire Dumfries, Dumfriesshire, Scotland, United Kingdom, Scotland in
2 CONT * '''Marion Laurie,''' Christening:  31 Dec 1616 in Dumfries, Dumfriesshire, Scotland, United Kingdom 
2 CONT * '''Isobel Laurie,''' Christening:  16 Jun 1618 in Dumfries, Dumfriesshire, Scotland, United Kingdom 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://archive.org/stream/transactionsjour33191415dumf#page/312/mode/2up/search/Laurie History of Stephen Laurie acquired Red Castle and Maxwellton in 1616, Transactions and journal of proceedings]
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen acquired Red Castle and Maxwellton in 1616
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen was a merchant burgess of Dumfries and bailie of that burgh in 1616, and acquired Redcastle and Maxwellton. ... that James Lawrie, of Bally- magorry, letters of administration for whose effects were granted in 1668, was hi
2 CONC s brother. ... allotted to Scotch undertakers," and that he was resident with his wife and family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen Laurie, merchant burgess of Dumfries (1608), purchased in 1611, the Maxwelton and other estates from Sir Robert Gordon, of Lochinvar, and took the designation "of Maxwelton". Notes & Queries, 1861 suggests Maxwelton was ac
2 CONC quired in 1614. He is said to have purchased Redcastle of Urr too. 1618 he was MP for Dumfries. This is actually a Commissioner to the Convention of Royal Burghs rather than MP as we understand it in the 21stC.. Baillie of Dumfrie
2 CONC s, 1615-18 and possibly earlier too. JP for Dumfries 1618-37. Possibly a maltster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another of the race was the progenitor of the Lauries, one of whom, Stephen, was a flourishing Dumfries merchant before James VI. became king. Prior to 1611 he espoused Marior, daughter of the Provost Corsane, proprietor of Meikle
2 CONC knox, getting with her a handsome marriage portion. About the same time he obtained a charter from John, Lord Herries, of the ten-merk land and barony of Redcastle, parish of Urr. His wealth enabled him afterwards to purchase, fro
2 CONC m Sir Robert Gordon of Lochinvar, Bithbought, Shancastle, and Maxwelton, for which estates he received a royal charter, dated 3rd November, 1611. Stephen Laurie, now a man of many acres, took the designation of Maxwelton, leaving 
2 CONC at his death the lands and title to his eldest son, John, married in 1630 to Agnes, daughter of Sir Robert Grierson of lag. The next head of the house, Robert, was created a baronet on the 27th of March, 1685. He was twice married
2 CONC , and had, by his second wife, three sons and four daughters. The birth of one of the latter is thus entered in the family register by her father: - “At the pleasure of the Almighty God, my daughter, Anna Laurie, was borne upon th
2 CONC e 16th day of December, 1682 years, about six o’clock in the morning; and was baptized by Mr. Geo.” [Hunter, minister of Glencairn]. [Barjarg Manuscripts.] The minute is worth quoting here, seeing that the little stranger, whose e
2 CONC ntry into life it announces, grew up to be the most beautiful Dumfriesian lady of the day, and the heroine of a song which has rendered her charms immortal: -
2 CONT
2 CONT “Her brow is like the snaw-drift,
2 CONT Her neck is like the swan, Her face it is the fairest That e’er the sun shone on –  That e’er the sun shone on; And dark blue is her e’e! And for bonnie Annie Laurie I’d lay me down and die.”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Page n16'''
2 CONT
2 CONT But what is there to say about Annie’s Home? It existed in her time, it exists now ; what has the old house to say about itself? The Maxwelton estate was bought in 1611 by Stephen Laurie, a merchant in this town, having previously
2 CONC  for some 200 years belonged to the Earls of Glencairn. In Van Gent's map of Scotland, bearing date 1654, the house is depicted as a castle, and called “ Glenkairn Castel,” with a farm near it called “ Maxweltown.” When the old na
2 CONC me was changed I do not know ; possibly Stephen Laurie or his son, having no connection with the family of Lord Glencairn, took the name of Laurie of Maxwelton, that being the name of the farm on which the castle stood, and that n
2 CONC ame gradually dispossessed the old one. The site of Glencairn Castle was well chosen, whether for beauty or for defence. It stands on the northern side of the Cairn valley, upon a small promontory of rock, running out from one of 
2 CONC the spurs of the Keir range of hills ; the ground behind it dips to the north before it reaches the steep slopes of the hillside ; it falls somewhat on the eastern and western sides, whilst to the south it falls at first abruptly,
2 CONC  but more leisurely afterwards, down to the river below. The house stands near the opening into Glencairn of the Clan pass, the only depression in the range of hills by which to cross from Nithsdale into the valley of the Cairn. T
2 CONC hus the ground fell on all four sides of the old castle, which must have stood out as a watch-tower, commanding the whole valley ; whilst it was admirably placed for disputing the passage of the Clan should any unfriendly attack b
2 CONC e attempted from that quarter. There can be no doubt, I think, that the present house stands on the site, and incorporates a large portion of the old castle ; the two in fact are practically one. It occupies three sides of a quadr
2 CONC angle, of which a portion of the larger or western wing was burnt down about the middle of the last century. But there remains the rude foundations of the whole house—the tower at the south-west corner and a small turret at the in
2 CONC ner north-west angle of the courtyard, two old arches in the eastern wing, and many portions of a wall of great thickness, that of the tower being five feet, and one within the western wing being twelve feet thick. In “The Castell
2 CONC ated and Domestic Architecture of Scotland,” by Macgibbon & Ross, the building which bears the nearest resemblance to Maxwelton is
2 CONT
2 CONT
