2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Kerr was the son of Walter Kerr and Agnes Crichton. He married Christian Rutherford, daughter of James Rutherford, on 12 February 1484. He died after 6 November 1500.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Sir Robert Kerr held the office of Master of the King's Artillery. He lived at Cessford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir Robert Kerr and Christian Rutherford
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Sir Andrew Kerr+ d. 18 Jul 1526
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.George Kerr d. a 12 Mar 1553/54
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir Robert Kerr M, #14891, d. after 6 November 1500
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Kerr|d. a 6 Nov 1500|p1490.htm#i14891|Walter Kerr|d. 25 Nov 1501|p1490.htm#i14893|Agnes Crichton||p2130.htm#i21295|Andrew Kerr|d. a 8 May 1481|p1490.htm#i14895|Margaret Tweedie||p1490.htm#i14896|William Crichton of that
2 CONC  Ilk, 1st Lord Crichton|d. bt Jan 1454 - Jul 1454|p2130.htm#i21296||||
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=29 Apr 2008
2 CONT      Sir Robert Kerr was the son of Walter Kerr and Agnes Crichton. He married Christian Rutherford, daughter of James Rutherford, on 12 February 1484. He died after 6 November 1500.
2 CONT      Sir Robert Kerr held the office of Master of the King's Artillery. He lived at Cessford. Children of Sir Robert Kerr and Christian Rutherford 1.Sir Andrew Kerr+ d. 18 Jul 1526 2.George Kerr d. a 12 Mar 1553/54
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The name of Carr, Ker, Kerr or Karre is as old as the 
2 CONT
2 CONT Norman Conquest by William of Normandy in 1066. One of the followers of William I is recorded in the Roll Of 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gattle Abbey as the name of Karre.The early descendants of this Norman soldier and succeeding generations spread on both sides of the border of England and Scotland.Direct lines can be traced through various peerage books of E
2 CONC ngland and Scotland to Andrew Kerr I, the 6th Baron of Ferniehirst, Scotland. He was born in 1450 , created Baron in 1480 and knighted in 1483. He and his son Andrew II, 7th Baron of Ferniehirst, were remarkable men for talent and
2 CONC  undaunted courage, conspicious in reigns of James IV and James V. Andrew Kerr II, died in 1543. His son Sir John Kerr, 8th Baron of Ferniehirst, did great service against the English and rescued Queen Mary from incursions by the 
2 CONC English against the Scots. Sir Thomas, 9th Baron and son of Sir John, was also devoted to Queen Mary's interests. (Quoted from Watson's 'The House of Carr'1926). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ...'''The Kers of Cessford and Ferniehirst''' had a slightly different problem
2 CONT with their kinship. They '''were rivals for the title of head of the Ker
2 CONT surname throughout the century''',, but in typical Border fashion they played
2 CONT on respect for this name in times of crisis and bound together to oppose
2 CONT the Scotts in their long-running bloodfeud. They each built up a powerful
2 CONT kin group through landholding in East Teviotdale and Jedforest,, but
2 CONT neither dominated the other. Exactly who held the more ancient line of
2 CONT descent was contentious even amongst nineteenth-century historians as it
2 CONT was within one generation of fifteenth-century origin.
2 CONT In 1602 James VI had to be very diplomatic when trying to appease their
2 CONT feud as the bitterness was deep-rooted. Even in pacifications of the
2 CONT Ker and Scott feud their kin groups were listed separately. In 1582 Sir
2 CONT Walter Ker of Cessford settled a local feud claiming to speak for the
2 CONT Iremanent surname of Kerris I) but in reality this only meant his own Ker
2 CONT allies of Littledean, Faldonsideq Linton, Primsideloch, Yair, Newhall,
2 CONT Maisondieu, Whitmuir and Kippilaw. None of Ferniehirst's allies, such as
2 CONT Ker of Ancrum or Cavers, was mentioned. A marriage between the two
2 CONT branches in 1540 made no real difference to the long-term animosity 
2 CONT between them for it only created a temporary truce. In 1593 reference was
2 CONT made to Tarniherst and his Kers', so the divisions were as deep as ever.
2 CONT Dissociation between the two branches of the Kers was generally
2 CONT accepted and central government knew better than to make a laird from one
2 CONT branch responsible for the actions of a Ker from the other side. Other
2 CONT surnames were not overlooked. Kinship had its disadvantages as well as
2 CONT its benefits and the policy of making a kinsman a cautioner under
2 CONT financial penalty was one of the drawbacks. John Haitlie of Mellerstain
2 CONT brought two troublesome Haitlies before the Privy Council in 1597 as he
2 CONT had been ordered to by a previous act of caution. He believed that his
2 CONT obligation to the Council had been fulfilled, but they tried to make him
2 CONT responsible for all the attempts made by these Haitlies, from before
2 CONT the time he became their cautioner. As a kin chief Haitlie was obliged
2 CONT to his kinsmen, but as a private individual he must have found kinship a
2 CONT tiresome business.
2 CONT The system of cautioning was not exclusively kin-based for friends,
2 CONT neighbours or kinsmen by marriage could be sureties, but in its broadest
2 CONT context kinship was an important part of this procedure. For lesser
2 CONT lairds it was advantageous to be able to appeal to the social conscience
2 CONT of a greater laird or even a wealthy distant kinsman. There are hundreds of routine examples of kinsmen acting as cautioners for kinsmen
2 CONT to keep the peace in a feud or to enter a rebel, but some reveal
2 CONT interesting links of kinship. For instance, John Ker of Kerchesters had
2 CONT Robert Ker, portioner of Duddingston and burgess of Edinburgh, as a
2 CONT cautioner in 1593. Robert Ker was a second-generation burgess, remote
2 CONT from his Border ancestry, yet still responsive to the needs of a very
2 CONT distant kinsman…….
2 CONT
2 CONT Disenchantment with Morton began in 1576 when he snubbed both Home of
2 CONT Cowdenknowes and Ker of Cessford. Cowdenknowes had to pay a 15000 Scots
2 CONT fine for not keeping the Brounfields peaceable whilst he was their surety.
2 CONT He was supposed to do this by law, but being a favoured Morton supporter
2 CONT he could have expected the fine to be rescinded. Other reasons for
2 CONT Cowdenknowes' defection connected to kinsmen, proffered by George Hewitt
2 CONT are inconclusive as the Home kinship was fragmentary in the 1570s. A
2 CONT disagreement between Morton and the Homes of Manderston was unconnected to
2 CONT this as Manderston and Cowdenknowes were rivals. Cowdenknowes
2 CONT disaffection lost him the wardenship of the East March to Wedderburn in
2 CONT September 1578. Ker of Cessford had been annoyed by Morton's experimental
2 CONT division of his Middle March, so it was not surprising that the Kers were
2 CONT generally 'malcontent' with Morton
2 CONT [https://era.ed.ac.uk/handle/1842/6875 ''Lairds and gentlemen: A study of the landed families of the Eastern Anglo-Scottish Borders c.1540-1603''] Vol 1 pp 55-8 & 106-7]
