1 NOTE Note: 9th Baron of Fennehurst
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John reportedly rescued Mary, Queen of Scots in theincursion against
2 CONT Scotland by the English against the Scots. Sir Thomas, 9th Baronand son
2 CONT of Sir John, was also devoted to Queen Mary's interests. (Quotedfrom
2 CONT Watson's 'The House of Carr'1926)
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was the son of Sir John Kerr and Katherine, daughter ofSir Andrew Kerr of Cesford. Sir John reportedly rescued MaryQueen of Scots in the incursion against Scotland. Sir John wasborn 17 June 1500 and died 1562.Thomas Kerr be
2 CONC came the 9th Baronof Ferniehirst Castle. He was also noted for his involvementwith Mary Stuart, Queen of Scots. Assisted Mary, Queen of Scots,escape the English.
2 CONT Sir John's son, Sir Thomas Kerr of Ferniehirst, was noted forhis loyalty to Mary Queen of Scots, for whom he built afortified house in the centre of Jedburgh. He raised the RoyalStandard for her in Dumfries, helping her and her hu
2 CONC sbandDarnley to put down an insurrection by a group of her nobles(she won at the time but was forced into exile a few yearslater). Subsequently he sheltered her English supporters afterthe rising of the Northern Earls (1568) and r
2 CONC escued LadyNorthumberland, stranded by illness in a Liddesdale outlaw'shide-out. He helped his father-in-law, Kirkcaldy of Grange, todefend Edinburgh Castle in the Queen's name; when it was takenhe lost precious family documents w
2 CONC hich were never seen again,but at least he escaped with his life (Kirkcaldy was beheaded)and fled abroad for some years. He was re-instated in his landsby James VI when the young King came of age and took power intohis own hands. 
2 CONC The townsmen of Jedburgh supported the RegentMorton (later also beheaded) against Mary; they "debagged" andpublicly caned a herald sent out by Ferniehirst to read out aproclamation of loyalty to the Queen, also compelling him to e
2 CONC athis document.
2 CONT
2 CONT From her English prison, Mary wrote to Sir Thomas, thanking himfor his past services and encouraging him to keep up hisloyalty. She seems to have taken a particular liking to hisyoung son Andrew, the first Lord Jedburgh, and may h
2 CONC ave knightedhim while still a child, for she asks in particular to beremembered to "Sir Andrew".
2 CONT
2 CONT Briefly imprisoned after the fall of Edinburgh Castle, SirThomas was in exile and unable to perform his duties as Wardenat the time of the last major clash on the Border, the Raid ofRedeswire. This incident developed on one of the
2 CONC  "days of truce"when the Wardens or their deputes met to resolve various localproblems and to exchange or hang wanted criminals. On thisoccasion the English Warden complained that the Scots had failedto hand over a thief known as 
2 CONC "Farnstein" (not a German refugeeor mercenary, as one might think, but an Englishman whose realname was Robson). This led to mutual insults, no doubtaggravated by the fact that both sides had been liquidating agreat deal of liquid
2 CONC . The argument grew into a scuffle and thescuffle grew into a fight. Eventually the Jedburgh men arrivedin strength and dispersed the English, killing a few andcapturing others, who were later released without ransom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though he missed this particular incident, Sir Thomas wasinvolved in a similar but smaller affray, on almost the samespot, ten years later. By then he was back in office as Wardenof the Middle March; Forster, now 84, was still in 
2 CONC charge on theother side, and Forster's son-in-law, who was also a son of theEarl of Bedford, was killed. Elizabeth Tudor was not amused, andinsisted on Ferniehirst's punishment, though the rights andwrongs of the whole affair were
2 CONC  by no means clear. Being anxiousto succeed to the English throne, James VI sought to ingratiatehimself with her, and exiled Sir Thomas to Aberdeen, where hedied within a year. The inscription on his memorial in JedburghAbbey read
2 CONC s "Sir THOMAS KERR of Fernyherst, Warden of theMarches, Provost of Edinburgh and Jedburgh, Father of AndrewLord Jedburgh, Sir James Kerr of Creylin (Crailing) and RobertEarl of Somerset. He died at Aberdeen on March 31, 1586 and l
2 CONC iesburied before the Communion Table. He was a man of action andperfit loyaltie and constancie to Queen Marie in all hertroubles. He suffered 14 years' banishment besides forfaulter(forfeiture) of his lands. He was restored to his
2 CONC  estates andhonours by King James the Sext."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas married twice. His children by his first wife, JanetKirkcaldy, included Sir Andrew of Ferniehirst, first LordJedburgh (see below) and William, who took the name of Kirkcaldyto continue his mother's line; his children, h
2 CONC owever, revertedto Kerr, having failed to inherit the Grange property. By hissecond marriage, to Janet Scott, Sir Thomas was the father ofSir James Kerr of Crailing (father of the second Lord Jedburgh)and of Robert Can, Earl of So
2 CONC merset (see below). He had severalother children by both his wives.
