1 OCCU Earl of Bothwell, Conspirator
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == FRANCIS STEWART Earl of Bothwell ==
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ss4tz/stewart20.php Stirnet: Stewart 20]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/9HPR-VKY
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Stewart-2323
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Stewart_Hepburn,_5th_Earl_of_Bothwell
2 CONT
2 CONT (He was either the 5th or the 1st Earl of Bothwell; it depends on which creation of the earldom you are referencing: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earl_of_Bothwell)
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Stewart, Earl Bothwell (b. c. December 1562  – d. April 1612, Naples), [n.b. note alternate death date of 1624, documented below] was Commendator of Kelso Abbey and Coldingham Priory, a Privy Counsellor and Lord High Admir
2 CONC al of Scotland. Like his stepfather, Archibald Douglas, Parson of Douglas, he was a notorious conspirator, who died in exile and poverty in Italy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis was the first cousin of King James VI of Scotland (they were both grandsons of James V of Scotland). Francis's maternal uncle James Hepburn, 4th Earl of Bothwell was the chief suspect of having murdered James VI's father H
2 CONC enry Stuart, Lord Darnley.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis was son to John Stewart, Lord Darnley, Prior of Coldingham (d. 1563), an illegitimate child of James V of Scotland by his mistress Elizabeth Carmichael. John Stewart's wife was Jane Hepburn, Mistress of Caithness, Lady Mor
2 CONC ham (d. 1599) sister to James Hepburn, the fourth Earl Bothwell. Francis is said to have been born in his mother's tower house at Morham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 December 1577, Francis, Earl Bothwell married Margaret (d. 1640), daughter of David Douglas, 7th Earl of Angus, and widow of Sir Walter Scott, of Branxholme & Buccleuch (d. 1574). Initially, after a brief honeymoon, the new e
2 CONC arl was not permitted to come within twenty miles of his new wife 'for reassone of his youngnes'. (Adv. Man. 35.4.2) They later had, at least, four sons and four daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Francis, Lord Stewart, Bothwell and Commendator of Kelso Abbey (b. 1584) - After his father's death, in spite of the attainder, he is occasionally styled 'Earl Bothwell', and Lord Stewart and Bothwell. married Isobel, daught
2 CONC er of Robert Seton, 1st Earl of Winton.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John (2nd son), the last Commendator of Coldingham Priory and 1st secular feudal Baron of Coldingham. On 16 June 1622 he transferred the barony to his elder brother, Francis. John and his son Francis were still living in Apr
2 CONC il 1636 but John was dead by August 1658.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Frederick, (3rd son) (b. 1594) mentioned in the Privy Council Registers in 1612 (vol. ix, p. 498).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henry (Harry), (4th son) (b. 1594?) signed many documents with his elder brothers, and who, in 1627, consented to a lease. Possibly twin with Frederick.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elizabeth (b. 1590) (eldest daughter) married James, Master of Cranstoun (appears to have been banished in 1610.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Helen, married John Macfarlane of that Ilk.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Jean (d. after 1624) married Robert Elliot of Redheugh.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margaret, married Allan Cathcart, 5th Lord Cathcart.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT Watson, Godfrey. Bothwell and the Witches. London: Hale, 1975. Print. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/2439874
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/0709151209
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.shanmonster.com/witch/witches/hepburn.html
2 CONT
2 CONT  A Scottish noble of the 16th century, Hepburn was accused of being the "Grand Master of a sodality or coven of witches" in North Berwick (Wedeck 201).
2 CONT
2 CONT Public opinon turned against Hepburn, and he "was eventually forced to fell the country in 1595. Many of [Hepburn's] alleged diabolical accomplices were burned alive at the King's insistence" (Sidky 58).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://witchofforestgrove.com/2010/04/16/the-witches-reel/
2 CONT
2 CONT This song originates from Scotland and has been dated to the year 1591. The words are from the witchcraft trial of the Earl of Bothwell Francis Stewart who was accused of using sorcery to try to kill King James VI. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://witchofforestgrove.com/2010/04/16/the-witches-reel/
2 CONT
2 CONT Hear the song here:  http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jmh3Ai6PE1Q
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.genealogy.com/users/h/e/p/Ross-G-Hepburn/FILE/0001text.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis STEWART-HEPBURN 5th Earl of Bothwell
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 1562
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 1624, Naples Italy in extreme poverty
2 CONT
2 CONT Res: 16 Jun 1581, created earl of Bothwell by King james VI
2 CONT
2 CONT Attempted to claim the title as fifth Earl of Bothwell but all the titles ceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref. "Mary, Queen of Scots" by Angus Hamilton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death Year per Gwen Hepburn's chart.
2 CONT
2 CONT n.b. Interesting footnotes here:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. It is perhaps significant that the confession of John Fian, and the trials of both Barbara Napier and of Bothwell himself for witchcraft, have disappeared from the Justiciary Records.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reading the accounts in the light of this supposition, it is seen that every one, including James, suspected Bothwell. Even if they did not acknowledge his divinity, they feared the magical powers which, as Chief of the Witches, h
2 CONC e was supposed to wield. It is impossible to study the details of this period without realizing the extraordinary fear which James had of his cousin; it was fear with an underlying horror, totally different from his feeling toward
2 CONC s his other turbulent subjects.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Bothwell, seeking pardon, was introduced into Holyrood Palace by Lady Athol in the early morning of July 24, 1593, he entered the King's chamber. James, always undignified, was caught in the middle of his morning toilet; he t
2 CONC ried to run into the Queen's room, but the way was barred by Bothwell's friends and the door was locked. 'The king, seeing no other refuge, asked what they meant. Came they to seek his life? let them take it--they would not get hi
2 CONC s soul.'[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT This remark, made in the urgency and excitement of the moment, is highly significant. Had Bothwell been, like many of James's other enemies, merely an assassin, James would not have spoken of his soul. But Bothwell as the Devil of
2 CONC  the witches had the right to demand the yielding of the soul, and James was aware of the fact.
2 CONT
2 CONT The birth of James's children removed Bothwell's hopes of succession; the power of the witch organization, of which he was the Chief, was broken by the death of its leaders. He had made a strong bid for power, he failed, fled the 
2 CONC country, and finally died in poverty at Naples. There George Sandys the traveller heard of him: 'Here a certaine Calabrian hearing that I was an Englishman, came to me, and would needs perswade me that I had insight in magicke: fo
2 CONC r that Earle Bothel was my countryman, who liues at Naples, and is in those parts famous for suspected negromancie.'[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from the HOME Tree:
2 CONT
2 CONT    Francis married Margaret DOUGLAS, daughter of David DOUGLAS, 7th Earl of Angus and Margaret HAMILTON.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Margaret DOUGLAS died in 1640.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Information from http://uk.geocities.com/nassarawa@btinternet.com/bothwell.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT    1st Earl of BOTHWELL, Francis STEART born 1563.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Son of John STEWART, 1st Lord DARNLEY (an illigitimate son of King James V) and Lady Jean HEPBURN born 1547 d 1599) daughter of Patrick HEPBURN, 3rd Earl of BOTHWELL, and so related to the previous holder. He succeeded to the t
2 CONC itle 2nd Lord DARNLEY in 1563 and was created 1st Earl of BOTHWELL and 1st Lord HAILES in 1591. In 1586 he was one of three Commissioners sent to negotiate a military alliance with England. He was made Lord High Admiral in 1583, a
2 CONC  post that proved eventful, as he was the holder during the period of the Spanish Armada, and spent some considerable time at sea. In 1589 he and several others, including HUNTLY, were charged with treason and plotting to seize th
2 CONC e King. They were found guilty, but sentence was deferred. BOTHWELL was also charged with witchcraft, though he denied it. He escaped from Edinburgh Castle, but was declared forfeit and outlaw. The next year BOTHWELL and others at
2 CONC tempted to capture the king from Falkland Palace, but failed. He was then pursued as a fugitive and all his lands taken into the Crown's Possession. In 1593 he was formally attainted by Act of Parliament, but managed to smuggle hi
2 CONC mself into Holyrood Palace to meet the King, who accepted his petitions, whereupon the  attainder was repealed. However, there was still bad feeling between the two men. Bothwell promoting the Protestand cause, and moves were made
2 CONC  to have him exiled. This resulted in BOTHWELL and his supporters taking to arms and clashing with the King's forces outside Edinburgh. He was now associated with the rebel Catholic lords Huntly, Errol and the like, and fled north
2 CONC  before the King's army, once again charged with treason.
2 CONT
2 CONT    He eventually left for Europe ending up in Naples, where he lived in poverty until his death. Because of his family connections, he could have been a realistic competitor for the throne, especially during the turbulent times ca
2 CONC used by the uneasy relationships between Protestant and Catholic, monarchy and Parliament, but he did not have the strength of character to take his chances when they arose. The title Earl of BOTHWELL was never used again.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT source ref:  http://www.genealogy.com/users/h/e/p/Ross-G-Hepburn/FILE/0001text.txt
2 CONT
