1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *''''''John Pringle1 
2 CONT *''''''M, #186331, d. before 1649
2 CONT *Last Edited=20 Sep 2008
2 CONT *''''''     John Pringle was the son of Robert Pringle, 1st of Stichill and Catherine Hamilton.2 He married Margaret Scott, daughter of Walter Scott, 1st Earl of Buccleuch.1 He died before 1649.3
2 CONT *''''''     He lived at Bartingbush.1
2 CONT *''''''Children of John Pringle and Margaret Scott
2 CONT ** 1.Sir Robert Pringle of Stichill, 1st Bt.+2
2 CONT ** 2.Walter Pringle2 d. a 1679
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 560. Hereinafter cited as Burke's P
2 CONC eerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT * 2.[S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT * 3.[S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition, volume 3, page 3221.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p18634.htm#i186331
2 CONT *_______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Pringle-527
2 CONT
2 CONT Scottish flag
2 CONT John (Pringle) Stichill was born in Scotland.
2 CONT John was the son of Robert Pringle of Stitchell [1] and Catherine Hamilton.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother James was baptized 4 July 1609.[3] He also has other siblings: Walter, Elanor, [4] Anna, and Christina.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT John married Margaret Scott on 31 August 1632.[1][5] Together they had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Robert
2 CONT # Walter of Graycrock
2 CONT # Francis of Rowiston
2 CONT # John
2 CONT # James
2 CONT # William
2 CONT # Catherine
2 CONT # Elizabeth[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 20 March 1637 John's father Robert was ratified as Baron of Stichill. John is here mentioned as Robert's eldest son and heir. This position granted sovereignty over the land of Stitchell "of all and whole the same lands of 
2 CONC Stichill, with the tower, fortalice, manor place, houses, buildings and other pertinents thereof foresaid, and of all and whole the kirk lands of Stichill particularly designed in the said charter, together with all and sundry the
2 CONC  teind sheaves and other teinds, parsonage and vicarage, fruits, rents, emoluments and duties of all and whole the parish and parish kirk of Stichill and whole lands lying therein, specially and generally mentioned therein, contai
2 CONC ning also a gift of novodamus, with a union of all in a whole and free barony, to be called the barony of Stichill."[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT John died in 1646 prior to his father's death, and thus, his son Robert became heir to the lands of Stichill.[2][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
