1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == WILLIAM, LORD CRANSTON ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William, Lord Cranstoun, here treated, is reported to have died in June 1627. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun02pauluoft/page/593/mode/1up The Scots Peerage II: 593] This is wrong. He was alive on 13 July 1628, whe
2 CONC n he and his son, John, Master of Cranstoun, gave their consent to the resignation of the lands and barony of Henderstoun by William Turnbull of Tour, [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=
2 CONC GD34%2f143&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=lord+cranstoun&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1630&di=n NRS: GD34/143] but he died before December 1628, by which date his son John had succeeded him as Lord Cranstoun. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.
2 CONC uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD26%2f3%2f1120&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=lord+cranstoun&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1630&di=n NRS: GD26/3/1120]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''13 July 1628: Instrument of Resignation by William Turnbull of Tour in Rowlwoode [Rulewood], with consent of William, Lord Cranstoun [Cranston], George, Lord Ramsay of Dalhoussie [Dalhousie], Sir John Cranstoun, Master of Crans
2 CONC toun, John Elphinstoun of Henderstoun, William Elphinstone, his son, and George Elphingstoun, brother of the said John, of the lands and barony of Henderstoun and the half lands and forest steading of Glensax, and of resignation b
2 CONC y Alexander Horsburghe [Horsburgh] of that Ilk and Philip Scott of Dryhoip [Dryhope] of the other half of the said lands and forest steading of Glensax, in the King's hands for new infeftment in favour of John Govane of Cardrono [
2 CONC Cardrona]. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD34%2f143&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=lord+cranstoun&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1630&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the family of Hay
2 CONC  of Haystoun, Peeblesshire, reference GD34/143]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note: A charter for the lands of Henderstoun, made in favour of John Govane, was registered under the Great Seal of Scotland on 13 July 1628. The consent of William, Lord Cranstoun, and his son John, Master of Cranstoun, is not
2 CONC ed. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi08scot/page/443/mode/1up RMS 1620-33: charter number 1288]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''December 1628: Decreet of Transumpt at instance of John, Lord Cranstoun of writs of lands of Eistquarter [Eastquarter] of Eistnisbit [East Nisbet]. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=
2 CONC GD26%2f3%2f1120&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=lord+cranstoun&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1630&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Leslie family, Earls of Leven and Melville, reference GD26/3/1120]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi08scot/page/n6/mode/1up Registrum Magni Sigilli Regum Scotorum. The Register of the Great Seal of Scotland. A. D. 1620-1633. Edited by John Maitland Thomson, MA., Advocate (H. M. Gen
2 CONC eral Register House, Edinburgh,1894), 1096 pp. including indexes and errata]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun02pauluoft/page/592/mode/1up The Scots Peerage II 592-4]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/cc4rz/cranstoun02.php Stirnet: Cranstoun 02]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>William Cranstoun, 1st Lord Cranstoun</strong> (died June 1627) was a Scottish Lord of Parliament, who played a prominent part in the pacification of the Anglo-Scottish border in the early 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cranstoun was the son of John Cranstoun of Morriestoun and grandson of Cuthbert Cranstoun of Thirlestanemains, belonging to one branch of a prominent Lowland family, whose estates had since at least the 14th century ( been centred
2 CONC  on Cranston (or "Cranstoun"), in Midlothian, and Denholm, in the Borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last Cranstoun of Cranstoun - Sir John, who was still alive in June 1609 - left five daughters, of whom William married the second, Sarah. Their contract of marriage was dated December 1580.
2 CONT
2 CONT King James appointed Cranstoun to the position of Captain of the Horse Garrison, or King's Guard, under the Earl of Dunbar, Lieutenant of the Borders. In 1605 he was keeper of Lochmaben Castle and a deputy-lieutenant of the Border
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cranstoun rapidly acquired a reputation for extreme ruthlessness. On one occasion, the inhabitants of Dumfries conspired unsuccessfully to assassinate him and he more than once received blanket pardons or indemnities, holding him 
2 CONC harmless in respect of prior atrocities. The summary approach adopted by Lord Dunbar and Cranstoun (hang first and try afterwards) became known as "Jeddart Justice".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1608, Cranstoun represented the small barons of Roxburghshire in the Convention of Estates. He was raised to the peerage, as Lord Cranstoun, by patent dated 19 November 1609. In 1611 he was promoted to succeed Lord Dunbar as Li
2 CONC eutenant of the Borders and was appointed to the Privy Council.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Balfour Paul's The Scots Peerage records of him that:
2 CONT >"He was a man of singular energy and fearlessness, and though his relentless rule on the Borders of necessity made him many enemies, yet his persistence therein brought back that region to a state of law and order such as it had 
2 CONC not known for many years, and from which it never really again lapsed."
2 CONT
2 CONT Cranstoun died in June 1627.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his wife, Cranstoun had at least seven children:
2 CONT * John Cranstoun, 2nd Lord Cranstoun (died in or before 1648)
2 CONT * James Cranstoun (died 1633), who married first Elizabeth Macgill and secondly Elizabeth Stewart, daughter of the Earl of Bothwell, and was banished from the country in August 1610
2 CONT * Henry Cranstoun (died after 1653), whowas a colonel of horse for Roxburghshire in 1644 and married Margaret Wauchope
2 CONT * Thomas Cranstoun
2 CONT * Agnes Cranstoun
2 CONT * Elizabeth Cranstoun, who married John Edgar, Younger of Wedderlie
2 CONT * Barbara (or Janet) Cranstoun, who married (contract 2 November 1615) John Seton of Touch."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'William Cranstoun, 1st Lord Cranstoun', Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 13 April 2010, 15:04 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=William_Cranstoun,_1st_Lord_Cranstoun&oldid=355765633
2 CONC > [accessed 7 April 2013]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
