1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Life Summary of David Carnagie
2 CONT
2 CONT When First Earl David Carnagie was born in October 1575, in Forfarshire, Scotland, his father, David Carnegie, was 42 and his mother, Euphane, was 42. He married Countess Margaret Lindsay on 24 November 1590. They were the parents
2 CONC  of at least 4 sons and 10 daughters. He died on 20 February 1658, in Kinnaird, Perthshire, Scotland, at the age of 82, and was buried in Kinnaird, Perthshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Carnegie Name Meaning:
2 CONT
2 CONT Scottish: habitational name from a place called Carnegie, near Carmyllie in Angus, probably named in Gaelic as cathair an eige ‘fort at the gap’.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dictionary of American Family Names © Patrick Hanks 2003, 2006.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: FamilySearch.Org
2 CONT Added to Geni by Janet Milburn 1/11/22
2 CONT  fifth cousin 12 times removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Knight in 1603.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Lord Carnegie of Kinnaird [Scotland] on 14 April 1616.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland] in January 1616/17.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Temporary President of the Scottish Council between April 1625 and May 1625.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Lord of Session from February 1625/26 to 1628.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Earl of Southesk [Scotland] on 22 June 1633.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Lord Carnegie of Kinnaird and Leuchars [Scotland] on 22 June 1633, with a special remainder to his heirs male for ever.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Sheriff of Forfarshire in 1646.
2 CONT
2 CONT He has an extensive biographical entry in the Dictionary of National Biography.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Southesk
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Question book-new.svg
2 CONT  This article does not cite any references or sources. Use as a basis for Research.   Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (April 2007)  
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Southesk is a title in the Peerage of Scotland. It was created in 1633 for Sir David Carnegie, an Extraordinary Lord of Session. He had already been created Lord Carnegie of Kinnaird in 1616 and was made Lord Carnegie, of 
2 CONC Kinnaird and Leuchars, at the same time he was given the Earldom. These titles are also in the Peerage of Scotland. The Earldom is named after the River South Esk in Angus. Carnegie’s younger brother John Carnegie was given the co
2 CONC rresponding title: Earl of Northesk. The Earl of Southesk also holds the Scottish feudal title of Baron of Kinnaird and is a baronet in the Baronetage of Nova Scotia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Earl of Southesk’s great-great-grandson, the fifth Earl, was involved in the Jacobite rising of 1715. As a result he was attainted by Act of Parliament and his titles and estates forfeited. The representation of the fami
2 CONC ly then passed to his third cousin Sir James Carnegie, 3rd Baronet, of Pittarrow (now recognized as the de jure 6th Earl of Southesk; for earlier history of the baronetcy, see below). In contrast to his cousin, he fought on the si
2 CONC de of King George II during the Jacobite rebellion, and later purchased his cousin’s forfeited estates. Carnegie also sat as a Member of Parliament for Kincardineshire. His son, the fourth Baronet and de jure seventh Earl, also re
2 CONC presented this constituency in the House of Commons. His son, the fifth Baronet and de jure eighth Earl, briefly represented Aberdeen in Parliament. His son, the sixth Baronet and de jure 9th Earl, obtained a reversal of the attai
2 CONC nder in 1855 and became the ninth Earl of Southesk. Lord Southesk notably served as Lord Lieutenant of Kincardineshire. In 1869 he was created Baron Balinhard, of Farnell in the County of Forfar, in the Peerage of the United Kingd
2 CONC om. This title gave the Earls an automatic seat in the House of Lords. His grandson, the eleventh Earl, married Princess Maud of Fife, the second daughter of Alexander Duff, 1st Duke of Fife and Princess Louise, eldest daughter of
2 CONC  King Edward VII. Princess Maud and her elder sister Princess Alexandra were in special remainder to the Dukedom of Fife created for their father in 1900, and on the Duke’s death in 1912 Alexandra succeeded as second Duchess in he
2 CONC r own right. On her death in 1959, the titles passed to her nephew James Carnegie, Lord Carnegie, the only son of the eleventh Earl of Southesk and Princess Maud (who died in 1945), who became the third Duke. On his father’s death
2 CONC  in 1992, the Duke also succeeded as twelfth Earl of Southesk. The Earldom and minor titles are now subsidiary titles of the Dukedom of Fife, with the title of Earl of Southesk used as a courtesy title by the Duke’s eldest son and
2 CONC  heir apparent.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Carnegie Baronetcy, of Pittarrow in the County of Kincardine, was created in the Baronetage of Nova Scotia on 20 February 1663 for David Carnegie. He was the son of Hon. Sir Alexander Carnegie, fourth son of the first Earl of 
2 CONC Southesk. As mentioned above, his grandson, the third Baronet, became representative of the family after his cousin’s attainder in 1715.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earls of Southesk (1633)[edit] David Carnegie, 1st Earl of Southesk (1575–1658) James Carnegie, 2nd Earl of Southesk (born before 1583–1669) Robert Carnegie, 3rd Earl of Southesk (born before 1649–1688) Charles Carnegie, 4th Earl 
2 CONC of Southesk (1661–1699) James Carnegie, 5th Earl of Southesk (1692–1730) (attainted 1716) James Carnegie, de jure 6th Earl of Southesk (c. 1715–1765) David Carnegie, de jure 7th Earl of Southesk (1753–1805) James Carnegie, de jure
2 CONC  8th Earl of Southesk (1799–1849) James Carnegie, 9th Earl of Southesk (1827–1905) (restored in 1855) Charles Noel Carnegie, 10th Earl of Southesk (1854–1941) Charles Alexander Carnegie, 11th Earl of Southesk (1893–1992) James Geo
2 CONC rge Alexander Bannerman Carnegie, 3rd Duke of Fife, 12th Earl of Southesk (b. 1929)
2 CONT
2 CONT Currently the Duke's son David Carnegie uses the title by courtesy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Carnegie baronets, of Pittarrow
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms Or, an eagle, displayed, azure armed and beaked gules 
2 CONT
2 CONT Crest A dexter hand, holding a thunder-bolt proper 
2 CONT
2 CONT Supporters Two greyhounds, collared 
2 CONT
2 CONT Motto Dread God 
2 CONT
2 CONT Carnegie baronets, of Pittarrow (1663)[edit] Sir David Carnegie, 1st Baronet (born before 1674–1708) Sir John Carnegie, 2nd Baronet (1673–1729) Sir James Carnegie, 3rd Baronet (c. 1715–1765) (de jure 6th Earl of Southesk) Sir Davi
2 CONC d Carnegie, 4th Baronet (1753–1805) (de jure 7th Earl of Southesk) Sir James Carnegie, 5th Baronet (1799–1849) (de jure 8th Earl of Southesk) Sir James Carnegie, 6th Baronet (1827–1905 (restored as Earl of Southesk in 1855)
2 CONT
2 CONT see above for further holders of the baronetcy
2 CONT
