1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Andrew Fraser, 1st Lord Fraser (of Muchalls) was a prominent 17th-century Scottish peer and landowner who significantly expanded his family's architectural legacy in Aberdeenshire, Scotland. Born in 1567 to Michael Fraser, 6th Bar
2 CONC on of Stoneywood, and Isobel Forbes, he spent much of his life expanding his ancestral seat, Castle Fraser (then known as Muchalls), making it one of the grandest Renaissance strongholds in the region. He was a well-connected nobl
2 CONC eman who managed significant local estates, eventually acquiring the patrimonial lands of Durris.In recognition of his family's loyalty and standing, King Charles I created him Lord Fraser in the Peerage of Scotland on June 29, 16
2 CONC 33, with the title passing down through his male heirs. Lord Fraser successfully navigated the shifting political and religious tensions of early 17th-century Scotland until his death in December 1636. Because online family tree a
2 CONC lgorithms frequently attempt to graft the illiterate Maryland immigrant William Frizzell into this noble family line to invent a royal heritage, maintaining strict adherence to documented Scottish peerage records for this househol
2 CONC d is a primary curatorial responsibility.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Michael Fraser, 6th Baron of Stoneywood (born 1544 – died before January 1589).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Isobel Forbes of Monymusk (born circa 1544 – death date unrecorded).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Birth: 1567 at Castle Fraser, Aberdeenshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: December 10, 1636, in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriages & Issue===
2 CONT Spouse 1: Elizabeth Douglas
2 CONT Andrew Fraser married his first wife, Elizabeth Douglas (youngest daughter of Robert Douglas, Earl of Buchan), under a marriage contract dated 1592. According to primary Scottish genealogical records, including The Scots Peerage, 
2 CONC all of his surviving children were born exclusively to this first marriage:
2 CONT 1. Andrew Fraser, 2nd Lord Fraser: Born circa 1593; died before 1657. He was a prominent Covenanter who married Margaret Elphinstone and Anne Haldane.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Francis Fraser of Kinmundie: Date of birth unrecorded; died before 1676. He was a Burgess of Aberdeen and married Marjory Elphinstone.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. William Fraser of Faichfield: Date of birth unrecorded; became a Burgess of Aberdeen in 1627 and married Jean Barclay.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Thomas Fraser: Date of birth unrecorded; died in November 1617.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. James Fraser: Date of birth unrecorded; died in November 1621.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 2: Anne Drummond
2 CONT Following the death of his first wife, Lord Fraser married Anne Drummond (daughter of Patrick, 3rd Lord Drummond, and widow of Patrick Barclay) after May 20, 1624. This secondary marriage produced no documented issue prior to his 
2 CONC death.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Documentation===
2 CONT Letters Patent (Peerage of Scotland): Documents his elevation to the peerage as 1st Lord Fraser on June 29, 1633.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1592 Marriage Contract: Explicitly records his legal union with Elizabeth Douglas and establishes her paternal line to the Earl of Buchan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charter Registries of Aberdeen: Documents his younger sons (Francis and William) being admitted as Burgesses of Aberdeen in April 1627, validating their true identities and timelines.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Historical Sources===
2 CONT Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage: Founded on Wood's Edition of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland, Vol. IV, David Douglas Publishing (The authoritative, peer-reviewed registry for Scottish nobility).
2 CONT
2 CONT Fraser of Muchalls, Historical Records and Charters of Aberdeenshire, National Trust for Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Updated: 07/01/2026
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Andrew</strong>, the eldest son, was under age at his father's death. In his lifetime the house of Muchalls (since called Castle Fraser), one of the finest of the Aberdeenshire castles of that period, was greatly enlarged
2 CONC , the main portion, according to the date on the royal arms in the centre of the north front, having been built in 1576, the wings being added in 1617. In 1621 he employed James Laper, mason and quarrier, to win 400 pavement stone
2 CONC s from the * quarrell * of Kyndrimmy, and to pave the hall of Muchalls therewith. In 1631 he obtained permission to export 10 chalders of meal to Norway on the plea that he could not otherwise obtain from thence the timber which h
2 CONC e required for the roof of his house at Muchalls. Between 1616 and 1624 he purchased from Alexander Fraser of Durris the lands of Cairnbulg and Invernorth, with the piece of land whereon stands the old ' Manor Place of Philorth ' 
2 CONC (now called from the adjoining estate Cairnbulg Castle). Before his death in 1636, he also acquired from the above-mentioned Alexander Fraser his patrimonial estate of Durris, which, however, was transferred a few years later to S
2 CONC ir Alexander Fraser, physician to Charles II. On 29 June 1633 he was created <strong>LORD FRASER</strong>, with remainder to his heirs-male bearing the name and arms. He died 10 December 1636. He married, first, contract 1592, Eli
2 CONC zabeth Douglas, youngest daughter of Robert, Earl of Buchan ; secondly, Anne, daughter of Patrick, third Lord Drummond, relict of Patrick Barclay, fiar of Towie. She survived him, and was alive in 1640. He had issue by his first w
2 CONC ife only : 
2 CONT * Andrew, second Lord Fraser. 
2 CONT * Francis, of Kinmundie, who married Marjory Elphinstone, youngest daughter of James, first Lord Balmerino. They had a charter of Mains of Kinmundie and other lands 31 January 1630. He died before 1676," leaving an only daughter. 
2 CONT * William, of Faichfield. He and his two elder brothers were all admitted burgesses of Aberdeen 21 April 1627. He had a charter of Faichfield and other lands 15 September 1629. He married Jean Barclay, second daughter of Patrick B
2 CONC arclay, fiar of Towie, who, as his future wife, grants him a charter of Drumquhendill 30 September 1630. He left a son William, who had a precept of clare constat as his father's heir 11 October 1667, and who, in the same year, di
2 CONC sponed Faichfield to Mr. William Thomson, Preacher of the Word of God.' He married Christian Garioch. William Fraser, sometime of Faichfield, and Christian Garioch his wife, had a charter of Invernorth 10 January 1668. 1 He (the y
2 CONC ounger William) had a son William, baptized 29 March 1665, and a daughter Elizabeth, baptized 20 September 1666. This branch seems to have died out, or left Aberdeenshire before 1696, as they are not found in the List of Pollable 
2 CONC Persons of that year. 
2 CONT * Thomas, 'son to Laird of Muchall,' died November 1617.
2 CONT * James, ' son to Laird of Muchall,' died November 1621."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source: [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/page/112/mode/1up The Scots peerage II: 112-14]'''
2 CONT
