1 NAME John /Johnstone/ Knt.
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Johnstone
2 NSFX Knt.
2 _MARNM Johnstone
2 NICK John Johnstone of Johnstone and Dunskellie
2 NICK Johnstone Warden of West Marches
2 NICK Sir John Marquis Johnstone of Annandale of that Ilk
1 NAME Sir John Johnstone of that Ilk
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1544
2 ADDR
3 CITY Dunnkellie
3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 JUN 1587
2 ADDR
3 CITY Johnstone
3 STAE Scotland
1 OCCU Marquis of Annandale
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p1037.htm#i10370
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Johnstone of that Ilk was born before 1550.1 He was the son of James Johnstone of Johnstone, younger and Margaret Hamilton.1 He married Margaret Scott, daughter of Sir William Scott of Kirkurd and Buccleuch, younger and G
2 CONC rizel Bethune.1 He died on 5 June 1587.1
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Justice-General in 1570.1 He held the office of Warden of the West Marches from 1580 to 1581.1 He held the office of Warden of the West Marches between 1582 and 1585.1 He was appointed Knight in 1584.1 He liv
2 CONC ed at Johnstone, ScotlandG.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir John Johnstone of that Ilk and Margaret Scott
2 CONT
2 CONT # Elizabeth Johnstone+1
2 CONT # Margaret Johnstone1
2 CONT # Grizell Johnstone+1
2 CONT # Sara Johnstone2
2 CONT # Sir James Johnstone of Johnstone+1 b. c 1567, d. 6 Apr 1608
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Johnstone was zealous in public affairs, and for a time an ardent supporter of Queen Mary.  He was, however, obliged to submit to the king's government.  He was twice warden of the West Marches, the second time under the 
2 CONC government of James Stewart, Earl of Arran.  In 1584 he was made a knight.  In 1585 his house of Lochwood was burned by the Maxwells, along with his charter-chest, and all his family muniments.  
2 CONT
2 CONT .....the documents lost in the fire that destroyed the Tower of Lochwood destroyed the proof of the existence of the earliest known Johnstones, who can only be traced in the contemporary charters granted by the Bruces of Annandale
2 CONC s to which the Johnstones were frequent witnesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Introductions and Illustrations of the Annandale Family Book" by Sir William Fraser
2 CONT
2 CONT Tradition tells of a dreadful feud between the Johnstones and the Maxwells, a more numerous and powerful clan also on the border.  The crest of the family refers to the old legend, that on one occasion a Laird of Johnstone was imp
2 CONC risoned by a Maxell, and that the wife of the latter implored her husband to grant the request that she might send him one more meal, on the night before his execution.  The Maxwell consented, and after making all necessary arrang
2 CONC ements for the Johnstone's escpae, by furnishing horses and getting hold of the keys, she sent him, at midnight, a spur set with feathers, in a covered dish, to signify that he was to fly with his life.  The prisoner changed clote
2 CONC hs with the messenger and escaped.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Johnston had his house burned down in one of the Maxwell raids, when the latter sent him word he "had lighted a fire for the Lady Johnstone to put on her hood by."
2 CONT
2 CONT Some Prominent Virginia Families by Louise Pecquet du Bellet
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Aka Sir John Marquis Johnstone of Annandale of that Ilk.
