1 NOTE William, born 1637, was a prominent figure in Scottish politics serving as Justice-Genreal of Scotland, Extraordinary Lord of Session, High Treasurer of Scotland, and Constable and Governor of Edinburgh Castle. He rose to the titl
2 CONC e of Marquess
2 CONT of Queensberry in 1682. He was created Duke of Queensberry in 1685. He was responsible for the construction of Drumlanrig Castle between 1679 and 1691. Legend holds that the Duke was so outraged at the cost of the castle's constru
2 CONC ction he
2 CONT refused to live in it. He obtained the peerage of Earl of March for his second son, for whom he purchased Neidpath Castle, and was succeeded by his first born son, James, upon his death in 1695.
1 NOTE William, born 1637, was a prominent figure in Scottish politics serving as Justice-Genreal of Scotland, Extraordinary Lord of Session, High Treasurer of Scotland, and Constable and Governor of Edinburgh Castle. He rose to the titl
