1 NAME Archibald /Campbell/
2 GIVN Archibald
2 SURN Campbell
2 _MARNM Campbell
1 NAME Archibald Campbell, 7th Earl of Argyll
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 25 AUG 1576
2 ADDR
3 CITY Taynuilt
3 CTRY Scotland
1 BURI
2 DATE 1638
2 PLAC Kilmun Parish Church and Argyll Mausoleum, Kilmun, Cowie, Stirlingshire, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1638
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Scotland
1 OCCU 7th Earl of Argyll, Peer of the Realm
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} his death date appears as  between October 1638 and 29 November 1638
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archibald_Campbell,_7th_Earl_of_Argyll
2 CONT http://geneagraphie.com/getperson.php?personID=I36962&tree=1
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p1668.htm
2 CONT https://www.ccsna.org/earl-of-argyll
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/98102004/archibald_gruamach_campbell
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/9S72-FJW/archibald-campbell-7th-earl-of-argyll-1576-1638
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Campbell-10875
2 CONT >>Archibald first married Lady Agnes Douglas. They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Archibald Campbell, 1st Marquess of Argyll+ b. bt Aug 1605 - Apr 1607, d. 27 May 1661
2 CONT #Lady Anne Campbell+ b. 1594, d. 14 Jun 1638, who married George, second Marquis of Huntly.  They had issue.
2 CONT #Lady Annabella Campbell+ d. 1652, who married Robert Kerr, Second Earl Lothian. They had issue. [2]
2 CONT #Jean, who married to John Gordon, first Viscount Kenmure. They had issue. [2]
2 CONT #Mary, who married to Sir Robert Montgomery of Sklemorly. They had issue. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>Secondly he married Anne Cornwallis, daughter of Robert Cornwallis.  Marriage: 30 Nov 1608 Anne Cornwallis, daughter of Sir William Cornwallis, Knight, to Archibald Campbell, Earl of Argyll. They had two children:
2 CONT #James, who was created Lord Kintyre in 1622 and later Earl of Irvine. he died without issue. [2]
2 CONT #Mary, married James, Lord Rollo 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ='''Archibald Campbell, 7th Earl of Argyll'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald Campbell, 7th Earl of Argyll, also called "Gillesbuig Grumach", was a Scottish peer, politician, and military leader.
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell was the son of Colin Campbell, 6th Earl of Argyll. In 1594 he commanded royal troops at the Battle of Glenlivet against Catholic Rebels, especially the Gordons of Huntly.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24 July 1592, he married his first wife - Lady Agnes Douglas, daughter of the Earl of Morton. Together they had at least five children, including a son and heir. His nickname, "Gillesbuig Grumach" is the Gaelic for Archibald th
2 CONC e Grim. This presumably originates from his first wife and after her 14th-century ancestor, Archibald Douglas, 3rd Earl of Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his first wife about 1607 or 1608, he married Anne (Cornwallis), with whom he had at least four more children. In 1618 Archibald Campbell converted to Roman Catholicism, the religion of his new wife, from Presby
2 CONC terianism.[1] By 1619, he had surrendered his estates to his son, Archibald Campbell.[2] He was made a Knight of the Golden Fleece in 1624. He had announced his new religion from the Netherlands and, as a consequence, he was decla
2 CONC red a traitor in Edinburgh on 16 February 1619 and banned from his country. He was very supportive of his new religion even after he was allowed back in 1621. He was interested in military solutions in Ireland in 1622, but he was 
2 CONC unable to raise an army.[2] He and his wife returned to Britain and lived at Drury Lane in London having abandoned everything apart from his title to his heir.[2] The 7th Earl of Argyll is buried at Kilmun Parish Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''References:''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marshall, R. (22 September 2005). Cornwallis, Anne, countess of Argyll (d. 1635), Roman Catholic benefactor and supposed author. Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. Retrieved 8 Dec. 2017, from link
2 CONT  Callow, J. (23 September 2004). Campbell, Archibald, seventh earl of Argyll (1575/6–1638), magnate and politician. Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. Retrieved 8 Dec. 2017, from link Prebble, John, The Lion in the North: on
2 CONC e thousand years of Scotland's history, London, 1971
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archibald_Campbell,_7th_Earl_of_Argyll]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald Campbell, 7th Earl of Argyll was born in 1575. He was the son of Colin Campbell, 6th Earl of Argyll and Lady Anne Keith. He married, firstly, Lady Agnes Douglas, daughter of William Douglas, 6th Earl of Morton and Lady A
2 CONC gnes Leslie, on 24 July 1592. He married, secondly, Anne Cornwallis, daughter of Sir William Cornwallis and Lucy Neville, on 30 November 1610 at St. Botolph's, Bishopsgate, London, EnglandG. He died between 9 October 1638 and 29 N
2 CONC ovember 1638 at London, England. His will was proven (by probate) on 29 November 1638.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded as the 8th Lord Campbell [S., 1445] on 10 September 1584. He succeeded as the 7th Earl of Argyll [S., 1457] on 10 September 1584. He succeeded as the 7th Lord Lorne [S., 1470] on 10 September 1584. He fought in the Ba
2 CONC ttle of Glenlivat on 3 October 1594, where he was completely defeated by the Catholic Lords, Huntly and Erroll. He held the office of Justice-General [Scotland]. In 1617 after expelling the Macdonalds of Kintyre, he received a gra
2 CONC nt of the whole of that Lordship and the island of Jura. In 1618 he served under Philip III of Spain against Holland. In 1618 he became a Roman Catholic. On 16 February 1618/19 at Market Cross, Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland, he 
2 CONC was declared a rebel and traitor. On 22 November 1621 his sentence was reversed. His last will was dated 9 October 1638. He has an extensive biographical entry in the Dictionary of National Biography
