1 OCCU 1st Marquis of Argyll, 8th Earl of Argyll, Marquess of Argyll (8th Earl), MARQUIS  OF  ARGYLL, Peer of the Realm
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archibald_Campbell,_1st_Marquess_of_Argyll
2 CONT
2 CONT "Archibald Campbell, 1st Marquess of Argyll, 8th Earl of Argyll, chief of Clan Campbell, (1607 – 27 May 1661) was the de facto head of government in Scotland during most of the conflict known as the Wars of the Three Kingdoms, als
2 CONC o known as the British Civil War. He was a major figure in the Covenanter movement that fought for the Presbyterian religion and what they saw as Scottish interests during the English Civil War of the 1640s and 1650s. He is often 
2 CONC remembered as the arch-enemy of the royalist general James Graham, 1st Marquess of Montrose."
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *   1. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, E
2 CONC xtinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume IV, page 438. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *   2. [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 203.
2 CONT *   3. [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 204.
2 CONT *   4. [S18] Matthew H.C.G., editor, Dictionary of National Biography on CD-ROM (Oxford, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1995), Campbell, Archibald. Hereinafter cited as Dictionary of National Biography.
2 CONT *   5. [S8] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 106th edition, 2 volumes (Crans, Switzerland: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 1999), volume 1, page 104. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peerage and Barone
2 CONC tage, 10
2 CONT
