1 NAME James /Maxwell/
2 GIVN James
2 SURN Maxwell
2 _MARNM Maxwell
1 NAME James Maxwell, 1st Earl of Dirletoun.
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1576
2 ADDR
3 CITY Edinburgh
3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 APR 1650
2 ADDR
3 CITY Edinburgh
3 STAE Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>James Maxwell</strong>, the third son, first appears in the records as witnessing a charter of James Murray of Cockpule 11 May 1606. He must have entered the King's household as a young man, for on 5 October in the same y
2 CONC ear he got, as <em>sanctioris cubiculi regis admissionalis palatinus</em>, together with Robert Douglas <em>hippocomus</em> to the King's eldest son, a grant of the lands of Tarres and others which were erected into a barony; 
2 CONT * on 15 January 1609 he had a charter of the lands of Newbellie and others in the county of Dumfries; 
2 CONT * another, on 24 August 1616, along with Sir Robert Douglas, of the lands and barony of Mortonwoods in Annandale, which they subsequently resigned; 
2 CONT * on 29 June 1621 he had a charter of the lands of Culcreuch and others; 
2 CONT * on 11 August 1622 he purchased from William, Lord Crichton of Sanquhar, half the lands of Ballegerno, Abernyte, and others, co. Perth, with remainder to the heirs-male of his body, whom failing, to the heirs of the late Robert M
2 CONC axwell of Kirkhouse, his father; 
2 CONT * on 30 January 1623 he had a charter from the King to himself and his wife of the teinds of the parish of Innerwick, and another on the same day from the Prince of the lands and barony of Innerwick on the resignation of Sir Alexa
2 CONC nder Hamilton; 
2 CONT * on 20 February 1623 he had a grant of the town and lands of Lochmaben; 
2 CONT * on 22 May 1630 he had a confirmation of the lands of Innerwick, erecting the town of Skaitraw into a burgh of barony; he had also a charter on 4 June 1631 of the lands and barony of Felton, Dirleton, and others, including the la
2 CONC nds of Kingston and Elbotle, and had Dirleton itself erected into a burgh of barony; 
2 CONT * on 13 October 1634 he had a charter to himself and others, granting them the privilege of trading on the west coast of Africa; 
2 CONT * on 27 June 1635 another of certain lands of Dirleton; 
2 CONT * on 10 January 1636 he, with other co-adventurers, obtained a lease of all the minerals in Scotland for period of twenty-one years;
2 CONT * on 22 April in the same year he and others had a charter authorising them to erect a lighthouse on the Isle of May, with the right of exacting a duty of ten shillings a ton on Scottish, and four shillings a ton on English ships;
2 CONC
2 CONT * on 13 September 1641 he and his wife had another confirmation of Innerwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her arms are on the Dirleton pew in Dirleton church - viz. a large cross moline between four smaller. The Earl's arms, both on that pew and on the exterior of Dirleton church, are the Maxwell saltire charged with thistles...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...He married, previous to 1628, Elizabeth or Bessie Besyne or Bowssie or Busson de Podolsko, and had by her (who was buried at St. Martins-in-the-Fields 20 April 1659) two daughters."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft#page/128/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. III, page 128-130
