1 NOTE {geni:about_me} there is no surviving, published record that gives a precise marriage date for John Maxwell of Kirkconnell (d. after 29 June 1614) and Marion Murray of Cockpool. We can, however, narrow it to a pretty tight window 
2 CONC around 1568–1572, with a best single-year estimate of about 1569–1570.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Who she is (to anchor the date)
2 CONT
2 CONT Scots Peerage and related material on the Murrays of Cockpool are clear that:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marion was a daughter of Charles (Sir James) / Charles Murray of Cockpool and Margaret Somerville.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is explicitly listed as “Marion, married to John Maxwell of Kirkconnell, and had issue.” 
2 CONT Internet Archive
2 CONT +1
2 CONT
2 CONT So the pairing is solid; it’s only the when that’s missing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Given Charles Murray of Cockpool’s birth around 1528 and death in 1605, his daughters’ marriages mostly fall in the late 16th / very early 17th century. Marion, whose husband is of an older cadet line, is likely one of the earlier
2 CONC -married daughters, not one of the very late ones (those we see marrying into Macbrair, Maxwell of Crustane, Butterwell, etc.).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. John Maxwell’s timeline
2 CONT
2 CONT From the History of Maxwells of Kirkconnel and related syntheses:
2 CONT
2 CONT John (this John of Kirkconnell) is retoured heir male to his father in the lands of Kirkconnell on 8 July 1574, and infeft in October 1574. 
2 CONT FamilySearch
2 CONT
2 CONT This implies he is already of age (or at least approaching it) by 1574 – so born c. 1550 (your working date) is reasonable and consistent with the narrative.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a laird in this milieu, marriage normally occurs roughly mid-20s to early 30s – often just before or around the time of succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Children of John & Marion – dating from below
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical reconstructions (Geni, Wikitree, FamilySearch, etc. – all secondary but mutually reinforcing) list children of John & Marion such as:
2 CONT
2 CONT James Maxwell of Littlebar, specifically described as son of John Maxwell of Kirkconnell and Marion (Murray) Maxwell, with an approximate birth around 1570. 
2 CONT Geni
2 CONT
2 CONT A John Maxwell (their son, later of Whitehill/Millhill in some accounts), often given a birth around 1577. 
2 CONT FamilySearch
2 CONT
2 CONT All are late-16th-century births, and they are already functioning as adults by the early 1600s, which fits those approximate dates.
2 CONT
2 CONT If we accept James (~1570) as the likely eldest or one of the eldest sons, then the marriage customarily would be:
2 CONT
2 CONT A year or two before that first birth, placing the marriage comfortably around 1568–1569 (allowing a little wiggle room either way).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Bracketing the window
2 CONT
2 CONT Putting it together:
2 CONT
2 CONT Earliest plausible date:
2 CONT Marion needs to be old enough to marry; if she was born mid-1550s (very plausible as a daughter of Charles Murray b. 1528), then before about 1565 is unlikely. 
2 CONT geneapedia.org
2 CONT
2 CONT Latest plausible date:
2 CONT With a child probably born c. 1570 and John already appearing as a substantial man by the early 1570s, a marriage after 1572 becomes increasingly implausible.
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession date (1574 retour and infeftment) indicates that the marriage almost certainly pre-dates or is very close to that year. 
2 CONT FamilySearch
2 CONT
2 CONT A cautious bracket is therefore:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage window: c. 1568–1572, most likely around 1569–1570.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Do any printed sources give an explicit contract date?
2 CONT
2 CONT From what’s available online:
2 CONT
2 CONT Scots Peerage notes the marriage but does not give a contract date; its references for this branch are the Book of Caerlaverock and the Mansfield (Cockpool/Annandale) charter-chest, which are mostly land/entail instruments. 
2 CONT Internet Archive
2 CONT
2 CONT The Maxwells of Kirkconnel history you and I have been using is detailed on sasines and retours, but again does not print a marriage contract for John & Marion. 
2 CONT FamilySearch
2 CONT
2 CONT If a formal contract survives, it’s likely buried in:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annandale / Murray of Cockpool / Mansfield charter-chest,
2 CONT
2 CONT Or unindexed protocol books / deed registers (e.g., Dumfries protocols),
2 CONT
2 CONT and hasn’t been widely quoted in modern printed summaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bottom line
2 CONT
2 CONT So, with the evidence we can see:
2 CONT
2 CONT No exact date is documented in the standard printed or digitised sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT On balance of the succession dates and the children’s estimated births:
2 CONT
2 CONT I would cite their marriage in your work as “c. 1570”, and if you want a slightly more explicit phrasing,
2 CONT
2 CONT “married by about 1570 (probably late 1560s)”.
