1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/45466479/person/232715984428
2 CONT
2 CONT New best estimate of birth date: 1576/78. Based on age of Herbert and John of Whitehall and marriage of parents. 
2 CONT
2 CONT can’t find any source that gives a precise marriage date for James Maxwell, Esq. of Littlebar and Nichola (Nichola/Nicola) Maxwell of Littlebar, but we can bracket it pretty well. A sensible working estimate is:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage c. 1587–1589, certainly by 20 January 1598, and almost certainly by about 1590.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here’s how that falls out:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Who they are (to make sure we’ve got the right couple)
2 CONT
2 CONT Online reconstructions (Geni, etc.) consistently show:
2 CONT
2 CONT James Maxwell, Esq. of Littlebar, son of John Maxwell of Kirkconnell and Marion (Murray) Maxwell, with birth around 1570. 
2 CONT Geni
2 CONT
2 CONT Nichola/Nicola Maxwell, daughter and co-heiress of John Maxwell of Littlebar and his wife Mariota, with an approximate birth c. 1565. 
2 CONT Geni
2 CONT
2 CONT These two are paired as husband and wife and parents of James Edward Maxwell of Littlebar. 
2 CONT Geni
2 CONT +1
2 CONT
2 CONT Nisbet’s System of Heraldry confirms the line in heraldic form, calling Colonel Thomas Maxwell “son to James Maxwell of Littlebar, who was a second son of Maxwell of Kirkonell,” so we’re clearly in the right branch. 
2 CONT Internet Archive
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. The key dated anchor: 20 January 1598
2 CONT
2 CONT Kerlie’s History of the Lands and their Owners in Galloway (Troqueer / Kirkconnell section) gives us a crucial datum for Nichola:
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 January 1598, John Maxwell of Kirkconnell “gave succession of the land of Kelton to Catherine and Nicolas, daughters and heirs of John Maxwell of Littlebar.” 
2 CONT Internet Archive
2 CONT
2 CONT This tells us:
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1598, Nichola is already an acknowledged co-heiress of Littlebar.
2 CONT
2 CONT That 1598 disposition is almost certainly bound up with the Kirkconnell–Littlebar consolidation: John of Kirkconnell uses Kelton to secure/compensate the Littlebar co-heiresses, one of whom is married to his son James.
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether Nichola was already married to James on or before 20 Jan 1598 isn’t explicitly said (Scottish deeds often identify women by their own name and paternal link even after marriage), but the political logic strongly suggests t
2 CONC he marriage was not later than this arrangement; more likely it triggered or accompanied it.
2 CONT
2 CONT So: they are married by 1598 at the latest.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Dating from below: their son James Edward Maxwell
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son James Edward Maxwell of Littlebar is given a death in 1659, with an estimated age span that implies a birth around 1586–1595, usually rounded to about 1590. 
2 CONT Geni
2 CONT
2 CONT If we take:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth of James Edward ≈ 1588–1592,
2 CONT
2 CONT then, under normal patterns:
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage of his parents would be a year or two before his birth,
2 CONT
2 CONT which puts it very comfortably in the late 1580s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nichola’s own approximate birth (c. 1565) plays nicely with that:
2 CONT
2 CONT If she marries in, say, 1587–1589, she is about 22–24.
2 CONT
2 CONT James (c.1570) would be about 17–19 – young, but not implausible for a carefully arranged match between important cadet lines, especially where an heiress is involved.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Bracketing the likely window
2 CONT
2 CONT Putting all of those pieces together:
2 CONT
2 CONT Earliest plausible:
2 CONT They probably did not marry much before 1585 – James would still be mid-teens and not yet clearly established; Nichola’s father is still alive and in possession.
2 CONT
2 CONT Latest plausible:
2 CONT They are almost certainly married before 1598 (kelton disposition to Catherine and Nichola) and almost certainly by around 1590 (to allow for a son of that approximate birth year). 
2 CONT Internet Archive
2 CONT +1
2 CONT
2 CONT So a realistic bracket is:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage window: roughly 1585–1595
2 CONT Most likely band: 1587–1589
2 CONT Safe citation formula: “m. by c. 1590 (probably late 1580s).”
2 CONT
2 CONT If you want a single year for a narrative, I’d pick “c. 1588” with a footnote explaining it’s inferred from their son’s birth range and the 1598 Kelton charter rather than from a surviving marriage contract.
