1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''''''FERMOR, Sir John (by 1516-71), of Easton Neston, Northants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Education
2 CONT *b. by 1516, 1st son of Richard Fermor, and brother of Jerome† and Thomas. 
2 CONT *educ. I. Temple. 
2 CONT *m. by Nov. 1544, Maud, daughter of Sir Nicholas Vaux, 1st Lord Vaux of Harrowden, 3 sons 3 daughters 
2 CONT *succeeded father 17 Nov. 1551. 
2 CONT *Kntd. 2 Oct. 1553.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT *Keeper of woods within Rockingham forest, Northamptonshire Apr. 1554; 
2 CONT *Justice of the Peace in Northamptonshire by 1556-64 or later; 
2 CONT *sheriff 1557-8.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT In common with his Vaux kin Fermor remained a Catholic and it was only under Mary that he cut any figure in public life. He probably took his stand for her during the brief conflict in Northamptonshire over the succession, as his 
2 CONC brother-in-law Sir John Mordaunt did in East Anglia, and so earned the knighthood which he received on the morrow of the coronation. It was, too, his Protestant cousin (Sir) Nicholas Throckmorton whom he replaced as senior knight 
2 CONC of the shire in the first Parliament of the reign; not surprisingly, he neither ‘stood for the true religion’ on this occasion against the government’s first measures towards restoring Catholicism nor opposed one of its bills in 1
2 CONC 555. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brought on to the commission of the peace and granted several local offices, in the last year of the reign he was pricked sheriff: in this capacity he corresponded with the Privy Council about the surety to be taken from subsidy c
2 CONC ollectors. Under Elizabeth his religious conservatism told against him and he may have been removed from the bench after his inclusion, in the report of 1564 on the attitudes of the local justices, among ‘great letters [hinderers]
2 CONC  of religion’.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fermor’s main interest throughout his life was his estates. These were augmented in 1556 by the Northamptonshire property of his uncle William Fermor, whose Oxfordshire lands, however, passed to Fermor’s younger brother Thomas. Fe
2 CONC rmor appears as an enterprising, indeed grasping, landowner. There had been trouble with neighbours over disputed property in Easton Neston before his father’s death, and when the matter went to arbitration he rejected the decisio
2 CONC n that he should retain the lands in question but give others in compensation, and persuaded his mother to refuse her consent. He was unscrupulous in his attempts to evict tenants of whom he disapproved, some of them probably beca
2 CONC use they had been given leases when the property was in the King’s hands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in Elizabeth’s reign he purchased the manor of Towcester, Northamptonshire, from Laurence Eaton, and other properties there of which one was later claimed by plaintiffs on the ground of defective title. At the same time as h
2 CONC e acquired the hundred of Wimersley he broke the entail on his Dorset and Somerset lands in order to create life tenures. When his heir George married in 1570 Fermor conveyed his lands to William, 3rd Lord Vaux of Harrowden, Sir W
2 CONC alter Mildmay and others in order to entail the bulk of them and to make provision for his younger children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on to Dec. 1571 and was buried at Easton Neston. George Fermor had licence to enter on 17 June 1572.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1509-1558
2 CONT
2 CONT Author: S. M. Thorpe
