1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Major Jasper Farmar'''
2 CONT * BIRTH	1610 - Somerset, England
2 CONT * DEATH	1685 (aged 74–75) - At Sea
2 CONT * BURIAL	Buried or Lost at Sea, Specifically: Atlantic Ocean 
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID	179130191 · View Source
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/179130191/jasper-farmar
2 CONT
2 CONT Jasper Farmar was born in 1610, in Somerset, Devon, England, to '''Robert Fermor''' 1575, in Easton Neston, Northamptonshire, England, and '''Mary Boles aka Bolles ''' was born about 1582 .
2 CONT .Robert and Mary (Jasper's parents, had 3 children
2 CONT # Jasper Fermor
2 CONT # Alice Scull (born Fermor) and 
2 CONT # John Fermor
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Jasper married Mary Gamble in 1649 - Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT They supposedly had 10 children (not all confirmed yer: 
2 CONT # Jasper Farmar, Jr, , 
2 CONT # Sarah Farmer , 
2 CONT # John Farmer , 
2 CONT # Katherine Farmer , 
2 CONT # Robert Farmer , 
2 CONT # Charles Farmer , 
2 CONT # Richard Farmer , 
2 CONT # Samuel Farmer , 
2 CONT # Webber Farmer and 
2 CONT # Edward Farmer .
2 CONT
2 CONT Maj. Jasper Farmar was a British officer living in Munster, Ireland, the son of Robert Farmar and Mary Bolles, daughter of the Lord Mayor of London. (A letter from Sir John Boles to Sir Robert Cecil describes Robert Farmar as his 
2 CONC brother-in-law.)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Penn granted to him tracts of land in Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania totaling more than 5,000 acres. Maj. Farmar and his large family along with 20 servants sailed to America on the ship BRISTOL MERCHANT.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Genealogies of Pennsylvania Families from the Pennsylvania Genealogical Magazine, p. 695:''
2 CONT "Jasper Farmar, Gentleman, of Ardevalaine, County Tipperary, Ireland, a Major in the British Army, (said to have been son of one Robert Farmar slain in battle in Ireland in 1616, and grandson of Sir George Fermor, Knt., of Easton 
2 CONC Neston, Northamptonshire, England), died testate at sea, onboard the ship BRISTOL MERCHANT. The vessel was bound for Philadelphia and arrived at that port 10th 9th month (November) of 1685. Major Farmar had agreed with William Pen
2 CONC n in 1682 for the purchase of 5,000 acres, to be laid out in Pennsylvania, which had been patented 31 January 1683/4 to the Major and his sons Richard Farmar and Jasper Farmar, Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Major's widow '''Mary Gamble''', 
2 CONT (confirmed) Children"
2 CONT # Edward, 
2 CONT # John, 
2 CONT # Robert and 
2 CONT # Charles Farmar, s 
2 CONT # Katherine and 
2 CONT # Jaspar Farmar, jr., of Garranekinnefeake, County Cork, died with him on the voyage
2 CONT # Sarah Farmar, 
2 CONT
2 CONT son-in-law Thomas Webb, and step-son Edward Batsford, who had set sail from Ireland with him, all arrived in the said ship. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote: "From the seal after the signature to the will of Major Jasper Farmar, 1685. These are the same arms as those of the family of Fermor, Earls of Pomfret, which title became extinct in 1867. See Sir Bernard Burke, GENERAL 
2 CONC ARMORY OF ENGLAND, SCOTLAND AND WALES (London, 1884), 340, 347."
2 CONT Note: The Fermor/Farmar seal, here referred to, was on the will of Mary Farmar, widow of Maj. Jasper Farmar.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is most commonly believed that Maj. Farmar and his son Jasper Farmer Jr. were buried at sea, rather than interred at St. Thomas Church where his widow Mary is thought to be buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT ANGEVINE-BOLLES-FARMAR
2 CONT The Bolles descent of the Farmars is given:
2 CONT John Bolles
2 CONT +Mary Angevine, d/o Wm Angevyne & Eleanor DeAlderby
2 CONT Wm Boles 1415-
2 CONT +Anne Kyme
2 CONT John Bolles
2 CONT +Katherine Haugh
2 CONT Godfrey Bolles of Bosberton, Co Lincoln d1532
2 CONT + Mrs. Bolles
2 CONT Thomas Bolles of Bosberkirke, Lincoln
2 CONT +Jane Winter
2 CONT George Bolles, 1537-1621, Lord Mayor of London
2 CONT +Jane Harte
2 CONT Mary Bolles
2 CONT +Robert Farmar
2 CONT Jasper Farmar Sr.
2 CONT +Mary Gamble
2 CONT
2 CONT The Angevines/Angevynes of Lincolnshire came from Anjou, in France, soon after the Norman Conquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT additional information
2 CONT Maj. Jasper Farmar was a British officer living in Munster, Ireland, the son of Robert Farmar and Mary Bolles, daughter of the Lord Mayor of London. (A letter from Sir John Boles to Sir Robert Cecil describes Robert Farmar as his 
2 CONC brother-in-law.) William Penn granted to him tracts of land in Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania, totaling more than 5,000 acres. Maj. Farmar and his large family along with 20 servants sailed to America on the ship BRISTOL MERCHA
2 CONC NT. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogies of Pennsylvania Families from the Pennsylvania Genealogical Magazine, p. 695: "Jasper Farmar, Gentleman, of Ardevalaine, County Tipperary, Ireland, a Major in the British Army, (said to have been son of one Robert Farm
2 CONC ar slain in battle in Ireland in 1616, and grandson of Sir George Fermor, Knt., of Easton Neston, Northamptonshire, England), died testate at sea, onboard the ship BRISTOL MERCHANT. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The vessel was bound for Philadelphia and arrived at that port 10th of the 9th month (September) of 1685. Major Farmar had agreed with William Penn in 1682 for the purchase of 5,000 acres, to be laid out in Pennsylvania, which had
2 CONC  been patented 31 January 1683/4 to the Major and his sons Richard Farmar and Jasper Farmar, Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Major's widow Mary, his sons Edward, John, Robert, and Charles Farmar, daughters Katherine and Sarah Farmar, son-in-law Thomas Webb, and step-son Edward Batsford, who had set sail from Ireland with him, all arrived in the said
2 CONC  ship. His son Jaspar Farmar, jr., of Garranekinnefeake, County Cork, Ireland, either had died in that place or on the same voyage to Pennsylvania."
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote: "From the seal after the signature to the will of Major Jasper Farmar, 1685. These are the same arms as those of the family of Fermor, Earls of Pomfret, which title became extinct in 1867. See Sir Bernard Burke, GENERAL 
2 CONC ARMORY OF ENGLAND, SCOTLAND AND WALES (London, 1884), 340, 347."
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: The Fermor/Farmar seal, here referred to, was on the will of Mary Farmar, widow of Maj. Jasper Farmar.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is most commonly believed that Maj. Farmar and his son Jasper Farmer Jr. were buried at sea, rather than interred at St. Thomas Church where his widow Mary is thought to be buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT ANGEVINE-BOLLES-FARMAR The Bolles descent of the Farmars is given:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bolles +Mary Angevine, d/o Wm Angevyne & Eleanor DeAlderby Wm Boles 1415- +Anne Kyme John Bolles +Katherine Haugh Godfrey Bolles of Bosberton, Co Lincoln d1532 + Mrs. Bolles Thomas Bolles of Bosberkirke, Lincon +Jane Winter G
2 CONC eorge Bolles, Lord Mayor 1537-1621 +Jane Harte Mary Bolles +Robert Farmar Jasper Farmar Sr. +Mary Gamble
2 CONT
2 CONT The Angevines/Angevynes of Lincolnshire came from Anjou, in France, soon after the Norman Conquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse	 Mary Gamble Farmar, 1635–1687
2 CONT
2 CONT Children	 
2 CONT Jasper Farmar, 1653–1685 
2 CONT John Farmer, 1664–1724	 E
2 CONT dward Farmar, 1672–1745
