1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lady Janet Campbell''' (styled as '''Lady MacLean''' during her first marriage and '''Lady MacLeod''' during her second) was a high-status 16th-century noblewoman from the House of Argyll whose marriages formed the core of West
2 CONC  Highland and Hebridean cross-channel alliances.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Birth and Maternal Lineage ===
2 CONT Janet was born circa 1538, likely at one of the principal seats of the Earldom of Argyll, such as Inveraray Castle or Dunoon Castle. She was the daughter of Archibald Campbell, 4th Earl of Argyll, and his second wife, Lady Margare
2 CONC t Graham (daughter of the 3rd Earl of Menteith). Because of her father’s successive marriages and outside relationships, her sibling cohort must be precisely demarcated to prevent identity conflation:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Full Siblings:''' Colin Campbell, 6th Earl of Argyll, and Lady Margaret Campbell (wife of James Stewart, 1st Lord Doune).
2 CONT * '''Maternal Half-Siblings:''' She is a paternal half-sister to Archibald Campbell, 5th Earl of Argyll (son of Lady Helen Hamilton), and to Lady Agnes Campbell (daughter of Janet Gordon).
2 CONT * '''The Identity Conflation:''' She must remain strictly decoupled from her older paternal half-sister, Lady Janet Campbell, Countess of Tyrconnell (wife of Calvagh O'Donnell), who was born to her father's first wife, Helen Hamil
2 CONC ton.
2 CONT
2 CONT === First Marriage: MacLean of Duart ===
2 CONT Janet married firstly, as his first wife, '''Hector Og MacLean, 13th Clan Chief of Duart''' (c. 1540 – c. 1573/4). This alliance tied the Campbells directly to the strategic maritime power of the MacLeans in the Inner Hebrides. Th
2 CONC is marriage produced her eldest son, the formidable Sir Lachlan Mòr MacLean, 14th Chief of Duart, alongside three daughters: Mary, Marian, and Janet MacLean.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the death of Hector Og in the early 1573, Janet held significant dower and liferent rights within the MacLean territories, which became a point of diplomatic negotiation for her next alliance.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Second Marriage: MacLeod of Dunvegan ===
2 CONT Following her first husband's death, Janet married secondly, by 1580, '''Tormod (Norman) MacLeod, 12th Chief of MacLeod''' (d. 1585). This alliance transferred her diplomatic weight to the Outer Hebrides and the Isle of Skye. The 
2 CONC marriage is securely anchored in the primary record by the Dunvegan Writs (Bundle 3, No. 1), which preserve a liferent charter dated December 3, 1583, in which Tormod MacLeod granted her extensive lands in Bracadale, Isle of Skye.
2 CONC  This marriage produced their daughter, Fionnvola (Florence) MacLeod.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Issue ===
2 CONT From her first marriage to Hector Og MacLean, 13th Chief of Duart:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sir Lachlan Mòr MacLean, 14th Chief of Duart''' (1558–1598); married Lady Margaret Cunningham, daughter of William Cunningham, 6th Earl of Glencairn.
2 CONT * '''Mary MacLean''' (c. 1560 – ?); married Angus MacDonald, 8th of Dunnyveg.
2 CONT * '''Marian MacLean''' (c. 1562 – ?); married Hector MacLean, 5th of Lochbuie.
2 CONT * '''Janet MacLean''' (c. 1565 – ?); married Roderick MacLeod, 1st of Tallisker.
2 CONT
2 CONT From her second marriage to Tormod MacLeod, 12th Chief of MacLeod:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Fionnvola (Florence) MacLeod''' (c. 1582 – ?); married firstly Hector MacLean, 15th of Duart, and married secondly Hector MacLean, 6th of Coll.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Heraldic Description ===
2 CONT As a married noblewoman of the 16th century, Lady Janet Campbell would bear an impaled shield (split vertically down the center) representing her marital alliances and paternal heritage. The sinister side (the viewer's right) alwa
2 CONC ys displays her paternal arms of the House of Argyll: a quarterly shield featuring the gold and black gyronny of eight triangles alternating with the black galley of Lorne.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Arms as Lady MacLean:''' The dexter side (the viewer's left) displays the quarterly arms of MacLean of Duart (a castle rock, a red hand holding a cross, a galley with sails furled, and a salmon).
2 CONT * '''Arms as Lady MacLeod:''' Following her second marriage, the dexter side displays the arms of MacLeod of Dunvegan (alternating quarters of a silver castle tower on a blue field and the three armed legs of the Isle of Man on a 
2 CONC red field).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Debunking the 1577 Wick Death Myth ===
2 CONT Many legacy online trees and unvouched databases erroneously record Janet’s death as occurring on February 12, 1577, at Wick, Caithness. This is a total chronological and geographic impossibility. The Campbells of Argyll, MacLeans
2 CONC  of Duart, and MacLeods of Dunvegan operated exclusively within the western maritime sphere; Wick sits on the northeastern tip of Scotland, entirely outside their geopolitical orbit. Furthermore, the 1583 Dunvegan land charter pro
2 CONC ves she was alive and legally active six years after this fictional death date. She ultimately died circa 1600 within the West Highland/Argyll sphere.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Primary and Archival Sources ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''The Scots Peerage:''' Founded on Wood's Edition of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul. Volume I (Argyll), pp. 339-342, provides the definitive marital chronology of Archibald, 4th Earl 
2 CONC of Argyll, establishing Margaret Graham as the mother of Colin and Janet. Text available at: [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/338/mode/2up])
2 CONT * '''The Dunvegan Writs / MacLeod Muniments:''' Bundle 3, No. 1 (Dated 3 December 1583). Liferent charter by Tormod MacLeod of MacLeod to his wife, Lady Janet Campbell, of lands in Bracadale, Isle of Skye. This primary instrument 
2 CONC definitively debunks the 1577 death date.
2 CONT * '''MacLean Historical Records:''' ''A History of the Clan MacLean from Its First Settlement at Duart Castle, or the Isle of Mull, to the Present Period'' (J.P. MacLean, 1889), detailing the family of Hector Og MacLean and his Ca
2 CONC mpbell marriage alignment. Text available at: [https://archive.org/details/historyofclanmac00macl/page/84/mode/2up])
2 CONT * '''The Clan Donald / O'Donnell Alliance Framework:''' Documents the cross-channel marital network of the 4th Earl of Argyll's daughters, confirming the distinct identity of her half-sister Janet, wife of Calvagh O'Donnell. Refer
2 CONC ence framework maintained via the official historical database at: [https://odomhnaill.com/]
2 CONT *[http://www.thepeerage.com/p50588.htm#i505877 Darryl Lundy's Peerage]
2 CONT *[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/mac/maclean02.php#linklo Stirnet: MacLean 02]
