1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == DAME ISOBEL HAMILTON Lady Seton ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Isobelle Hammiltoun (1550) Isobelle Hammyltoun (1552) Domine Isobelle Hammiltoune (1554) (1554) Isobelle Hammiltoune (1554) Dame Isobel Hammiltoun (1596) dame Isobel Hamiltoun, lady Seytoun,  conjunct fiar of barony
2 CONC  of West Nyddrie, called Wyncheburgh (1603)'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Isobel Hamilton was the daughter of Sir William Hamilton of Sorn and Sanquhar and Jean Campbell.  A contract for the marriage of Isabel Hamilton and George Seton 5th, Lord Seton was signed on 2 August 1550. She survived him, di
2 CONC ed 13 November 1604 aged about seventy-five years, and was buried at Seton Church. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun08pauluoft/page/588/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VIII: 588]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''7 April 1550: Charter by which George, Lord Seton, confirms that he has given certain lands in liferent to Isobelle Hammiltoun, daughter of William Hammiltoun of Sanquhar. His charter was confirmed under the Great Seal of Scotl
2 CONC and on 8 August 1550. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi04scot/page/115/mode/1up RMS 1546-80: charter number 497]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''23 May 1552: Charter under the Great Seal of Scotland which confirms possession of the lands and barony of West-Nudry to George, Lord Seton, and his wife Isobelle Hammyltoun. This land was to be held by them in conjunt fee and 
2 CONC liferent. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi04scot/page/155/mode/1up RMS 1546-80: charter number 697]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 August 1554: Charter issued under the Great Seal of Scotland, made in favour of George Seton, son and apparent heir of George, Lord Seton, which confirms possession of the lands and barony of West-Nudry to George Seton, junio
2 CONC r. George; Lord Seton, had previously resigned the barony in favour of his son, and reserved his own liferent and that of his wife, Lady Isobelle Hammiltoune. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi04scot/page/213/mode/1up R
2 CONC MS 1546-80: charter number 951]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 August 1554: Charter issued under the Great Seal of Scotland, made in favour of George Seton, son and apparent heir of George, Lord Seton, which confirms possession of the lands and barony of Seton and Winton to George Seton,
2 CONC  junior. George; Lord Seton, had previously resigned the barony in favour of his son, and reserved his own liferent and that of his wife, Lady Isobelle Hammiltoune. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi04scot/page/214/mode
2 CONC /1up RMS 1546-80: charter number 952]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     5
2 CONT
2 CONT '''12 August 1589: Charter by which Robert, Lord Seton, confirms that he has given the lands and barony of Seton and Winton in liferent to his wife Margaret Montgomery (following from a contract of marriage dated 1 August 1589). G
2 CONC eorge Lord Seton reserved the liferent interest of his mother, Lady Issobelle Hammyltoun: "Salvo vitali redditu Domine Issobelle Hammyltoun matri dicti Roberti dom. S." [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi05scot/page/589/
2 CONC mode/1up RMS 1580-93: charter number 1720]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2 March 1596: Extract contract by which John Hammiltoun of Drumry renounces the twenty pound lands thereof in favour of John Crawfurd of Kilbirnie and assigns a tack of the teinds by Dame Isobel Hammiltoun, Lady Seytoun, and Wi
2 CONC lliam Seytoun, her son. Registered B of C and S on same date. (With discharge by said John, dated 30 May 1596). [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD20%2f1%2f656&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n
2 CONC &k=isobel+hammiltoun&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1605&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Earls of Glasgow (Crawford Priory), reference GD20/1/656]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''15 January 1603: Disposition by dame Isobel Hamiltoun, lady Seytoun, conjunct fiar of barony of West Nyddrie, called Wyncheburgh, to Sir Walter Dundas of that ilk, kt., of nonentry maills of castle of Dundas, and lands of Plewl
2 CONC andfeild, in said barony, sheriffdom of Linlythqw, since death of George Dundas of that ilk. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD75%2f11&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=isobel+hamiltoun&ko=p
2 CONC &r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1605&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Dundas Family of Dundas, West Lothian, reference GD75/11]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi04scot/page/n6/mode/1up Registrum Magni Sigilli Regum Scotorum: The Register of the Great Seal of Scotland, A.D. 1546-1580. Edited by John Maitland Thomson, MA,, Advocate. (H,M, Ge
2 CONC neral Register House, Edinburgh, 1886), 1160 pp. including indices]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi05scot/page/n6/mode/1up Registrum Magni Sigilli Regum Scotorum: The Register of the Great Seal of Scotland, A.D. 1580-1593. Edited by John Maitland Thomson, MA,, Advocate. (H,M, Ge
2 CONC neral Register House, Edinburgh, 1888), 1154 pp. including indices]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun08pauluoft/page/588/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VIII: 588]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://thepeerage.com/p11023.htm#i110228 Biographical Summary for Isobel Hamilton from Darryl Lundy's Peerage] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT *Isabel Hamilton1 
2 CONT * http://thepeerage.com/p11023.htm#i110228
2 CONT * F, #110228, b. circa 1529, d. 13 November 1604
2 CONT * Last Edited=6 Jun 2020
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Hamilton was born circa 1529.2 She was the daughter of William Hamilton of Sanquhar and Jean Campbell.1,3 A contract for the marriage of Isabel Hamilton and George Seton, 5th Lord Seton was signed on 2 August 1550.4,5 She d
2 CONC ied on 13 November 1604.2 From 2 August 1550, her married name became Seton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Isabel Hamilton and George Seton, 5th Lord Seton
2 CONT
2 CONT # George Seton, Master of Seton5 d. Mar 1562
2 CONT # Sir William Seton+5 d. Jul 1635
2 CONT # Margaret Seton+1 b. bt 1551 - 1558, d. b 10 Feb 1615/16
2 CONT # Robert Seton, 1st Earl of Winton+5 b. 1552, d. 22 Mar 1603
2 CONT # Sir John Seton of Barns5 b. b 1555, d. b 11 Jun 1594
2 CONT # Alexander Seton, 1st Earl of Dunfermline+5 b. 1555, d. 16 Jun 1622
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Seton,_7th_Lord_Seton Biograhical Summary for her Husband] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton V, 7th Lord Seton (1531–1586), was a Lord of the Parliament of Scotland, Master of the Household of Mary, Queen of Scots, and Provost of Edinburgh. He was the eldest son of George Seton, 6th Lord Seton, and Elizabeth 
2 CONC Hay, a daughter of John Hay, 3rd Lord Hay of Yester. His childhood and schooling were in France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''Isobel Hamilton''' (died 1604), daughter of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Hamilton_of_Sanquhar Sir William Hamilton of Sanquhar], Captain of Edinburgh Castle in August 1550.[39] The wedding was celebrated wi
2 CONC th a feast at Edinburgh Castle.[40] According to Richard Maitland, Hamilton of Sanquhar organised some of the rebuilding of Seton Palace, which had been damaged by the English army that burnt Edinburgh in May 1544.[41]
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Lord Seton and Isobel Hamilton included;
2 CONT
2 CONT # George, Master of Seton, (died 1562)
2 CONT # Robert Seton, 1st Earl of Winton
2 CONT # Sir John Seton of Barnes, attendant to the Earl of Leicester in 1575,[42] Master Carver to Philip II of Spain and Master of Horse to James VI.
2 CONT # Alexander Seton, 1st Earl of Dunfermline, Lord Urquhart, Lord Fyvie, and Prior of Pluscarden
2 CONT # William Seton of Kylesmure, who married Janet Dunbar
2 CONT # Margaret Seton, who married Claud Hamilton, 1st Lord Paisley, on 1 August 1574 at Niddry Castle with "great triumphs."[43]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a Spanish diplomatic report, in 1592 Anne of Denmark told several ladies at court, including Isobel Hamilton, Lady Seton, that she was a Catholic and prayed with a rosary.[44] In December 1596 Lady Seton was said to b
2 CONC e a great favourite of Anne of Denmark.[45]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Hamilton_of_Sanquhar Biographical Summary for her father] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Hamilton of Sanquhar (c. 1510–1570) was pursemaster for James V and the Captain of Edinburgh Castle during the Regency of Regent Arran. William Hamilton was the son of William Hamilton, also of Sanquhar, and Katherine 
2 CONC Kennedy, a daughter of David Kennedy, 1st Earl of Cassilis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton married Jean Campbell. His children included:
2 CONT
2 CONT #''' Isobel Hamilton''' (died 1604), who married George Seton, 7th Lord Seton in 1550. Hamilton paid £2,000 for the ward and marriage right of the young Lord Seton.[7] The wedding was celebrated with a feast at Edinburgh Castle on
2 CONC  12 August 1550 paid for by Regent Arran.[8] Richard Maitland wrote that Hamilton helped Lord Seton rebuild Seton Palace, which had been damaged by the English army that burnt Edinburgh in May 1544.[9]
2 CONT # William Hamilton of Glenmoor. In December 1551 Regent Arran gave him the substantial sum of £53 Scots for clothes.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT
