1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == SIR JOHN COLQUHOUN OF LUSS Baronet ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1605: Contract between Thomas, Lord Boyd, on the one part, and Helen Buchanan, spouse to Alexander Colquhoun of Luss, on the other part anent the fulfilment of a contract, between the said Lord and the said Alexander, as admini
2 CONC strator to John Colquhoun of Luss, his son, anent the £10 land of Dynnarbuk, Auchentuerly and Spittal. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD8%2f504&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=alexander+co
2 CONC lquhoun&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1620&di=n National Records of Scotland, Boyd Papers, Burgh of Kilmarnock, reference GD8/504]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT
2 CONT "<strong>John Colquhoun, of Luss and Tilliquhoun</strong>, co Dumbarton, son and heir of Alexander Colquhoun, of the same, by Helen, daughter of Sir George Buchanan of Buchanan, succeeded his father, 23 May 1617, was M.P. [S] for 
2 CONC Dumbartonshire, 1621, and was created a Baronet [S], 30 August 1625, sealed 20 October following, with rem. to heirs male whatsoever, and with a grant of, apparently, 16,000 acres in Nova Scotia, afterwards called Tilliquhoun, of 
2 CONC which seizen was made in the said month of October. He was 'fugitated and excommunicated', and deprived of the life rent of his estates, as he result of a criminal suit in 1632 for his seduction of his wife's sister, Lady Katherin
2 CONC e Graham. He married (contract 30 June and 6 July 1620) Lilias, 1st daughter of John (Graham), 4th Earl of Montrose [S], by Margaret, daughter of William (Ruthven), 1st Earl of Gowrin [S]."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524382#page/n311/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1900; Vol. II; page 293-294
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From his English Wikipedia page:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_John_Colquhoun,_1st_Baronet
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Colquhoun, 1st Baronet
2 CONT *Born	c. 1596
2 CONT *Nationality	Scotland
2 CONT *Known for	Baronet
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Colquhoun (c.1596 – aft. 8 November 1647),[1] Sixteenth Lord Colquhoun and the Eighteenth of Luss,[2] was the first Colquhoun baronet of Nova Scotia, Canada. The Baronetage of the Colquhoun clan of Nova Scotia was establi
2 CONC shed on 30 August 1625.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Colquhoun was born in Scotland, probably in 1596, as his parents were married in 1595.[2] He was the eldest son of Sir Alexander Colquhoun and Lady Helen Buchanan.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1620, he married Lady Lilias Graham, eldest daughter of John Graham, Fourth Earl of Montrose, and elder sister of the Great Montrose,[5] which was what her younger brother, James Graham, 5th Earl of Montrose, came to be known, 
2 CONC in addition to being the Marquis of Montrose. In 1632 Sir John was accused of absconding his wife's sister, Lady Catherine Graham. It was alleged that he had used witchcraft and sorcery to accomplish this. As a fugitive he was exc
2 CONC ommunicated and his estates forfeited. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT When and where John died is unknown. He was alive on 8 November 1647.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnotes
