1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Bio notes: Biography of Joseph Grove
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Hawn, part of the parish of Halesowen and baptised in 1713. According to Davenport, he was baptised privately then later in the church because there are two entries for his baptism [3]; this fits with the 2 citation
2 CONC s in the IGI -one just says he was born in Halesowen [1], the other says he was born in Hawn in the parish of Halesowen [2].  He was illiterate because he made his mark on his will [4]. He was still living at Hawn in 1734, was in 
2 CONC Cakemore in 1744, Hunnington in 1759 [5] then a farmer in Frankley for some time until his son Samuel took over some time before 1771 [4]. He died on 15 March 1784 and there is an inscription on the west wall of the church in Hale
2 CONC sowen [1][3]  but according to Davenport he was buried on 22 March 1784 which seems a long time till burial [3].
2 CONT
2 CONT [1] IGI  www.familysearch.org accessed on 30/03/2006
2 CONT Joseph Grove
2 CONT Christening: 27 FEB 1713,  Halesowen, Worcester, England
2 CONT Death: 15 MAR 1784 Halesowen, Worcestershire
2 CONT Father: Joseph Grove
2 CONT Mother: Hannah Butler
2 CONT Spouse: Hannah Stringer
2 CONT Marriage: 01 SEP 1734
2 CONT [2] IGI  www.familysearch.org accessed on 30/03/2006
2 CONT Joseph Grove
2 CONT Christening:  1713,  Hawn, Worcester, England
2 CONT Death: 1784
2 CONT Father: Joseph
2 CONT Mother: Hannah Butler
2 CONT [3] James Davenport, The Grove Family of Halesowen, Methuen and Co, 1912 p.23
2 CONT "There were two children of the marriage (of Joseph Grove and Hannah Butler) who comprise the 8th generation
2 CONT 1. Anne bap 14 Oct 1711
2 CONT 2. Joseph bap 1713. both named as children of Joseph and Hannah of Hawn. Joseph apparently was privately baptized and afterwards received into the Church because there are two entries for his baptism,
2 CONT 1713 February, Joseph Grove Son of Joseph & Hanah Grove de Hawn Bapt ye 27th
2 CONT 1713 March, Joseph Grove, son of Joseph and Hanah de Hawn Bapt the 14th
2 CONT He married Hannah Stringer of Rowley on 1 Sep. 1734 and had five sons: Joseph, Samuel, Thomas, Benjamin and Matthew. He was buried 22 March 1874 and she 19 Oct 1791 aged 77. Their stone near the west wall of the Halesowen church i
2 CONC s inscribed thus:
2 CONT In
2 CONT Memory of
2 CONT Joseph Grove who
2 CONT departed this life
2 CONT March the 15th 1784
2 CONT Aged 77 [sic - stone cutter's error for 71- Davenport]
2 CONT Also Hannah wife of
2 CONT the abovesaid Joseph
2 CONT Grove died October
2 CONT the 15 1791 Aged 77
2 CONT We live and labour get and save
2 CONT Till Death commands us to the grave
2 CONT Reader, in all thy gains besure
2 CONT Of an Estate that will endure
2 CONT [4] James Davenport, The Grove Family of Halesowen, Methuen and Co, 1912 p. 24
2 CONT Will of Joseph Grove
2 CONT "In ye name of God Amen. I Joseph Grove of  ye parish of Halesowen in ye County of Salop [Shropshire] Husbandman [ie farmer] being in perfect health (Blessed be God for ye same) but minding ye uncertainty of human life do make & o
2 CONC rdain this my last Will & Testament in manner following, that is to say First of all I commit my body to ye earth to be decently buried at ye discretion of my Executor hereinafternamed. And as to my Worldly Estate I dispose therof
2 CONC  as followeth. First I hereby order that all my just debts & Funeral expenses be fully paid off & discharg'd. After which I give & bequeath & devise unto my loving wife Hannah Grove ye use of all my money, household goods, stock &
2 CONC  Utensils in Huzbandry & also all that my Messuage [a dwelling house and its adjacent buildings and the adjacent land used by the household] Lands & Buildings in ye parish of Frankly in ye County of Worcester now in ye holding of 
2 CONC my son SAMUEL GROVE  for and during ye term of her natural life provided she continueth so long in Widowhood but not otherwise. And after her decease or second marriage I give & bequeath & devise all my said money, household goods
2 CONC , Stock, Utensils in Huzbandry & all my Personal effects whatsoever and wheresoever & all that my saide Messuage Lands and Buildings in ye Parish of Frankly in ye County of Worcester now in ye holding of my son Samuel Grove afores
2 CONC aid to all my children be equally divided among them and share and share alike. But if my son BENJAMIN GROVE should not repay me ye sum of twenty pounds which I lately lent him in my life time it is my Will that ye said sum of twe
2 CONC nty pounds shall be deducted out of his Share of my real & personal estate hereinbefore mentioned. Lastly I hereby nominate & appoint my said son Joseph Grove executor of this my last Will & Testament. In Witness whereof I have he
2 CONC reunto set my hand & seal this [blank] day of September 1771
2 CONT Ye marke X of Joseph Grove
2 CONT Sign'd seal'd publish'd  & declar'd
2 CONT by ye within named Testator to be his
2 CONT last will & testament in the presence of
2 CONT P. Wilmot  Anne Stamps
2 CONT George Gearey
2 CONT (Proved 6 May 1784 by the executor Joseph & sworn under ?100)
2 CONT The seal on the Will is striking and seems to represent a cherub riding a lion; the motto round it is "omnia vincit amor" [love conquers all]. It would seem that P. Wilmot wrote out the will."
2 CONT [5] James Davenport, The Grove Family of Halesowen, Methuen and Co, 1912 p.25
2 CONT "He was living in different parts of Halesowen at different periods of his life. In 1734 when his eldest son Joseph was bap. he was of Hawn: in 1744 when Benjamin was bap. he was of Cakemore: in 1759 when his youngest son Matthew 
2 CONC was bap. he was living at Hunnington. So far as can be judged from the register of Frankley which adjoins Halesowen the tholding there was occupied by his sons successively."
