1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Mariota Leslie''', Mairead, also called Mary and Margaret (died about 1440 at Tulloch Castle, Dingwall, Ross, Scotland) was the daughter of Euphemia I, Countess of Ross and her husband, the crusading war-hero Wa
2 CONC lter Leslie, Lord of Ross, their only daughter.  Upon the death of her brother, Alexander Leslie, Earl of Ross, she became the heir-presumptive of her niece Euphemia II, Countess of Ross although her husband Domhnall of Islay, Lor
2 CONC d of the Isles pressed Mariota's superior claim to the earldom.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Title
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulloch_Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LBBH-XMW
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Leslie-101
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was the sister of Alexander Leslie, Earl of Ross. Alexander had a daughter Euphemia who was a minor when her father died. Euphemia, holding the important and strategic title to Ross, became an important ward. She became a war
2 CONC d of the Crown and her wardship was held by her grandfather, Robert Stewart, Duke of Albany, and Regent of Scotland. Although Robert Stewart made attempts to marry her these were unsuccessful and he persuaded her to relinquish the
2 CONC  titles to the Crown and join the church. Robert Stewart then granted the titles to his son John. Mary's husband, Donald, died in 1423 and John Stewart died in 1424. After the death of John Stewart, Mary appealed the decision by t
2 CONC he Regent, on the basis of the 1370 Charter, and won her case. At some point prior to her death, in 1440, she was acknowledged in the Earldom. '''She never called herself Countess''', instead granting title to her son, Alexander, 
2 CONC who was acknowledged as Earl of Ross in 1437.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is said to have died after her son gained the title and in 1440 but she was certainly alive in 1437 when her claim was allowed.
2 CONT Family
