2 CONT '''-in CLAN MACDONALD III by REV A Macdonald (1904)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT THE  MACDONALDS  OF  SLEAT.    
2 CONT The  Macdonalds  of  Sleat  are  descended  from
2 CONT 1.  HUGH,  son  of  '''Alexander,  Earl  of  Ross,  and Lord  of  the  Isles''',  by  the  daughter  of  O'Beolan,  lay Abbot  of  Applecross,  and  are  known  patronymically as  Clann  Uisdein. Hugh  married,  first,   Fynvola, 
2 CONC  daughter  of  Alexander Maclain  of  Ardnamurchan,  and  had  by  her — 
2 CONT >1.  John,  his  successor, d1502
2 CONT He  married,  secondly,  Elizabeth  Gunn,  daughter  of the  Crowner  of  Caithness,  and  had  by  her—
2 CONT >2.  Donald  Gallach.
2 CONT He   married,    thirdly,    a    daughter    of    Macleod   of Harris,  by  whom  he  had—
2 CONT >3.  Donald  Herrach,  from  whom  Clann  Domhnuill  Herraich. Hugh  had  also  several  sons,  whose  claims  to  legiti- macy do  not  seem  to  have  been  admitted  even  by the  social  canons  of  the  time,  viz. : —
2 CONT >4.  Archibald  Dubh,  by  a  daughter  of  Torquil  Macleod  of Lewis.
2 CONT >5.  Angus  Collach,  by  a  daughter  of  the  Laird  of  Coll.
2 CONT >6.  Angus   Dubh,   by   a   daughter  of  Maurice   Vicar  of  S.Uist,
2 CONT Hugh  Macdonald  of  Sleat  died  in    1498,   and  was succeeded  by  his  oldest  son,
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/clandonald03macduoft/page/n547/mode/2up?q=Camus&view=theater
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander of Islay or Alexander MacDonald (died 1449; Scottish Gaelic: Alasdair MacDomhnaill, Dòmhnallach or MacDhòmhnaill) was a medieval Scottish nobleman, who succeeded his father Domhnall of Islay as Lord of the Isles (1423–14
2 CONC 49) and rose to the rank of Earl of Ross (1437–49). His lively career, especially before he attained the earldom of Ross, led Hugh MacDonald, the 17th century author of History of the MacDonalds, to commemorate him as "a man born 
2 CONC to much trouble all his lifetime".[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander allied himself with King James I of Scotland against the power of the Albany Stewarts in 1425 but, once the Albany Stewarts were out of the way, Alexander quickly found himself at odds with the new king. War with King Ja
2 CONC mes would initially prove Alexander's undoing, and would see the King's power in Scotland greatly increased, but at the Battle of Inverlochy Alexander's army prevailed against the forces of the King. Alexander died in 1449, having
2 CONC  greatly extended his family's landed wealth and power. He was buried, not in the Isles of his ancestors, but at Fortrose Cathedral in his mainland Earldom of Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander MacDonald, 9th Lord of the Isles, 12th Earl of Ross died on 8 May 1449 at Tulloch Castle, Dingwall, Ross & Cromarty, Scotland.3
2 CONT
