1 NOTE The Clan Donald Genealogy Database at 
2 CONT http://www.clandonald.org.uk/Genealogy/d0003/g0000070.html#I0172
2 CONT  shows mother of most of his children to be Elizabeth SETON. Neither Burke's nor The Clan Donald Genealogy Database mention a "Jacobella"
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The case of the identity of Alexander's wife, who was mother of his heir John and two daughters, illustrates some of the difficulties faced in identifying spouse's maiden names and their parents. Even such meticulous and experienc
2 CONC ed researchers as Jean Munro, Ph.D., and R. W. Munro can apparently be led astray, as the two letters from Douglas Hickling below illustrate. 
2 CONT The "note of a discharge" mentioning "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, countess of Ross," is dated 40 years after Isabel (of Albany) Stewart first husband's death and close to that after her second marriage.  Given that, and the differe
2 CONC nces in first names, this may not be the end of this discussion. So  Elizabeth (probably of Dirleton) Halyburton shown here as possibly one of his wives may or may not have existed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st letter from Douglas Hickling based on research of Jean Munro, Ph.D., and R. W. Munro.
2 CONT "I am going to comment on the name of Alexander's wife.  Virtually all of the authorities state, without any documentary proof, that Alexander's wife was Elizabeth Seton, a daughter of Sir Alexander Seton and his wife Elizabeth Go
2 CONC rdon.  The Munros in their ACTS OF THE LORDS 0F THE ISLES, at 302-303 and elsewhere provide convincing evidence that Elizabeth's surname was Haliburton.  At, 63, the Munros set forth a 1443 note of a discharge to Sir John Scrymgeo
2 CONC ur by "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, countess of Ross," apparently the only contemporary record which gives Elizabeth's surname.  The Munros, at 241, set forth a reference to a papal indult, dated 19 October 1433, to Alexander and Ja
2 CONC cobella, his wife, for a portable altar.  There seems to be no other record of this earlier marriage or of any children resulting therefrom.  On 2 November 1467, Alexander's son John granted a charter confirming a gift to the mona
2 CONC stery of Fearn "for the salvation of the souls of his parents, Alexander earl of Ross and Elizabeth his wife."  (See Munros, at 143.)  This shows that Elizabeth, not Jacobella, was John's mother.    BURKE'S PEERAGE & BARONETAGE (1
2 CONC 06th edition), at 328, accepts "Elizabeth Halyburton, probably of Dirletoun," as Alexander's wife. "
2 CONT Douglas Hickling
2 CONT Dhhic@comcast.com
2 CONT 516 Blair Avenue
2 CONT Piedmont CA 94611
2 CONT (August 2003)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd letter from Douglas Hickling
2 CONT Based on my previous e-mail to you, you now show that the wife of 
2 CONT Alexander Macdonald ID: I37488, and the mother of John, Lord of the 
2 CONT Isles, was Dame Elizabeth Haliburton.
2 CONT
2 CONT A correction seems to be needed.  In the fall 2003 issue of THE 
2 CONT GENEALOGIST, there is an article by Andrew B. W. MacEwen, beginning at 
2 CONT 222, on Cristina de Brus, Countess of Dunbar.  At page 225, the author, 
2 CONT as an illustration of the difficulty in sometimes determining a woman's 
2 CONT maiden name, says:
2 CONT
2 CONT      And the twice married Isabella Stewart of Albany in a discharge dated 
2 CONT 10 December 1443 was styled "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, countess of 
2 CONT Ross," taking the rank of her first husband, Alexander Lesley, Earl of 
2 CONT Ross, and the surname of her second husband, Sir Walter Haliburton of 
2 CONT Dirleton--and seriously misleading such acute modern scholars as      the 
2 CONT Munros.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote 13 says:  "Jean Munro, Ph.D., and R. W. Munro, eds. ACTS OF 
2 CONT THE LORDS OF THE ISLES 1336-1493, Scottish History Society, 4th ser., 
2 CONT 22 (1986); 63, No. 41.  Corrected in WEST HIGHLAND NOTES & QUERIES 
2 CONT [Isle of Coll. Argyll], ser. 2, No. 19 (March 1999): 24-25."
2 CONT
2 CONT The foregoing means that Isabel, daughter of Robert Duke of Albany, 
2 CONT married (in 1398) Alexander, Earl of Ross (died 1402).  She 
2 CONT subsequently married Sir Walter Haliburton of Direlton.  The wife of 
2 CONT Alexander, Lord of the Isles and Earl of Ross (died 1499) and the 
2 CONT mother of their son John, the last earl of Ross and lord of the Isles, 
2 CONT cannot be named with certainty.  As the Munros point out at p. 63 in 
2 CONT their comment regarding the note of a discharge No. 41, and in a 
2 CONT subsequent comment at p. 303, Elizabeth Seton, daughter of Sir 
2 CONT Alexander Seton and Elizabeth Gordon, and sister of the first earl of 
2 CONT Huntly, has been accepted as the wife of Alexander, but she is not so 
2 CONT named in any contemporary record.  Her place as the wife of Alexander, 
2 CONT Lord of the Isles and Earl of Ross depends upon a 16th century Gordon 
2 CONT genealogy by John Ferrerius .  Ferrerius is generally well-regarded and 
2 CONT I suggest that you may wish to show Elizabeth Seton as the earl's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Munros were not alone in failing to discern the true identity of 
2 CONT "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, Countess of Ross," referred to in the note 
2 CONT of a discharge cited above.  Complete Peerage V. 11, p. 151, published 
2 CONT in 1949, makes the same mistake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Doug Hickling
2 CONT Dhhic@comcast.net
2 CONT (November 2003)
