1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hector was the eldest son of William Munro, 12th Baron of Foulis who died in 1505.[2] Hector was so young when he succeeded to his estates that;
2 CONT
2 CONT "management of the estates was attended to by his relatives"
2 CONT
2 CONT 13th Baron of Foulis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Was at the Battle of Flodden Field 9 Sep 1513.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Extensive lands confirmed to him by James V at Stirling 1541. Married Kathrine, daughter of chief of MacKenzie of Kintail.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector Munro, 13th Baron of Foulis From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector Munro, 13th Baron of Foulis was a Scottish clan chief of the highland Clan Munro in Rosshire. He is by tradition 13th Baron of Foulis and 16th overall chief of the clan.[1] He is however only the 6th chief of the Clan Munro
2 CONC  who can be proved by contemporary evidence.[2] Lands and charters
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector was the eldest son of William Munro, 12th Baron of Foulis who died in 1505.[2] Hector was so young when he succeeded to his estates that management of the estates was attended to by his relatives.[1] In 1514/15 after attain
2 CONC ing his majority Hector Munro was appointed Lieutenant along with John Mackenzie, 9th of Kintail, chief of Clan Mackenzie for the protection of Wester Ross.[1][2][3] They were to protect the lands from the incursion of Sir Donald 
2 CONC MacDonald of Lochalsh, chief of the Clan MacDonald of Lochalsh, who at that time proclaimed himself Lord of the Isles.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a charter under the Great Seal, dated 10 December 1516 to Hector Munro of Foulis, granting him the salmon fishing in the Kyle of Oykel, between the counties of Ross and Sutherland upon the resignation of the same subjects
2 CONC  in his favour by Sir Donald MacDonald of Lochalsh.[1] Hector Munro also acquired by charter dated at Glengarry the 2nd of October 1524, from Margaret MacDonald of the Isles, sister of Sir Donald MacDonald of Lochalsh with the con
2 CONC sent of her husband Alexander MacDonald, 6th of Glengarry, chief of the Clan MacDonell of Glengarry part of the lands of Lochalsh, Lochcarron, Lochbroom, and Feorin-Coscarrie, in Breachatt and superior of the lands of Creichmore a
2 CONC nd fishings of the Killis Ockell.[1] She further sold and confirmed to her cousin, Hector Munro of Foulis the half of the lands of Inveran, Linisetroy, Linisetmore, Altasbeg, Altasmore and Auchness for a certain sum of money deliv
2 CONC ered to her for her need and urgent necessity.[1] These grants were further confirmed to Hector Munro at Court by king James V of Scotland by charter dated at Stirling on the 20th April 1541.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT A charter found at Cawdor Castle between the Knight of Calder and others dated at Inverness on the 30th April 1527 includes Hector Munro of Foulis as one of the subscribers. There is also a bond of Manrent between Hector Munro and
2 CONC  Hugh Fraser, 5th Lord Lovat dated 19 March 1529.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Different sources give different dates of death for Hector Munro. The Martine MS written by George Martine in the late 17th century gives the date as 1549.[2] However contemporary records, the Munro Writs of Foulis give the date a
2 CONC s 8 March 1541.[2] Hector died at Culrain in the parish of Kincardine and his remains were interred in the ancestral burying ground in the Chanonry of Ross.[1] Family and descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector Munro, 13th Baron married firstly Catherine, daughter of Sir Kenneth Mackenzie, 7th of Kintail, chief of the Clan Mackenzie.[1] They had two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Robert Munro, 14th Baron of Foulis (heir and successor).
2 CONT     Hugh Munro of Contullich, appears to have died unmarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector Munro married secondly Catherine, daughter of John Macleod of Lewis, chief of the Clan MacLeod of Lewis but without issue. However, Hector Munro, 13th Baron of Foulis is known to have had three illegitimate sons from whom s
2 CONC everal large Munro families are descended and had land granted to them in Rosshire.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hugh Munro, 1st of Little Findon.
2 CONT     Robert Munro, 1st of Killichoan.
2 CONT     John Roy Munro, the "red", 1st of Wester Fyrish.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     ^ a b c d e f g h i j k l "History of the Munros of Fowlis". pages 34 - 40. By Alexander Mackenzie. Published 1898.
2 CONT     ^ a b c d e "The Munro Tree 1734". Published in 1978, Edinburgh. By R. W. Munro. ISBN 0-9503689-1-1.
2 CONT     ^ Register of the Privy Seal of Scotland. Dated 15th December 1515.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hector Munro, 13th Baron of Foulis From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search
