1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Laurence Oliphant, 3rd Lord Oliphant From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Laurence Oliphant, 3rd Lord Oliphant (died 1566) was a Scottish nobleman.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Colin, Master of Oliphant (killed at the battle of Flodden in 1513), by Lady Elizabeth Keith, second daughter of William Keith, 3rd Earl Marischal. He succeeded his grandfather John Oliphant, 2nd Lord Oliphant, i
2 CONC n 1516, and was one of the Scottish nobles taken prisoner at the battle of Solway Moss on 25 November 1542, reaching Newark on 15 December, on the way to London. The annual value of his lands was then estimated at two thousand mer
2 CONC ks Scots.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Oliphant remained in England in the custody of Sir Thomas Lee, knt., but on 1 July 1543 was allowed to be ransomed for eight hundred merks sterling, on condition that, along with other captive Scottish nobles, he should acknowledg
2 CONC e Henry VIII of England as lord-superior, should co-operate in procuring him the government of Scotland, and should exert his influence to get the infant Queen Mary delivered to Henry, to be brought up in England. He later made no
2 CONC  attempt to fulfil these pledges. In 1560 at a meeting at Aberdeen he nominally adhered to the Protestant cause, but probably never joined against Queen Mary.[1]
2 CONT Oliphant died on 26 March 1566.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Additional References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT *  "Publications of the Scottish History Society" Genealogy of the Oliphants starts pg 97  [http://archive.org/stream/scothistorysoc34scotuoft/scothistorysoc34scotuoft_djvu.txt] 
