2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilbert_Kennedy,_3rd_Earl_of_Cassilis Gilbert Kennedy, third Earl of Cassillis]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert Kennedy, 3rd Earl of Cassilis (/ˈkæslz/ KASS-lz) (1515 – 28 November 1558) was Scottish landowner, soldier, politician and judge. He served as Treasurer of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Gilbert Kennedy, 2nd Earl of Cassilis, he succeeded to the titles of 5th Lord Kennedy and 3rd Earl of Cassillis in August 1527. On 6 February 1540/41 he had a charter of the Fief of Cassilis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert Kennedy was taken prisoner by Thomas Dacre and Sir William Musgrave in the Battle of Solway Moss in 1542. http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Solway_Moss
2 CONT
2 CONT Being committed to the custody of Thomas Cranmer, Archbishop of Canterbury, was, by the instructions of that venerable martyr, induced to embrace the reformed religion. He became a Protestant, and went over to the English Party. H
2 CONC e was released in February 1545, and held the office of Treasurer of Scotland in 1554, of Extraordinary Lord of Session between 1546 and 1558. Gilbert Kennedy fought in the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh on 10 September 1547.
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Pinkie_Cleugh
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1558 he was present, as one of the eight Commissioners appointed by the Scottish Parliament, at the marriage of Mary, Queen of Scots, to the Dauphin of France, to whom the Scottish deputies unanimously refused the Crown matrimo
2 CONC nial. The Court of France appeared deeply mortified by this disappointment, and the Earl of Cassilis, with two others of the Commissioners, dying in one night, on 28 November 1558, at Dieppe, a report was raised that they had been
2 CONC  poisoned, which was further countenanced by the death of a fourth Commissioner, Lord Fleming, at Paris, on 16 December 1558.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II of France had appointed Gilbert as Gentleman in Ordinary of his Chamber on 4 May 1558, and this honour was transferred to his heir, Gilbert, 4th Earl of Cassilis on 10 February 1559.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl married Margaret Kennedy in 1540 and they had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine Kennedy
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert Kennedy, 4th Earl of Cassilis (c. 1541–1576)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas Kennedy, Master of Cassilis (b. between 1543–1558, d. 1602)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane or Jean Kennedy, (bef. 1558–c.1598), married Robert Stewart, 1st Earl of Orkney
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Isobel Kennedy b. 24 July 1542 d. 12 Jan 1598 and had descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gilbert Kennedy''', ''3rd Earl of Cassillis'', was born between 1515 and died 28 November 1558.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert, 3rd Earl of Cassillis, was a famous Scottish statesman and he was appointed Lord High Treasurer in 1554. He was one of the Commissioners who went to France to arrange the marriage of Mary, Queen of Scots, with the Dauphin
2 CONC , and, because he refused to agree that the Scottish crown should go to the French heir, he was poisoned at Dieppe and his body was also brought back for burial in the "Auld College".
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: son of Gilbert Kennedy, 2nd Earl of Cassillis and Lady Isabel Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedigree: (Gilbert12, David11, John10, Gilbert9, James8, Gilbert7, John6, Gilbert5de Carrick, Gilbert4, Roland3, Nicol,2, Duncan1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married:
2 CONT # Margaret Kennedy, daughter of Alexander Kennedy of Bargany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children include:
2 CONT # Lady Janet Kennedy+1 d. 1598
2 CONT # Gilbert, fourth Earl of Cassillis ancestor of the fifth, sixth, seventh and eighth Earls of Cassillis. 
2 CONT # Hon. Sir Thomas of Culzean, Master of Cassillis, b. bt 1543 - 1558, d. 11 May 1602 m. Elizabeth McGill. 
2 CONT # Lady Jean; m. Robert (--?--) Earl of Orkney
2 CONT # Lady Catherine; m. Sir Patrick Vaus.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded as 3rd Earl of Cassillis. William Kennedy Abbot of Crossraguel was his guardian for eleven years. He was at the University of St. Andrew's in 1527, afterwards completed his education om Paris, remaining there until 15
2 CONC 37; of excellent disposition and promising talents. He had many charters; when taken prisoner at rout of Solway Moss by the English he was committed to Archbishop Cranmer, 1542, because an Anglican. Henry VIII loaded him with gift
2 CONC s. He was high Treasurer of Scotland, Chief Commander in the Army which attacked Berwick and invaded England 1557. Sent to France to negotiate Queen Mary's marriage with the Dauphin but met mysterious death at Dieppe in 1558; buri
2 CONC ed at Maybole. 
2 CONT
