2 CONC hree sons and five daughters 1. John, who succeeded. 2. James, who had no issue. 3. Thomas, first laird of Drummawhance. 4. Sybilla, married, 1st, to William Reidoch of Aberledanach ; 2ndly, to - Campbell of Lochinzell. 5. Isabell
2 CONC a, married to Mr. Alexander Inglis of Byres. 6. - married to Duncan Tossach of Piifenzie. 7. to John Mushet, brother german to the laird of Mushet. And 8. - to Patrick Drummond 4th laird of Milnab. – (David Malcom 1808, Genealogic
2 CONC al Memoir of the Most Noble and Ancient House of Drummond)
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN DRUMMOND, 5TH OF COLQUHALZIE, succeeded his father and was appointed “custodian of the lands, Baronies with the eastland Fortalice of Dunkeld, and other houses, castles, fortalices thereof with the power to create offices and
2 CONC  baillies” on 16 October 1551. He was complained of in 1553 by Mr James Hepburn, dean and vicar of Dunkeld, for his wrongful occupation of the Bishop’s Castle, and had Sasine of Colquhalzie and Duchlas on 30 May 1560.1 '''He m. in
2 CONC  around 1548, to Janet, daughter of Donald Campbell, Abbot of Coupar Angus Abbey, with whom he had a charter from her father for the lands of Blacklaw, Cotyards and the Mill of Blacklaw, in the Lordship of Cupar, on 10 January 155
2 CONC 8, (she d. by 23 September 1583)''' - (Gordon MacGregor: The Red Book of Scotland Project - 2017. www.redbookofscotland.co.uk)
