1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John Erskine, 5th Lord Erskine''' (<1513-1555)
2 CONT s/o Robert Erskine, 4th Lord Erskine & Isabel Campbell.
2 CONT x '''Lady Margaret Campbell''', d/o Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll, & Elizabeth Stuart.
2 CONT *Robert, Master of Erskine x Margaret Graham, eldest d/o William, 2nd Earl of Montrose. xx mistress Mrs Jean Home
2 CONT *Thomas, Master of Erskine, Abbot or Commendator of Dryburgh x  Margaret, d/o, 3rd Lord Fleming, and wid/o Robert, Lord Graham xx mistress Jonet Abernethy, he had:
2 CONT *Elizabeth x  Robert Graham of Gartmore, bro/o John, Earl of Menteith.
2 CONT *John, 6th Lord Erskine
2 CONT *Sir Alexander Erskine of Gogar, ancestor of the Earls of Kellie.
2 CONT *Arthur Erskine of Blackgrange x Magdalen Livingstone, d/o Alexander, 5th Lord Livingstone 
2 CONT *Catherine Erskine x Alexander, 2nd Lord Elphinstone
2 CONT *Margaret Erskine x Robert Douglas of Lochleven xx as mistress, King James V 
2 CONT *Janet Erskine x John Murray of Touchadam, ancestor of the Polmaise family.
2 CONT *Elizabeth Erskine x Walter Seton of Touch, Tullibody
2 CONT *Margaret Erskine x George Home of Lundies
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == John Erskine, 5th Lord Erskine ==
2 CONT
2 CONT John, fifth Lord Erskine, succeeded his father after the Battle of Flodden in 1513. During his father's lifetime, he was known as Sir John Erskine of Nisbet and had attained the rank of knight before June 1510. He received sasine 
2 CONC of numerous lands in November 1513.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is said to have been sent in 1515 as Scottish ambassador to France to obtain Scotland’s inclusion in the treaty with England, but the only known reference to him from that year shows he was a witness to the acceptance of the tr
2 CONC eaty by the Scottish nobles, dated at Edinburgh, 15 May 1515.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed one of three lords to act as personal guardians of the young King James V. From September 1517 to September 1518, he received a salary of £200 for one-third of the year; he continued to receive this sum annually f
2 CONC rom September 1518 to May 1522. Later, his salary was increased to £50 per month.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 August 1522, ordinances were issued regulating arrangements at Stirling Castle and officers' salaries. A special Act of Parliament on 2 September 1523 confirmed these arrangements.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is said to have served as ambassador to France in 1535, but this role was actually fulfilled by Sir Thomas Erskine of Brechin.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1540, when King James V sailed on his expedition to the Isles, Lord Erskine was appointed guardian to the king’s infant son James. In 1542, the infant Queen Mary was placed under his charge.
2 CONT
2 CONT He retained keepership of Stirling Castle until his death and also held custody of Edinburgh Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although commonly said to have died in 1552, Lord Erskine was party to his son Alexander’s marriage-contract on 16 January 1554–55 and was apparently alive on 11 July 1555, when his son John was still Commendator of Dryburgh. He d
2 CONC ied between that date and November 1555, when his son received sasine of his lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Margaret, eldest daughter of Archibald, 2nd Earl of Argyll, and they had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Robert, Master of Erskine''', who received a charter of the lands of Kellie on his father's resignation, dated 20 May 1536, and of Sheirgarton and other lands on 23 February 1541–42. He was captured at Solway on 24 November 1
2 CONC 542, and again at Pinkie on 10 September 1547. He died without lawful issue by his wife, Margaret Graham, eldest daughter of William, 2nd Earl of Montrose. He had a natural son by a Mrs Jean Home:
2 CONT ** David, who became Abbot of Inchmahome and Dryburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Thomas, Master of Erskine''', succeeded his brother. From 1541 to 1547, he held office as Abbot or Commendator of Dryburgh. He concluded a treaty with England in June 1551. On 7 October 1551, he received sasine of the lands o
2 CONC f Brotherstanes, Berwickshire, which had been sold to him by Sir John Home of Coldenknowes. He died soon afterward, likely before his father. He married, by contract dated 30 January 1548–49:
2 CONT ** Margaret, daughter of Malcolm, 3rd Lord Fleming, and widow of Robert, Lord Graham, who fell at Pinkie. They had no issue.
2 CONT ** By Jonet Abernethy, he had:
2 CONT *** Adam, Commendator of Cambuskenneth.
2 CONT *** Elizabeth, married (contract dated 29 January 1562–63) to Robert Graham of Gartmore, brother of John, Earl of Menteith.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''John, 6th Lord Erskine''', of whom later.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sir Alexander Erskine of Gogar''', ancestor of the Earls of Kellie.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Arthur Erskine of Blackgrange''', referred to as brother of John and Alexander Erskine in December 1557. He was reportedly Queen Mary’s favourite equerry and was in personal attendance the night David Rizzio was murdered. He 
2 CONC married (contract dated 7 January 1561–62):
2 CONT ** Magdalen Livingstone, daughter of Alexander, 5th Lord Livingstone (see title Linlithgow). He died without issue before January 1570–71, when his brother Alexander succeeded him.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Catherine Erskine''', contracted on 25 June 1518 to marry John Murray of Touchadam, with a tocher of 650 merks. This marriage did not take place. She instead married (contract dated 20 November 1525):
2 CONT ** Alexander, 2nd Lord Elphinstone, with a tocher of 1300 merks.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Margaret Erskine''', named in her sister Catherine’s 1518 marriage contract. She was contracted on 11 July 1527 to:
2 CONT ** Robert Douglas of Lochleven, and married him. Around 1531, she became the mistress of King James V and bore him:
2 CONT *** James Stewart, later Regent Moray.
2 CONT ** She was living as Douglas’s wife in 1540 and died on 5 May 1572.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Janet Erskine''', married (contract dated 1 September 1532):
2 CONT ** John Murray of Touchadam, ancestor of the Polmaise family.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Erskine''', married:
2 CONT ** Walter Seton of Touch, Tullibody. She is said to have been a daughter of this Lord Erskine.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Margaret Erskine''' (possibly a different daughter from the one above), married before August 1526:
2 CONT ** George Home of Lundies, with a tocher of 400 merks. A memorandum on the 25 June 1518 marriage contract links her to the earlier-mentioned Margaret, but it is likely she was a separate daughter, possibly illegitimate. She and he
2 CONC r husband were both alive on 25 March 1556.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p609-12]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == John Erskine, 5th Lord Erskine (<1513-1555) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT John Erskine, styled Sir John Erskine of Nisbet during his father's lifetime, was a Scottish nobleman and courtier, later retrospectively acknowledged as the 17th Earl of Mar after the family's claim to the earldom was recognized 
2 CONC in 1565. He succeeded his father, Robert Erskine, 4th Lord Erskine, after the Battle of Flodden in 1513, and received sasine of extensive lands that November.
2 CONT
2 CONT By June 1510, he had attained the rank of knight. In 1515, he was present at the Scottish nobles’ acceptance of the Anglo-French treaty, and was appointed one of three lords responsible for the personal guardianship of the infant 
2 CONC King James V. He received a salary for this service from 1517 to 1522, and later a monthly allowance of £50. On 3 August 1522, he was appointed Keeper of Stirling Castle and guardian of the 10-year-old king, under instructions fro
2 CONC m Margaret Tudor, the Queen Mother, including to hold the castle keys and to set the night password for the King's guards. These instructions were confirmed by the Parliament of Scotland in 1523.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1535, Erskine travelled to England to collect the collar of the Order of the Garter on behalf of King James V. The investiture took place at Windsor Castle, and he later met Henry VIII at Thornbury Castle. James V had given Ers
2 CONC kine precise instructions regarding royal precedence in the Garter Chapel, seeking a position next to the King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in London, Thomas Cromwell’s servant, John Gostwyk, presented Erskine with a gift of silver plate. Additional gifts were given to his heraldic companions, including £20 to Lyon King of Arms David Lyndsay and 80 crowns to the
2 CONC  Rothesay Herald.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine also served as a commissioner for the marriage negotiations between James V and Mary of Bourbon. After James's eventual marriage to Madeleine of Valois, Erskine took possession of Dunbar Castle, previously held by John Ste
2 CONC wart, Duke of Albany, on behalf of the French.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1540, he was again appointed guardian of the infant Prince James during the king’s expedition to the Isles, and following King James V’s death in 1542, Erskine became custodian of the infant Queen Mary. He also held the custody
2 CONC  of Edinburgh Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though traditionally said to have died in 1552, records confirm that he was alive in July 1555, when he witnessed the marriage contract of his son. He died sometime between that date and November 1555.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family and Issue ===
2 CONT
2 CONT John Erskine was the son of Robert Erskine, 4th Lord Erskine and Isabel Campbell. He married Lady Margaret Campbell, daughter of Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll, and Elizabeth Stuart.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Sons ====
2 CONT * '''Robert Erskine, Master of Erskine''' – Granted lands of Kellie (1536) and Sheirgarten (1542). Captured at the Battle of Solway Moss in 1542, he was killed at the Battle of Pinkie in 1547. He left no lawful issue by his wife, 
2 CONC Margaret Graham (daughter of the 2nd Earl of Montrose), but had a natural son:
2 CONT ** David Erskine – Became Abbot of Inchmahome and Dryburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sir Thomas Erskine, Master of Erskine''' – Succeeded as Master after Robert's death. He held the abbacy of Dryburgh (1541–1547), concluded a treaty with England in 1551, and received sasine of Brotherstanes in October 1551. H
2 CONC e likely died before his father. He married Margaret Fleming, widow of Lord Graham, but had no legitimate issue. His natural children included:
2 CONT ** Adam Erskine – Commendator of Cambuskenneth.
2 CONT ** Elizabeth Erskine – Natural daughter by Jonet Abernethy, married Robert Graham of Gartmore.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''John Erskine, 6th Lord Erskine''' – Succeeded his father and was later created Earl of Mar, de facto and de jure.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sir Alexander Erskine of Gogar''' – Ancestor of the Earls of Kellie.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Arthur Erskine of Blackgrange''' – Described in 1557 as brother of John and Alexander. He was a favoured equerry of Queen Mary and present at the murder of David Rizzio. He married Magdalen Livingstone but died childless befo
2 CONC re 1571.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Daughters ====
2 CONT * '''Catherine Erskine''' – Contracted in 1518 to John Murray of Touchadam, but married Alexander, 2nd Lord Elphinstone in 1525.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Margaret Erskine''' – Contracted in 1527 to Robert Douglas of Lochleven, whom she married. She was also a mistress of King James V and the mother of James Stewart, later Regent Moray. She died on 5 May 1572.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Janet Erskine''' – Married in 1532 to John Murray of Touchadam, ancestor of the Polmaise family.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Erskine''' – Said to have married Walter Seton of Touch, Tullibody.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''(Another) Margaret Erskine''' – Possibly an illegitimate daughter, married George Home of Lundies before 1526, receiving a tocher of 400 merks.
