1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John, 6th Lord Erskine, Later 1st Earl of Mar (<1548-1572), Regent of Scotland'''
2 CONT s/o Sir John Erskine, 5th Lord Erskine & Margaret Campbell
2 CONT x c 29 January 1556–57 Annabella Murray, d/o William Murray of Tullibardine
2 CONT * John Erskine, Earl of Mar.
2 CONT * Mary Erskine x 13 June 1573  Archibald Douglas, 8th Earl of Angus
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT == VI. John, Sixth Lord Erskine, Later 1st Earl of Mar (d. 1572) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Before inheriting the estates, John Erskine held several ecclesiastical offices, including Abbot of Dryburgh and Commendator of Inchmahome, and apparently also the Commendatorship of Cambuskenneth. He held the first two in 1548, a
2 CONC nd was referred to as a cleric in the 1556 appointment of his nephew David to Inchmahome. This suggests he was originally intended for the Church and likely took minor orders. However, the deaths of his elder brothers led him to a
2 CONC dopt a secular path. Upon succeeding between July and November 1555, he resigned his preferments, which were reassigned within the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a time, he remained politically inactive, but in 1559, during early tensions between the Queen-Dowager and the Lords of the Congregation, he maintained a policy of neutrality as Keeper of Edinburgh Castle. Nonetheless, he rece
2 CONC ived the Queen-Dowager in her final illness, and she died there on 11 June 1560. Though accused of being a Papist and swayed by the Queen-Regent, after her death he joined the Protestant party, along with Lord James Stewart and ot
2 CONC hers.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was later a member of the Queen’s Council after Mary returned to Scotland. In 1564, he expressed support for the Queen marrying an Englishman and showed little support for the Lennox family. In May 1565, he was served as the ne
2 CONC arest and lawful heir to Robert, Earl of Mar and Garioch, Lord Erskine — his direct ancestor. This legal preparation preceded his restoration to the earldom. On 23 June 1565, Queen Mary granted him the '''Earldom of Mar''', which 
2 CONC included the territories of Strathdon, Braemar, Cromar, and Strathdee, and the lordship of Garioch, to be held in free earldom, fee, and heritage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary restored the earldom due to his undoubted hereditary right and the injustices suffered by his predecessors, as well as in recognition of his services to the Crown. A precept of sasine was issued the same day, and he was forma
2 CONC lly infeft in the regality of Garioch on 24 July 1565. He sat as Lord Erskine for the last time on 28 July and as Earl of Mar for the first time on 1 August 1565. The Queen’s marriage to Darnley took place between these dates, on 
2 CONC 29 July. One contemporary noted that “to honour the feast, the L. Erskine was made Earl of Mar,” along with the knighting of others.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the Queen’s letters of 9 August 1565 ordering tenants of the earldom to pay their dues to the new Earl, opposition to his claim arose. The Queen issued a mandate affirming that Erskine’s right was based both on descent and
2 CONC  his surrender of a liferent interest in the lands and lordship of Menteith. Nevertheless, difficulties in collecting rents, such as those assigned to trumpeters from the Wardres lands, persisted. Mary instructed that these obliga
2 CONC tions be met from other sources and that no part of the earldom should be infringed upon. The Parliament of Scotland ratified her grant and the accompanying gifts to Lord Mar on 19 April 1567.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 19 March 1566–67, Mar was relieved of his post as Keeper of Edinburgh Castle and was honourably discharged. Shortly thereafter, as Captain of Stirling Castle, he was entrusted with the care of the infant Prince James, to be “nu
2 CONC rst and upbrocht” under his supervision. From this point, his life became deeply intertwined with the fate of the Scottish monarchy. He protected the young King during the turbulent times under the regencies of Moray and Lennox, a
2 CONC nd after the latter's assassination, Mar was appointed '''Regent of Scotland''' on 5 September 1571 — a choice met with widespread approval.
2 CONT
2 CONT His regency was relatively uneventful. Key moments included his reluctant involvement in handing over the rebel Earl of Northumberland to Queen Elizabeth of England and discussions regarding the return of Queen Mary to Scotland to
2 CONC  be tried under Scottish law — an outcome that would almost certainly have resulted in her execution. His opinion on this matter remains unclear, as he died on 28 October 1572 while negotiations were still underway.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had made his will at Stirling on 9 August 1568, requesting burial at Alloa and that the remains of his ancestors from Cambuskenneth be reinterred there. He named his wife as tutrix (guardian) to his two children, entrusted her 
2 CONC with paying his debts, and ensuring his servants were cared for.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, around 29 January 1556–57, '''Annabella Murray''', daughter of William Murray of Tullibardine. She survived him, dying in February 1602–03.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John Erskine, Earl of Mar, and Annabella Murray:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''John Erskine, Earl of Mar.'''
2 CONT * '''Mary Erskine''', married on 13 June 1573 to '''Archibald Douglas''', 8th Earl of Angus. Part of her tocher (dowry), amounting to 8,000 merks Scots, was paid on 1 May 1573.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Reference
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p612 -15]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05pauluoft/page/590/mode/1up The Scots Peerage V: see pp. 590-636 for Erskine, Earl of Mar]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ee/erskine01.php Stirnet: Erskine 01]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ee/erskine02.php Stirnet: Erskine 02]'''
2 CONT # '''[http://thepeerage.com/p1936.htm#i19354 Darryl Lundy's Peerage]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Erskine,_Earl_of_Mar_%28d._1572%29 Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The 17th Earl of Mar.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Erskine, Earl of Mar (died 28 October 1572), regent of Scotland, was a son of John, 5th Lord Erskine, who was guardian of King James V, and afterwards of Mary, Queen of Scots. He is regarded as both the 18th earl (in the 1st 
2 CONC creation) and the 1st earl (in the 7th).
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT John was Commendator of Dryburgh Abbey from 1547,[1] and succeeded his father as 6th Lord Erskine in 1552. He joined the religious reformers, but was never very ardent in the cause. He did subscribe to the letter asking the Calvin
2 CONC ist reformer John Knox to return to Scotland in 1557. The custody of Edinburgh Castle was in his hands during the struggle between the regent, Mary of Guise, and the Lords of the Congregation he appears to have acted consistently 
2 CONC in the interests of peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Mary, Queen of Scots, returned to Scotland in 1561 Lord Erskine was a member of her council and was in favor of her marriage with Henry Stuart, Lord Darnley. His wife was Annabella Murray, daughter of William Murray of Tullib
2 CONC ardine. She was a frequent companion of Queen Mary, John Knox called her a "verray Jesabell", . In 1565 Erskine was granted the earldom of Mar. As guardian of James, afterwards King James VI at Stirling Castle he prevented the you
2 CONC ng prince from falling into the hands of James Hepburn, 4th Earl of Bothwell, and when the Scottish nobles rose against Mary and Bothwell, Mar was one of their leaders; he took part in the government of Scotland during Marys impri
2 CONC sonment at Lochleven Castle, and also after her subsequent abdication.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regent of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 1571 he was chosen Regent of Scotland, but he was overshadowed and perhaps slighted by James Douglas, 4th Earl of Morton. He died at Stirling on 29 October 1572 after a short illness, widely agreed to have been natura
2 CONC l causes. However, some sources indicate that he may have been poisoned at the will of the Earl of Morton. Mar's illness, wrote James Melville, followed a banquet at Dalkeith Palace given by Morton.[2] John began building the hous
2 CONC e at Stirling called 'Mar's Wark', now a ruin the care of Historic Scotland, the other seat of the family was Alloa Tower. James VI continued to regard Annabella Murray with affection and wrote to her as "Minnie", she was the gove
2 CONC rness of his son Prince Henry at Stirling.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT # Jump up ^ Scottish Correspondence of Mary of Lorraine, SHS (1927), 220 note.
2 CONT # Jump up ^ Melville, James, Thomson, Thomas, ed. (1827). Memoirs of his own life. Bannatyne Club, (1827), pp 248–249.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan Stewart Cradle King
2 CONT
