1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John Erskine, 5th Lord Erskine''' (<1513-1555)
2 CONT s/o Robert Erskine, 4th Lord Erskine & Isabel Campbell.
2 CONT x '''Lady Margaret Campbell''', d/o Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll, & Elizabeth Stuart.
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT * '''Robert Erskine, Master of Erskine'''
2 CONT * '''Sir Thomas Erskine, Master of Erskine''' 
2 CONT * '''John Erskine, 6th Lord Erskine''' 
2 CONT * '''Sir Alexander Erskine of Gogar''' 
2 CONT * '''Arthur Erskine of Blackgrange''' 
2 CONT Daughters 
2 CONT * '''Catherine Erskine''' x Alexander, 2nd Lord Elphinstone in 1525.
2 CONT * '''Margaret Erskine''' x Robert Douglas of Lochleven xx mistress of King James V 
2 CONT * '''Janet Erskine''' x John Murray of Touchadam
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Erskine''' x Walter Seton of Touch, Tullibody.
2 CONT * '''(Another) Margaret Erskine''' – Possibly an illegitimate daughter x George Home of Lundies 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT == John Erskine, 5th Lord Erskine (<1513-1555) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT John Erskine, styled Sir John Erskine of Nisbet during his father's lifetime, was a Scottish nobleman and courtier, later retrospectively acknowledged as the 17th Earl of Mar after the family's claim to the earldom was recognized 
2 CONC in 1565. He succeeded his father, Robert Erskine, 4th Lord Erskine, after the Battle of Flodden in 1513, and received sasine of extensive lands that November.
2 CONT
2 CONT By June 1510, he had attained the rank of knight. In 1515, he was present at the Scottish nobles’ acceptance of the Anglo-French treaty, and was appointed one of three lords responsible for the personal guardianship of the infant 
2 CONC King James V. He received a salary for this service from 1517 to 1522, and later a monthly allowance of £50. On 3 August 1522, he was appointed Keeper of Stirling Castle and guardian of the 10-year-old king, under instructions fro
2 CONC m Margaret Tudor, the Queen Mother, including to hold the castle keys and to set the night password for the King's guards. These instructions were confirmed by the Parliament of Scotland in 1523.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1535, Erskine travelled to England to collect the collar of the Order of the Garter on behalf of King James V. The investiture took place at Windsor Castle, and he later met Henry VIII at Thornbury Castle. James V had given Ers
2 CONC kine precise instructions regarding royal precedence in the Garter Chapel, seeking a position next to the King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in London, Thomas Cromwell’s servant, John Gostwyk, presented Erskine with a gift of silver plate. Additional gifts were given to his heraldic companions, including £20 to Lyon King of Arms David Lyndsay and 80 crowns to the
2 CONC  Rothesay Herald.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine also served as a commissioner for the marriage negotiations between James V and Mary of Bourbon. After James's eventual marriage to Madeleine of Valois, Erskine took possession of Dunbar Castle, previously held by John Ste
2 CONC wart, Duke of Albany, on behalf of the French.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1540, he was again appointed guardian of the infant Prince James during the king’s expedition to the Isles, and following King James V’s death in 1542, Erskine became custodian of the infant Queen Mary. He also held the custody
2 CONC  of Edinburgh Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though traditionally said to have died in 1552, records confirm that he was alive in July 1555, when he witnessed the marriage contract of his son. He died sometime between that date and November 1555.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family and Issue ===
2 CONT
2 CONT John Erskine was the son of Robert Erskine, 4th Lord Erskine and Isabel Campbell. He married Lady Margaret Campbell, daughter of Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll, and Elizabeth Stuart.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Sons ====
2 CONT * '''Robert Erskine, Master of Erskine''' – Granted lands of Kellie (1536) and Sheirgarten (1542). Captured at the Battle of Solway Moss in 1542, he was killed at the Battle of Pinkie in 1547. He left no lawful issue by his wife, 
2 CONC Margaret Graham (daughter of the 2nd Earl of Montrose), but had a natural son:
2 CONT ** David Erskine – Became Abbot of Inchmahome and Dryburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sir Thomas Erskine, Master of Erskine''' – Succeeded as Master after Robert's death. He held the abbacy of Dryburgh (1541–1547), concluded a treaty with England in 1551, and received sasine of Brotherstanes in October 1551. H
2 CONC e likely died before his father. He married Margaret Fleming, widow of Lord Graham, but had no legitimate issue. His natural children included:
2 CONT ** Adam Erskine – Commendator of Cambuskenneth.
2 CONT ** Elizabeth Erskine – Natural daughter by Jonet Abernethy, married Robert Graham of Gartmore.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''John Erskine, 6th Lord Erskine''' – Succeeded his father and was later created Earl of Mar, de facto and de jure.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sir Alexander Erskine of Gogar''' – Ancestor of the Earls of Kellie.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Arthur Erskine of Blackgrange''' – Described in 1557 as brother of John and Alexander. He was a favoured equerry of Queen Mary and present at the murder of David Rizzio. He married Magdalen Livingstone but died childless befo
2 CONC re 1571.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Daughters ====
2 CONT * '''Catherine Erskine''' – Contracted in 1518 to John Murray of Touchadam, but married Alexander, 2nd Lord Elphinstone in 1525.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Margaret Erskine''' – Contracted in 1527 to Robert Douglas of Lochleven, whom she married. She was also a mistress of King James V and the mother of James Stewart, later Regent Moray. She died on 5 May 1572.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Janet Erskine''' – Married in 1532 to John Murray of Touchadam, ancestor of the Polmaise family.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Erskine''' – Said to have married Walter Seton of Touch, Tullibody.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''(Another) Margaret Erskine''' – Possibly an illegitimate daughter, married George Home of Lundies before 1526, receiving a tocher of 400 merks.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT *[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p609-12]
