2 CONT
2 CONT Find A Grave: Margaret Erskine Douglas Birth:  1515 East Linton East Lothian, Scotland Death:  May 5, 1572 North Middleton Midlothian, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Margaret Erskine Birth: Abt.1515 Linlithgow Palace, West Lothian, Scotland Death: 5 May 1572 Edinburgh,Edinburghshire, Midlothian,Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress to King James V of Scotland ( 1512-1542 )
2 CONT *Their Son* James Stewart ( 1st Earl of Moray ) and illegitimate son of the KING"  Birth: 1531 in Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland Death: 23 January 1570 in Linlithgow, West Lothian, Scotland (Assasination ) ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~* P
2 CONC arents: Father: John Erskine Birth: 1487 in Of, Nisbet, Scotland Death: Nov 1555 in Dunbarton, Dunbartonshire, , Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Lady Margaret Campbell Birth: 1485 Bridgend Lochgilphead, Scotland Death: 11 July 1555 Moray, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~*
2 CONT
2 CONT HUSBAND:  Sir Robert Douglas ( Killed in battle) Son of Sir Robert Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT James V had a number of mistresses in his time, but some accounts describe her as his favourite. She was the mother of the most important of his illegitimate children, James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray, who was Regent during the mi
2 CONC nority of James VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1527, Margaret married Sir Robert Douglas of Lochleven, who was killed at the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh. Her son Robert Douglas was sent to England and Cambridge University in 1560 as a hostage for the Treaty of Berwick.[1] She w
2 CONC as the châtelaine of Lochleven Castle when Mary, Queen of Scots was kept there, with her eldest surviving son William Douglas, 6th Earl of Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Margaret Erskine had married Robert Douglas there is evidence that James V may have considered arranging their divorce and marrying his mistress. It seems that James or one of his advisors sought the advice of the Pope in
2 CONC  the matter in June 1536. Shortly before James V finalised his marriage contract with Madeleine of Valois in November 1536, Charles, Bishop of Macon and French ambassador at the Vatican, wrote discussing his audience with the Pope
2 CONC . The Bishop had told the Pope that James never intended to marry Margaret and the petition was an imposture. The Pope replied that he had postponed any grant, thinking that the proposal was made without the King's knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronicle accounts and English letters also mention this scheme and the involvement of James Hamilton of Finnart. One of the English ambassador Sir William Howard's informants was Margaret Tudor, and he reported to Henry VIII;
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sire, I hear, both by the Queen's Grace your sister and diverse others that the marriage is broken between the King's Grace your nephew and the Monsieur de Vendôme, and he will marry a gentlewoman in Scotland, the Lord of Erskine
2 CONC 's daughter, who was with your Grace the last summer at Thornbury; by whom he has had a child, having a husband, and his Grace has found means to divorce them. And there is great lamentation made for it in this country as far as m
2 CONC en dare. Sire, there was no man made privy to this matter but Sir James Hamilton." (25 April 1536)
2 CONT
2 CONT Had the marriage gone ahead, their son James Stewart, the future Regent, could have been declared legitimate. By July 1536, the Imperial ambassador in London, Eustace Chapuys, and Spanish diplomats at the Vatican believed the marr
2 CONC iage had already taken place.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1570s Margaret Douglas looked after her granddaughters at the New House of Lochleven and kept up a correspondence with their mother, Agnes Keith, Countess of Moray. In January 1570, she wrote that Lochleven Loch was frozen,
2 CONC  and her son was in the "Loich", the old castle on the lake island, because he was keeping the Earl of Northumberland, who was a fugitive from the Northern Rebellion. In June 1571, she wrote of her health and complained that Agnes
2 CONC , her daughter-in-law had not visited;
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ye sall onderstand that I have beyne wery extreme seik baith in my bodye and stomak, and with ane sair leg, quhairoff (I) am nocht throichlie conwelleseit as yett ... I wald skarslie have belevit ye would have bene neir (at) hand
2 CONC  and veseit me nocht, and frindis heir." ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~*  
2 CONT Family links:   Parents:   John Erskine (1487 - 1555)   Margaret Campbell Erskine (1485 - 1555)  
2 CONT  Spouse:    James, King of Scots (1512 - 1542)*  
2 CONT  Children:   James Stewart (1531 - 1570)*  
2 CONT Burial: All Saints Churchyard  Cambridge City of Cambridge Cambridgeshire, England  
2 CONT Created by: Ro§e❈ Record added: Apr 23, 2015  '''Find A Grave Memorial# 145480299'
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Margaret Erskine (died 5 May 1572) was a mistress of King James V of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of John Erskine, 5th Lord Erskine and Lady Margaret Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT James V had a number of mistresses in his time, but some accounts describe her as his favourite. She was the mother of the most important of his illegitimate children, James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray, who was Regent during the mi
2 CONC nority of James VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1527, Margaret married Sir Robert Douglas of Lochleven, who was killed at the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh. Her son Robert Douglas was sent to England and Cambridge University in 1560 as a hostage for the Treaty of Berwick. She was 
2 CONC the châtelaine of Lochleven Castle when Mary, Queen of Scots was kept there, with her eldest surviving son William Douglas, 6th Earl of Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rumoured as royal bride
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Margaret Erskine had married Robert Douglas there is evidence that James V may have considered arranging their divorce and marrying his mistress. It seems that James or one of his advisors sought the advice of the Pope in
2 CONC  the matter in June 1536. Shortly before James V finalised his marriage contract with Madeleine of Valois in November 1536, Charles, Bishop of Macon and French ambassador at the Vatican, wrote discussing his audience with the Pope
2 CONC . The Bishop had told the Pope that James never intended to marry Margaret and the petition was an imposture. The Pope replied that he had postponed any grant, thinking that the proposal was made without the King's knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronicle accounts and English letters also mention this scheme and the involvement of James Hamilton of Finnart. One of the English ambassador Sir William Howard's informants was Margaret Tudor, and he reported to Henry VIII;
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sire, I hear, both by the Queen's Grace your sister and diverse others that the marriage is broken between the King's Grace your nephew and the Monsieur de Vendôme, and he will marry a gentlewoman in Scotland, the Lord of Erskine
2 CONC 's daughter, who was with your Grace the last summer at Thornbury; by whom he has had a child, having a husband, and his Grace has found means to divorce them. And there is great lamentation made for it in this country as far as m
2 CONC en dare. Sire, there was no man made privy to this matter but Sir James Hamilton." (25 April 1536)
2 CONT
2 CONT Had the marriage gone ahead, their son James Stewart, the future Regent, could have been declared legitimate. By July 1536, the Imperial ambassador in London, Eustace Chapuys, and Spanish diplomats at the Vatican believed the marr
2 CONC iage had already taken place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1570s Margaret Douglas looked after her granddaughters at the New House of Lochleven and kept up a correspondence with their mother, Agnes Keith, Countess of Moray. In January 1570, she wrote that Lochleven Loch was frozen,
2 CONC  and her son was in the "Loich", the old castle on the lake island, because he was keeping the Earl of Northumberland, who was a fugitive from the Northern Rebellion. In June 1571, she wrote of her health and complained that Agnes
2 CONC , her daughter-in-law had not visited;
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ye sall onderstand that I have beyne wery extreme seik baith in my bodye and stomak, and with ane sair leg, quhairoff (I) am nocht throichlie conwelleseit as yett ... I wald skarslie have belevit ye would have bene neir (at) hand
2 CONC  and veseit me nocht, and frindis heir."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  Lady Margaret Erskine (c1510 – 1572) was a Scottish noblewoman and courtier, the mistress of King James I (1513 – 1542), she was the daughter of John, fourth Baron Erskine, and sister to John Erskine, earl of Mar, and was married
2 CONC  (1527) to Sir Robert Douglas, of Lochleven. He was killed at the battle of Pinkie Cleugh (1547).
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her liasion with the king, Margaret became the mother of James Stewart (1531 – 1570), first Earl of Moray, the half-brother to Queen Mary Stuart, and regent for James VI (I of England). She was not the king’s only mistress
2 CONC , but Margaret was his acknowledged favourite. After the birth of their son, King James had made enquiries concerning a papal dispensation which would enable him to marry Margaret, and make her his queen, thus legitimating their s
2 CONC on and making him the heir (1536), but Lady Margaret having been a married woman at the time of her son’s birth rendered that solution impossible. Her son’s later legitimization (1551) was in regard only to inheritable property, a
2 CONC nd had nothing to do with rights to the succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Erskine was the model for ‘Lady Sensualitie’ in Sir David Lyndsay’s work, Ane Pleasant Satyre of the Thrie Estaitis (c1535). Lady Margaret died (May 5, 1572), aged in her early sixties.)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.douglashistory.co.uk/history/margaret_erskine.htm
