1 NAME Isabel /Ogilvy of Linthrathen/
2 GIVN Isabel
2 SURN Ogilvy of Linthrathen
2 _MARNM Ogilvy of Linthrathen
2 NICK Isabel Lyon
2 NICK Lady Kennedy
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1406
2 ADDR
3 CITY Auchterhouse
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1484
2 ADDR
3 CITY Glamis
3 CTRY Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Isabel Ogilvy:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10823.htm#i108229
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Ogilvy[1]
2 CONT *F, #108229, 
2 CONT *d. 1484
2 CONT *Last Edited=30 Apr 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Ogilvy married, firstly, Patrick Lyon, 1st Lord Glamis, son of Sir John Lyon of Glamis and Elizabeth Graham, before 1459.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married, secondly, Gilbert Kennedy of Dunure, 1st Lord Kennedy, son of Sir James Kennedy of Dunure, younger and Lady Mary Stewart, after 1460.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in 1484.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Sir Walter Ogilvy of Lintrathen and Isabel Glen.[2],[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT From before 1459, her married name became Lyon.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result of her marriage, Isabel Ogilvy was styled as Lady Kennedy. From after 1460, her married name became Kennedy.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Isabel Ogilvy and Patrick Lyon, 1st Lord Glamis
2 CONT *1. Elizabeth Lyon
2 CONT *2. John Lyon, 3rd Lord Glamis+ d. 1 Apr 1497
2 CONT *3. Alexander Lyon, 2nd Lord Glamis d. c 1486
2 CONT *4. William Lyon+
2 CONT *5. Patrick Lyon d. a 1481
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=maclaren&id=I16244
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother is sometimes listed as Duward. However From Doug Hickling:
2 CONT
2 CONT "You list the mother of Isabella (or Isabel) Ogilvy, who married Patrick (1st  Baron Glamis) Lyon, as a Durward. The better evidence is that she was Isabel  Glen, daughter of John (Sir) Glen of Inchmartin (sometimes shown as of Ba
2 CONC lmuto)  and Margaret Erskine. Margaret Erskine was the daughter of Isabel Inchmartin  and Allan (Sir) Erskine. Allan (Sir) was a younger son of William (Sir) Erskine  (of Erskine). Isabel Inchmartin was the daughter of John (Sir) 
2 CONC Inchmartin and  Margaret Wemyss, who in turn was the daughter of David (Sir) Wemyss. SP 4:16  says that "Walter (Sir) Ogilvy of Auchlevyn and Desford was the eldest son of  Walter (Sir) Ogilvy of Carcary and Lintrathen by his seco
2 CONC nd wife Isobel, one of  the three daughters and heirs-portioneers of John (Sir) Glen of Balmuto by his  wife Margaret Erskine." The information on the Inchmartins comes from an  article by Bruce McAndrew, who is considered very re
2 CONC liable, in the periodical  DOUBLE TRESSURE, No. 4 (1982) pp 2-10'
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VII, page 121. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *2. [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 228. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Lord Glamis died at Belhelvies on 21 March 1459, and was buried at Glamis. Judging from the period at which their children began to take an active part in public life, the marriage of Lord Glamis with Isobel Ogilvy, daughter of Si
2 CONC r Walter Ogilvy of Lantrathen, must have taken place soon after his return from England in 1427. After her first husband’s death Lady Glamis married Gilbert, first Lord Kennedy, whom she also survived. She had a full share of the 
2 CONC pugnacity of the race form which she sprang. She fought her sons, her tenants, her neighbours, and her creditors, and had a tough struggle with the representatives of her second husband for the possession of the family plate. On 2
2 CONC 0 June 1480 she entered into an indenture with the Prior and convent of the Preaching Dominicans, friars of Ayr, who, with consent of Brother John More, Vicar-General of that Order in Scotland, agreed, in return for a liberal endo
2 CONC wment of lands in the town and sheriffdom of Ayr, to perform divine service for the benefit of the souls of James and Margaret, King and Queen of Scotland, of Isobel herself and her father and mother, and of Patrick, Lord Glamis, 
2 CONC and Gilbert Kennedy, Lord of that Ilk, her husbands. After Lord Kennedy’s death, who was succeeded by his son by a previous marriage, her Ladyship was reconciled to her family, and returned to Forfarshire. ‘She in her widdowedheid
2 CONC  finished the old House of Glamis, built the two ston bridges, and the ille in the Kirk of Glames, wherein, with her first husband, she was interred in anno 1484, as the inscription upon the tomb bears witnes.’ [The Scots Peerage 
2 CONC VIII:270-272]
