2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Kennedy of Dunure, younger was the son of Sir Gilbert Kennedy of Dunure. He married Lady Mary Stewart, daughter of Robert III Stewart, King of Scotland and Annabel Drummond, circa 1404.1 He died before 8 November 1408 ki
2 CONC lled.2 Sir James Kennedy of Dunure, younger lived at Dunure, Ayrshire, Scotland. Children of Sir James Kennedy of Dunure, younger and Lady Mary Stewart Sir John Kennedy d. a 14341 Mary Kennedy+ 3 Gilbert Kennedy of Dunure, 1st Lor
2 CONC d Kennedy+ b. c 1406, d. a 6 Mar 1478/791 James Kennedy b. 1405/6, d. Jul 14651 Citations [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 228. Hereinafter cited as Britai
2 CONC n's Royal Family. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom
2 CONC , Extant, Extinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 155. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage. [S157] Sir Andrew, of Lochna
2 CONC w Agnew, The Hereditary Sheriffs of Galloway (n.n.: n.n., 1893), page 271. Hereinafter cited as The Hereditary Sheriffs of Galloway.
2 CONT ===
2 CONT
2 CONT From ODNB
2 CONT
2 CONT "John Kennedy's successor, Sir Gilbert Kennedy of Dunure (d. 1408x29), who had been a hostage in England for King David's ransom in 1357, appears to have been twice married, first to Marion Sandilands of Calder and then to Agnes M
2 CONC axwell of Pollok, but Sir James Kennedy (d. 1408), the eldest son of the second rather than the first marriage, was during his father's lifetime designated as heir to the family's lands and titles, possibly following his marriage 
2 CONC to Mary Stewart, a daughter of King Robert III. This marriage established cousinship with the royal house of Stewart which was to be important in the progress of Kennedy fortunes in the fifteenth century. ... Gilbert was still ali
2 CONC ve when James was killed on 8 November 1408, perhaps by disinherited members of his kindred—in that year he made an indenture with the duke of Albany, the governor of Scotland, under which in return for the latter's consent to an 
2 CONC entail of Kennedy lands, Gilbert became the duke's retainer."
2 CONT
2 CONT "James Kennedy left three infant sons, and during their minority, control of the family patrimony seems to have passed into the hands of his four brothers, in particular Alexander of Ardstinchar, who is described as tutor of James
2 CONC 's sons in one source ..."
