1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gilberto M'ylveyne
2 CONT * Birth:	Circa 1430 - Ayrshire, Scotland
2 CONT * Parents:	Nigel M'Ilveyne, Unknown M'Ilveyne
2 CONT * Wife:	 (born Kennedy)
2 CONT * Death:	1537 - Ayrshire, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT # John M'Ylveyne (1470 - 1530)
2 CONT # Allan M'Ylveyne, Laird of Grimmet (1470 - 1545)
2 CONT # David M'Ylveyne (1470 - 1530)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Residence:	the estate of his late father, Ayrshire, Scotland. 1484
2 CONT * Military Service:	Col. of horse, Army of Robt. the Bruce
2 CONT * Occupation:	Laird of Grimmet  Between 1484 and 1528/29
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.maybole.org/history/surnames/macilvean/of_carrick.htm THE MACILVEANS OF CARRICK]
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Macilvean is of Scottish Gaelic origin and means ‘the son of the servant of St Beathan (or Bain), MAC - GILLE - VAIN, (in Gaelic ‘b’ is very close to ‘v’ and the name originates in Carrick and Galloway.  For over 200 year
2 CONC s the senior MacIlvean was known as the Laird of Grimmet.  The original lands were those of the farm, still called after Grimmet, two miles east of Maybole.  (The name is also used for a farm on the eastern edge of Straiton parish
2 CONC ).  Black gives the earliest records of the MacIlvean family as '''Gilbert Macilmeyn of Grimmet''' in the Register of the Privy Seal of Scotland in 1503, and his son Alano granted the charter of the lands of Grimmet in 1529 and a 
2 CONC Bailie of Crossraguel.                 
