2 CONT
2 CONT '''George Campbell of Loudoun, here treated, is the eldest son and heir of https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-George-Campbell-of-Loudon-Sheriff-of-Ayr/6000000003828423007?through=6000000002980014179  [https://archive.org/details/regi
2 CONC strummagnisi02scot/page/395/mode/1up RMS: 1870] His mother is https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Stewart/6000000020119728860?through=6000000002980014179 [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05pauluoft/page/493/mode/1up 
2 CONC The Scots Peerage V: 493-4]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''According to the late Francis J. Grant, George Campbell of Loudoun, here treated: "had a tack of the lands of Collyndach in Glenkens, Kirkcudbright, 1488, a charter as son and heir of his father of the office of Sheriff of Ayrs
2 CONC hire on his father's resignation, 4 July 1489, [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi02scot/page/395/mode/1up RMS: 1870] a charter erecting Newmills into a burgh of barony 9 January 1490-91, was seised in the lands of Martn
2 CONC ame, Loudoun, and Scheilin 1492, and died shortly after 1492. He is said to have married, first, a daughter of Gilbert, first Lord Kennedy, and had issue, and secondly, Marion, probably daughter of Sir John Auchinleck of that Ilk,
2 CONC  who survived him, and was married, secondly, to William Cunningham, as appears from a decree dated 6 March 1504, narrating that there appeared Hew Campbell of Loudoun on the one part, and Marion Auchinleck, Lady of Loudoun, and W
2 CONC illiam Cunningham, her spouse, on the other, and that the said Hew obliged himself to pay to the said Marion and William the sum of 230 merks owing to them for the assedation made by them to the said Hew of said Marion's terce and
2 CONC  the third part of the lands pertaining or that may pertain to her by reason of the decease of umquhile George Campbell of Loudoun, Sheriff of Ayr, her spouse, in terms of an indenture made between the said Hugh and Marion and Wil
2 CONC liam, dated at Edinburgh 7 March 1502." [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05pauluoft/page/493/mode/1up The Scots Peerage V: 493-4]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''George Campbell of Loudoun, here treated, is reported to have died shortly after 1492. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05pauluoft/page/493/mode/1up The Scots Peerage V: 493-4] It seems quite likely, therefore, prob
2 CONC able even, that he died before 4 October 1492, when his son and heir https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Hugh-Campbell-of-Loudoun-sheriff-of-Ayr/6000000002980028165?through=6000000002980014179 is noticed holding the office of Sheriff 
2 CONC of Ayr. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f1%2f10%2f108&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=hugh+campbell+of+loudoun&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=n NRS: GD3/1/10/108]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4 July 1489: REX concessit familiari armigero suo GEORGIO CAMPBELL, filio et heredi apparenti Georgii Campbell de Lowdoun, militis, vicecomitis de Ayr, et heredibus ejus, — officium vicecomitatus de Ayr ; — quod dictus Geo. pat
2 CONC er personaliter resignavit. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi02scot/page/395/mode/1up RMS 1424-1513: charter number 1870]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 February 1492-93: Royal summons under quarter seal, at instance of John Haldane of Glennegas [Gleneagles] and James Haldane, his son and heir, and one of legitimate and nearest heirs of deceased Duncan, earl of Levenax [Lenno
2 CONC x], legitimately descended from his elder daughter, viz., John, lord Dernely [Darnley], and Matthew Stewart, his son and heir, charging Donald Malynny [Malleny], Symon Spardour and George Schaw [Shaw], messengers, to summon said J
2 CONC ohn, lord Dernely [Darnley], and Matthew Stewart, with Malise, earl of Kenteth [Menteith], Thomas, lord Erskyn [Erskine], James, lord Hammiltoun [Hamilton], Robert, lord Lile [Lyle], Laurence, lord Oliphant, William Emonstoune [Ed
2 CONC miston] of Duntreith [Duntreath], GEORGE CAMPBELL OF LOWDOUNE [Loudoun], James Sandelandis [Sandilands] of Caldour, John Campbell of Nether Lowdoune [Loudoun], John Ross of Halkhed [Halkhead], William Symple [Semple], Henry Dowgla
2 CONC s [Douglas] of Lochlevin [Loch Leven], Robert Hammiltoun [Hamilton] of Prestoune [Preston], William Streveling [Stirling] of Cadere [Calder], Archibald of Dundas of that ilk, John Maxwell, younger, kt., Robert Creichtoun [Crichton
2 CONC ] of Kynnoull [Kinneil], Arthur Stewart, John Stewart of Cragy [Craigie], Philip Mowbray [Moubray] of Dunmanyng, Thomas Spreull [Sproul] of Coldoune [Coldon], Thomas Buchquhannane [Buchanan], John Blayre [Blair] of Fynwik [Finnich
2 CONC ], Malcolm Levenax [Lennox], Gilbert Naper [Napier], Duncan Naper of Kilmahew, Alexander Arngable [Ardincaple] of that ilk, John Lindssay [Lindsay] of Bullule, Robert Makcalpyn [McAlpine], Malcolm Mcclery [McCleary] and Angus Camp
2 CONC bell, members of inquest on which precept from chancery, 20 [24?] July 1473, followed in favour of said John, lord Dernely [Darnley], as one of heirs of said deceased Duncan, earl of Levenax [Lennox], appear at Edinburgh or where 
2 CONC king is, 18 April next to come, (said precept being before deceased James Schaw [Shaw] of Sauchy [Sauchie], sheriff of Striviling [Stirling] in that part) to see said precept reduced, retracted and declared to be of no effect. In 
2 CONC dorso: executions, 21 Feb-22 March, 1492/3, by Donald Malinny, messenger, against Robert, lord Creichtoun [Crichton] of Sanchar [Sanquhar], Robert, lord Lile, Laurence, lord Oliphant, John Stewart of Cragy [Craigie], Sir John Camp
2 CONC bell of Nether Lowdoune at Lowdoune [Loudoun], Thomas Sproile [Sproul] of Coldoune, John the Ross of Halkheid [Halkhead], kt., at Halkheid [Halkhead], John, lord Darnly [Darnley], Mathew Stewart, his son and heir, Alexander Harthc
2 CONC apill [Ardincaple] of that ilk, Thomas of Buchanane [Buchanan], John Lindesay [Lindsay] of Bunull, Malcolm Mcclery [McCleary], John Blare [Blair] of Fynewik [Finnich] at Fynewik, and Thomas, lord Erskyne [Erskine]. Witnesses: Geor
2 CONC ge Ermane, John ?Bu, Sir William Lindesay [Lindsay], kt., Robert Lile [Lyle], John Lindesay [Lindsay], John Richardsone [Richardson], Thomas Bissat [Bisset], Robert of Erskyne [Erskine], James Cambell [Campbell], sir Thomas Blare 
2 CONC [Blair], chaplain, Stevin Sproile [Sproul], Watt Alansone [Allanson], Robert Stewart, Mr. Alexander Stewart, Walter of Buchanane [Buchanan] of that ilk, Thomas Sympill [Semple], Alexander Cambell [Campbell], John of Calbraith, Mal
2 CONC colm Richardsone William Patriksone [Patrickson], James Auchinlek [Affleck], James Haire [Hair], Andrew Brangly. Note, 15th June, that action between Lord Dernely [Darnley] and his son on one part, and laird of Glennegas [Gleneagl
2 CONC es] and his son on other part, is continued till 8 October. Tongue, seal gone. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD198%2f58&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=george+campbell&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=14
2 CONC 88&dt=1495&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Haldane Family of Gleneagles, Perthshire, reference GD198/58]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4 October 1492: Instrument of sasine in favour of John Boyll as heir of his father John Boyll of Wanfra in the lands of Rysholme in the Barony of Dalry. James Fergushill, sheriff depute of Hugh Campbell of Loudoun and sheriff o
2 CONC f Ayr. Witnesses: John Blayr of that ilk and others. John Boyd, notary. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f1%2f10%2f108&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=hugh+campbell+of+loudoun&ko=p&r=&
2 CONC ro=s&df=&dt=&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Montgomerie Family, Earls of Eglinton, reference GD3/1/10/108]'''
