1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == ALEXANDER MONTGOMERIE 1st Lord Montgomerie =
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Keeper of Brodick Castle on the Island of Arran; Baillie of the Barony of Cunningham in Aryshire; Baillie of Kilbride in the Sheriffdom of Lanark===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''It has been suggested that Alexander, Lord Montgomery, here treated, may have died in 1470. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/page/434/mode/1up The Scots Peerage III: 434]. Howev
2 CONC er, it would appear that this may be wrong. Certainly, it would appear that Alexander, Lord Montgomery, was still alive on 5 February 1480. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f1%2f1
2 CONC %2f63%2f1&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=lord+montgomery&ko=p&r=&ro=m&df=1469&dt=1480&di=n NRS: GD3/1/1/63/1]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''30 June 1458: Compotum Alexandri domini Mountgomery, ballivi de Kilbride infra vicecomitatum de Lanark, redditum apud Perth ultimo die mensis Junii anno Domini etc. quinquagesinio octavo, de omnibus receptis suis et expensis pe
2 CONC r firmas et exitus bailie sue a septimo die mensis Julii anni Domini etc. quinquagesimi usque in diem hujus compoti, et sic de octo annis integris infra hoc compotum. . . . . . Et de iijli. vj s. viij d. de firmis terrarum de Kitt
2 CONC osyde de termino Sancti Martini ultimo existencium in manibus regis, saisina non recuperata de eisdem. Et de ij rosis rubeis per duplicacionem albe firme regi debite per saisinam datam Ade Mure de dictis terris. [https://archive.o
2 CONC rg/details/rotuliscaccariir06grea/page/441/mode/1up Exchequer Rolls: 441-42]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''7 October 1469: Charter granted by Alexander, Lord Montgomery in favour of his beloved son George Montgomery of Skelmorlie and the heirs male lawfully procreated or to be procreated of his body of his lands of Loch of Raynsay a
2 CONC nd of Cathadill lying in Arran within the sheriffdom of Bute; to hold of him for services used and wont. Dated at Ardrossan. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f1%2f7%2f15%2f1&st=1&
2 CONC tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=lord+montgomery&ko=p&r=&ro=m&df=1469&dt=1480&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Montgomerie Family, Earls of Eglinton, reference GD3/1/7/15/1]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                   3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''5 February 1480: Charter granted by King James II in favour of Adam Davisoun, son of John Davisoun and his heirs of the lands of Snodgerss, lying in the bailliary of Cunningham and Shire of Ayr; on the resignation of the said J
2 CONC ohn Davisoun made at Strivelin; to hold of the King and his heirs, Stewarts of Scotland, for services used and wont. Witnesses: William, bishop of Glasgow, William, Lord Crichton, chancellor, Alexander, Lord Montgomery, William, L
2 CONC ord of Someruel and William of Edmonstone of Cullodin, knights, Masters John Arrous, archdeacon of Glasgow and George Schoviswod, rector of Cuthir. Given under the Privy Seal at Are and in the 14th year of his reign. [https://cata
2 CONC logue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f1%2f1%2f63%2f1&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=lord+montgomery&ko=p&r=&ro=m&df=1469&dt=1480&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Montgomerie Family, Ea
2 CONC rls of Eglinton, reference GD3/1/1/63/1]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note: James II, King of Scots, was killed on 3 August 1460. James III, King of Scots, died on 11 June 1488.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[https://archive.org/details/rotuliscaccariir06grea/page/n8/mode/1up Rotuli Scaccarii Regum Scotorum. The Exchequer Rolls of Scotland. Edited by George Burnett, Lyon King of Arms. Vol. VI. A. D. 1455-1460. (H. M. General Regist
2 CONC er House, Edinburgh, 1883), 783 pp. including index]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/page/n6/mode/1up The Scots Peerage : founded on Wood's ed. of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland; containing an historical and genealogi
2 CONC cal account of the nobility of that kingdom. Edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, Lord Lyon King of Arms. Volume III. (David Douglas, Edinburgh, 1906), 618 pp.]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Montgomerie,_1st_Lord_Montgomerie Biographical Summary by Wkipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie (died 1470?) was a Scottish nobleman and diplomat.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was the eldest son''' of Sir John Montgomerie of Ardrossan, by his wife Agnes, daughter of John of Islay, Lord of the Isles.[1] '''In 1425 he was chosen a member of the privy council of James I. He succeeded his father some 
2 CONC time before 22 November 1429, and in August 1430 he was, jointly with his brother-in-law, Sir Robert Cunningham of Kilmaurs, appointed governor of Cantyre and Knapdale.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''On 30 November 1436 Montgomerie was appointed a commissioner to conclude a treaty with England, and he was one of the conservators of the truce concluded on 31 March for nine years. With the other Scottish commissioners he rece
2 CONC ived the present of a silver cup from Henry VI of England. On 5 February 1444 he had a safe-conduct to go to Durham to treat for the extension of the truce and the return of the Scottish hostages.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1444 Montgomerie was appointed keeper of Brodick Castle on the Isle of Arran. He was one of those who set their seals to instruments passed by the parliament held at Perth, Scotland on 9 June 1445 against the lords who had r
2 CONC ebelled against James II. He was created a lord of parliament by the title of Lord Montgomerie some time before 3 July 1445; and on 14 August 1451 he was a conservator for a truce with England, and in subsequent years he was sent 
2 CONC to England on further embassies. He died about 1470.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''With his wife Margaret, Montgomerie had three or four sons and three daughters:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Alexander, master of Montgomerie and father of Hugh Montgomerie, 1st Earl of Eglinton'''
2 CONT # '''George, ancestor of the Montgomeries of Skelmorlie'''
2 CONT # '''Thomas, parson of Eaglesham, and rector of the university of Glasgow'''
2 CONT # '''John of Giffen (doubtful)'''
2 CONT # '''Margaret, married to John Stewart, 1st Earl of Lennox'''
2 CONT # '''Elizabeth, to Gilbert Kennedy, 1st Lord Kennedy'''
2 CONT # '''Agnes, to William Cunningham of Glengarnock'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Montgomerie,_1st_Lord_Montgomerie Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________
2 CONT *Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 38
2 CONT *'''Montgomerie, Alexander de'''		by Thomas Finlayson Henderson
2 CONT *'''MONTGOMERIE, Sir ALEXANDER de, of Ardrossan, first Lord Montgomerie (d. 1470?), was the eldest son''' of Sir John Montgomerie of Eaglesham, Eglinton, and Ardrossan, by his wife Agnes, daughter of Alexander, earl of Ross, lord 
2 CONC of the Isles. '''His grandfather''', Sir John Montgomerie (d. 1398?), is separately noticed. The father was a hostage for the Earl of Douglas in 1408, a hostage for James I in 1423, and one of the jury on the trial of Murdac, duke
2 CONC  of Albany, in 1425. '''In 1425 the son was chosen a member of the privy council of James I. He succeeded his father some time before 22 Nov. 1429, and in August 1430 he was, jointly with his brother-in-law, Sir Robert Cunningham 
2 CONC of Kilmaurs, appointed governor of Cantyre and Knapdale. On 30 Nov. 1436 he was appointed a commissioner to conclude a treaty with England (Cal. Documents relating to Scotland, iv. 1103), and he was one of the conservators of the 
2 CONC truce concluded on 31 March for nine years (ib. p. 1111). With the other Scottish commissioners he received the present of a silver cup from Henry VI (ib. p. 1109). On 5 Feb. 1444 he had a safe-conduct to go to Durham to treat for
2 CONC  the extension of the truce and the return of the Scottish hostages (ib. p. 1162).
2 CONT *In 1444 Montgomerie was appointed keeper of Brodick Castle in the Isle of Arran (Exchequer Rolls of Scotland, v. 163). He was one of those who set their seals to instruments passed by the parliament held at Perth on 9 June 1445 a
2 CONC gainst those lords who had rebelled against James II. He was created a lord of parliament by the title of Lord Montgomerie some time before 3 July 1445 (Acta Parl. Scot. ii. 59; Hist. MSS. Comm. 11th Rep. pt. vi. p. 16). On 31 Jan
2 CONC . 1448-9 he had a grant of the office of bailiary of Cunningham. On 14 Aug. 1451 he was a conservator for a truce with England (Cal. Documents relating to Scotland, iv. 1239), and in subsequent years he was sent to England on vari
2 CONC ous other important embassies. He died about 1470. By his wife Margaret, second daughter of Sir Thomas Boyd''' of Kilmarnock, father of the first Lord Boyd, '''he had three, or possibly four sons and three daughters:''' Alexander,
2 CONC  master of Montgomerie, who died in 1452, leaving by his wife Elizabeth, eldest daughter of Sir Adam Hepburn of Hales a son, Alexander, second lord Montgomerie, father of Hugh, first earl of Eglinton [q. v.]; George, ancestor of t
2 CONC he Montgomeries of Skelmorlie; Thomas, parson of Eaglesham, and rector of the university of Glasgow; John of Giffen (doubtful); Margaret, married to Sir John Stewart of Darnley, who was created Lord Darnley, and for a time was tit
2 CONC ular Earl of Lennox; Elizabeth, to John, lord Kennedy, seventh earl of Cassillis; and Agnes, to William Cunningham of Glengarnock.
2 CONT *[Cal. Documents relating to Scotland, vol. iv.; Exchequer Rolls of Scotland, vol. ii.; Reg. Mag. Sig. Scot. vol. i.; Sir William Fraser's Earls of Eglinton; Douglas's Scottish Peerage (Wood), i. 495-6.]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Montgomerie,_Alexander_de_(DNB00) _____________________________
2 CONT *'''Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie, Lord Androssan1,2,3
2 CONT *'''M, #4211, b. circa 1390, d. 1470
2 CONT *Father	Sir John Montgomerie d. b 22 Nov 1429
2 CONT *Mother	Agnes of the Isles d. b Mar 1414
2 CONT *'''     Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie, Lord Androssan married Margaret Boyd, daughter of Sir Thomas Boyd, 5th Baron Kilmarnock. Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie, Lord Androssan was born circa 1390. He die
2 CONC d in 1470; Living on 7 October 1469.
2 CONT *'''Family	Margaret Boyd d. a 16 Sep 1453
2 CONT *Children	
2 CONT **Elizabeth Montgomery+
2 CONT **Alexander Montgomerie, Master of Montgomerie+ d. 1452
2 CONT **George Montgomerie
2 CONT **Thomas Montgomerie, Parson of Eaglesham
2 CONT **Agnes Montgomerie
2 CONT **Margaret Montgomerie+2,3 d. a 20 Jul 1461
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S694] Unknown author, Lineage and Ancestry of HRH Prince Charles by Paget, Vol. II, p. 320; Europaische Stammtafeln by Isenburg, chart 640, Vol. 3.
2 CONT *[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. III, p. 586.
2 CONT *[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. I, p. 664.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p140.htm#i4211 ____________________
2 CONT *'''Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie1
2 CONT *'''M, #5221, b. before 1413, d. 1469/70
2 CONT *Last Edited=24 Mar 2011
2 CONT *'''     Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie was born before 1413.2 He was the son of Sir John Montgomerie of Ardrossan and Agnes of the Isles.2 He married Margaret Boyd, daughter of Sir Thomas Boyd, 5th of Kilmarnock.3 He
2 CONC  died in 1469/70.3
2 CONT *'''    He was a Commissioner to negotiate with England in 1437.1 He was created 1st Lord Montgomerie [Scotland] circa 3 July 1445.1 He was a Commissioner to negotiate with England in 1449.1 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (
2 CONC P.C.) [Scotland].1 He was a Commissioner to negotiate with England from 1451 to 1452.1 He was a Commissioner to negotiate with England in 1456.1
2 CONT *'''Children of Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie and Margaret Boyd
2 CONT **1. Margaret Montgomerie+4
2 CONT **2. Alexander Montgomerie, Master of Montgomerie+1 d. 1452
2 CONT **3. Elizabeth Montgomerie+5
2 CONT **4. George Montgomerie1
2 CONT **5. Reverend Thomas Montgomerie1
2 CONT **6. Agnes Montgomerie1
2 CONT **7. Margaret Montgomerie+1
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 1, page 1279. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct
2 CONC  or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume IX, page 134. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume IX, page 135.
2 CONT *[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 198.
2 CONT *[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume III, page 74.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p523.htm#i5221 ___________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical history of the family of Montgomery: including the Montgomery ...   By Thomas Harrison Montgomery
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=vWs6AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA52&lpg=PA52&dq=john+de+montgomery+1429+Maxwell&source=bl&ots=nIdETM-zd_&sig=7l0LL9C_Ow_XarL-6BWACB2Wrig&hl=en&ei=qX1EToriBqPkiAK96u3YAQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=4&ved
2 CONC =0CCkQ6AEwAzgK#v=onepage&q=john%20de%20montgomery%201429%20Maxwell&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 47
2 CONT *   XV. SIR JOHN de MONTGOMERIE of Eaglesham and Eastwood, and afterwards of Eglinton and Ardrossan, was the son of Sir Alexander. "He married in 1361, Elizabeth, the daughter and heiress of Sir Hugh Eglinton of Eglinton, and by h
2 CONC er obtained the large possessions of that family on the death of her father, which occurred about  the year 1374, together with Ardrossan, the heiress of which estate one of her ancestors had married. Sir John quartered with his o
2 CONC wn the Eglinton arms, which were gules, three rings or, gemmed azure."2
2 CONT * .... etc. 
2 CONT *Pg. 48
2 CONT *   Sir John de Montgomerie of Eaglesham, greatly distinguished himself at the battle of Otterburne, where James Earl Douglas, who was his uncle, was slain.2  .... etc.  Hugh, the eldest son of Sir John, lost his life there, as th
2 CONC e ballad has it: 
2 CONT **	.... etc. 
2 CONT *   Sir John granted on October, 1396, to William de Blakeford, the lands of Ardbennane, within two years from which time he died; he had by his wife, Elizabeth, who survived him, four sons,
2 CONT **	   I. SIR HUGH, who died at the battle of Otterburne, in August 1388, from the good aim of an English archer, in revenging the death of Earl Douglas. . . . 
2 CONT **	Pg. 49
2 CONT **	   II. SIR JOHN, who succeeded his father.
2 CONT **	   III. ALEXANDER, to whom his mother gave a charter of the lands of Bonnington, in the barony of Ratho. .... etc.
2 CONT **	Pg.50 
2 CONT **	   IV. HUGH, who was -- it is presumed-- born after the death of the eldest son. He was tutor to Hugh, the third Lord Montgomerie, and lived to a very advanced age, having survived four generations of the chief of his family.
2 CONT *   XVI. SIR JOHN de MONTGOMERIE, "Dominus Ejusdem, or of the Ilk," which title shows he was the male heir and chief of the French house of Montgomerie.
2 CONT *   "This ancient and noble title-- That Ilk -- is peculiar to the Scots, and has never been hitherto defined well. It is the title which denotes -- not the gentleman, as Johnson's degrading appellation, (viz., whose surname and t
2 CONC he title of his estate are the same,) but also the chief of all the clan of his own surname. It does not necessarily or essentially refer to the estate. McFarlan, McIntosh, Brisbane, Tweedie, &c., of that Ilk, have never had lands
2 CONC  of the name of their surnames. .... etc. 
2 CONT *Pg. 51
2 CONT *   Sir John de Montgomerie " succeeded his father before 1398. In 1402 he formed on the chiefs of the Scotch army which invaded England, and was taken prisoner at the disastrous battle of Halidon Hill." He was not long a captive,
2 CONC  however, for two years after he was in .... etc.
2 CONT *   Sir John married Margaret, daughter of Sir Robert Maxwell of Caerlaverock, (ancestor of the Earls of Nithsdale,) and dying previous to November, 1429, left by her three sons and three daughters.
2 CONT **'''	I. ALEXANDER, who succeeded him.
2 CONT **	II. SIR ROBERT MONTGOMERIE, knight, of Giffen.  This barony was given by Robert II. in 1370, the first year of his reign, to Sir Hugh Eglinton, his sister's husband, and upon the marriage of the latter's heiress, came into the 
2 CONC Montgomerie family; it seems to have been regarded as the messuage of the Master or heir apparent of Eglinton. At different times, subsequently, it was given off, either whole or in portions, to the younger members of the family.2
2 CONC  "It included originally, besides Giffen of the present day, the lands of Hesselhead, Broadstone, [or Braidstane, which was given by the first Lord Montgomerie to his grandson, Robert Montgomerie, ancestor of Sir Hugh Montgomerie 
2 CONC of Braidstane, afterwards Viscount Montgomerie,] Ramshead, Trearne, and Roughwood, extending in all to L3788 9s. 10d. of valuation, 
2 CONT **	Pg. 52
2 CONT **	which is considerably more then half the valued rent of Beith parish."1  About the year 1452, both Braidstane and the lands of Hesselhead were given off to the younger brothers of the second Lord Montgomerie, and formed two dis
2 CONC tinct families. And about the same period, another part of the lordship of Giffen was granted to the family of Mure of Caldwell, and forms that part of their estate situated in the parish of Beith.2
2 CONT **	Sir Robert was the ancestor of the late Sir George Montgomery, Baronet, of Macbie Hill, and of Sir Graham G. Montgomery, Baronet, of Stanhope, for whose descents see pp. 145, 148.
2 CONT **	III. HUGH MONTGOMERIE, is mentioned in a charter of his brother, Lord Montgomerie, Polnoon, July 29, 1452.
2 CONT **	IV. ANNE, married June 16, 1425, to Sir Robert Cuninghame of Kilmaurs, father of the first Earl of Glencairn. . . . .
2 CONT **	V. JANET, married Sir Thomas Boyd of Kilmarnock, (his second wife,) .... etc. 
2 CONT **	VI. ISABEL, married Archibald Mure of Rowallan.
2 CONT *'''   XVII. ALEXANDER de MONTGOMERIE, the first Lord Montgomerie, succeeded his father before November 22, 1429, as on that date "Alexander de Montgomery, lord of that Ilk, is one of the assize in an action  .... etc. 
2 CONT *Pg. 53
2 CONT *'''   Lord Montgomerie married Margaret, daughter of Sir Thomas Boyd of Kilmarnock, by whom he had four sons and four daughters. He died prior to October 14, 1465, and was succeeded by his grandson.
2 CONT **	I. ALEXANDER DE MONTGOMERIE, Master of Montgomerie, whose son became the second Lord Montgomerie. (See P. 55.)
2 CONT **	II. GEORGE DE MONTGOMERIE of Skelmorlie, 
2 CONT **	III. JOHN DE MONTGOMERIE of Giffen, in which barony he appears to have had lands . . . 
2 CONT **	IV. THOMAS DE MONTGOMERIE, Parson of Eaglesham.  . . . .   Thomas died unmarried.
2 CONT **	V. MARGARET, married John Earl of Lennox, Lord Darnley. There is an indenture, dated at Houstoun, May 15, 1438, between Alexander Montgomerie, Knight, Lord of Ardrossan, on the one part, and Alan Stewart, Lord Dernely, .... etc
2 CONC . 
2 CONT **	Pg.54
2 CONT **	VI. ELIZABETH, married John, second Lord Kennedy, and was mother of David, first Earl of Cassilis. Lord Kennedy's sister, Catherine, was the wife of the second Lord Montgomerie.
2 CONT **	VII. JANET, married a Cuninghame of the Kilmaurs family.
2 CONT **	VIII. AGNES, married William Cuninghame of Glengarnock. __________________________
2 CONT *'''Name	Sir Alexander Montgomery, 1st Lord Montgomerie  [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7] 
2 CONT *'''Born	Abt 1385 	of, Ardrossen, Ayrshire, Scotland  [8] 
2 CONT *'''Died	1470  [8, 10, 11] 
2 CONT *Father	Sir John Montgomery, Lord of Ardrossan,   b. of, Eaglesham, Renfrewshire, Scotland  d. 14 Feb 1426-22 Nov 1429 
2 CONT *Mother	Agnes of the Isles,   d. Bef 9 Mar 1413-1414 
2 CONT *'''Family	     Margaret Boyd,   b. Abt 1390,   d. Aft 16 Sep 1453  (Age ~ 63 years) 
2 CONT *Children 	
2 CONT ** 	1. Alexander Montgomery, Master of Montgomery,   d. 1452
2 CONT ** 	2. Margaret Montgomery,   b. Abt 1412, of, Ardrossen, Ayrshire, Scotland   d. Aft 30 May 1493  (Age ~ 81 years)
2 CONT ** 	3. George Montgomery, of Skelmorlie
2 CONT ** 	4. Thomas Montgomery, parson of Eaglesham
2 CONT ** 	5. Elizabeth Montgomery,   b. of, Kilmarnock, Ayrshire, Scotland   d. Bef 24 Aug 1467
2 CONT *Sources 	
