1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===ALEXANDER HOME Lord Home===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''28 February 1491: Procuratory of resignation by Patrick Hume of Fastcastell [Fastcastle] to Thomas Hume of Langschawis [Langshaws], his brother, Alexander Ellaham, Alexander Hume, Gilbert Hume and Alexander Jaksone [Jackson], h
2 CONC is curators, to resign into the hands of Alexander, Lord Hume, his father, the lands of Balwolssy and Gordounehall [Gordonshall], in sheriffdom of Fife. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference
2 CONC =GD63%2f7&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=thomas+hume&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=y National Records of Scotland, Papers of Phineas Bell Brander, solicitor, Edinburgh, reference GD63/7]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/hh4bz/home01.php Stirnet: Home 01]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=REG&db=namegame&id=I6302
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.thepeerage.com/p2811.htm#i28110 thePeerage.com]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Home, 1st Lord Home was the son of Sir Alexander Home of Home and Dunglas and Janet Hay. He married, firstly, Marion Lauder, daughter of John Lauder and Katherine de Landells, before 1424. He married, secondly, Margaret 
2 CONC Montgomerie, daughter of Alexander Montgomerie, 1st Lord Montgomerie and Margaret Boyd, before 14 July 1467. He died between February 1490 and April 1491.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1450 he founded the Collegiate Church of Dunbar. He held the office of Scottish Ambassador to England in 1459.1 He was created 1st Lord Home [Scotland] on 2 August 1473.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Alexander Home, 1st Lord Home and Margaret Montgomerie
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Sir Thomas Home
2 CONT #     Elizabeth Home
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Alexander Home, 1st Lord Home and Marion Lauder
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Alexander Home, Master of Home+ d. c 1456
2 CONT #     John Home
2 CONT #     George Home
2 CONT #     Patrick Home
2 CONT #     Helen Home+1,3 d. a 1512
2 CONT #     *Jonet Home+ d. a 1471
2 CONT #     Katherine Home
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Home,_1st_Lord_Home
2 CONT
2 CONT Before July 1467 Sir Alexander Home remarried (2) Margaret, daughter of Alexander, Master of Montgomerie by his spouse Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Adam Hepburn of Hailes. They had a further three boys:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sir Thomas, of Langshaw, Ayrshire.
2 CONT * Nicholas (indicating that Nicholas by the first marriage had probably died young)
2 CONT * David.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I3093&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT The title Earl of Home (pronounced /ˈhjuːm/ "Hume") was created in 1605 in the Peerage of Scotland for Alexander Home of that Ilk, who was already the 6th Lord Home.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl of Home holds the subsidiary titles of Lord Home (created 1473), and Lord Dunglass (1605), in the Peerage of Scotland; and Baron Douglas, of Douglas in the County of Lanark (1875) in the Peerage of the United Kingdom. Var
2 CONC ious Earls of Home have also claimed the title of Lord Hume of Berwick. The Earl is also Chief of the Name and Arms of Home and heir general to the House of Douglas. The title Lord Dunglass is the courtesy title of the eldest son 
2 CONC of the Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most famous recent holder of the title was the 14th Earl, Alexander Frederick Douglas-Home, better known as Sir Alec Douglas-Home. After the unexpected resignation of Harold Macmillan, the 14th Earl was named Prime Minister by
2 CONC  the monarch. For the first time in over sixty years, a sitting Prime Minister was a member of the House of Lords rather than of the House of Commons. Because he believed that it was impractical and unconventional to remain a memb
2 CONC er of the Lords, the Earl disclaimed his peerages in 1963 under the Peerage Act passed in the same year. He then contested the House of Commons seat of Kinross and Western Perthshire by standing in the Kinross and West Perthshire 
2 CONC by-election, 1963. The seat had been vacated by the death of the previous Member of Parliament, Gilmour Leburn. The Earldom and subsidiary titles belonged to no-one until the death of Sir Alec Douglas-Home, when they passed to his
2 CONC  son, the present holder, David Douglas-Home, 15th Earl of Home.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family seats are The Hirsel, Berwickshire and Castlemains, Douglas, South Lanarkshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lords Home (1473)
