1 NAME Thomas /Bruce/ of Lethbertscheilles
2 GIVN Thomas
2 NSFX of Lethbertscheilles
2 _MARNM Bruce
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =='''Thomas Bruce, 1st of Lethbertscheilles and Woodside (c. 1475 – c. 1530)'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Overview and Feudal Origins'''
2 CONT '''Thomas Bruce''' was a prominent Scottish nobleman of the late 15th and early 16th centuries, serving as the patriarch, university-educated lawyer '''("Mr." / Magister)''', and founder of the Lethbertscheilles and Woodside cadet
2 CONC  branch of the House of Bruce of Airth and Stenhouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born as the second son of '''Sir John Bruce of Stanehouse''' (who was tragically killed in 1483 by his brother-in-law, William Menteith of the Kerss) and '''Elizabeth Menteith'''. His maternal grandfather was Sir William Me
2 CONC nteith of West Kerse. Following his father’s murder and the subsequent settlement of family estates, the lands of Lethbertscheilles were formally allocated to Thomas as his patrimony. He married '''Elizabeth Auchmuthie (Auchmoutie
2 CONC )''', with whom he founded the line that directly led to the establishment of the noted French branch of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The History of the Lethbertscheilles and Woodside Estates'''
2 CONT The history of Lethbertscheilles goes back much further than that of Woodside. In the 14th century, during the reign of King David II, the estate was owned by Adam Salter and later by William Lundie. Upon Lundie's forfeiture, it c
2 CONC ame into the possession of Adam de Argent prior to 1369. In 1377, it was resigned in favour of Robert Stewart, Earl of Fife (the second son of King Robert II and later Duke of Albany).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bruce family secured the estate in '''1451 via a royal charter granted to Alexander Bruce of Stanehouse''' (Thomas’s grandfather). The lands subsequently passed to Alexander's son, Sir John Bruce of Stenhouse (Thomas’s father)
2 CONC , and finally to Thomas himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas is historically recognized as the first to be styled distinctively as "of Lethbertschiells and Woodside." This dual designation marks a critical evolution: it indicates that during Thomas's tenure on the newly expanded esta
2 CONC te (which latterly included Stanrigmill), the family constructed their first fortified seat — '''Woodside Castle''' — on the eastern edge of the Tor Wood. While the castle was later demolished in the mid-19th century to be replace
2 CONC d by the mansion of Glenbervie House, its original 16th-century appearance likely mirrored the contemporary architectural expansions (the east and north wings) of nearby Airth Castle. It was prominent enough to be explicitly marke
2 CONC d on Timothy Pont’s late 16th-century map as a fortified tower named "Woodsyide".
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hereditary Legacy'''
2 CONT While standard printed visitations often truncated younger cadet lines to focus primarily on the senior Lairds of Airth, contemporary court registers and family papers confirm that Thomas Bruce left a resilient and expanding offsp
2 CONC ring:
2 CONT * '''Thomas Bruce, 2nd of Lethbertscheilles and Woodside''' — his eldest son and lineal heir, who succeeded to the fortified tower of Woodside and continued the senior territorial line in Stirlingshire. It was during his and his s
2 CONC on Robert's tenure that the family became embroiled in a fierce and bloody generational border feud with their neighbours, the Forresters of Torwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the foundational historical records of the family, Thomas the Elder also left a numerous younger issue whose specific names were routinely omitted from heavily summarized heraldic visitations. A notable international 
2 CONC lineage emerged from this branch through Thomas's great-grandson, Adam Bruce of Waltown. In 1633, Adam migrated to France, established himself there, and married Ève Marie de Hermant, officially founding the aristocratic house of 
2 CONC the Counts Bruce in France. This lineage became prominent in French history, producing figures such as Adam's great-grandson, Louis Daniel, Count de Bruce, Seigneur de Montlerard, and Charles Hector, Count de Bruce, who headed the
2 CONC  French house in the 19th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources''':
2 CONT # Armstrong, William Bruce. The Bruces of Airth and Their Cadets. Edinburgh: Private Print, 1892, [https://archive.org/details/brucesofairththe00arms/page/n44/mode/1up '''pp. 13-14'''] (Annotated evidence of extended Bruce offspri
2 CONC ng, the Lethbertscheilles property, and the educational title of Thomas).
2 CONT # Weeks, Lyman Horace. Book of Bruce: Ancestors and Descendants of King Robert of Scotland. New York: The Americana Society, 1907, p. 134 (Documentary proof of the marriage to Elizabeth Auchmuthie and the descent of the Counts Bru
2 CONC ce in France via Adam Bruce of Waltown).
2 CONT # Balfour Paul, Sir James (ed.). The Scots Peerage. Vol. III, p. 469 (Entry: Bruce, Earls of Elgin and Ailesbury - The Stenhouse Branch).
2 CONT # Gibson, John. Lands and Lairds of Larbert and Dunipace Parishes. Glasgow: Hugh Hopkins, 1908 (Historical context of the Lethbertscheilles/Woodside tenure and the splitting of the Barony of Stenhouse).
2 CONT # Pont, Timothy. Map of Stirlingshire, Lower Strathearn and western Clackmannanshire (Late 16th Century), National Library of Scotland (Cartographic evidence of Woodside Tower)..
