1 NAME William /Cunningham/ 14th of Glengarnock
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Cunningham
2 NSFX 14th of Glengarnock
2 _MARNM Cunningham
2 NICK William Cunningham of Glenarnock
2 NICK William Cuninghame
2 NICK William Cunynghame
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Musselburgh, East Lothian, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1490
2 PLAC Glengarnock, Ayrshire, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 SEP 1547
2 PLAC Battle of Pinkie Cleugh
2 ADDR
3 CITY Musselburgh
3 STAE Scotland
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of William Conyngham, 13th of Glengarnock & Isabella Cunningham
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 - XIV. WILLIAM CUNINGHAME of Glengarnock, who was slain at Pinkie. His mother, at all events, "Lady Isabella Cunyngham," was alive at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before taking his departure for the army, he made his will.  It was probably this William Cuninghame of Glengarnock who was engaged in so many of the local feuds of his time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Elizabeth, daughter of Lord Saint-Clair. Issue:-
2 CONT * 1. John, who succeeded.
2 CONT * 2. William, who died intestate before his father. The inventory of his effects was confirmed before the Commissary Court of Glasgow, on the 5th of January 1547. He is styled in that document "Will. Conynghame, filii quond. nobil
2 CONC is viri Willmi. Conynghame de Glengarnok." His executors dative were Isabella, Johanna, and Agnes Cuninghame, "sorrores legittime."
2 CONT * 3. Cuthbert
2 CONT * 4. Alexander
2 CONT * 1. Elizabeth, married to Alexander Schaw of Sauchie, who died before 1547.
2 CONT * 2. Isobel, married to William Porterfield, younger of that Ilk, in 1506. [SB-1560]
2 CONT * 3. Margaret, married to John Blair of that Ilk.
2 CONT * 4. Johanna.
2 CONT * 5. Agnes.
2 CONT
2 CONT (This family were in possession of Glengarnock until 1609.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ("History of the County of Ayr with a Genealogical Account of the families of Ayrshire' by James Paterson")
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT Written August, 1547
2 CONT
2 CONT Before taking his departure for the army, he made his will, of which the following is a literal translation:
2 CONT * " Death is certain the hour most uncertain.
2 CONT * Hence it is that I, William Cuninghame of Glengarnock, taking my way to rencounter our old enemies, and, in the event of sudden death, make and ordain my testament and last will. 
2 CONT *In the first place, I give and leave my soul to Almighty God, the most blessed Virgin Mary, and all saints, and my bones to be buried where it shall please the Most High. 
2 CONT *And I leave iiij pence to the fabrick of St Kentingern, and xx pound to be given to a chaplain duly ordained to pray for my soul in the parochial church of Kilbirny.
2 CONT * Also, I leave to Sir Robert Cunyngham, chaplain, x merkis of money; likewise I leave to the Friars Minor of Air and Glasgow, xx merkis.
2 CONT * Also, I appoint and ordain that my tenants have the liberty of compounding their debts. 
2 CONT *Further, I appoint my executors, viz., '''Elizabeth Synclair, my wife, and Lady Isabella Cunyngham, my mother''', only; 
2 CONT *and I leave my four best horses to my four sons, in such a way that the eldest shall have the first choice of the same, viz., ay the eldest to cheia first, and that the heirship horse shall stand to my heir for his choice. 
2 CONT *I leave to Alexander Cunyngham, my younger son, the younger horse called the ' seur staig,' and to John Blair, my son-in-law, the younger horse, called the ' brown staig.'
2 CONT *I grant and assign to Elizabeth Cunynghame, my daughter, relict of the late Alexander Schaw of Sauchguy, that sum of 400 merkis, resting upon the lands and buildings of Sir John Makghe, vicar of Arbruthven, lying within the city 
2 CONC of Glasgow ; as also, that sum of 200 merkis, owing by the said Sir John, I assign to the foresaid Elizabeth, towards relieving my executrices of the forementioned sum of 700 merkis due to the said Elizabeth. 
2 CONT *Moreover, all my goods, moveable and immoveable, my debts being taken out and paid, I give and leave to my three daughters, to be distributed and apportioned at the will of my executrices aforesaid, as they shall answer to the Su
2 CONC preme Judge."
2 CONT * This testament was made by the mouth of the departing, "the xxix day of the month of August, in the year of our Lord 1547, my manuel subscription bearing testimony, at Glengarnock." Follows the form of subscription,
2 CONT * " William Cunyngham of Glengarnock, with my hand."
2 CONT * This testament was confirmed, &c., 4th November, 1547
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT More data on his life and children are found [http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I7474&tree=CC MacFarlane Clan and Family webpage].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Pinkie Cleugh, where Sir William died is described in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Pinkie_Cleugh Wikipedia]. The names of a number of other Scottish casualties are known from legal records or Scottish chro
2 CONC nicles,[51] and include; … William Cunninghame of Glengarnock Glengarnock Castle
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000185616656855&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Glengarnock Castle, which was owned by the Cunninghams, is described in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glengarnock_Castle Wikipedia].
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000185616609852&size=large}} 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I7474&tree=CC
