1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == SIR BRYCE BLAIR OF THAT ILK (formerly BRYCE BLAIR OF LOCHWOOD) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Bryce Blair of that Ilk (formerly Bryce Blair of Lochwood) is the son of John Blair of that Ilk and Grizel Sempill. [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/bb4fz/blair02.php StirnetL Blair 02]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Bryce Blair of that Ilk is reported to have died on 4 February 1639. [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/bb4fz/blair02.php StirnetL Blair 02]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Bryce Blair of that Ilk is reported to have married Annabell Wallace. [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/bb4fz/blair02.php StirnetL Blair 02]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''23-26 October 1605: Contract between John Blair of Blair, Bryce Blair of Lochwode now his eldest son and Annabel Wallace, his spouse, Robert Blair of Auldmwre, son of the said John, and Jean Cunynghame, his spouse, and Master H
2 CONC ugh Blair, son of the said John, by which the said Bryce resigns the lands of Lochwode in favour of the said Robert and the said John, Robert, and Hugh dispone to Bryce the lands of Balruger and Over and Nether Meirburne in the pa
2 CONC rish of Lochquhinzeoch, regality of Pasley and sheriffdom of Renfrew, and others. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD21%2f4&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=&ko=a&r=GD21%2f4&ro=s&df=1605&dt=1
2 CONC 605&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Cuninghame Family of Thorntoun, reference GD21/4]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                    2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''23 April 1621: Contract between Bryce Blair of that ilk on the one part and Malcolm Crawfurde in Pathfute of Corsehill on the other part, whereby Bryce Blair in consideration of 500 merks Scots dispones to Malcolm, his heirs an
2 CONC d assignees the 10s land of new extent of Corsehill called the Pathfute (Pathfoot), lying within the parish and regality of Kilwinning, bailliary of Cunningham and sheriffdom of Ayr: to hold of him and his heirs male succeeding to
2 CONC  him in the lands, living and lordship of Blair for payment yearly of 5 merks Scots and to his immediate superiors thereof of the feu duties contained in the original infeftments. Dated at Irvine. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.
2 CONC uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f1%2f3%2f39%2f11&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=&ko=a&r=GD3%2f1%2f3%2f39%2f11&ro=s&df=1621&dt=1621&di=n National Records of Scotland. Papers of the Montgomerie Family, Earls of Eglinto
2 CONC n, reference GD3/1/3/39/11]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/bb4fz/blair02.php Stirnet: Blair 02]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NOTES===
2 CONT
2 CONT The term "Blair" is the genitive of a gaelic word, "Blar" meaning field or battle field. It is used widely for both places and surnames, one of the earliest known for the former being the Barony of Blair in Ayrshire granted by Kin
2 CONC g William in the mid-12th century. An example of the latter is Sir Robert de Blair, Norman Knight. Sir Bryce Blair was an adherent of Sir William Wallace but was executed by the English at the Barns of Ayr in 1296. His brother's s
2 CONC on, Roger, was knighted by King Robert the Bruce for his services before & at Bannockburn. Blair House at Dalry, Ayrshire was occupied continuously by the family between 1202 and 1980.  http://www.clanblair.org/cbshstry.html
2 CONT
2 CONT With the 1604 death of his elder brother, John, Bryce became the heir and acquired the right to be called "of that Ilk". Their father died in 1608; and the next surviving document extract at the NRS site, dated 1621, in fact calls
2 CONC  Bryce "Bryce Blair of that Ilk" ... http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD3%2f1%2f3%2f39%2f11&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=&ko=a&r=GD3%2f1%2f3%2f39%2f11&ro=s&df=1621&dt=1621&di=n
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Scottish law, of that Ilk was used only by the chief of the family and his heir (the eldest son). Again, in the event the heir died, the right and privilege of being designated "of that Ilk" fell to the eldest survivi
2 CONC ng son.
