0 @N6000000001968475992@ NOTE Sir James inherited the Barony of Drumlanrig from his grandfather in 1578. In 1581 he married Mary, daughter of John, 5th Lord Fleming. Sir James died in 1615. 
0 @N6000000001968479002@ NOTE Although the father of 9 children through two marriages, Sir James sired only one son, Sir William of Hawick. Sir William predeceased his father in 1572. Sir James died in 1578 and the family honors passed to his grandson. 
0 @N6000000005940228253@ NOTE of Drumlanrig
0 @N6000000003216110139@ NOTE 3rd Lord Montgomery and 1st Earl of Eglinton.
0 @N6000000003043431123@ NOTE Notes from Sally Walmsley <Geniedash@bigpond.com>:
1 CONT Hugh, the third Lord Montgomerie was a minor when he succeeded to the family property, and on reaching his majority he executed a revocation dated 11th October 1484 of all grants made by his tutors during his minority.  He found g
1 CONC reat favour with King James IV, who  nominated him for the Privy Council in 1489, and in 1507 or '08 created him the first Earl of Eglington.  After King James IV was killed at Flodden Field, the Earl was one of the peers who deci
1 CONC ded the coronation of King James V, and was one of the Queen's Council during the minority ofJames V.  He was appointed Justice-general for northern Scotland in 1526.  Howeer, his office of Bailie of cunninghame exacerbated the fe
1 CONC ud with Cuthbert, Earl of Glencairn, who in that year ravaged his lands and burn down his castle at Eglington: this misfortune encouraged King James V to grant him a new charter for his lands in Ayrshire, Renfrew, etc.  In 1536, h
1 CONC e and the Earl of Huntley were appointed Governors of Scotland whilst the King went to France to bring back his bride, Princess Magdalene.  The Earl of Eglington was married to Lady Helen Campbell, daughter of Colin the first Earl
1 CONC  of Argyll.  he died in 1545, at the age of 84, having lived in the reigns of five sovereigns of Scotland.   they had 14 children.
1 CONT Hugh, the third Lord Montgomerie was a minor when he succeeded to the family property, and on reaching his majority he executed a revocation dated 11th October 1484 of all grants made by his tutors during his minority.  He found g
1 CONC reat favour with King James IV, who  nominated him for the Privy Council in 1489, and in 1507 or '08 created him the first Earl of Eglington.  After King James IV was killed at Flodden Field, the Earl was one of the peers who deci
1 CONC ded the coronation of King James V, and was one of the Queen's Council during the minority ofJames V.  He was appointed Justice-general for northern Scotland in 1526.  Howeer, his office of Bailie of cunninghame exacerbated the fe
1 CONC ud with Cuthbert, Earl of Glencairn, who in that year ravaged his lands and burn down his castle at Eglington: this misfortune encouraged King James V to grant him a new charter for his lands in Ayrshire, Renfrew, etc.  In 1536, h
1 CONC e and the Earl of Huntley were appointed Governors of Scotland whilst the King went to France to bring back his bride, Princess Magdalene.  The Earl of Eglington was married to Lady Helen Campbell, daughter of Colin the first Earl
1 CONC  of Argyll.  he died in 1545, at the age of 84, having lived in the reigns of five sovereigns of Scotland.   they had 14 children.
