0 @N6000000001877478333@ NOTE Master of Angus
0 @N6000000001783349019@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT George Douglas, Master of Angus (1469-9 September 1513) was a Scottish Nobleman. The son of Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus and Elizabeth Boyd, daughter of Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd, he was born at Tantallon Castle and died 
1 CONC at the Battle of Flodden.
1 CONT In 1489 the 5th Earl of Angus, George's father resigned his lordships of Tantallon, Douglasdale, Liddesdale, Ewesdale, Eskdale, Selkirk, and Jedburgh Forest to the crown. James III then granted a new charter in favour of the Maste
1 CONC r.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the stand off between James III and the party backing his son James, then Duke of Rothesay, the Master attended the last parliament of the king before his death the Battle of Sauchieburn. It is not recorded to which faction
1 CONC  the younger Angus adhered to. He attended parliament again under the newly crowned James IV in 1490.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Master did not take an active part in Public affairs until 1499 when he took formal control over his lordships of Eskdale and Ewesdale. These lordships had become renowned for lawlessness and banditry, and the Master was appoi
1 CONC nted by the King as warden of Eskdale. In his capacity of Warden he met with his English counterpart Lord Dacre at Canonbie to here grievances and fix punishments.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Younger Angus was not a particularly effective at restoring law and order. His undue leniency to his own tenantry caused the King to deprive him of office in 1506, granting it instead to Alexander Home, 3rd Lord Home. George d
1 CONC id however, gain the Barony of Crawford-Lindsay in 1510, and his father made over his estates of Kirriemuir, Abernethy, and Horsehopcleugh in the same year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1485 the Master of Angus was contracted in marriage to Margaret, daughter of Laurence Oliphant, 1st Lord Oliphant. It appears that this contract was not fulfilled as George was wed in 1488 to Elizabeth Drummond, daughter of Joh
1 CONC n Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond. The strictures of a small feudal society such as Scotland, it was found out some years after the marriage that it was within the prohibited degrees of consanguinity. The Master of Angus applied for a
1 CONC nd successfully obtained Papal dispensation in 1495.
1 CONT
1 CONT By Elizabeth Drummond, he had three sons and four daughters:
1 CONT Archibald Douglas, 6th Earl of Angus
1 CONT Sir George Douglas of Pittendreich
1 CONT William Douglas, Prior of Coldingham and Abbot of Holyrood
1 CONT Elizabeth Douglas, married John Hay, 3rd Lord Yester
1 CONT Alison Douglas, married David Home of Wedderburn
1 CONT Janet Douglas, married John Lyon, 6th Lord Glamis
1 CONT Margaret Douglas, married Sir James Douglas of Drumlanrig
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT In late August of 1513 the Master of Angus rode out with his father the Earl, and his younger brother Sir William Douglas of Glenbervie at the head of a large Douglas contingent and their adherents. On reaching the mustering point
1 CONC  at the Ellemford, north of Duns, they joined with the largest and most modern army that Scotland had ever fielded. The army proceeded under King James into England where it eventually met with the army of the Earl of Surrey at Fl
1 CONC odden Edge.
1 CONT
1 CONT A petulant Earl of Angus having had his advice snubbed by King James left the field and returned to Scotland, leaving the Master and his brother in charge of the Douglas contingent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nothing more is recorded of the Master of Angus except an anecdote recorded by David Hume of Godscroft recording the last moments of King James. When Sir Edward Stanley had broken the Scottish left under the Earl of Lennox and Ear
1 CONC l of Argyll. King James in the centre dismounted and prepared to make his stand amongst his spearmen. On noticing the Master of Angus still on horseback, he cried to him, "asking if it had been in the manner of his race to remain 
1 CONC mounted while their sovereign fought on foot". To which the Master replied asking whether "it was the fashion of the King of Scots to wear his mail and armorial bearings while fighting on foot". The master hit a raw nerve in James
1 CONC  chivalric mind and he replied "I dare fight upon my feet as well as you or any subject I have, and that without coat-armour or royal cognisance."
1 CONT
1 CONT The English Billmen now closed on the Scottish centre and King James was found within a spear length of Surrey. Whether Godscroft's anecdote is true or not, that the Master of Angus taunts drove him to his death, the Master was eq
1 CONC ual to the King in reckless gallantry. The Master's corpse was found amongst the twelve Scottish Earls and seventeen Lords which lost their lives. According to Godscroft over 200 men of the name of Douglas died also.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Douglas,_Master_of_Angus>
