1 CONT Archibald Douglas, 6th Earl of Angus was the son of George Douglas, Master of Angus <p10957.htm> and Elizabeth Drummond <p10957.htm>.2 He was born circa 1490.2 He married by contract, firstly, Margaret Hepburn <p10958.htm>, daught
1 CONC er of Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell <p10827.htm> and Janet Douglas <p10827.htm>, on 26 June 1509.2 He married, secondly, Lady Margaret Tudor <p10143.htm>, daughter of Henry VII Tudor, King of England <p10142.htm> and Eliza
1 CONC beth Plantagenet <p10142.htm>, on 4 August 1514 at Kinnoul Church.3,4 He and Lady Margaret Tudor were divorced on 11 March 1527/28.2 He married, thirdly, Margaret Maxwell <p10958.htm>, daughter of Robert Maxwell, 4th Lord Maxwell 
1 CONC <p10958.htm> and Janet Douglas <p10958.htm>, on 9 April 1543.5 He died in January 1556/57 at Tantallon Castle, Scotland, from erysipelas.5 He was buried at Abernethy, Perth & Kinross, Scotland.5  He succeeded to the title of 6th E
1 CONC arl of Angus [S., 1389] on 9 September 1513.2 He held the office of one of the Council of Regency between 1517 and 1521.2 He held the office of one of the Council of Regency between 1523 and 1526.2 He held the office of High Chanc
1 CONC ellor [Scotland] in August 1527.2 In 1528 the sentence of fordeiture was pronounced against him, and he retired to England.6 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) in 1528.2 In March 1542/43 his sentence of forfeiture was re
1 CONC versed.6 He fought in the Battle of Ancrum Muir in 1545.2 He fought in the Battle of Pinkie in 1547.2 He resigned as Earl of Angus on 31 August 1547 and had a regrant of the same, limited to his heirs male.6  This Earl had, for a 
1 CONC time, supreme power in Scotland, but in 1528 the young King James V escaped his hands and the sentence of forfeiture was passed against Angus and his kinsmen. On the death of James V, Angus returned to Scotland, and was restored t
1 CONC o his honours and possessions.2
