0 @N6000000005940228332@ NOTE XV-2
1 CONT of Terregles, Knight. M. 1547, Agnes Herries, daughter of William, Lord Herries. Became Lord Herries in 1566. D. January 20, 1582.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Castle of Scotland by Maurice Lindsay
1 CONT page 280
1 CONT Hills Castle - ... Sir John Maxwell, second son of Lord Maxwell, built the original tower in the middle of the sixteenth century. He married Agnes Herries, heiress to the third Lord Herries, and in his wife's right was duly made t
1 CONC he fourth Lord Herries. Descendants included the heir to the Maxwell barony, the Nithsdale earldom, and the owners of Traquair, Peebleshire. Hills passed by marriage to the McCullochs of Ardwall, who still own it.
1 CONT
1 CONT page 405
1 CONT Repentance Tower - A unique fortified watch-tower, Repentance Tower stands on the summit of Trailtow Hill six miles north-west of Annan. ...  "Repentance" is inscribed over the lintel. ... The builder of this mid-sixteenth-century
1 CONC  castle was John Maxwell, Lord Herries, whose seat was nearby Hoddam Castle.  Having pulled down a chapel to build Hoddam, he ahd to appease either (or both) his conscience and the Archbishop of Glasgow by providing a communal fac
1 CONC ility.  It withstood English siege in 1570 though, as Herries told the King (James VI King of Scotlandand England) nine years later, it 'mon be mendit of the litill defacing the Englische army maid of it' on that occasion.
1 CONT
1 CONT page 280-282
1 CONT Hoddam Castle - ... Hoddam was a stronghold of the Maxwells, held from the late fourteenth century by successive Lords Herries. The present structure, possibly incorporating part of its predessor, is said to have resulted in the d
1 CONC emolition of the chapel, causing such unease to the Lord Herries who ordered this action to be taken that he built a repentance tower to appease his conscience and the wrath of the Archbishop of Glasgow.  The sixth Lord Herries so
1 CONC ld Hoddam in 627 to Sir Richard Murray oof Cockpool....
