0 @N6000000003615466061@ NOTE  Translated 'Aeneid' into Scottish. 
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Douglas, Gawin, also spelled Gavin, (1474?-1522), Scottish poet andecclesiastic, the son of the 5th earl of Angus. After being educatedfor the priesthood at St. Andrews University, he was made provost atSaint Giles Cathedral, Edin
1 CONC burgh, in 1501. Pope Leo X appointed himbishop of Dunkeld in 1515, but Douglas became involved in thepolitical quarrels of the time, and before he could be consecrated, hewas imprisoned for a year by the regent. In 1516 he was con
1 CONC secratedbishop, but four years later was deprived of his jurisdiction afterhis nephew fell out of favor with the queen. He went to London to askHenry VIII for aid and died there of the plague.
1 CONT Douglas's poems include the allegories The Palice of Honour,  KingHart, and Conscience, all written in the Lowland Scots version ofEnglish. He is best known for his translation of Virgil's Aeneid, withoriginal prologues, one of th
1 CONC e first English versions of the Latinclassic that lacked the formal, classical diction and somber tone ofother translations.
1 CONT Source: "Douglas, Gawin," Microsoft(R) Encarta(R) 98 Encyclopedia. (c)1993-1997 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.
