0 @N6000000002187896962@ NOTE David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT David Lindsay, 1st Duke of Montrose (1440-December 25, 1495) was a Scottish nobleman.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford, and inherited the Earldom of Crawford on his father's death in 1453. During his political career he held the posts of Lord High Admiral of Scotland, Master of the Royal Ho
1 CONC usehold, Great Chamberlain and Justiciar.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1488 he was created Duke of Montrose, the first Scotsman not of royal blood to be granted a Dukedom. Lindsay had won the favour of James III, by remaining loyal to the king during the rebellion of his son Prince James. Lindsay 
1 CONC was deprived of his dukedom by James IV when he acceded to the throne later that year, but it was restored in 1489 for life only. On his death in 1495 the title therefore became extinct, although the Earldom contiues to this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Elizabeth Hamilton, daughter of James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton in 1459. They had three children before divorcing in the 1480s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander Lindsay, Master of Crawford (1485-September 16 1489) 
1 CONT Elizabeth Lindsay (b. 1495) 
1 CONT John Lindsay, 6th Earl of Crawford (c. 1495-1513) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
