0 @N6000000002188095583@ NOTE Battle of Pinkie Cleugh
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Battle of Pinkie Cleugh 
1 CONT Part of Anglo-Scottish Wars 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Date September 10, 1547 
1 CONT Location Musselburgh, Lothian, Scotland 
1 CONT Result Decisive English Victory 
1 CONT
1 CONT Combatants 
1 CONT Scots English 
1 CONT Commanders 
1 CONT Earl of Arran Duke of Somerset 
1 CONT Strength 
1 CONT Between 23000 and 36000 17000
1 CONT 30 warships 
1 CONT Casualties 
1 CONT 5000 killed
1 CONT 1500 prisoners 500 killed 
1 CONT Anglo-Scottish Wars 
1 CONT Flodden Field – Solway Moss – Ancrum Moor – Pinkie Cleugh 
1 CONT The Battle of Pinkie Cleugh, along the banks of the River Esk near Musselburgh on 10 September 1547, was part of the War of the Rough Wooing. It was the last battle to be fought between the Scottish and the English Royal armies an
1 CONC d the first "modern" battle to be fought in the British Isles. It was a catastrophic defeat for the Scots caused by poor discipline and weak command. In Scotland it is known as Black Saturday.
1 CONT
1 CONT This was historically significant as the first "modern" battle fought in Britain, demonstrating active cooperation between the infantry, artillery and cavalry with a naval bombardment in support of the land forces.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Background to the War 
1 CONT 2 The Campaign 
1 CONT 3 Prelude 
1 CONT 4 The battle 
1 CONT 5 Aftermath 
1 CONT 6 Clans at Pinkie 
1 CONT 7 Sources 
1 CONT 8 External link 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background to the War
1 CONT In the last years of his reign King Henry VIII had tried to secure an alliance with Scotland, and the marriage of the infant Mary Queen of Scots with his young son, the future Edward VI. When persuasion and diplomacy failed, he la
1 CONC unched a ruthless war against Scotland, an episode known as the Rough Wooing.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Henry died, Edward Seymour, uncle to Edward VI, became Protector with the title of Duke of Somerset and with initially unchallenged power. He too wished to forcibly ally Scotland to England by marrying Mary to Edward, and al
1 CONC so to impose an Anglican Reformation on the Scottish church establishment. Early in September 1547, he led a well-equipped army into Scotland, supported by a large fleet.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Campaign
1 CONT Somerset's army was partly composed of the traditional county levies, summoned by Commissions of Array and armed with longbow and bill as they had been at the Battle of Flodden, thirty years before. However, Somerset also had seve
1 CONC ral hundred German mercenary arquebusiers, a large and well appointed train of artillery, and 6000 horse, including a contingent of Spanish mounted arquebusiers under Don Pedro de Gamboa. The cavalry were commanded by Lord Grey, a
1 CONC nd the infantry by the Earl of Warwick, Lord Dacre of Gillesland, and Somerset himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Somerset advanced along the east coast of Scotland, to maintain contact with his fleet and thereby keep in supply. Scottish Border Reivers harassed his troops but could impose no major check.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, the Scottish Regent, the Earl of Arran, had levied a large army, consisting mainly of pikemen with contingents of Highland archers. Arran also had large numbers of guns, but these were apparently not as mobile or as wel
1 CONC l-served as Somerset's. His horse consisted only of 2000 lightly equipped riders under the Earl of Home, most of whom were potentially unreliable Borderers. His infantry were commanded by the Earl of Angus, the Earl of Huntly and 
1 CONC Arran himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arran occupied the slopes on the west bank of the River Esk to bar Somerset's progress. The Firth of Forth was on his left flank, and a large bog protected his right. Some fortifications were constructed, in which cannon and arque
1 CONC buses were mounted. Some guns pointed out into the Forth, to keep English warships at a distance.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Prelude
1 CONT On September 9, part of Somerset's army occupied Falside Hill (then known as Fawside), three miles east of Arran's main position. In an absurdly chivalric gesture, the Earl of Home led 1500 horsemen close to the English encampment
1 CONC  and challenged an equal number of English cavalry to fight. With Somerset's approval, Lord Grey accepted the challenge, but engaged the Scots with 1000 heavily armoured men-at-arms, and 500 lighter demi-lances. The Scottish horse
1 CONC men were badly cut up, and chased west for three miles. This action cost Arran most of his cavalry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later during the day, Somerset sent a detachment with guns to occupy the Inveresk Slopes, which overlooked the Scottish position. During the night, Somerset received two more anachronistic challenges from Arran. One request was fo
1 CONC r Somerset and Arran to settle the dispute by single combat. Another was for 20 champions from each side to decide the matter. Somerset rejected both proposals.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The battle
1 CONT On the morning of September 10, Somerset advanced his army to close up with the detachment at Inveresk. He found that Arran had moved his army across the Esk by a Roman bridge, and was advancing rapidly to meet him. Arran knew him
1 CONC self to be outmatched in artillery, and therefore tried to force close combat before the English artillery could deploy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arran's left wing came under fire from English ships offshore. (Their advance meant that the guns on their former position could no longer protect them.) They were disordered, and pushed into Arran's own division in the centre.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the other flank, Somerset threw in his cavalry to delay the Scots' advance. The Scottish pikemen successfully drove them off with the English suffering heavy casualties. Lord Grey himself was wounded by a pike thrust to the fac
1 CONC e.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, the Scottish army was now stalled, and under heavy fire from three sides from ships' cannon, artillery, arquebusiers and archers to which they could not reply. When they broke, the English cavalry rejoined the battle. Man
1 CONC y retreating Scots were slaughtered, or drowned as they tried to swim the fast-flowing Esk or cross the bogs.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Aftermath
1 CONT Although they had suffered a resounding defeat, the Scottish government refused to come to terms. The infant Queen Mary was smuggled out of the country, and sent to France to be betrothed to the young dauphin Francis. Somerset occ
1 CONC upied several Scottish strongholds and large parts of the Lowlands and Borders, but without peace, these garrisons became a useless drain on the Treasury of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT A violent Reformation in Scotland was only a few years away, but Scots refused to have Reformation imposed on them by England. During the battle, the Scots taunted the English soldiers as loons [persons of no consequence], tykes a
1 CONC nd heretics. A thousand monks from various orders formed part of the Earl of Angus's division. Many died in the battle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of the Scottish prisoners, few were nobles or gentlemen. It was claimed that most were dressed much the same as common soldiers, and therefore not recognised as being worth ransom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although the Scots blamed traitors within their own ranks for the defeat, it is probably fair to say that a Renaissance army defeated a Mediaeval army. Henry VIII had taken steps towards creating standing naval and land forces, wh
1 CONC ich formed the nucleus of the fleet and army with which Somerset gained the victory.
1 CONT
1 CONT The battle-site is now part of East Lothian.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Clans at Pinkie
1 CONT The following is a list of clans that fought at the battle of Pinkie.(list currently incomplete)
1 CONT
1 CONT House of Stuart (The Scottish Royal family) 
1 CONT Clan Buchanan 
1 CONT Clan Munro 
1 CONT Clan Graham 
1 CONT Clan MacKenzie 
1 CONT Clan Campbell 
1 CONT Clan Cameron 
1 CONT Clan MacFarlane 
1 CONT Clan MacLaren 
1 CONT Clan MacGregor 
1 CONT Clan Agnew 
1 CONT Clan Gordon 
1 CONT Clan Urquhart 
1 CONT Clan Farquharson 
1 CONT Clan Hunter 
1 CONT Clan Colquhoun 
1 CONT Clan Napier 
1 CONT Clan Mure 
1 CONT Clan Livingstone also known as Clan MacLea 
1 CONT Clan MacKinlay 
1 CONT Clan Cathcart 
1 CONT Clan Boyle 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Famous Scottish battles, Philip Warner, Leo Cooper, London, 1975, ISBN 0-85052-487-3
