0 @N6000000003043306000@ NOTE The elder son of the second marriage, John, the master of Forbes above mentioned, is stated to have been a young man of great courage and good education, but of a bold and turbulent spirit. On 10 October 1530, with two others, he 
1 CONC was indicted
1 CONT at the justiciary court at Dundee for the slaughter of Alexander Seton of Meldrum, but the same date he obtained a remissions for the crime, under the great seal. His father, Lord Forbes, appears to have been inculpated in the sam
1 CONC e charge, as
1 CONT on 27 August 1530, no fewer than seventeen landed gentlemen were fined for not appearing to enter on his assize. On 26 April 1536, he and four others became cautioners to satisfy the parties for assythment of the slaughter. On 12 
1 CONC June 1536, the
1 CONT master was accused by the earl of Huntly, before the king and privy council, of treasonable conspiracy against his majesty's life, and plotting the destruction of the king's army at Jedburgh. Protesting his innocence, he offered t
1 CONC o maintain it
1 CONT against his accuser by single combat, an ordeal often allowed under the feudal system. The council, on this occasion, did not authorize the resort to judicial combat, as it was styled; but Huntly was required to give a bond, under
1 CONC  the penalty
1 CONT of 30,000 marks, to make good his accusation, while the master was ordered to enter himself prisoner in the castle of Edinburgh, or to find security in 20,000 marks to stand his trial. On 8 December, in consequence of a warrant fr
1 CONC om the king,
1 CONT the council ordered Lord Forbes, as well as his son, to find security that they should remain in Edinburgh castle till they each found bail to the extent of 10,000 marks to answer to the charge when called upon. The former, as alr
1 CONC eady stated,
1 CONT was freed from the accusation, but the master was brought to trial before the high court of justiciary, 14 July 1537, and being found guilty, was condemned to be drawn on a hurdle through the causeway or High Street of Edinburgh, 
1 CONC and hanged on
1 CONT a gallows, and his body quartered as a traitor, his lands and goods being forfeited. To spare his relatives the more ignominious part of the sentence, he was beheaded instead of being hanged, on the 17th of the month. On the scaff
1 CONC old he
1 CONT declared his innocence of the crime of treason of which he had been convicted, but acknowledged that he deserved death for the murder of the laird of Meldrum. The principal evidence against him was an unprincipled follower named J
1 CONC ohn Strachan,
1 CONT who, on being refused a gift which he had asked of the master, went to the earl of Huntly, the enemy of the Forbeses, and accused him of having for a long time designed the death of the king, that the Douglases might be restored. 
1 CONC It is also
1 CONT stated that, to procure the conviction of the unfortunate master, Huntly, the main accuser, did not scruple to bribe his judges. Strachan, for being a participator in, and treasonably concealing, the alleged conspiracy, was ordere
1 CONC d by the
1 CONT king's letter to be banished beyond the water of Dee.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Anderson, W. The Scottish Nation. 3v. 1862, slightly edited]
