1 CONT It is said this Janet was exceedingly beautiful, and that young Creich havingfallen in love with her, under promise of marriage conveyed her away privately to St. Andrew's. He lodged her in a house if that city, and went himself i
1 CONC mmediately to the Castle to the Archbishop, who was his uncle
1 CONT , told him what he had done, and desired that he might be married to the lady. The Bishopat first refused to marry them, because neither of them had their parents' consent; but being told thaat she desired to be married, he sent f
1 CONC or her to the Castle, and seeing her exceedingly handsome, 
1 CONT and they both being very urgent with him, he made no further scruple to marry them.
1 CONT The Bishop then wrote letters both to his father and to her father, acquainting them with what he had done, and his motives for doing it. This lady's portion was six thous
1 CONT and marks, at that time a prodigious sum.
