0 @N6000000002188078222@ NOTE Domhnall of Islay, Lord of the Isles
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald or properly Domnhall of Islay, Lord of the Isles († 1423) was the son and successor of John of Islay, Lord of the Isles. The Lordship of the Isles was based in and around the Scottish west-coast island of Islay, but under J
1 CONC ohn of Islay had come to include most of the other islands of the west coast, as well as Morvern, Garmoran, Lochaber, Kintyre and Knapdale. While it is customary to portray the Lords of the Isles as divorced from the mainstream of
1 CONC  Scottish political life, and as representatives of an alternative brand of lordship, this view largely obscures the fact that Domhnall was only one of many magnates in late 14th and early 15th century Scotland who held large lord
1 CONC ships with little interference from the crown. The Douglas kindred of southern Scotland and the Albany Stewarts easily parallel Domhnall's lordship. Domhnall was, moreover, the grandson of King Robert II of Scotland and first cous
1 CONC in of King Robert III and took pride in his royal blood, even adopting the royal tressure to surround his coat of arms.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domhnall spent some of his first years as Lord of the Isles suppressing a revolt by his brother John Mór. John was Domhnall's younger brother, and resented his meagre portion of his father's inheritance. Although he was recognized
1 CONC  as heir-apparent (tanaiste), he only received patches of land in Kintyre and Islay in the inheritance. The rebellion started in 1387 and went on into the 1390s, and John found the support of the MacLean (MacGill-Eain) kindred. Ho
1 CONC wever, John and the MacLeans were eventually forced to submit to Domhnall, and by 1395 John Mór had been forced into Ireland where he entered the service of King Richard II of England and later established a MacDonald lordship in 
1 CONC Antrim.
1 CONT
1 CONT Suppression of the revolt enabled Domhnall to turn his attention northwards and eastwards. Most of the area to the north and east of the Lordship, that is Skye, Ross, Badenoch and Urquhart was controlled by Alexander Stewart, Earl
1 CONC  of Buchan, famously known as the "Wolf of Badenoch". Alexander had been building up a powerbase in the central Highlands and north of Scotland since the death of John Randolph, 3rd Earl of Moray in 1346. He had acquired control o
1 CONC f the lordship of Badenoch, the earldom of Buchan and the Justiciarship of Scotia, as well as effectively taking the place of the earls of Moray and Ross as "Lieutenant of the North". He was at once the de facto ruler of northern 
1 CONC Scotland as well as the means by which the crown itself exercised control.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, there had been complaints the activities of his caterans (warbands), and more importantly, Alexander's position was threatening not only to the crown, but also to the Euphemia I, Countess of Ross, her son Alexander and th
1 CONC e titular Dunbar Earl of Moray. Late in 1388, soon after becoming Guardian of the Kingdom, Robert Stewart, Earl of Fife (created Duke of Albany in 1398) deprived Alexander of the Justiciarship. The assault of Alexander's position 
1 CONC continued into the 1390s. Domhnall and his brother Alexander of Lochaber were in a perfect position to benefit. In 1394 the latter entered a 17-year agreement with the titular Earl of Moray taking over Alexander Stewart's role as 
1 CONC "protector" of the wealthy comital and episcopal lands in the Moray lowlands. The MacDonalds were is possession of Urquhart by the end of 1395, and gave control of the castle to the MacLean (MacGill'Eain) kindred of Duart.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, the Guardian soon turned his hostility against the MacDonalds. Alexander of Lochaber had been using his role as "protector" to further his own lordship, including granting episcopal lands to his military followers. In 139
1 CONC 8, Robert Stewart (now Duke of Albany) was called upon to take action, but the well-prepared expedition in the end came to nothing. Lochaber continued his activities, and in a raid of 1402 burned the burgh of Elgin, for which he w
1 CONC as excommunicated by William Spynie, bishop of Moray. However, later in the year Alexander visited Spynie to seek forgiveness.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domhnall himself was causing still further concern when in the same year, following the death of Alexander Leslie, Earl of Ross, Domhnall pressed the claims of Mariota, Alexander Leslie's sister and Domhnall's wife, to the possess
1 CONC ion of Ross, and attempted to gain control of the earldom. Sometime after 1405 but before 1411, Domhnall gained control of Dingwall Castle, the chief seat of the Earldom. In the year after the death of the nominal king Robert III 
1 CONC (1406), in August 1407 Domhnall sents emissaries to England to the heir of the throne, the captive James Stewart. King Henry IV of England sent his own emissaries the following year to negotiate an alliance against Albany.
1 CONT
1 CONT With control over the principle seat of the earldom of Ross and support of the exiled heir to the Scottish throne, in 1411 Domhnall felt strong enough to march against Albany's main northern ally, Alexander Stewart, Earl of Mar. A
1 CONC t the Battle of Harlaw Domhnall failed to inflict a decisive victory, and withdrew back to the western highlands. In the aftermath, Albany was able to retake Dingwall and seize control of Easter Ross. In 1415, the heir of Alexande
1 CONC r Leslie, Euphemia II, resigned the earldom to Albany. Domhnall prepared for war and proclaimed himself "Lord of Ross". Although Albany appointed his own son John Stewart to the earldom, Domhnall's wife continued to regard herself
1 CONC  as the rightful Countess.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domhnall died in 1423 on Islay, the historic centre of his Lordship. He was succeeded by his son Alexander.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Boardman, Stephen, The Early Stewart Kings: Robert II and Robert III, 1371-1406, (East Linton, 1996) 
1 CONT Brown, Michael, James I, (East Linton, 1994) 
1 CONT Oram, Richard, "The Lordship of the Isles, 1336-1545", in Donald Omand (ed.) The Argyll Book, (Edinburgh, 2005), pp. 123-39 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT John Lord of the Isles
1 CONT 1386-1423 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alexander 
0 @N6000000003615405954@ NOTE self styled 9th Earl of Ross.
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald MacDonald, Lord of the Isles (so designated as successor by his father in preference to his elder half-brothers (by 1st wife Amie dau of Roderick MacRuari) under the Celtic system called tanistry) and self-styled 9th Earl o
1 CONC f Ross in right of his wife; either in support of his claim to the Earldom or as a sovereign ruler in alliance with England or France he invaded the Scottish mainland with an army made up of men of the Isles and Ross but was check
1 CONC ed at Harlaw 24 July 1411 in a pyrrhic victory by the Regent of Scotland who despite his "victory" failed to prevent the MacDonald's styling themselves Earls of Ross for at least two generations as well as promulgating charters fr
1 CONC om Dingwall Castle, the chief place of the territory over which the Earls of Ross were supposed to hold sway, though in the immediate aftermath of the Battle of Harlaw the Regent captured the Castle.  [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------
1 CONT
1 CONT LORD OF THE ISLES (VII)
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald, Lord of the Isles, who m. Marry, suo jure Countess of Ross, daughter of Sir Walter Leslie and Eupheme, sur jure Countess of Ross.  In right of his marriage Donald claimed the Earldom of Ross, and the battle of Harlaw (1411
1 CONC ) was fought in consequence.  He d. about 1423.  [Complete Peerage VII:76-7]
0 @N6000000002188081903@ NOTE Alexander of Islay, Earl of Ross
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander de Yle
1 CONT (Alasdair MacDomhnaill) 
1 CONT Earl of Ross and Lord of the Isles 
1 CONT Reign 1423/37-1449 
1 CONT Died 1449 
1 CONT  Dingwall 
1 CONT Buried Fortrose Cathedral, Fortrose 
1 CONT Predecessor Domhnall of Islay 
1 CONT ' John Mór († 1428)
1 CONT Domhnall Ballach ?
1 CONT John of Islay (* 1434) 
1 CONT Successor John of Islay 
1 CONT Consort Elizabeth Seton 
1 CONT Issue John of Islay 
1 CONT Royal House Clann Domhnaill 
1 CONT Father Domhnall of Islay 
1 CONT Mother Mariota MacDonald
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander of Islay or Alexander MacDonald († 1449; Scottish Gaelic: Alasdair MacDomhnaill or MacDhòmhnaill) was a medieval Scottish nobleman, who succeeded his father Domhnall of Islay as Lord of the Isles (1423-49) and rose to th
1 CONC e rank of Earl of Ross (1437-49). His lively career, especially before he attained the earldom of Ross, led Hugh MacDonald, the 17th century author of History of the MacDonalds, to commemorate him as "a man born to much trouble al
1 CONC l his lifetime".[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Alexander, the Albany Stewarts and King James 
1 CONT 2 Captivity 
1 CONT 3 War against the King 
1 CONT 4 Second captivity 
1 CONT 5 Alexander, Earl of Ross 
1 CONT 6 Notes 
1 CONT 7 References 
1 CONT 8 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Alexander, the Albany Stewarts and King James
1 CONT  Oil--painting of James I, King of Scots (1406/24-1437). Inverness Castle, the location of Alexander's first captivity. Although the modern castle is not medieval, the site is.Alexander inherited his father Domhnall's alliance wit
1 CONC h King James I of Scotland against the power of the Albany Stewarts, who by the time James returned to Scotland from English captivity in 1424 ruled more of Scotland than King James could. By 1425 James had decided to destroy the 
1 CONC Albany Stewarts once and for all. In May of this year, Alexander attended the Stirling parliament which in the end ordered the execution of Murdoch (Muireadhach), Duke of Albany, along with his son Alexander and his ally Donnchadh
1 CONC , Earl of Lennox. However, the destruction of the Albany Stewarts removed the main reason for the co-operation between the King and the Lord of the Isles.[2] It is possible that, as Michael Brown believes, James acknowledged Alexa
1 CONC nder's control of the earldom of Ross as a reward for his support against Albany, as in 1426 Alexander used the style "Master of the Earldom of Ross".[3] However, Richard Oram takes a different view, and sees Alexander's adoption 
1 CONC of this title and occupation of much of the earldom as a provocation towards James, since it had past to him after the death of John Stewart, Earl of Buchan and Ross in 1424, and James was legally entitled the hold the earldom. Al
1 CONC exander's use of this title, if it were provocation, would have been compunded in the king's mind by the fact that Alexander's uncle John Mór was harbouring and protecting James Mór (or James the Fat), the son of Duke Murdoch, whi
1 CONC le James Mór was claiming James' throne.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Captivity
1 CONT At any rate, the king had certainly adopted a more hostile attitude towards Alexander. In 1428, James travelled into the north of Scotland both to assert his authority in Ross and to bring order to the north. James requested a mee
1 CONC ting with Alexander, and in August Alexander travelled in good faith to meet James at Inverness, where James was holding court. James however, in an act typical of his kingship, imprisoned Alexander, his mother Mariota (by whose l
1 CONC ineage Alexander claimed Ross) and around fifty of his followers, including his uncle and heir-designate John Mór, in the tower of Inverness Castle. Included among the other prisoners were Alexander's most important Ross allies; m
1 CONC en such as Aonghas Dubh MacAoidh, the chief of the MacKays of Strathnaver, a man who was reputed to have a warband 4000 strong; a prisoner also was Aonghas' son Niall Óg, the husband of the daughter of the head of the Foulis Munro
1 CONC  kindred of Easter Ross, one of Ross' most important families. The head of the Munros himself, George Munro, may also have been arrested, but if he was he was quickly released. William Leslie and John de Ross of Balnagown, two imp
1 CONC ortant landowners and kinsmen of Mariota, were also imprisoned, as were the heads of the Wester Ross Lochalsh MacMhathain (Matheson) and the Kintail MacChoinnich (MacKenzie) kindreds. Most of these men, including John Mór, seem to
1 CONC  have been released within a short time, although James took a few back to the south with him.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Michael Brown and the 17th century History of the MacDonalds, James attempted to do a deal with John Mór, probably offering him the Lordship of the Isles, to which he was heir and and for which he had revolted against
1 CONC  his brother Domhnall decades before. John however refused to negotiate until Alexander was released. Furthermore, King James' plans met disaster when his messenger James Campbell attempted to arrest "Johannis de Insulis" and kill
1 CONC ed him in the attempt. King James tried to distance himself from the killing, and had Campbell hanged. Before the end of 1428. Alexander was released on a promise of good behaviour.[6]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] War against the King
1 CONT  18th century illustration of depictions of west-highland warriors on some of the tombs of Oronsay Priory, founded by Alexander's grandfather John of Islay, Lord of the Isles sometime before 1358.Almost as soon as he was released,
1 CONC  Alexander was at war with the king. Domhnall Ballach ("the Freckled"), son of John Mór, may have been seeking revenge for his father's death, and if this was the case, he was supported by Alasdair Carrach ("the Curly"), Lord of L
1 CONC ochaber. Together, these two men, the two most important nobles in the lordship, probably helped pressure Alexander into war. In Spring 1429, Alexander's forces advanced on Inverness. Although Maol Choluim Mac an Tóisich ("Malcolm
1 CONC  MacKintosh"), head of Clan Chattan and custodian of the castle, managed to hold Alexander off, Alexander was still able to burn down the burgh. Alexander, meanwhile, was planning to support James Mór, son of Duke Murdoch, in his 
1 CONC claim on the Scottish throne. James Mór had become a serious threat to King James, not merely because was he likely to have the support of Murdoch's former vassals in Lennox, Menteith and Fife, but also because he had obtained the
1 CONC  backing of the King of England, who was angry that King James was ignoring his superior status and the terms of his release from captivity in England several years before. Now James Mór had the support of Alexander too.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT At this point, however, James was saved by the sudden death of his rival. As the Annals of the Four Masters reports:
1 CONT
1 CONT Semus Stiuard Mac Rígh Alban, & rioghdhamhna Alban beos iarna indarbadh a h-Albain i n-Erinn do écc, iar t-techt loingis ó fheraibh Alban for a chend dia Ríoghadh.[8] James Stuart, son of the King of Scotland, and Roydamna of Scot
1 CONC land, who had been banished from Scotland to Ireland, died, after the arrival of a fleet from the men of Scotland to convey him home, that he might be made king.[9] 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the summer, King James raised a large army and after a swift march north through Atholl and Badenoch, the royal army encountered Alexander somewhere around the borders of Lochaber and Badenoch. Although according to Walter Bowe
1 CONC r Alexander had 10,000 men, when the royal standard was unfurled the Chattan and Cameron kindreds switched over to the King. In the following engagement Alexander was defeated. Although Alexander got away, the king capitalized on 
1 CONC his victory by marching further north and seizing the castles of Urquhart and Dingwall. The king now sought Alexander's capture, and sent an expedition armed with artillery into the Hebrides. Alexander, who had probably fled to Is
1 CONC lay, found himself in a very difficult position, and on August 27, 1429 surrendered to King James at Holyrood Abbey, near the burgh of Edinburgh. King James was persuaded my his magnates to give Alexander grace, and sent him to Ta
1 CONC ntallon Castle under the custody of William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus, King James' nephew.[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Second captivity
1 CONT  Tantallon Castle, the location of Alexander's second captivity.While he had Alexander in custody, King James delegated the northern campaign to Alexander Stewart, Earl of Mar, with more minor roles going to Maol Choluim Mac an Tó
1 CONC isich, Alexander Seton of Gordon, Hugh Fraser and Aonghas de Moravia. James would never again return to Ross or Moray. Mar was given support in his role as Lieutenant when Alan Stewart, the second son of Walter Stewart, Earl of At
1 CONC holl, was made Earl of Caithness in Spring 1430. The royal earldoms of Buchan and Ross, and the castle of Urquhart were put under Mar's control; by 1431 the lordship of Lochaber, held by Alasdair Carrach, was assigned to Mar's com
1 CONC mand; and by 1432 Mar had received papal dispensation to marry Margaret Seton, the mother of the heiresses to the earldom of Moray, which he would administer on their behalf. James, moreover, arranged a marriage between Lachlan Ma
1 CONC cLean, captain of the MacLeans of Duart, an important vassal kindred of the Lordship of the Isles, to Mar's daughter, bringing Mar's influence into the Lordship of the Isles itself. In 1431 Aonghas de Moravia was sent on a campaig
1 CONC n against Aonghas Dubh MacAoidh in Strathnaver. However the main campaign was in Lochaber, where Mar hoped to make his status as Lord of Lochaber a reality. In both campaigns, however, the results were defeats for the king's force
1 CONC s. At the Battle of Inverlochy Mar's forces were met by both Domhnall Ballach and Alasdair Carrach of Lochaber; although Mar managed to make a long escape on foot back to Kildrummy Castle, the Earl of Caithness and 990 men were sl
1 CONC ain. In Strathnaver, at the Battle of Drum na Coup, Aonghas Dubh MacAoidh was also victorious over royal forces. Both defeats were incurred before September 1431. James' first reaction was to raise taxes, taxes which were granted 
1 CONC on October 16, in order to deliver a counter-attack; but this revenue was insufficient and James had other problems to deal with. King James therefore arranged a reconciliation with Alexander, who was pardoned for past offences an
1 CONC d released from captivity.[11]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Alexander, Earl of Ross
1 CONT  The seal of Alexander. It includes his arms, which contains the arms of the Lordship of the Isles and the Earldom of Ross, surrounded by a royal tressure, indicating Alexander's royal blood as the grandson of King Robert II of Sc
1 CONC otland.Alexander would never again be the king's enemy, and remained subdued for the next few years. Luck, however, was on Alexander's side. The earl of Mar was in either his fifties or sixties, and his son and heir Thomas had alr
1 CONC eady died in 1430.[12] When the earl himself died in 1435, James' settlement in the north collapsed. James was in Mar by June 1436, where he was taking control of the earldom. It was probably in this time that James finally acknow
1 CONC ledged Alexander as earl of Ross, the only magnate who could now offer security in the north-eastern Highlands. Alexander not only received control of Dingwall, but Inverness too, which he would hold until at least 1447. Morever, 
1 CONC the Ross earldom came with Kincardine in the Mearns, Kingedward in Buchan and Greenan in Ayrshire. By January 1437 Alexander was styling himself "Earl of Ross" in his charters, and this style was acknowledged in royal documents by
1 CONC  1439. Finally, by February 1439, Alexander had been appointed Justiciar of Scotia, an office which made Alexander the chief legal official in the Kingdom of Scotland.[13]
1 CONT
1 CONT Having achieved the chief object of his career, Alexander spent the last decade of his life consolidating his position in Ross. His charters seem to indicated that he was chiefly based at the castles of Dingwall and Inverness, and
1 CONC  rarely anywhere else. The large amount of charters issued by Alexander at Inverness is probably explained by his role as Justiciar of Scotia. Alexander's move east led to less direct lordship in the west, his original political h
1 CONC eartland. Alexander's bastard sons Uisdean ("Hugh") and Gilleasbaig ("Celestine") were given Sleat and Lochalsh respectively, Domhnall Ballach became more independent in Islay and Kintyre, the MacLeod kindred took greater control 
1 CONC in Skye and Lewis, the MacLean greater control in Mull and the MacKintosh greater control in Lochaber.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander MacDonald of Islay died at Dingwall in May 1449. He was buried in Fortrose Cathedral. Alexander had two partners with whom he fathered offspring. The daughter of MacPhee produced a number of bastard sons, and Elizabeth, 
1 CONC the daughter of Alexander Seton, lord of Gordon and Huntly, produced John, who succeeded Alexander aged only fifteen.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Norman MacDougall, "Achilles' Heel? The Earldom of Ross, the Lordship of the Isles, and the Stewart Kings, 1449-1507", in Edward J. Cowan & R. Andrew McDonald (eds.), Alba: Celtic Scotland in the Medieval Era, (Edinburgh, 2000),
1 CONC  p. 248. 
1 CONT ^ Richard Oram, "The Lordship of the Isles, 1336-1545", in Donald Omand (ed.) The Argyll Book, (Edinburgh, 2005), pp. 132-3. 
1 CONT ^ Michael Brown, James I, (East Linton, 1994), p. 58. 
1 CONT ^ Richard Oram, "The Lordship of the Isles", p. 133; Michael Brown, James I, pp. 74-5 
1 CONT ^ Michael Brown, James I, pp. 97-9. 
1 CONT ^ Ibid. p. 100; Boardman, The Campbells, 1250-1513, (Edinburgh, 2006), p. 127, however has given reasons to doubt that this John was John Mór, but acknowledges that it may well have been him. 
1 CONT ^ Michael Brown, James I, pp. 101-2 
1 CONT ^ Annals of the Four Masters, s.a. 1429.1, here. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of the Four Masters, s.a. 1429.1, here. 
1 CONT ^ Michael Brown, James I, pp. 100-3. 
1 CONT ^ Michael Brown, James I, pp. 103-5, 136-40, 147; Richard Oram, "The Lordship of the Isles", p. 133. 
1 CONT ^ Michael Brown, James I, pp. 147-8. 
1 CONT ^ Michael Brown, James I, pp. 159-60; Richard Oram, "The Lordship of the Isles", p. 134; Norman MacDougall, "Achilles' Heel?", p. 248. 
1 CONT ^ Richard Oram, "The Lordship of the Isles", p. 134-5 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
