1 CONT Guardian to James v
1 CONT Governor of Stirling and Edinburgh castles
0 @N6000000002115996190@ NOTE Present at death of James V 1542, and protected Mary from Henry VIII, escorting her to France 1548.
0 @N6000000002115996146@ NOTE Mary Queen of Scots, in restoring him to the title in 1565, created a new title which passed only down the male line.  The original title was restored to him as the true blood descendant at the same time and this could pass throug
1 CONC h the female line.  This title had previously been given to Lord James Stuart, who was then granted the Earldom of Moray, and became Regent of Scotland until he was murdered in 1567.  The 11th Earl of Kellie successfully claimed  
1 CONC the later title in 1875, while the original title passed through the female line.
0 @N6000000002188043962@ NOTE John Erskine, 17th Earl of Mar
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John Erskine, 7th Earl of Mar (died 29 October 1572), regent of Scotland, was a son of John, 5th Lord Erskine (d. 1552), who was guardian of King James V, and afterwards of Mary, Queen of Scots.
1 CONT
1 CONT John, who succeeded his father as 6th Lord Erskine in 1552, and joined the religious reformers, but he was never very ardent in the cause, although he subscribed the letter asking the Protestant reformer John Knox to return to Sco
1 CONC tland in 1557. The custody of Edinburgh Castle was in his hands, and during the struggle between the regent, Mary of Guise, and the lords of the Congregation he appears to have acted consistently in the interests of peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Mary Stuart returned to Scotland in 1561 Lord Erskine was a member of her council, he favored her marriage with Henry Stuart, Lord Darnley, and his wife, Annabella Murray, called by Knox a "verray Jesabell", was a frequent co
1 CONC mpanion of the queen. In 1565 Erskine was granted the earldom of Mar. As guardian of James, afterwards King James VI, he prevented the young prince from falling into the hands of James Hepburn, 4th Earl of Bothwell, and when the S
1 CONC cottish nobles rose against Mary and Bothwell, Mar was one of their leaders; he took part in the government of Scotland during Marys imprisonment at Lochleven, and also after her subsequent abdication. In September 1571 he was cho
1 CONC sen regent of Scotland, but he was overshadowed and perhaps slighted by the James Douglas, 4th Earl of Morton, and he died at Stirling on the 29 October 1572.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his son John Erskine, 2nd Earl of Mar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT John Erskine Earl of Mar Succeeded by:
1 CONT John Erskine 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Earl of Mar 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
0 @N6000000001877484023@ NOTE 1st Earl of Mar
1 CONT Regent of Scotland
