0 @N6000000001783359800@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir James Kennedy of Dunure, younger was the son of Sir Gilbert Kennedy of Dunure.  He lived at Dunure, Ayrshire, Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Lady Mary Stewart, daughter of Robert III Stewart, King of Scotland and Annabel Drummond, circa 1404.  There children were:
1 CONT Sir John Kennedy d. a 1434
1 CONT Mary Kennedy
1 CONT Gilbert Kennedy of Dunure, 1st Lord Kennedy b. c 1406, d. a 6 Mar 1478/79
1 CONT James Kennedy b. 1405/6, d. Jul 1465 Bishop of Dunkeld and St Andrews
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died before 8 November 1408, killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://thepeerage.com/p10823.htm#i108224>
0 @N6000000005940228030@ NOTE of Gleneagles
0 @N6000000005940228024@ NOTE X-1
1 CONT Served heir of his father, October 16-1421. Created Lord Maxwell, c. 1440.  Built the castle of Mearns. M. first a daughter of Herbert Herries, of Gleneagles, and second Katherine, daughter of Sir William Seton, of Seton. D. befor
1 CONC e February 14, 1453-4.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Castles of Scotland by Maruice Lindsay
1 CONT page 352
1 CONT Mearns Castle - A mile south of Newton Mearns, on the outskirts of Glasgow, stands Mearns Tower, a four-storey keep built under royal warrant by Herbert, Lord Maxwell, in 1449. ...  The lands of Mearns came to the Maxwells through
1 CONC  marriage with a Pollock heiress in 1300. One Lord maxwell fell at Flodden (XIII-1 John, fourth Lord Maxwell. M. Agnes, daughter of Sir Alexander Stewart, of Garlies. Killed at Flodden, September 9, 1513. p.g.9). His son found him
1 CONC self in the Tower of London, having been taken prisoner at Solway Moss (XIV-4 Edward, a prisoner at Solway in 1542 p.g.9). James VI (King of Scotland & England, 1567-1625) instructed the fifth Lord Maxwell to deliver up Mearns to 
1 CONC the Crown (XIV-1 Robert, fifth Lord Maxwell. Guardian of the West Marshes, 1517 and 1540. In 1540 he got from James V. (King of Scots, 1513-1542) a charter of Novodamus of the Barony of Maxwell. D. July 9, 1546. M. first Janet Dou
1 CONC glas, of Drumlanrig, issue, and second, Agnes, daughter of James, Earl of Buchan, no issue. p.g9) Mearns was sold in the mid-seventeenth century to Sir George Maxwell of Nether Pollock, from whose family it eventually passed to th
1 CONC e Shaw-Stewarts.
0 @N6000000005940228108@ NOTE XI-1
1 CONT second Lord Maxwell, served heir of his father, February 4, 1543. m. Janet Forester. D. May, 1485.
0 @N6000000005940228054@ NOTE XI-2
1 CONT of Triswald. Obtained Monreith in 1481-2. Ancestor of the Maxwells, of Monreith.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Castles of Scotland, by Maurice Lindsay
1 CONT p 194
1 CONT Dowies House, otherwise known as Moure, stands in the valley of the Monreith burn, two miles north-east of Monreith village. ... Moure, as the house was then known, was a Maxwell possession, put up in the late seventeenth century 
1 CONC near the site of an earlier castle. the descendants of Sir Edward Maxwell of Tinwald (XI-2 Sir Edward Maxwell, of Triswald. Obtained Monreith in 1481-2. Ancestor of the Maxwells, of Monreith. pg. 9), second son of the first Lord M
1 CONC axwell (X-1 Sir Herbert Maxwell, of Caerlaverock. Served heir of his father, October 16, 1421. Created Lord Maxwell, c. 1440. Built the castle of Mearns. M. first a daughter of Herbert Herries, of Gleneagles, and second Katherine,
1 CONC  daughter of Sir William Seton, of Seton. D. before February 14, 1453-4. p.g.9), occupied this castle until 1683, when they moved to the nearby ruined Myretoun Tower, which they bought from the McCullochs. Altered the rooflines, D
1 CONC owies House became a farmer's home. It has recently been admirably restored by Stewart Tod for the Landmark Trust.
